HT_DATA.load("detail", {"SCAGc-rkIfo":{"wy":"Repeatedly called the best way to understand ADHD, and recommended to anyone before commenting on it.","q":{"x":"Here's the best explanation I've ever seen of the subject","i":23522394,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23522394,"author":"RickS","month":"2020-06","story":"Night owls have more grey matter in their brains than early birds","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best explanation of ADHD the commenter has seen.","quote":"Here's the best explanation I've ever seen of the subject","text":"ADHD is terribly misnamed, and doesn't usually manifest as physical hyperactivity in adults. It's an executive function disorder. This means things like time blindness – difficulty conceptualizing and persisting tasks into the future, and difficulty keeping short term goals in mind. I think about it like packet loss, but for intent. With healthy executive function, one's goals and intents are persistent streams that can be held on to like a train track. (This is based on other people's description of how their mind works – I would consider that ability a superpower). With ADHD, the tracks will just fall out like a trapdoor every so often – sometimes every few seconds. You have to reinitiate the connection, but because the thing that failed is the same thing that remembers what to connect to, I have to reestablish what I want to do in the first place, and it's usually not the thing I was doing before. So I end up shooting around in semi-productive bursts, unable to see things to completion. The other counterintuitive thing is hyperfocus – people with ADHD periodically experience the inverse. I spent nearly every waking moment in the last 3 days working intently on a software project I'm interested in. Short bouts of intense concentration (especially if you don't feel in control of their application) do not mean you don't have ADHD. They could mean the opposite. Here's the best explanation I've ever seen of the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19896548,"author":"thrav","month":"2019-05","story":"How I Run a Company with ADHD","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Barkley's talk completely altered commenter's understanding and led to treatment.","quote":"Dr. Barkley’s talk completely altered my understanding of the disorder.","text":"I also like the idea that it would be better labeled, “intention deficit disorder”. Dr. Barkley’s talk completely altered my understanding of the disorder. I always knew I had it. My life roughly paralleled the author’s. After failing out of school, I found workarounds that got me through the second time (thanks to extremely supportive parents). This video, combined with my wife’s urging, caused me to find a legitimate doctor who specialized in it (he’s ADHD too), and get treatment. I was already doing well with my coping mechanisms, but my life took off like a rocket with treatment. If anything in the article or the comments resonates, I would watch this ASAP: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12801723,"author":"brazzledazzle","month":"2016-10","story":"Noisy Coworkers And Other Sounds Are A Distraction In Workplace","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helps understand why you fail so you can work around gaps.","quote":"they really help with understanding exactly what you're dealing with","text":"One important thing to note for people reading this is that hyperactivity doesn't always persist into adulthood with the other symptoms or is greatly reduced and is often turned inward (mental fidgeting). As far as your firings go, if you haven't watched this video and/or read this doctor's books they really help with understanding exactly what you're dealing with: [VIDEO] It's long but worth watching at faster playback speed and in manageable chunks. Key takeaways: \"Attention deficit\" is less of a symptom and more of a measurable consequence of other symptoms: Impaired inhibition/impulse control, excessive irrelevant activity and impaired persistence (think discipline). Once you understand why you fail you can start addressing or working around your gaps, particularly the ones that any medication you may be on fail to address."},{"id":11262611,"author":"brazzledazzle","month":"2016-03","story":"ADHD is vastly overdiagnosed and many children are just immature, say scientists","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear examples of what ADHD is like, useful beyond parents.","quote":"this is basically the best video you could watch in my opinion","text":"If you'd like to learn more about it this is basically the best video you could watch in my opinion: [VIDEO] It's quite long so I'd watch it at 1.5x and/or in chunks. Even though it's for parents of children with ADHD it makes it clear with good examples what they're dealing with. Also worth keeping in mind that the disorder is different for adults in some ways."},{"id":10156998,"author":"brazzledazzle","month":"2015-09","story":"To Stop Procrastinating, Start by Understanding the Emotions Involved","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Barkley's lecture is a great starting point to understand ADHD.","quote":"For anyone who wants to really understand the disorder","text":"Yep. ADHD is not a problem of perception, it's a problem of motivation. A problem of inhibition. \"ADHD is a failure to direct behavior forward in time.\" \"They are best thought of as executive deficits, not attention deficits.\" For anyone who wants to really understand the disorder (even those who have it and think they have a good grasp on it) Dr. Russell A Barkley, PhD is a great place to start. In particular this video, \"Essential Ideas for Parents\": [VIDEO] If you have ADHD I can't recommend his books enough."},{"id":9896430,"author":"brazzledazzle","month":"2015-07","story":"Coping with context-switching madness","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helps understand what people with ADHD suffer through.","quote":"I encourage everyone to watch this before they comment on ADHD","text":"I encourage everyone to watch this before they comment on ADHD: [VIDEO] While it won't make you an expert, it will perhaps help you understand what it is people with ADHD suffer through. There are bad doctors and bad policies out there which are being resolved. They don't diagnose young children anymore even if it seems likely since some children will \"catch up\" and stop exhibiting symptoms. This is the source of many teens and adults today that discover that once they stop taking medication they don't need it. As our understanding of the disorder grows so does our ability to accurately diagnose it. That said, while I can't speak for the person you replied to that shit is incredibly frustrating. Your empathy is unusual and appreciated but regardless of the rate of misdiagnosis you are not a doctor and are admittedly entirely ignorant about it. I can't emphasize enough to those reading this that no matter how much you might doubt it, the people who work in this field do in fact know more about it than you do and making an armchair diagnosis or questioning a doctor's diagnosis does nothing but make you look like the kind of know-it-all that really knows nothing while maligning someone who has to read ignorant comments like yours in every damn discussion on the internet where ADHD comes up. I know it's easy to think you've got all of the answers when understanding complex problems comes naturally but these doctors don't spend nearly a decade or more in school sitting around with"},{"id":9480264,"author":"nickysielicki","month":"2015-05","story":"Entrepreneurial Tendencies Among People with ADHD","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Will completely change how you see ADHD.","quote":"You should all watch this video, it will completely change how you see ADHD... It's long, and it's worth it.","text":"You should all watch this video, it will completely change how you see ADHD... It's long, and it's worth it.[0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9481274,"author":"anupshinde","month":"2015-05","story":"Entrepreneurial Tendencies Among People with ADHD","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing 3-hour video on ADHD that shifted commenter's view.","quote":"I watched this amazing 3 hour long video/info on ADHD","text":"I watched this amazing 3 hour long video/info on ADHD shared by nickysielicki in this thread below. [VIDEO] After viewing this video, it seems like many people diagnosed for ADHD could be in the CDD/SCT like. This distinction was not made few years back. In fact ADHD sounds seriously scary after looking at this video."},{"id":9437118,"author":"nickysielicki","month":"2015-04","story":"ADHD: A Disease called childhood","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Needed for an informed opinion on ADHD.","quote":"If you want to have an informed opinion on ADHD, watch this video","text":"Via /r/adhd: >\"In her book she advocates a \"gluten free diet\" and makes the repeated claims that \"big pharma\" are pushing these drugs on kids, who are simply a bit \"different\"\" I don't know about you, but IMO if a person is advocating against gluten to cure ADHD, they probably aren't worth listening to on anything. If you want to have an informed opinion on ADHD, watch this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42608490,"author":"dmacedo","month":"2025-01","story":"Ask HN: My 12 year-old nephew just got diagnosed with ADHD, any advice?","stance":"rec","why":"Russell Barkley on ADHD, shared for parents of a newly diagnosed child.","quote":"I would echo this and share with the parents","text":"I would echo this and share with the parents: [VIDEO] Also Russell Barkley has since fully retired though has been very active in diaseminating what's happening in (good and bad) research, plus other lectures on [LINK]"},{"id":24157339,"author":"thrav","month":"2020-08","story":"Behaviors resembling ADHD may be linked to increased entrepreneurial behavior","stance":"rec","why":"Beginning explains how the ADHD brain forms differently.","quote":"it’s explained in the beginning of this lecture","text":"The brain forms differently in ADHD individuals. Certain regions have slower or stunted growth. If you’d like to know more, it’s explained in the beginning of this lecture: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19896598,"author":"thrav","month":"2019-05","story":"How I Run a Company with ADHD","stance":"rec","why":"Look into it to understand ADHD's wide-ranging impacts.","quote":"ADHD is a big disorder. Look into it","text":"It a literal brain development impairment. We can look at the bright side, I do too, but it is a biological difference in our brain construction from the norm, and it has wide ranging impacts that go far beyond our ability to do work. Do you have extreme bouts of emotional distress? Emotional / angry outbursts? Blurt things out that get you into trouble? Experience difficulty with dating? Lose shit constantly? Participate in risky / dangerous / mid-guided behaviors? ADHD is a big disorder. Look into it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18575156,"author":"ts1543636885","month":"2018-12","story":"When starting school, younger children are more likely to be diagnosed with ADHD","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley's lecture offered as a helpful resource.","quote":"Lecture by Dr. Barkley. Essential Ideas for Parents","text":"> ... he started hitting himself on the head ... :( I know what that feels like. I see that there are numerous helpful responses already. Here's a couple links: 1) How to ADHD ( [LINK] ) 2) Lecture by Dr. Barkley. Essential Ideas for Parents: [VIDEO] Type \"Dr. Barkley ADHD\" to see shorter snippets."},{"id":12354309,"author":"joshuak","month":"2016-08","story":"Overselling A.D.H.D.","stance":"rec","why":"A more complete talk by Dr. Barkley on the reality of ADHD.","quote":"If you are interested in understanding the reality of ADHD I'd suggest watching some of the talks by Dr. Russel Barkely.","text":"These are very old studies. It is now known that ADHD is a genetic disorder. In fact it has unusually high correlation. It is absolutely not caused by environmental factors. Though there is one virus (that I'm aware of) that may cause it. It is not only an issue of attentiveness, but ranter all executive functions. The poor naming of the disorder, and the observability of a persons focus, causes people to over emphasize the inattentiveness symptom. If you are interested in understanding the reality of ADHD I'd suggest watching some of the talks by Dr. Russel Barkely. Here: [LINK] For a more complete talk: Here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9072040,"author":"falcolas","month":"2015-02","story":"There are too many shiny objects and it is killing me","stance":"rec","why":"Shows true problems faced by those with ADHD.","quote":"I recommend watching the following video to see what the true problems encountered by those with ADHD are.","text":"> You don't think in the whole time humans have evolved that we haven't had this kind of condition already in some form Of course we have. Know how we, as humans, dealt with people with mental disabilities (those who survived natural selection) in the past? A combination of \"Man up\", and sanitariums. We told them, \"Stop being <pick a mental condition here>, or we'll throw you in a hole from which you'll never escape, and we can stop worrying about it.\" These two methods \"worked\" for a lot of mental conditions we just now have names, diagnoses, and cures for. > Or is it maybe because we are fed the idea that we _need_ to be focused If you think that the only problem with having ADHD is the lack of focus, you don't know what ADHD is. I recommend watching the following video to see what the true problems encountered by those with ADHD are. [VIDEO] > those who have come up with the idea that we should be medicated for this condition Like those who have received medication, and are suddenly able to think like everyone else is capable of? The medication is not a crutch, it's not a magical focus pill, it's not a \"i can focus better because I have more energy\"... it's a bridge between \"can't\" and \"can\". It doesn't make you focus, it makes you capable of focusing. It doesn't make you not procrastinate, it lets you actually consider the consequences which are separated from the action by a time period greater than 30 minutes. It doesn't expand your memory, it makes you capable of acces"},{"id":34948725,"author":"spiralx","month":"2023-02","story":"Buddhist Temple Left Without Monks After They All Test Positive for Meth","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Haven't been able to get my new prescription yet (ran out yesterday) but other than that, I take 30mg/day dexedrine XR. Heh, snap. I ran out of my medication yesterday and haven't got my new prescription either. What got me this month was that I only got 28 days worth of pills which is so petty - I don't get 31 days worth on the majority of months that have more than 30 days! But anyway, that aside I'm glad to be in the UK where I get my 108mg/day methylphenidate and 10mg/day Ramipril for a tenner a month. > I've consistently been recommended ritalin/methylphenidate as something to try, I do plan on trying it at some point but dexedrine helps me so much I don't think I need to go to anything else. I still want to try methylphenidate though to see how it is. Be aware that you might be underwhelmed when compared to dexedrine, the effects are way less obvious immediatetly - methylphenidate is closer to cocaine chemically than amphetamine in fact. There's an old (2006) book called \"Speed, Ecstasy, Ritalin: The Science of Amphetamines\" that's pretty interesting if you want more detail. > Probably~ I was somewhat high for 4-5 days at first but it was actually incredibly distracting because I would do crazy stuff like fall over in the shower because it felt too good for me to focus. That shower was just repaired, I nearly broke it again. Lol, I've managed to destroy a freezer and my own PC through amphetamine-induced over-zealous housecleaning sessions. > Always nice hearing someo"},{"id":26805942,"author":"interleave","month":"2021-04","story":"How to Read Books When You Have ADHD","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I very much agree and share a similar experience. Struggling with ADHD is not a matter of not being able to focus. Part of the issue is directing and keeping attention on non-pleasant tasks that are stacked up against a brain chronically low on dopamine and norepinephrine transmission. As someone with diagnosed and treated ADHD, these articles are misleading. In my opinion it's dangerous to claim \"Do this ONE behavioural thing and your ADHD goes away (or gets easier.)\" After struggling with ADHD for 25+ years I can tell you: These life-hacks don't work. Please, consult with a medical professional instead. It'll save you years of agony and disappointment. Again, if you have – or suspect – ADHD, work with a psychiatrist to discuss diagnosis and treatment options. Period. Quoting Dr. Russell Barkeley \"ADHD is one, if not the best-understood psychiatric conditions. We understand it for several decades now - it's also called the diabetes of psychiatry because there's treatment\"[^1]. So, we know what it is and have known how to treat it. It's a neuro-genetic disease, and we have had working medications for – I think – 50 years including the obvious methylphenidate and dexamphetamines. After reading the article and so many like it, I feel frustrated because I think they're predatory. They're targeting neuro-divergent people like myself. And boy, did I fall into those traps over and over again until I enrolled in actual medical treatment. I'll assume that Jacqueline writes and publis"},{"id":12354073,"author":"joshuak","month":"2016-08","story":"Overselling A.D.H.D.","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is FUD. ADHD is well researched, and well understood by those who study it. The problem is that people think ADHD is just an issue of self discipline or attentiveness. It is not. It is a serious disorder with serious effects that are unambiguous. No doubt there are stimulant seeking people who claim to have ADHD to get stimulants, and people without ADHD representing that they have ADHD may lead to the impression that the disorder is ambiguous when it isn't. Also if someone has ADHD for long periods of time they may have co-mobidity with other disorders such as depression, etc. and this also may muddy perceptions. ADHD is not strictly an attention issue. It's named for attention because that is an early and obvious symptom but it is much worse then that. Calling the disorder an attention deficit disorder has turned out to be very bad as it leads people to the wrong mental model of the disorder. Thinking that it's just laziness on the part of the patent or the patents parents, in the case of children. This is a tragically misguided perception, based in ignorance and the poor choice of names. In actuality ADHD is a developmental disorder that is a more general impairment of executive function that includes regulation of emotion, working memory and the internal perception of time and other effects in addition to inattentiveness. This impairment is due to abnormal dopamine transport that can be clearly observed in MRI studies. These studies have been repeated many times in m"}],"r":[["KT18KJouHWg",1],["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["v19cu2hj2Ms",1],["SEwTjZvy8vw",1],["o_AIw9bGogo",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["UJ7Qc3bsxjI",1],["I8Xc2_FtpHI",1],["X2wLP0izeJE",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1]]},"W3aieHjyNvw":{"wy":"HN treats it as the best real-world AAA example of ECS and game netcode, and cites it constantly in discussions of data-oriented design.","q":{"x":"This Overwatch GDC talk is the best breakdown/example of data-oriented ECS in a AAA game that I know of","i":27664002,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38730175,"author":"bcrosby95","month":"2023-12","story":"I'm open-sourcing my game engine","stance":"strong_rec","why":"ECS talk with useful networking tips","quote":"There's an awesome talk by someone in the overwatch team in youtube.","text":"There's an awesome talk by someone in the overwatch team in youtube. It's about ECS but it has a big section on networking that had some useful tips. Edit: here it is - [VIDEO]"},{"id":28456218,"author":"adampk","month":"2021-09","story":"Unity patents ECS","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite resource explaining ECS and netcode in Overwatch","quote":"My favorite resource to explain ECS + NetCode","text":"[VIDEO] \"In this 2017 GDC session, Blizzard's Timothy Ford explains how Overwatch uses the Entity Component System (ECS) architecture to create a rich variety of layered gameplay.\" (My favorite resource to explain ECS + NetCode)"},{"id":27664002,"author":"Bekwnn","month":"2021-06","story":"Data-oriented design or why you might shoot yourself in the foot with OOP (2009)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best breakdown of data-oriented ECS in a AAA game","quote":"This Overwatch GDC talk is the best breakdown/example of data-oriented ECS in a AAA game that I know of","text":"> The components on the entity define not only it's data but also it's behavior by the set of systems that act on the corresponding components. And you can take these object definitions and inherit them to add additional behavior or change the existing behavior by adding more components to the new definition. This seems to miss what ECS actually is, unless you're just referring to the old-school way of doing entity components and not the data-oriented way. Data-oriented ECS way of doing things is to separate state and behaviour. Entity components essentially become structs where their only behaviour is potentially some getter/setter utilities. Behaviours are then state-less systems (just functions, essentially) which act on a set of components. For example, a PhysicsUpdateBehaviour might take in a RigidBodyComponent and a HealthComponent to perform a physics update and apply physics/fall based damage. The main benefit of ECS (imo) isn't even really performance. It makes code in complicated game projects much easier to manage by clarifying the game loop and by making it much more obvious how and when entity state is being modified. It's the kind of thing that potentially complicates a smaller project, but makes larger more complex projects easier to manage. This Overwatch GDC talk is the best breakdown/example of data-oriented ECS in a AAA game that I know of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25937558,"author":"nightski","month":"2021-01","story":"OO in Python is mostly pointless","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How ECS made Overwatch's interacting subsystems easier to understand","quote":"There is a fantastic GDC video about Blizzard's use of ECS in Overwatch","text":"There is a fantastic GDC video about Blizzard's use of ECS in Overwatch and how it made it far easier to understand the system due to all of the interacting subsystems. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24532378,"author":"DecoPerson","month":"2020-09","story":"rg3d: Rust 3D game engine with an FPS demo game and scene editor","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good resource to learn about ECS from the Overwatch team","quote":"I can highly recommend it as a good resource to learn about ECS","text":"Entities are a collection of component instances and nothing more. Components in ECS are data-only. Systems in ECS are logic-only. If you follow this strictly, then you cannot support encapsulation or data-hiding. There is no sole definition of OOP, but it is widely accepted that the ability to do encapsulation, including data-hiding, is a key part of OOP. Therefore, ECS is not OOP. QED If you’ve not seen the Overwatch team’s talk in which they discuss ECS, I can highly recommend it as a good resource to learn about ECS: [VIDEO] There may be models commonly used in those languages you mentioned that are similar to ECS, but you seem to be implying that ECS is a form of OOP; it is not. Even if you implemented OOP using Structs of Arrays, a thread per object, or whatever you like, you cannot strictly conform with ECS’s requirement that “components have no logic” while also strictly conforming with OOP’s requirement that “objects should not know about each others’ internals” (object hierarchy)."},{"id":23124662,"author":"DavidVoid","month":"2020-05","story":"PC games are fighting a new surge of cheaters and hackers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains server-authoritative netcode making games responsive and fair","quote":"Blizzard's GDC talk about it is really good if you want an explanation of how to make a game feel responsive and fair","text":"Overwatch allows none of that and seems to have some pretty well-designed netcode. Clients only send their controller inputs and the server has full authority of what actually happens. Blizzard's GDC talk about it is really good if you want an explanation of how to make a game feel responsive and fair without giving clients authority over everything [1]. Ofc this does not eliminate aimbot or wallhacks, but it gets rid of pretty much all other forms of cheating. [1] [VIDEO] (Netcode talk starts at 22:22)"},{"id":46053025,"author":"dkbrk","month":"2025-11","story":"Trillions spent and big software projects are still failing","stance":"rec","why":"Nearly complete schematic of Overwatch's architecture when watched frame by frame","quote":"it provides a nearly complete schematic of the game's architecture","text":"Have you seen the presentation from GDC 2017 on the architecture of Overwatch [0]? If you watch the video in detail -- stepping through frame-by-frame at some points -- it provides a nearly complete schematic of the game's architecture. That's probably why the video has since been made unlisted. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28449020,"author":"Rd6n6","month":"2021-09","story":"Unity patents ECS","stance":"rec","why":"Example of ECS tech talk predating Unity's patent","quote":"here’s a great tech talk about overwatch.","text":"Isn’t there a ton of prior art? I’ve seen tech talks about ecs implementations for years. From the top of my head, here’s a great tech talk about overwatch. [VIDEO] Here’s a tech talk by Bob Nystrom (the game programming patterns guy) that mentions ecs from years ago [LINK] Edit: letsInvalid in another thread above linked to an article from 2013: [LINK]"},{"id":26161390,"author":"milesvp","month":"2021-02","story":"Functorio","stance":"rec","why":"ECS architecture separating data and functions, plus impressive netcode prediction","quote":"the parts talking about the netcode are quite amazing","text":"Your comment made me feel compelled to mention a Blizzard talk I came across recently on the ECS (Entity Component System) architecture they used for Overwatch. It looks suspiciously like a function first architecture in that they don't allow mixing of data and functions. I'm not sure how purely functional it is, but it certainly strikes me as the kind of architecture that Carmack would appreciate in dealing with complexities that lead him to a more functional programming style. This talk is 3 years after release, and includes all the hindsight they gained from that. Also, the parts talking about the netcode are quite amazing, and the precision of their predictors that they attribute to using ECS. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26276468,"author":"mathgladiator","month":"2021-02","story":"The future of web software is HTML over WebSockets","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of Overwatch netcode and client-side prediction","quote":"The explanation for how the net-code behind Overwatch is a good explanation","text":"This is a next step for my project as I'm trying to figure out how to generalize client side predictions in a sane way. Basically, I'm happy with my latency (for board games) which I hope to migrate closer to people as more edge compute offerings emerge. However, I'm wondering what the path is to find some of these ideas in more latency sensitive applications. The explanation for how the net-code behind Overwatch is a good explanation: [VIDEO] and I'm just trying to figure out how to build the reactive tree in a way to support it sanely."},{"id":26286841,"author":"jonhermansen","month":"2021-02","story":"Why isn't Godot an ECS-based game engine?","stance":"rec","why":"Overwatch developer explains their use of ECS","quote":"For anyone curious to know more about usage of ECS in Overwatch, check out this GDC talk from one of the developers","text":"For anyone curious to know more about usage of ECS in Overwatch, check out this GDC talk from one of the developers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24510039,"author":"nikki93","month":"2020-09","story":"An Introduction to Data Oriented Design with Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Concrete real-world example of ECS reducing coupling in Overwatch","quote":"I think this talk on how an ECS actually reduced coupling in the case of Overwatch would be a good concrete real world example to look at.","text":"[VIDEO] I think this talk on how an ECS actually reduced coupling in the case of Overwatch would be a good concrete real world example to look at. It enabled some architectural benefits for implementing replay cameras or for networking code in their game through actual reduced coupling between input ->commands and commands->simulation."},{"id":23881089,"author":"rcxdude","month":"2020-07","story":"How do I design a game from scratch? A primer on core loops","stance":"rec","why":"ECS design makes safely parallel parts of the system easy to identify","quote":"Here's an interesting presentation on Overwatch's ECS system","text":"Here's an interesting presentation on Overwatch's ECS system, though he doesn't go into great detail in terms of multithreading, he points out how the design makes it easy to identify what parts of the system can be safely run in parallel. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23130670,"author":"mtsr","month":"2020-05","story":"EnTT: Gaming meets Modern C++","stance":"rec","why":"General overview of Overwatch netcode, light on technical detail","quote":"this video has a great general overview","text":"Since you generally want to keep a set number of time steps the commonly used data structure for this is a ring buffer. No garbage and all you do is increment the index (with wraparound) every step. Not a lot of technical details, but this video has a great general overview [VIDEO]"},{"id":23370065,"author":"Mirioron","month":"2020-05","story":"Show HN: Unity MMORPG Boilerplate – Multiplayer in Unity Made Easy","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on Overwatch netcode and client prediction with server authority","quote":"Here is a talk by Timothy Ford about Overwatch's netcode (it's really fascinating)","text":"The important two points are that the client must react immediately to a user's input, but at the same time the server has to have the authoritative game state. One way of doing that is that the server runs the authoritative simulation and the clients send input to the server, but the clients also run their own simulations. In some games the client will try to predict what the server will do. If there's a conflict with what the client simulates compared to the server then the client gets rolled back to the server's state. This usually happens when there's a second player that interferes in some way. Here is a talk by Timothy Ford about Overwatch's netcode (it's really fascinating): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19587332,"author":"endgame","month":"2019-04","story":"Correctness – A paradigm for sustainable software development","stance":"rec","why":"Shows why large simulations need to depart from OO instincts","quote":"it really seems like you have to go against your OO instincts to create larger simulations","text":"> running a simulation (what OOP was invented for) I used to believe that. I no longer do. Among other things, I watched this talk about Overwatch's architecture and it really seems like you have to go against your OO instincts to create larger simulations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41937659,"author":"yayitswei","month":"2024-10","story":"Riot Games: Peeking into Valorant's Netcode (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Comparison point for Overwatch netcode tradeoffs","quote":"here's a talk about Overwatch's netcode","text":"For comparison, here's a talk about Overwatch's netcode: [VIDEO] Overwatch has a long time-to-kill, interesting to compare tradeoffs."},{"id":40958860,"author":"FrostKiwi","month":"2024-07","story":"Flecs v4.0 Is Out","stance":"reference","why":"Example of big-budget project embracing ECS","quote":"","text":"Flecs implements a relatively new programming paradigm \"ECS\". For context, here is a big budget project, Blizzard's Overwatch embracing ECS [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27753707,"author":"pphysch","month":"2021-07","story":"Open 3D Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support that ECS suits large projects","quote":"","text":"I'm seeing Godot mentioned a lot: one major distinction between O3DE and Godot is that O3DE is \"ECS-based\" while Godot is \"OOP-based\" [1][2]. This makes little difference for the hobbyist gamedev, but it has ramifications for large projects with many interacting systems. Proper ECS architecture better supports the latter case [3]. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":25934201,"author":"setr","month":"2021-01","story":"OO in Python is mostly pointless","stance":"reference","why":"Example of ECS used in industry by Blizzard","quote":"","text":"It's definitely been around but I think Unity's (never-finishing) ECS + Rust gamedev community's focus on it has really spiked its popularity/interest lately. Otherwise pretty much every recommendation/engine is OOP-based, with a few straggling extensions/libraries for ECS here and there. No idea about usage in industry though, but it comes up randomly e.g blizzard: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["yy8jQgmhbAU",1],["W4SxKWwFhA0",1],["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["Cj4y0EUlU-Y",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["OOK5xkFijPc",1],["-gPuH1yeZ08",1],["prXuyMCgbTc",1],["JWD1Fpdd4Pc",1],["J3FcbFqSoQY",1],["9vpqilhW9uI",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["PDL_pIPScSI",1],["7ttG90raCNo",1]]},"FvmTSpJU-Xc":{"wy":"A staple on must-watch lists, recommended as a starting point for Kay's thinking and for why software is often poor and ahistorical.","q":{"x":"his talk 'Normal Considered Harmful' in particular is worth a look as it pretty much goes straight to the heart of the issue","i":34737744,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":32784769,"author":"morphle","month":"2022-09","story":"How Transistors Work [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Second most important thing to see after Feynman","quote":"De second most important thing would be half of Alan Kay's lectures","text":"Richard Feynman is the best teacher we know (explaining electricity): [LINK] He also explains seeing, heat, electro magnetism, elasticity and mirrors among other things. His academic lectures are just as good but too long and hard for laymen to follow. - Every time I switch on an electrical device I hear Feynman say 'Zzzzinggg' and I see the copper bars jiggling across town. - Every time I see a cup of hot liquid I hear Feynman say \"jiggling atoms\" - Watch his hands and fingers telling the more accurate science story, simulating the electrons and atoms. - I would say that this the most important video to see for any human being on the planet. De second most important thing would be half of Alan Kay's lectures [VIDEO] Great video's to watch with your kids! (from 3-4 years and older)."},{"id":13578728,"author":"bluetomcat","month":"2017-02","story":"Ask HN: What are some 'must watch' talks?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Listed as a must-watch talk","quote":"Normal Considered Harmful\" by Alan Kay","text":"\"The Mess We're In\" by Joe Armstrong: [LINK] \"Normal Considered Harmful\" by Alan Kay: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47101854,"author":"morphle","month":"2026-02","story":"CERN rebuilt the original browser from 1989","stance":"rec","why":"Kay explains why the web and HTML are a broken wheel","quote":"Alan Kay, the inventor of personal computing, explains why","text":"You can't fix a broken wheel. This web link post, the original NEXT webbrowser, tries to celebrate and revive the reinvention of the broken wheel. The World Wide Web, browser and html standards are a very broken wheel. Alan Kay, the inventor of personal computing, explains why: [VIDEO] Some of the comments of youtube are fun too. This lecture Alan aimed at this particular audience, the computer science (programming) students at University of Illinois, where they programmed the second browser, the second broken wheel 20 years after Alan and Dan had showed them how do do it better. Dan Ingalls implemented most of Alan Kay's invention of the personal computer, in the following demo's he shows how to fix the webbrowser's broken wheel a bit. The Lively Kernel would be another way to fix html but retain the web. Two demos says it all: [LINK] [LINK] Their Squeak, Etoys and Croquet fixed it completely: Early Croquet demo: [LINK] Croquet in webbrowser: [LINK] Demo of webbrowser replacement: [LINK] Squeak and all its predecessors: [LINK] Etoys: [LINK]"},{"id":46001886,"author":"jjuran","month":"2025-11","story":"New OS aims to provide (some) compatibility with macOS","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter appreciates Kay's thinking","quote":"I do appreciate Alan Kay's thinking, in particular his talk \"Normal Considered Harmful","text":"If you're okay with a System 6 appearance, I've already made one < [LINK] >, for Advanced Mac Substitute < [LINK] >. I do appreciate Alan Kay's thinking, in particular his talk \"Normal Considered Harmful\" < [VIDEO] > My own high-level language, Varyx, has somewhat LISPy internals and is very dynamic — for example, you can annotate a variable with a type that's determined only at run time — and has an eval() that insulates the caller from the payload and vice versa. You can sequester mutable state within a closure, which can't be cracked open. Using an experimental Varyx build with some bindings for Apple's Core Graphics API, I wrote a script that rendered an arrow cursor (which I donated to the ravynOS project). Perhaps we should talk. :-)"},{"id":34737744,"author":"logicalshift","month":"2023-02","story":"Why is there so much useless and unreliable software?","stance":"rec","why":"Goes to the heart of why software is unreliable","quote":"his talk 'Normal Considered Harmful' in particular is worth a look as it pretty much goes straight to the heart of the issue","text":"Alan Kay has done a few lectures about exactly this phenomenon: his talk 'Normal Considered Harmful' in particular is worth a look as it pretty much goes straight to the heart of the issue: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29122611,"author":"j-pb","month":"2021-11","story":"What memory model should Rust use?","stance":"rec","why":"Good philosophical starter on Kay's thinking","quote":"this might be a good philosophical starter","text":"Definitely! Although I'm not sure if Morphic[1] is the way forward for UX on the web, despite it only being 10kloc. XD [Anybody stumbling upon this comment, and wondering what Alan Kay is/was up to, this might be a good philosophical starter: [VIDEO] ] 1. [LINK]"},{"id":18317918,"author":"skadamat","month":"2018-10","story":"Dynamicland: The Next Big Thing Is a Room","stance":"rec","why":"Kay on computing not being a real field and forgetting pioneers","quote":"watch the first 20 mins of this talk","text":"It's funny you say that. This is something Alan Kay talks about at length. For example, watch the first 20 mins of this talk - [VIDEO] Essentially, he asserts that computing (or computer science even) isn't a real field (like biology or physics). Most people can't name the early computing pioneers whose work they build on (not true in Physics or Biology, we celebrate the pioneers) nor are they familiar with the work that was done in the Xerox PARC days. It's gotten so bad that a lot of computer science research just assumes that what we have now is what's going to remain forever. In the 70's, all kinds of interesting ideas and experiments were tried. Hardware implemented VM's, direct manipulation programming, OS-free computing environments, highly re-configurable computing a la FPGA's, and hundreds more. Nowadays, we think that creating custom ASIC to run machine learning algorithms quicker is innovative and novel. All this to say that many of the ideas in Dynamicland aren't new. They're rooted in ideas that are decades old. If you look at Bret's papers he uses as references frequently, you'll notice how many of them are over 10 years old - [LINK]"},{"id":17625316,"author":"MichaelAO","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: Where can one learn about the history of the internet and the protocols?","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on computing in general","quote":"Alan Kay's 'Normal Considered Harmful' is a good place to start","text":"Alan Kay's 'Normal Considered Harmful' is a good place to start (more about computing in general): [VIDEO]"},{"id":6638075,"author":"konstruktor","month":"2013-10","story":"Toyota's firmware: Bad design and its consequences","stance":"rec","why":"Ignoring history and basics leads to inferior solutions","quote":"want to force automotive engineers to watch some Alan Kay talks","text":"Very well said. The focus on convenience and cool features over safety makes me really sad, and want to force automotive engineers to watch some Alan Kay talks. He loves to talk how people who don't know the history and basics of their craft will arrive at inferior solutions, for example in this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4578510,"author":"agentultra","month":"2012-09","story":"Light Table - Embracing Abstraction","stance":"rec","why":"Kay explains history of interactive programming environments","quote":"Alan Kay does a far better job than I can in explaining some of this history","text":"I think Mr. Granger should refrain from using inaccurate remarks about the history of programming. Light Table is neat but it is not the first attempt at an interactive development environment. His ideas were quite common 30+ years ago (20 years ago would have been right about the time when this style of programming started falling out of vogue). Alan Kay does a far better job than I can in explaining some of this history: [VIDEO] Update I wasn't being terribly fair. Granger might simply not have been aware. In that case I hope he sees my comment and watches the above video. :)"},{"id":4089476,"author":"cturner","month":"2012-06","story":"Meebo discontinued: shutting down on July 11, 2012","stance":"rec","why":"Kay says computing is more pop-culture than science","quote":"You might find this entertaining","text":"You might find this entertaining - I'm watching a speech by Alan Kay at the moment where he says that computing is more pop-culture than science: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38075224,"author":"jocaal","month":"2023-10","story":"Autism and Responding to Authority","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the quote about perspective and IQ points","quote":"A change of perspective is worth 80 IQ points\" - Alan Kay","text":"\"A change of perspective is worth 80 IQ points\" - Alan Kay He said that in a video \"nornal considered harmful\" that you can find on Youtube[1]. He talks about it at 34:29 [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35932539,"author":"jocaal","month":"2023-05","story":"WebAssembly: A new development paradigm for the web","stance":"reference","why":"Kay says web should have been like an OS kernel","quote":"There is a talk from Alan Kay[1] where he says the web should've been like an operating system kernel from the start","text":"I hope the time spent was worth learning something new :). I think having new languages have access to the dom can have huge benefits to end users but as you said, the initial indicators aren't looking good. It seems that there isn't a general consensus on how the web should be handled as a target platform. There is a talk from Alan Kay[1] where he says the web should've been like an operating system kernel from the start (if you want the juicy part it is at 23:45). The fact that both the inital users of this tech, after unlocking these new abilities, opted to use it to increase performance for canvas, instead of trying to create real web experiences leads me to think that those developers share his viewpoint. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28008474,"author":"jhgb","month":"2021-07","story":"The Itanic Has Sunk","stance":"reference","why":"Anecdote found in the video's comments","quote":"Found in comments to","text":"I love the function call timing story: > The iAPX 432 failure could best be summed up by a meeting of Intel marketroids and Tandem engineers who wanted to use the chip in their next generation machine, after the slides, the senior engineer asked: “How long does it take to execute a procedure call?” > The presenter looked it up. “Two hundred and fifty microseconds.” > Tom immediately walked out, followed by the majority of the Tandem software department. The presenter was poleaxed. “What did I say?” Found in comments to [VIDEO] , but I saw it somewhere else, too, I just don't recall where. Doesn't anyone know?"},{"id":14645424,"author":"0xdeadbeefbabe","month":"2017-06","story":"JavaScript Fatigue or My History with Web Development","stance":"reference","why":"Kay calls computing a pop culture","quote":"Maybe it's \"pop culture\" like this cranky old guy says","text":"> as in other fields Well maybe it isn't a field after all. Maybe it's \"pop culture\" like this cranky old guy says [VIDEO] ."},{"id":14335690,"author":"armitron","month":"2017-05","story":"Lisp, Smalltalk, and the Power of Symmetry (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a reference from the masters","quote":"","text":"You're all over the place here and your arguments make no sense whatsoever when examined. First, you conflate mass-appeal with some sort of objective \"better\" criterion which is of course bonkers. To use one of your own examples against you, there are hundreds of thousands of Java monkeys out there that are using glue other people made to tie together rocks to build stonewalls. Which do fail as soon as the weather stops being nice. Security (you should look into Java deserialization bugs), reliability, performance what do they know about any of these things? Second, you conflate late-binding as present in Lisp and Smalltalk with late-binding present in other dynamic languages. The two are not equivalent, a perfect example of the whole is greater than the sum of its parts. Lisp and Smalltalk will never become popular (read my previous comment), but that does not mean that they do not sit on an apex and still have a lot to give. To anyone interested in the \"craft of programming\", \"the Art\", there is nothing better period. Here are some references for you, from the masters themselves: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12309650,"author":"avindroth","month":"2016-08","story":"“It's The Future”","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in support of Kay's logic","quote":"","text":"Of course not. I am in undergrad. But I think Alan Kay has been \"exposed\" to computer science, and I follow his logic, based on my limited scope of knowledge. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12118712,"author":"armitron","month":"2016-07","story":"Pack Hunters of the Silicon Savannas","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among masters' references","quote":"","text":"This is obviously entirely false if one takes \"genius\" to mean someone who does things a non-genius would not be able to do. There have been attempts in recent years to roll back and debase the definition of genius to be more compatible with egalitarianism (especially in parts of Europe) and the tenet of homogeneity but this is not what genius originally stood for. Fabian Tasano writes: \"Regarding the version of “genius” that is currently in retreat but still occasionally used: many people seem to have a simplistic idea of what it takes to be one. According to one popular model, all that is required is an increase in the magnitude of certain qualities which everyone already possesses in some measure. Make the particular qualities pronounced enough, and you get to genius. But a better way to understand the concept — assuming we’re applying the word to (say) Gauss or Picasso, rather than John Cleese or Wayne Rooney — may be that a genius has a particular capacity, which on a certain level can seem obvious or unremarkable, but which no one else has. A genius, on this understanding, is a person uniquely capable of making a leap ‘off the path’. With hindsight the leap may seem simple or obvious, but at the time no one else was, apparently, capable of making it. A potential leap of this kind is made possible by preceding leaps. Nevertheless its actual occurrence may go on not happening for decades. During that time there may be clear pointers towards it. Yet it is not until a geniu"},{"id":11552802,"author":"tnorgaard","month":"2016-04","story":"The average size of Web pages is now the average size of a Doom install","stance":"reference","why":"Kay advocated a bytecode VM for the web","quote":"this was what Alan Kay was advocating for on the web","text":"Interestingly, this was what Alan Kay was advocating for on the web: \"A bytecode VM og something like X for graphics\" [1]. When Lars Bak and Kasper Lund launched Dart [2], I found it sad that they weren't more bold - left CSS and the DOM alone, and created an alternative Content-Type. So you can choose to Accept 'application/magic-bytecode' [3] before text/html, if your client supports so. Sadly, we ended up with Web Assembly, which by the few talks I've seen, appears to only cater to that of graphic/game developers, with no support for dynamic or OO languages. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] Or in Dart lingo, a VM snapshot."},{"id":4584687,"author":"grn","month":"2012-09","story":"The Programmers Before Us Were Better","stance":"reference","why":"Kay on 90s ignoring past generations' work and criticizing the Web","quote":"he pointed out that the people in the 90s ignored the work and experience of the past generations of programmers","text":"Similar claim was made by Alan Kay in his lecture Normal Considered Harmful [1]. However he didn't complain about lower barriers to entry. Instead he pointed out that the people in the 90s ignored the work and experience of the past generations of programmers. He especially criticized the Web. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["N9c7_8Gp7gI",2],["OB-bdWKwXsU",1],["Ee3EtYb8d1o",1],["Wp-WiNXH6hI",1],["-4Yp3j_jk8Q",1],["QyJZzq0v7Z4",1],["0Cu6EbELZ6I",1],["HnOix9TFy1A",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["KTHOwgpMIiU",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["awADEuv5vWY",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["P1ww1IXRfTA",1],["QTJRwKOFddc",1]]},"gwf5mAlI7Ug":{"wy":"HN treats it as the classic demonstration of how analog mechanical computation works, especially the differential gear section.","q":{"x":"I especially like the section on differential gears, demonstrating their use as computing units","i":41304187,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38245821,"author":"ethbr1","month":"2023-11","story":"Lost Doctor Who episodes found but owner is reluctant to hand them to BBC","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Effective presentation of complex topics in old film","quote":"It still amazes me how effectively they presented complex topics then.","text":"Two gems (among many)... Periscope Films (on YouTube): [LINK] Digitized 40-60s era reels. It still amazes me how effectively they presented complex topics then. E.g. mechanical naval gunnery computers: [VIDEO] And this on-the-ground movie on the Columbia student occupation in 1968: [LINK]"},{"id":41304187,"author":"turtledragonfly","month":"2024-08","story":"Basic Mechanisms In Fire Control Computers (1953) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Section on differential gears as computing units","quote":"I especially like the section on differential gears, demonstrating their use as computing units","text":"I especially like the section on differential gears, demonstrating their use as computing units: (timestamped link) [VIDEO]"},{"id":38516789,"author":"maxglute","month":"2023-12","story":"1962 Military Innstructional Video on Magnetic Cores","stance":"rec","why":"Classic fire control computer film from PeriscopeFilm","quote":"mechanism computer of fire control computer (a classic)","text":"From top of head I really enjoy: MAPMAKING PROCESS, THE [LINK] mechanism computer of fire control computer (a classic) [VIDEO] Physical science documentary series whose exact title I can't recall. It has been a while. To find more, my method is to do the occasional PeriscopeFilm binge (on youtube that digitizes a lot of old media, with great descriptions), always find find something interesting I haven't watched. If style, delivery really good, there's usually something in description to point to more. Like \"U.S. NAVY TRAINING FILM\", which usually returns playlists, and videos in same era often share quality. The technical ones generally don't disappoint. Another one is The Best Film Archives with good military training playlists. Tangent for others: the one I wish to remember, is a deleted/banned channel, one of the original US archival footage channel, with I think 1000s of videos, that got wiped many years ago right when youtube started drm crackdown. It had a companion website with obsessive amount of detail and tags to find the perfect video. And from what I recall, it didn't feel like a business like periscopefilm, but a passion project. It was an incredible amount of work to have been wiped out."},{"id":35315951,"author":"tiedieconderoga","month":"2023-03","story":"Reverse-engineering the Globus INK, a Soviet spaceflight navigation computer","stance":"rec","why":"Classic Navy training film on solving differential equations on battleships","quote":"there's a classic US Navy training film which talks about how they were used to solve differential equations in battleships","text":"You've probably already seen it, but there's a classic US Navy training film which talks about how they were used to solve differential equations in battleships around the WWII era. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31465126,"author":"pkaye","month":"2022-05","story":"Collection of US Navy Training Courses Developed","stance":"rec","why":"Principles of computing functions with cams and gears","quote":"The US Navy had this great video on principles of mechanical computers.","text":"The US Navy had this great video on principles of mechanical computers. Using cams and gears to compute functions. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30990471,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2022-04","story":"Why a Slide Rule Works","stance":"rec","why":"Details on cam-cylinder mechanism of fire control computers","quote":"The cylinder mechanism (of 2x inputs mapped to 1x output) is specifically talked about here","text":"So I've known about slide-rules for a while. What I didn't realize was that the mechanical principles of slide-rules were applied to fire-control computers on WW2-era Battleships. [LINK] These computers had slide-rule like cylinders that would rotate. As you aimed the gun sights \"at\" a target, the gun's coordinates were being mapped to a cylinder, which would move the gun a different direction (you have to aim the gun \"higher\" the further away the target is, to account for gravity \"drooping\" the bullet). In effect; the weirdly shaped cylinder inside the fire-control computer is a pre-computed solution that maps x/y coordinate inputs to a z-coordinate output (where \"z\" would be fed into the gun's raw aiming system). The different parameters of the gun were programmed in with gears and cranks, moving the cylinder around physically. For more details, see: [VIDEO] Its a 20-minute video. The cylinder mechanism (of 2x inputs mapped to 1x output) is specifically talked about here: [VIDEO] . Carefully crafted cylinders could be any pre-computed 2-input / 1-output function. --------- Mechanical computers are fun. Slide rules are but the simplest kind. People were using more complicated \"mechanical computers\" for very serious combat / rangefinding / etc. systems just a few decades ago, before the advent of modern digital computers."},{"id":25544294,"author":"segfaultbuserr","month":"2020-12","story":"1937 film explains how a car differential works","stance":"rec","why":"Shows computation with gears and shafts","quote":"I learned you can do a lot of computations with gears and shafts.","text":"If you enjoy this, don't miss the video lecture on World War 2-era mechanical fire control computers by the U.S. Navy. I learned you can do a lot of computations with gears and shafts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24938644,"author":"fallous","month":"2020-10","story":"Show HN: What would mechanical programming look like?","stance":"rec","why":"Mechanical analog computing example","quote":"Mechanical analog computing FTW!","text":"I went a bit further back when I saw \"mechanical programming\" and thought of this video. [VIDEO] Mechanical analog computing FTW!"},{"id":22807640,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-04","story":"How to Use a Differential Analyzer","stance":"rec","why":"1953 Navy film on how fire control computers worked","quote":"Here's a film that goes over a lot of the basics","text":"This could use a lot more visual aids. Here's a film that goes over a lot of the basics: \"BASIC MECHANISMS IN FIRE CONTROL COMPUTERS\" [1]. It's a 1953 US Navy instructional film explaining how mechanical fire control computers worked. These machines took inputs giving the speed and direction the ship was traveling, the speed and direction the target was moving, the distance and direction to the target, the wind speed and direction, and other factors, and computed how to aim the guns to shell the target. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21000483,"author":"Scaevolus","month":"2019-09","story":"UCLA’s 1948 Mechanical Computer [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains computation with mechanical systems","quote":"If you want more information on how you can do computation with mechanical systems, try this video","text":"If you want more information on how you can do computation with mechanical systems, try this video: U.S. NAVY BASIC MECHANISMS OF FIRE CONTROL COMPUTERS MECHANICAL COMPUTER INSTRUCTIONAL FILM 27794 [VIDEO]"},{"id":17747224,"author":"jeffwass","month":"2018-08","story":"Tide-Predicting Machine No. 2","stance":"rec","why":"US Navy fire control computer using cams and differential gears","quote":"You might like this video about the US Navy fire control computer.","text":"You might like this video about the US Navy fire control computer. Mechanical yet also programmable. Eg with cam-based calculation modules like 1/x or differential gearings to perform addition : [VIDEO]"},{"id":47819665,"author":"aequitas","month":"2026-04","story":"The electromechanical angle computer inside the B-52 bomber's star tracker","stance":"reference","why":"Old training videos showing how fire control worked","quote":"There are some old training videos that show how this worked","text":"There are some old training videos that show how this worked: [VIDEO] Also the Battleship New Jersey YouTube channel has some nice content on this: [LINK]"},{"id":34621485,"author":"oldsklgdfth","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: Why do people think the USA did not go to the moon in 1969?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is what I think computers were then","text":"There’s just something fun about having your bubble burst. I think most people (including me) think the tech was too primitive. This is what I think computers were then [1] Ultimately, I think bad ass people banded together and worked their butt off to pull off a stretch goal. I wonder if I romanticize engineers from then or there are similar folks today. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29516726,"author":"db48x","month":"2021-12","story":"Antikythera Mechanism: An ancient 'computer' that 'shouldn't exist' [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a special-purpose computer, WWII firing computer","quote":"The firing computer on a WWII destroyer computed the correct elevation of the guns","text":"A special–purpose computer is any device which computes an answer to a specific question given some input or initial conditions. An abacus or slide rule is not a computer because they are not designed for a specific question or problem (but they can of course be an aid to computation). With the Antikythera mechanism, the operator sets the machine to the current date, and then turns the crank to advance the date. The dials on the face would continuously show the positions of several planets, the phase of the moon, the eclipses, and possibly one or two other things. Charles Babbage’s Difference Engine computed the values of polynomials. The operator would set the coefficients of the polynomial, then turn the crank to compute successive values of that polynomial for successive values of X. He never finished it though. The firing computer on a WWII destroyer computed the correct elevation of the guns, based on inputs that encoded the position and velocity of the ship, the position and velocity of the target, and several other variables. [VIDEO] General–purpose computers take as input a _program_, and the program specifies the problem to be solved. You could load a program for computing eclipses one minute, and the next minute load a program for computing polynomials or firing solutions, or load one to check your email. The hardware hasn’t changed, only the program and inputs have changed. Contrast that with the Difference Engine: to make the difference engine compute eclipses or "},{"id":29267998,"author":"ezconnect","month":"2021-11","story":"Megaprocessor – A micro-processor built large (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Antikythera mechanism was built before the Dark Ages, he just wanted to go back to the Dark Ages so he can do precision work by that time, he can probably build a battleship fire control system at that time. edit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20579165,"author":"dharmab","month":"2019-07","story":"First Programmable Memristor Computer","stance":"reference","why":"Example of mechanical fire control computers","quote":"mechanical computers used to be used for fire control on naval vessels","text":"[LINK] has many examples. For example, mechanical computers used to be used for fire control on naval vessels: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41570502,"author":"tway_GdBRwW","month":"2024-09","story":"In 1926, TV Was Mechanical","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I love this unique insight: \"Fire control computers ... solve ... fire control problems.\" [VIDEO] U.S. NAVY BASIC MECHANISMS OF FIRE CONTROL COMPUTERS MECHANICAL COMPUTER INSTRUCTIONAL FILM 27794"}],"r":[["DovunOxlY1k",2],["C_r5UJrxcck",1],["ySuUZEjARPY",1],["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["94ed4v_T6YM",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["RkTvDjhImwo",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["4d0EGIt1SPc",1],["p45T7U5oi9Q",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HF-9Dy6iB_4",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1],["2EWejmkKlxs",1],["bSkpMdDe4g4",1]]},"0CDXJ6bMkMY":{"wy":"For over a decade HN has pointed bootstrappers and skeptics of hyper-scale startup culture to it as a motivating, influential talk.","q":{"x":"The 2008 DHH boostrapping talk during Startup School is still one of the most influential videos on my career as a founder.","i":25395820,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25395820,"author":"leelin","month":"2020-12","story":"The Rise and Fall of Ambrosia Software (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Influential bootstrapping talk on founder career","quote":"The 2008 DHH boostrapping talk during Startup School is still one of the most influential videos on my career as a founder.","text":"Reading the ending hits closer to home and made me more sad than expected! Andrew Welch was uneasy about the idea of scaling up, especially when they could survive as they were just fine off their utility products Way back in the day, YC always said not to worry about competitors raising tons of money (not sure if that's still the rec). The 2008 DHH boostrapping talk during Startup School is still one of the most influential videos on my career as a founder. But that path is increasingly less popular! [VIDEO] Lastly, for all the bolo comments out there, I remember the huge transition from modems to school ethernet that made it playable - it was honestly one of the reasons I was looking forward to dorm life!"},{"id":3850718,"author":"bira","month":"2012-04","story":"Show HN: We decided not to apply to YC so we could try Kickstarter","stance":"strong_rec","why":"DHH on making money online and profitable bootstrapping","quote":"If not, watch it ASAP.","text":"Have you already watched DHH's \"How to Make Money Online\" presentation ( [VIDEO] )? If not, watch it ASAP. If you go down the bootstrapping road, you must have profits coming in, to fund and further the development of your product. Sooner or later you will have to make money and since you don't seem to have a lot of funds laying around, the sooner the better. Moreover, have you validated your idea(s) (included the previous, failed attempts to excute them and build a viable business)? Are you sure that there are enough people out there with the burning desire to throw money at you for this app to make it worth the hustle? (assuming you want to sell your app, which would be the best and most simple way to make money) As PG says, the worst mistake one can make trying to build a company is building something THEY think is NEEDED by their customers and not what the market actually needs. Listen to your audience. Do the people need an iphone app to \"truly define us\"? Creating a kickstarter campaign was a good move, by the response you'll get you'll understand if you are solving a real problem or not. To expose more people to your app (and validate it or not with a wider set of data) you can use an adwords\\facebook voucher and create a campaign (just search for them). Link to your homepage, track how many visitors opt in vs how many of them just land and bounce away, not interested. Ask questions on Answers sites, open threads on forums your ideal customer hangs out, the more eyebal"},{"id":3284640,"author":"csallen","month":"2011-11","story":"Startups Are Hard. So Work More, Cry Less, And Quit All The Whining","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Motivating DHH talk on building a profitable small business","quote":"This talk by DHH never fails to motivate me","text":"I haven't read the book that some others have been recommending, but it looks good (I respect a lot of the people who gave it favorable reviews on its website). Aside from that, I'd say you should do some research on others who've successfully followed this path. They exist -- they're just less likely to show up in tech news, which is heavily influenced by investors (who only care about big wins) and bloggers (who only care about big news). Take a look at the early blog posts from Balsamiq's Peldi, anything by Patrick Mackenzie, the Wufoo guys, Gary Vaynerchuck, or anything from the 37Signals guys. This talk by DHH never fails to motivate me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34106120,"author":"_448","month":"2022-12","story":"I am done. I give up","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation against hyper-scale mindset, by a recognizable speaker","quote":"There is a very interesting presentation at YC startup school 2008","text":"Cold email is a hit and miss approach. Just go talk to people face to face. Initially it seems like a non-scalable approach but it works wonders. The silicon valley, VC preached mantra of hyper scale is what muddles the minds of most entrepreneurs. Everyone starts thinking of million of users rather than 100s of users. There is a very interesting presentation at YC startup school 2008: [VIDEO] You will recognise the speaker :)"},{"id":7982721,"author":"graeme","month":"2014-07","story":"Ask HN: Did you get \"never have to work again rich\" through software?","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of building a sustainable business without startup scale","quote":"This talk by DHH is a good overview","text":"There is more to business than startups. If you can program, then you can bootstrap a \"lifestyle\" business. I don't love that term, but people know what it means. You can make a business that doesn't depend on traction and scale. Find a prosaic need, and serve it. Could start with consulting, or products. Start small, stay small by Rob Walling is an excellent primer, as is the four hour workweek. The latter glosses over how much effort is actually required to make a \"muse\", but the mindset is spot on. I'm currently doing this. I'll never get a $10 million payout, but I've built good revenue streams and have complete freedom of time. And I did it without knowing programming. You've basically got a superpower in the internet world. You can program AND you have a baseline of entrepreneurial knowledge. Just shift out of startup mode. As long as you've got enough money or time that you can work on something new without starving, it's not THAT hard to make a business that gives you freedom. (No guaranteed success, but the odds are significantly better than in a startup. This talk by DHH is a good overview) [VIDEO]"},{"id":6219003,"author":"hello_newman","month":"2013-08","story":"The Best Companies Don’t Do Accelerator Programs","stance":"rec","why":"DHH on building small companies that matter","quote":"Definitely worth watching","text":"\"Not every important idea is a huge world-changing event. Sometimes building a small company that makes a big difference to a small number of people is more important.\" Totally agree. DHH actually gave a talk about this subject at Start-Up School back in 2008. Definitely worth watching [VIDEO]"},{"id":4046539,"author":"AVTizzle","month":"2012-05","story":"Workplace experiments: A month to yourself","stance":"rec","why":"DHH argues against standard startup focus on investment and exits","quote":"a very refreshing and empowering voice for entrepreneurs","text":"The other answers aren't wrong, but I think listening to 37Signals Partner DHH from Startup School would speak best: [VIDEO] Almost more than their products, they're known for their very vocal broadcasting against the standard startup focus of investments, valuations, exits, etc... Their books \"Getting Real\" and \"Rework\" represent their bootstrapping, sustainable-business values - they're not timid in speaking out against the nonsense of the modern startup conventional wisdom. Personally, I think it's a very refreshing and empowering voice for entrepreneurs. It's not without controversy though, so dig in to their content for yourself to make your own judgement. (FWIW - \"Getting Real\" is a free PDF out there somewhere. I recommend it, though others may or may not.)"},{"id":2611174,"author":"mgl","month":"2011-06","story":"10 YouTube Videos Every Entrepreneur Should Watch","stance":"rec","why":"DHH on bootstrapping a profitable startup","quote":"this one with David Heinemeier Hansson on bootstrapping a profitable startup is definitely missing","text":"Nice collection but this one with David Heinemeier Hansson on bootstrapping a profitable startup is definitely missing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36890729,"author":"mrweasel","month":"2023-07","story":"Stack Overflow's CEO Doesn't Understand Stack Overflow","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"DHH talked about this 15 years ago.","text":"> Not everything needs to be a damn unicorn. More people needs to understand this. It's fine being a small(ish) business that turns a profit and provides a service that's beneficial to society. You don't need to be a billion dollar company to be important or do great work. DHH talked about this 15 years ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25093616,"author":"takezo","month":"2020-11","story":"Having 170 competitors in not an obstacle","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The idea of an Mom & Pop store on the internet","text":"Reminds me of DHH from Startup School 08. The idea of an Mom & Pop store on the internet: [VIDEO] Good luck with Pingr :)"},{"id":23343821,"author":"aazaa","month":"2020-05","story":"Idea Generation","stance":"reference","why":"Funders push going big but small well-run companies are fine","quote":"plenty of room for a small, well-run company with no aspirations of getting bigger","text":"> A good question to ask yourself early in the process of thinking about an idea is “could this be huge if it worked?” There are many good ideas in the world, but few of them have the inherent advantages that can make a startup massively successful. Most businesses don’t generate a valuable accumulating advantage as they scale. Think early about why an idea might have that property. It’s obvious for Facebook or Airbnb, but it often exists in more subtle ways. This reminds me of David Heinemeier Hansson's talk at Startup School waaayyy back. [VIDEO] The gist: funders tell founders all the time to catch a wave. Go big or don't bother. The reason is that the funder must have a big payoff and small outcomes don't cut it. But there's plenty of room for a small, well-run company with no aspirations of getting bigger. I believe the analogy was: best Italian Restaurant in the city. The other advantage of thinking smaller than \"huge\" is that success is so much more likely because the competition tends to be less intense. Maybe the author and I have a different idea of \"huge,\" but the examples suggest otherwise. If you're looking for a community that embraces the small startup idea and has developed a lot of material for exploring it, check out Microconf: [LINK]"},{"id":20257277,"author":"vram22","month":"2019-06","story":"Startup idea checklist","stance":"reference","why":"Full DHH talk behind the little Italian restaurant analogy","quote":"Full DHH talk at Startup School 2008","text":">But what do you call a new business that a founder is fully committed to (so, not a side business) but without an aim to grow to a huge valuation (just enough profit to live)? You call it \"a little Italian restaurant on the web\". At least, @DHH (Rails and Basecamp founder) does: Short compilation of his original talk, by Werner Vogels, Amazon CTO: [LINK] Full DHH talk at Startup School 2008: [VIDEO] And it can give more than just enough profit to live. A lot more."},{"id":13117669,"author":"saluki","month":"2016-12","story":"Ask HN: As a single bootstrapper, how do you deal with the pressure?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"What would your friends and family think if you showed them something that looks like craigslist? Just because something isn't owned by Fbook or Google or doesn't look polished doesn't mean it isn't or couldn't make money. Or if you're not working for Fbook or Google or working on working there that you're doing it WRONG. My friends usually gloss over me talking about developing SaaS apps till I mention ones making $30k/mo or $60k/mo . . . that gets their attention. I love when they follow up with \"wait, wait that's per month?\" Making a product/SaaS that is successful isn't easy, but it's something most developers can accomplish, it's not s sure thing, but definitely worth the effort if it's something that interests you. It's worth a shot/putting in the effort. So if you're interested in products/SaaS keep at it. Granted most of us have day jobs or are contractors along the way to pay the bills till we hit \"SaaScess\" but don't give up on it because of what unsupportive family and friends think. You do need to find a better support network, maybe fellow entrepreneurs/web developers, connect with other like minded individuals online. (feel free to connect with me via email HNusername at gmail, I'm always up for bouncing ideas around, being supportive, reviewing apps/products offering constructive criticism). Don't share what you're doing with people who aren't supportive. Just give them a simple answer like I'm consulting, etc. Making any web application is hard so making any f"},{"id":11163770,"author":"elliotec","month":"2016-02","story":"Fundraising Advice for YC Companies","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"DHH's talk at startup school","text":"This sentiment reminds me of DHH's talk at startup school ( [VIDEO] ) in which he explains that the best way to create a business is to make money, but not just make money, make it on your own. I am a little removed from the Silicon Valley echo chamber and enjoy the ability to watch it from the sidelines, but I don't understand the obsession with the 'rapid growth, raise millions from investors, become a unicorn, own the world' mentality. I just don't get it. As a founder, your life is going to be just as good (probably better) if you made a couple million dollars in actual money that you don't have to divide up in odd percentages to all sorts of already rich guys who gave you tons of money in the first place. Why isn't everyone just trying to build products that work, that people like, that make money, and going from there? Why the need to go to YC, or raise any capital AT ALL, for the very very low chance of becoming gajillionaires? I understand the appeal of the #1 king on top thing, but not really, and not with the risks and pain associated with the journey and the reality. Whatever I guess. People will not stop investing or raising money, at least for now."},{"id":8903559,"author":"hbhakhra","month":"2015-01","story":"The number of seed rounds in 2014 fell compared to 2013","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This talk by DHH explains why I prefer to bootstrap a company","text":"This talk by DHH explains why I prefer to bootstrap a company: [VIDEO] The main point being, that if you start off by selling a product (rather than getting people to sign up for a free product and then monetizing later) and can get a fraction of the people to sign up then you are in profit. Further, if you own 100% of the company, you are profitable much faster so you don't need to grow as large. Ex: $30/month * 500 users = $15,000 a month. If that was a bootstrapped business with just a solo founder...$$$"},{"id":7168728,"author":"rudedogg","month":"2014-02","story":"12 websites in 12 months","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No, that looks interesting, especially with the ratings. A few years ago I remember reading various blogs and having it drilled into my head that there's a lot of money to be made with niche websites. Also, this talk for startups reminds me of these points: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5008085,"author":"asc76","month":"2013-01","story":"The Internet, new media, and the new American dream","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"DHH said it pretty well","text":"\"Focus on building a community first, and worry about monetization later.\" I don't entirely agree with this statement. This is precisely why a lot of startups fail because they slap on the business model as an afterthought. DHH said it pretty well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3178064,"author":"amirmc","month":"2011-10","story":"Steve Jobs' memo to startups","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The talk you link to in that essay leads to a 404. Is it this talk on YouTube ( [VIDEO] )? If so, maybe you want to update the link."},{"id":2547880,"author":"thetrumanshow","month":"2011-05","story":"Is it hard to build a web app that makes at least $1000 a month?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Its hard, but not THAT hard!\" [VIDEO] 6 years into building web-apps and finally I am just now seeing a few bucks rolling in (still <$1000 month). I wasted lots of time, primarily pursuing the wrong kinds of business models (free!!!) or putting effort into the wrong areas of a business, ultimately burning out because things weren't working. But, even if you've picked a good product with a good market, for the un-initiated engineer there's this mysterious delta between being able to build something (anything!), and making that something successful. My recommendation (because its working for me), is to find a co-founder who is a business guy employed at a successful small software company. Painting with broad strokes here, but try to pick a sales or marketing guy over a biz dev guy, I think they are connected better with the product. You've heard this advice before. Its true. Engineers think of the world as meritocratic. But good product != success. You need someone to help you get past this way of thinking."},{"id":1670711,"author":"sreitshamer","month":"2010-09","story":"Staying Healthy and Sane At a Startup","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it's never going to be less work, as DHH suggested at Startup School","text":"I'd add that it's a good idea to take it easy because it's never going to be less work, as DHH suggested at Startup School [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["B_nGEj8wIP0",1]]},"dPHPNgIihR0":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the definitive critique of Fortnite's monetization and of manipulative free-to-play design.","q":{"x":"There's a great video[1] that goes into detail on the psychological manipulation used in Fortnite's monetization model.","i":19899592,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":43870707,"author":"philipwhiuk","month":"2025-05","story":"Apple App Store guidelines remove ban on encouraging external payments in US","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thorough takedown of Fortnite's monetization process.","quote":"The tour-de-force on Fortnite's insane process","text":"> Fortnite, in my opinion, has been a gold standard for F2P monetization. No gambling, no randomized loot boxes, etc. Hard disagree. The tour-de-force on Fortnite's insane process [VIDEO]"},{"id":35059095,"author":"preinheimer","month":"2023-03","story":"Mario Kart Tour Triggers You into Gambling Your Money","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of Fortnite for readers who liked the Mario Kart Tour piece.","quote":"If you enjoyed reading that you might like Folding Ideas breakdown of Fortnite -","text":"First, that was an amazing breakdown of what's happening. If you enjoyed reading that you might like Folding Ideas breakdown of Fortnite - [VIDEO] Second, I've personally become very wary of any mobile app that introduces multiple currency types. There's gold, and rubies, and power balls, and stardust! At some point pretty early on I decide it's not worth it to figure out how much things /actually/ cost and delete the thing. My understanding is that the math shows companies that microtransactions are the winning move over and over again. I hope that eventually there's enough grumpy consumers both parties become worth serving better."},{"id":30826974,"author":"bccdee","month":"2022-03","story":"I hate what video games have become","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed exploration of the dark side of Fortnite's monetization.","quote":"For a more detailed exploration of the dark side of Fortnite's monetization model, check out","text":"Fortnite's a good example about how your two rules aren't enough, actually. Because of the way the game's designed, it pressures its audience (of primarily children) into purchasing microtransactions, to the point where there's been widespread bulling of kids who don't pay for premium Fortnite skins [1]. For a more detailed exploration of the dark side of Fortnite's monetization model, check out \"Manufactured Discontent and Fortnite\" [2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28931896,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2021-10","story":"Facebook plans to change its name as part of company rebrand","stance":"rec","why":"Good critique of Fortnite, notably the central role of the shop.","quote":"this video[1] presents a good critique without mentioning aesthetics.","text":"If you have 20 minutes, this video[1] presents a good critique without mentioning aesthetics. It's especially notable how central the shop is to the whole experience. I personally found Fortnite to be a huge slog to play - with tons of downtime compared even to games like CS: GO, let alone old-school arena shooters like Q3. The only thing that kept me coming back is the season pass reward tracks and daily missions. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27057453,"author":"gizmo385","month":"2021-05","story":"Blizzard has lost almost 29% of its overall active playerbase in three years","stance":"rec","why":"Dissects Fortnite's monetization systems and their negative aspects.","quote":"Folding Ideas has a great video [2] (that the Errant Signal video references) dissecting the monetization systems in Fortnite and their negative aspects.","text":"I think Errant Signal has a great video [1] dissecting the appeal and breadth of content in Fortnite. It's not my cup of tea, but I certainly don't think you can call it \"shallow\". On the other side, Folding Ideas has a great video [2] (that the Errant Signal video references) dissecting the monetization systems in Fortnite and their negative aspects. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19899592,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2019-05","story":"Fortnite is free, but kids are getting bullied into spending money","stance":"rec","why":"Details psychological manipulation in Fortnite's monetization model.","quote":"There's a great video[1] that goes into detail on the psychological manipulation used in Fortnite's monetization model.","text":"Make no mistake, this is an entirely intentional consequence of Fortnite's design. There's a great video[1] that goes into detail on the psychological manipulation used in Fortnite's monetization model. It saddens me that a hobby that brought me so much joy as a kid/teenager now preys on the same insecurities it once sheltered me from. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19585309,"author":"pxc","month":"2019-04","story":"The golden age of YouTube is over","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favorite thoughtful culture video essay channels.","quote":"lengthy, thoughtful, and often artistic video essays and skits addressing culture and politics","text":"My favorite corner of YouTube— lengthy, thoughtful, and often artistic video essays and skits addressing culture and politics— is flourishing right now. Some examples: Unfolding Ideas – [VIDEO] ContraPoints – [LINK] Philosophy Tube – [LINK] Lindsay Ellis – [LINK] Innuendo Studios – [LINK] HBomberguy – [LINK] and so on. And it seems like it's only in the past couple of years that many of these channels have reached their current levels of artistry and production value. Most stuff on YouTube is trash, but there's plenty else to watch."},{"id":27735190,"author":"scollet","month":"2021-07","story":"The Metaverse: A brave, new virtual world","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as summarizing the commenter's disdain for Fortnite.","quote":"Some of my disdain can be summarized via the Folding Ideas essay [1].","text":"Okay, let me preface with an apology. The parent comment received the brunt of my ire and I feel that's inappropriate as no one person deserves that as my angst is more directed to the fundamental system architecture and not the surrounding commentary. at_19, sorry for the rebuke and the sarcasm. That being said. Metaverses are great. I think there is an underpinning philosophical element to these discussion around aggregate universes that needs to be addressed. My particular disdain is targeted at Fortnite. Some of my disdain can be summarized via the Folding Ideas essay [1]. My disdain may or not be intrinsically linked to the ideals of capitalism, for which I think there is a larger discussion to be had. I believe, by all interpretation, Fortnite is a vehicle solely for capitalist ideals and doesn't allow room for alternative economic models (including capitalist synergies). This, in concert with its branding (a childrens' game) is problematic for me. Fortnite, in its essence, is an anarcho-capitalist model. The literal goal of any drop is zero sum by nature. Kill everyone else -> read kill as not literally kill, but subsume their resources and remove players so that one dominant entity can capture resources. Part of the core gameplay is leveraging resources \"enemies\" have already obtained, mechanical efficiency, and coordination (if not solo-queued) against \"the others\". The game, by its mechanics, is troublesome to me. This is not to mention the pure pay-to-win psycholog"},{"id":26980832,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2021-04","story":"Fortnite’s cash cow is Playstation, not iOS, court documents reveal","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as evidence that consoles allow Fortnite.","quote":"","text":"> The consoles would never allow them They allow Fortnite[1], don't they? Or more recently Genshin Impact, which is a pure \"gatcha\" game - as \"mobile\" as it gets - and is probably doing very well[2] on PS4/5. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] Edit: Oh, I totally forgot about the original console \"strip you of your money\" game: Fifa[3]. [3] [LINK]"},{"id":24149015,"author":"janderson3","month":"2020-08","story":"Epic Games releases \"Nineteen Eighty-Fortnite\" ad","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Dan Olson's manufactured discontent thesis about skins.","quote":"This really supports Dan Olson manufactured discontent assertion[0].","text":"I find it fascinating that in that ad the schlubs that are sitting there watching the television are Fortnite default skins and not say, replicas of the characters that hold the same position in the original Apple ad. This really supports Dan Olson manufactured discontent assertion[0]. Even if you support them and play their game, if you don't spend money for skins, you're still in Big Brother's audience. The wildest thing is that the Jackboots behind the hammer thrower are approximate replicas of the Apple Ad. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19610251,"author":"Nzen","month":"2019-04","story":"Fortnite and the Good Life","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of how Fortnite obscures prices by showing few items.","quote":"Folding Idea's analysis of Fortnite [0] suggests that it does.","text":"I haven't played fortnite, but Folding Idea's analysis of Fortnite [0] suggests that it does. As a specific example, it presents only a small subset of the items available to purchase any given day. This allows them to obscure the relative prices in a way that would not be possible with many more viewable for comparison. A microphone hammer is 800v today. How does that price compare to a gold hammer, or what was its price two seasons ago ? [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["4PHT-zBxKQQ",1],["EiZhdpLXZ8Q",1],["jQIHqkudgNY",1],["NOAgplgTxfc",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["agzNANfNlTs",1],["dljNabciEGg",1]]},"lw6TaiXzHAE":{"wy":"Treated as a must-watch for language designers, and cited whenever extensibility and language size are debated.","q":{"x":"In case anyone hasn't seen this most excellent talk on this topic, I highly recommend","i":30068289,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32636011,"author":"MrDOS","month":"2022-08","story":"The Up-Goer Five Text Editor","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk; better upload without capture errors.","quote":"This is an excellent talk, and exactly where my mind went, too.","text":"This is an excellent talk, and exactly where my mind went, too. I might suggest watching this upload of it instead, to avoid capture errors in the older upload: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30068289,"author":"nfoz","month":"2022-01","story":"In defense of complicated programming languages","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Most excellent talk on growing a language.","quote":"In case anyone hasn't seen this most excellent talk on this topic, I highly recommend","text":"In case anyone hasn't seen this most excellent talk on this topic, I highly recommend: Guy Steele - Growing a Language (OOPSLA '98) [VIDEO]"},{"id":48804177,"author":"pohl","month":"2026-07","story":"A Forlorn Hope of Fortran Modernisation","stance":"rec","why":"A valuable talk from Guy Steele.","quote":"At least we got this talk from guy steele","text":"It’s a tragedy. I feel the work they were doing was important. At least we got this talk from guy steele [VIDEO]"},{"id":35304744,"author":"craftuser","month":"2023-03","story":"How Big Should a Programming Language Be?","stance":"rec","why":"Related talk on language size, broad concepts beyond Java.","quote":"A good watch for the fine people in this thread","text":"This Guy Steele talk is related to this topic: [VIDEO] A good watch for the fine people in this thread (and please ignore explicit or implied linkages to Java--it's full of the broad concepts, not narrowly JVM stuff)"},{"id":33658992,"author":"pfarrell","month":"2022-11","story":"Fred Brooks has died","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among inspiring talks.","quote":"Growing a Language by Guy Steele","text":"Not writing, but I have found all of Brett Victor's speeches to be incredibly inspiring on how I think about what I want to work on and where we're going. Inventing on Principle by Brett Victor: [LINK] Growing a Language by Guy Steele: [VIDEO] We Really Don't Know How to Compute! by Gerald Sussman: [LINK]"},{"id":24385622,"author":"jdontillman","month":"2020-09","story":"Growing a Language (1998) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Cleaned-up version of the Growing a Language talk.","quote":"But very recently the Computer History Museum published a cleaned-up version","text":"For some reason that version has a large number of audio glitches. But very recently the Computer History Museum published a cleaned-up version: [VIDEO] Good!"},{"id":48583567,"author":"another-dave","month":"2026-06","story":"Loreline – Tools for writing interactive fiction","stance":"reference","why":"Reminded of the talk; seen as fitting for interactive fiction.","quote":"Always reminds me of Guy Steele's \"Growing a Language\" talk","text":"Always reminds me of Guy Steele's \"Growing a Language\" talk - [VIDEO] Think it's a great fit for IF alright."},{"id":44435066,"author":"teraflop","month":"2025-07","story":"Aesop in Words of One Syllable","stance":"reference","why":"Compared to Steele's famous monosyllable-style talk.","quote":"Guy Steele's famous \"Growing a Language\" talk","text":"Neat. I wonder if this was an inspiration for Guy Steele's famous \"Growing a Language\" talk, or if he came up with the same idea independently. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38277779,"author":"gavinhoward","month":"2023-11","story":"A decade of developing a programming language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"As someone who has been designing a language since late 2012 (and it's about to have its first public release early next year O.o), here are some thoughts. Avoid gradual typing : Absolutely. I had gradual typing and abandoned it. Gradual typing is not as good as simple Go-like inference, and that simple inference is easy to implement and takes care of 95% of the \"problem.\" However, you also need one or more dynamic typing escape hatches. When I implemented a config file format by tweaking JSON, I had to implement dynamic typing in C. Avoid self-hosting your compiler : It depends. You probably should avoid it by default, unless your language is just so much better than the original. Rust is an example of this (compared to C++). I'm writing in C, and I want memory safety [1], so bootstrapping makes sense. Avoid writing your own code generator, linker, etc. : This is excellent advice! Use the standard tools. Of course, me being me, I'm breaking it. :) I am trying a different distribution method, where you distribute an LLVM-like IR, and the end machine then generates the machine code on first run. In that case, there's no linker necessary, but I do have to write my own code generator. Fun. Avoid bike shedding about syntax : Yes, absolutely. I did this, but the syntax still changed enormously ! Cross-platform support is a challenge : Yes, in more ways than one. First, you have to somehow generate code for all of the platforms, then you have to make sure your library works on all "},{"id":34351775,"author":"j-pb","month":"2023-01","story":"Zig Package Manager MVP","stance":"reference","why":"Suggested Zig team should heed this talk.","quote":"The zig folks should really have this talk running on a loop","text":"Really feels like the project is spreading itself too thin. There are existing community build Package Managers for Zig. It's weird to start replacing things that are good enough™, while core stuff like the languag and compiler is not even ready for hobby projects. The zig folks should really have this talk running on a loop: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34416730,"author":"gavinhoward","month":"2023-01","story":"We Need to Know LR and Recursive Descent Parsing Techniques","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele's advice on parsers mentioned in passing.","quote":"In that case, I agree with Guy Steele.","text":"> What do more experienced people here think of that? \"Experienced\" person here. I've done a parser generator grammar or two. I've implemented several parsers. I mostly agree, but it depends on your use case. Most people writing parsers are doing so for a bespoke language, usually for config of an internal-use-only program or something like that. Such parsers won't be great, but they will work for people that: 1. Know the language, or 2. Are expected to know the language. In that case, I agree with Guy Steele. Why these two caveats? Because as people have said, error reporting from the parser will be subpar. This includes hand-written recursive descent ones. So knowing the language is important. (The second item is specifically for situations where new employees might not know the language yet, but it's part of their job, so they will learn it.) This is, by far, the greatest amount of parsers in use. I would estimate 99.5% or more. Of course, most people don't think about those parsers; they think about the hardened parsers for general-purpose languages in wide use, but while they get a lot of attention, they are few and far between. Guy Steele's advice doesn't apply as well to such situations. For me, I wrote a parser for an existing language. I had to write the parser to that, and that language had a lot of baggage. It made no sense in that case. For the parser I'm currently writing, it also makes no sense. This is for what I hope to become a widely-used general purpose lan"},{"id":29096208,"author":"ghoward","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: If you designed a new programming language, what would its features be?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on why languages must be extensible.","quote":"See \"Growing a Language\" by Guy Steele, Jr.","text":"Funny you ask that, because I am making a programming language! (1) Structured concurrency, best explained at [1], [2], [3], and [4]. More on this later. (2) Extensibility. Most people think that this means macros. It doesn't. However it's done, as curryst said, languages are not perfect, and extending a language is important. See \"Growing a Language\" by Guy Steele, Jr. [5] More on this later. (3) Like many other posts, having real-world units like feet, meters, pounds, kilograms, etc., for calculations and type checking, but the default number type should also be a BigRational. (4) Ranged types, like Ada, allowing programmers to explicitly limit the range a type can be, eliminating errors. For example, a type that is used in the denominator of a division could be restricted to being not zero, and you don't need to worry about divide-by-zero for those divisions. (5) Consistency, a massively underrated feature. The use of something in the language should be the same everywhere . This simplifies learning and makes it easier to understand features you haven't seen yet. (6) Design-by-Contract and other formal methods \"in the small\". It should be possible to use formal methods on little pieces of code. Even if you can't go so far as to prove them correct, it should be easy to increase your confidence that they are. (7) Conditions and restarts instead of exceptions. Conditions and restarts come from Lisp, and it is one of the greatest ideas in computer science that almost nobody kn"},{"id":29112357,"author":"j-pb","month":"2021-11","story":"What memory model should Rust use?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the model for growing a language from a small core.","quote":"like described in Guy Steeles talk on \"growing a language","text":"The shallowness of rusts correctness has pretty much killed my interest in it. Much of its purported virtue seems to be mostly lore and myth, and doesn't keep its promises under close inspection and scrutiny. For example the \"Rustonomicon\" is only half finished and doesn't have that much real insight that couldn't be gained from a lot of basic-ish rust tutorials. The memory model is not propery defined and only kinda sorta borrowed from C++ as the article mentions. The borrow checker has only been formally specified and veryfied in a much simplified form and only very recently [2] ,with the actual model \"just being what the borrow checker implementation does\". Having a fancy datalog based borrow checker is cool, but I'd much rather have a formal specifiation of the semantics. Rust took the big bang approach of building everything at once, implementing stuff first without any formal semantics and just floating on an iceberg of implementation definedness, with endless discussions on how things COULD be formalized. That's pretty much the antithesis to rusts safety philosophy; it's teaching water but drinking barrels of wine. It wants your code to be well defined and follow its rules, but its rules are completely arbitrary, not properly defined nor written down. I wish instead they had just started with a small borrow checked core language, and grown it from there, like described in Guy Steeles talk on \"growing a language\" [1]. I feel that Zig is doing a much better job in this r"}],"r":[["HB5TrK7A4pI",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["vN4U5FqrOdQ",1],["1MGjFKs9bnU",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["8Z685OF-PS8",1]]},"MBnnXbOM5S4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the clearest visual explanation that uncertainty is intrinsic to waves, separate from measurement disturbance.","q":{"x":"The clearest, most intuitive explanation of the Fourier Uncertainty Principle that I have come across is by Grant Sanderson of 3Blue1Brown.","i":41441867,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41441867,"author":"Cadwhisker","month":"2024-09","story":"The Fourier Uncertainty Principles [pdf] (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clearest intuitive explanation of Fourier uncertainty principle","quote":"The clearest, most intuitive explanation of the Fourier Uncertainty Principle that I have come across is by Grant Sanderson of 3Blue1Brown.","text":"The clearest, most intuitive explanation of the Fourier Uncertainty Principle that I have come across is by Grant Sanderson of 3Blue1Brown. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44822997,"author":"ben_w","month":"2025-08","story":"Project Hyperion: Interstellar ship design competition","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why position and momentum info cannot coexist via Fourier","quote":"It's easier to see why if you think about Fourier transforms","text":"The reason Trek has it is likely a common misunderstanding of QM: it's not only that we cannot record both position and momentum, the information does not exist in the first place . It's easier to see why if you think about Fourier transforms: [VIDEO] TLDW: an infinitely long wave does not (cannot) have any definite location, but it does have a definite periodic wavelength; conversely, a single impulse noise (a shockwave from e.g. a bullet or an explosion) has a definite location (in the direction of motion and at any given point in time) but no meaningful wavelength. The more you constrain the possibility space of one, the looser the other becomes in a physical sense, not just the information you have about it ."},{"id":38667973,"author":"nextaccountic","month":"2023-12","story":"An Interactive Guide to the Fourier Transform","stance":"rec","why":"Uncertainty principle follows from Fourier transform, not just quantum mechanics","quote":"So there's an uncertainty principle for signal processing, entirely because of the fourier transform and not because of quantum mechanics","text":"Yes, but that's because the momentum is the fourier transform of the position and vice versa So there's an uncertainty principle for signal processing, entirely because of the fourier transform and not because of quantum mechanics See this video [VIDEO]"},{"id":25741069,"author":"uoaei","month":"2021-01","story":"Is the Schrödinger Equation True?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how Heisenberg uncertainty arises from waves","quote":"(3b1b on the Heisenberg uncertainty principle and how it arises)","text":"This may help answer some of your question, though I recognize it does not address it directly: [VIDEO] (3b1b on the Heisenberg uncertainty principle and how it arises) Think of a free neutron as the wave envelope, rather than any of the bumps in the wave."},{"id":23659812,"author":"Kednicma","month":"2020-06","story":"A Physicist Trying to Fix Quantum Mechanics","stance":"rec","why":"Visual explanation of uncertainty principle via Fourier analysis","quote":"3blue1brown has an excellent two-video visual explanation [1][2]","text":"In many cases, Wikipedia actually lists the analogy for classical probability theory. For several theorems, the proof for Hilbert spaces can be directly brought to the classical world. The main difference to keep in mind is that we have amplitudes, not probabilities, and they can be negative or complex. We recover typical probability theory by taking norms/squares of amplitudes. The uncertainty principle follows via doing signal analysis on Fourier-transformed wavefunctions. A fun professional treatment from Baez et al. is [0], and 3blue1brown has an excellent two-video visual explanation [1][2]. The uncertainty principle turns out to be a special case of the sampling theorem (yes, that one! [3]), which itself turns out to be a special case of a result in sheaf theory [4]. Measurements matter because \"observables don't commute\"; taking linear operators on the complex numbers or other Hilbert spaces can have lasting effects which can't easily be undone. Combine this with \"conservation of probability\", which is formally mostly abstract nonsense [5], and we get mostly to Aaronson's point of view. (I would go further using the Free Will Theorem. [6]) When Aaronson says that the no-cloning principle is provable using probability, but for complex numbers, he's referring to the standard proof [7]. There are two connections to draw to typical probability theory. The first, and bigger, connection is that random variables can't be cloned in typical probability theory either! The second"},{"id":19705619,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-04","story":"Why have so many physicists shrugged off the paradoxes of quantum mechanics?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains intrinsic uncertainty as wave packet tradeoff, separate from measurement","quote":"3Blue1Brown has an extremely good explanation[1] of the intrinsic uncertainty, and why it's separate from measurement uncertainty.","text":"3Blue1Brown has an extremely good explanation[1] of the intrinsic uncertainty, and why it's separate from measurement uncertainty. (the previous episode[2] is a recommended prerequisite for background on how the Fourier Transform works) > emerges through a different, as yet unknown, mechanism. In 3Blue1Bron's explanation[1], he shows how the intrinsic uncertainty is an inherent trade-off of trying to measure both position and frequency. A short wave packet only a few wavelengths long correlates with a narrow (precise) range of positions, but also correlates well with a very wide range of frequencies due. A Heisenberg-like uncertainty exists any time you are working with weave packets with length near the wavelength. 3Blue1Brown gives a very good example using Doppler radar. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19733967,"author":"chriswarbo","month":"2019-04","story":"Why have so many physicists shrugged off the paradoxes of quantum mechanics?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows uncertainty principle as a property of waves, not observation","quote":"3blue1brown did a nice video on this","text":"The 'measuring something disturbs it' idea described above is called the observer effect [LINK] The observer effect is a real thing, and even has interesting effects in quantum mechanics, e.g. the quantum zeno effect [LINK] Yet the observer effect is not the reason why we can't know an object's position and velocity at the same time. There are two ways we can see that this supposed explanation is a red herring: - We don't need to interact with (e.g. 'bounce a photon off') a quantum system in order to observe it ( [LINK] ). I particularly like the \"quantum bomb detector\" ( [LINK] ), which can tells us whether a photon detector connected to a bomb is working or not, without hitting it with a photon and hence triggering the bomb (50% of the time, at least). - The actual reason, the uncertainty principle ( [LINK] ), doesn't require any notion of observation at all, let alone interaction. It's a simple property of waves (the relationship between duration and frequency). 3blue1brown did a nice video on this [VIDEO]"},{"id":40580049,"author":"westurner","month":"2024-06","story":"An animated introduction to Fourier series","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as related material alongside convolution theorem notes","quote":"The more general uncertainty principle, regarding Fourier transforms\" by 3blue1brown","text":"\"The more general uncertainty principle, regarding Fourier transforms\" by 3blue1brown [VIDEO] From [LINK] : > Convolution is in fact multiplication in Fourier space (this is the convolution theorem [1]) which says that Fourier transforms convert convolutions to products. 1. [LINK] : >> In mathematics, the convolution theorem states that under suitable conditions the Fourier transform of a convolution of two functions (or signals) is the pointwise product of their Fourier transforms. More generally, convolution in one domain (e.g., time domain) equals point-wise multiplication in the other domain (e.g., frequency domain). Other versions of the convolution theorem are applicable to various Fourier-related transforms. \"QFT and iQFT; Inverted Quantum Fourier Transform\" [LINK]"},{"id":25643126,"author":"wruza","month":"2021-01","story":"Physics Without Probability (2015) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows uncertainty is a general property of wavy systems","quote":"The uncertainty is not exclusive to QM and is a property of any ‘wavy’ system, it seems.","text":"The uncertainty is not exclusive to QM and is a property of any ‘wavy’ system, it seems. It is inherent. Here is the example: [VIDEO] — if you want to defeat QM, attack its wave-part. It would be nice to have much better (deterministic, measurable) physics, but some properties just emerge from a fundamental level and you can’t do much about it (monster group etc). Nature and maths don’t care about our confusion and convenience. >should have refuted it and looked for something better instead Afaik, the time when theories were driven by ideas is over. Today it’s petabytes of data that you can’t really argue with over a mailbox, only discuss. (I’m just a layman physicist with deep interest, but shallow understanding, so please don’t quote me on any of this)"},{"id":23647955,"author":"krastanov","month":"2020-06","story":"What is a photon? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Related wave packet and frequency uncertainty explanation","quote":"3blue1brown was somewhat useful, somewhat tangential video on the topic","text":"It is a neat trick actually. If you superimpose a few of these waves with frequencies that are fairly close, then you will see how they cancel out in the distance, while they reinforce each other in the center. This is called a \"wave packet\". 3blue1brown was somewhat useful, somewhat tangential video on the topic [VIDEO] The more rigorous way to say \"superimpose a few of these waves\", is to say that there is some uncertainty in the frequency. This uncertainty in the frequency lets you have more certainty about the position (the wave packet is centered somewhere, instead of being completely distributed the way an infinite plane wave is)"},{"id":22564514,"author":"jerf","month":"2020-03","story":"Superdeterminism may help us overcome the current crisis in physics","stance":"reference","why":"Ties Fourier transform limits to the uncertainty principle","quote":"The limitations of the Fourier transform are intimately tied to the uncertainty principle","text":"The limitations of the Fourier transform are intimately tied to the uncertainty principle: [VIDEO] Reality does appear to be limited by the limitations of the Fourier transform."}],"r":[["wrwgIjBUYVc",2],["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1]]},"v1WiCGq-PcY":{"wy":"HN applies it to job interviews and confidence, recommending the mindset of not interviewing from a place of desperation.","q":{"x":"Nobody explains this better than Bryan Cranston (possibly one of the most successful TV actors of recent times)","i":11393813,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11393813,"author":"soham","month":"2016-03","story":"For anyone who has been turned down by 38 companies","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cranston on approaching auditions without desperation to land the job.","quote":"Nobody explains this better than Bryan Cranston (possibly one of the most successful TV actors of recent times)","text":"There is only one way to toughen up: Practice. Be it interviews or sports. Be it negotiations or cooking. Practice and practice perfectly. 1. Take mock interviews from experienced engineers Go one topic at a time e.g. Recursion or Trees or DP or Multithreading etc. Before you walk into the interview, practice well. Do at least 20 problems on that topic, if not more. 2. Do practice interviews at companies you don't want to work for Don't feel an imposter in doing this. Realize that companies also go thru several candidates before they pick one. They are normal people like you, they have a learning curve to fill the position, and so do you. Finding the right company, at the right stage of your life, is not a trivial thing to do; it's a project and needs to be managed like that. Going thru various interviews hardens your skills of negotiation, your understanding of business models, and more importantly, gives you clarity about what your strengths and limitations are. 3. Practice the right mindset Never go to interviews with a loaded agony of getting a job. Go simply with the intent of solving interesting problems with interesting people (\"to add life to your character\"). Nobody explains this better than Bryan Cranston (possibly one of the most successful TV actors of recent times): [VIDEO] I make these observations based on my experience running a successful bootcamp for interview prep ( [LINK] ) and also being an early engineer and a hiring manager (Director of engineering) at "},{"id":11393936,"author":"eric_h","month":"2016-03","story":"For anyone who has been turned down by 38 companies","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clip that raised the commenter's respect for Bryan Cranston.","quote":"That clip just increased my already substantial respect for Bryan Cranston","text":"> Nobody explains this better than the meth guy: [VIDEO] That clip just increased my already substantial respect for Bryan Cranston, though I feel as though calling him the \"meth guy\" does him a disservice. Yes, Breaking Bad brought him a great deal of exposure, but I think that even his role in Malcolm in the Middle showed how capable he is as an actor. The fact that he performed both roles excellently is also a testament to his skills. He's also won a Tony, and was nominated for a few director awards. ( [LINK] )"},{"id":31964412,"author":"mandeepj","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: I’m 41 and still unmarried – what should I do?","stance":"rec","why":"Advice to present your best self and not control who likes you.","quote":"You can only present the best version of yourself, but you can't control who will and won't like you","text":"> You can only present the best version of yourself, but you can't control who will and won't like you if you want to listen to that advice - [VIDEO]"},{"id":12319470,"author":"tomohawk","month":"2016-08","story":"Ask HN: How does someone get more confident?","stance":"rec","why":"Mindset advice for job hunting from Bryan Cranston.","quote":"keep this in mind","text":"First, find a mentor you can talk to and who can guide you. In your case, getting a mentor who does not work where you work would be better. Perhaps someone at church, or within the local tech community. Second, it sounds like you may be overthinking things. Get yourself out there and start interviewing. You'll find that your thought processes will often change in response to actions. When you get out there and start looking, keep this in mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11804410,"author":"soham","month":"2016-05","story":"How to win the coding interview","stance":"rec","why":"Grounding mindset for interviews, from an actor's perspective.","quote":"you'll progress faster if you keep your mindset grounded. See this","text":"[Disclaimer: I run [LINK] . It's a hyper-focused bootcamp on coding interview prep] Probably one of the biggest mistakes developers make, is to treat coding interviews like they are standardized tests with a checklist of evaluation. They are not. They are like a date. The other person is judging whether they want to work with you, more than anything else. No matter how good you are, you won't get into a long-term relationship (date) if you don't confirm to the (technical and social) beliefs (and biases) of your interviewer (date). The reverse is also true viz. no matter how mediocre you are, you'll get in, if you do. So, do everything what the author says, but you'll progress faster if you keep your mindset grounded. See this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11460415,"author":"soham","month":"2016-04","story":"Ask HN: I do terrible in algorithm questions in interviews","stance":"rec","why":"Advice on not interviewing from a place of desperation.","quote":"Don't interview with the agony of getting a job. Just go to solve the problems. Listen to this","text":"You're thinking of interviews as standardized tests (exam). To some extent, they are, but overall they are closer to a date , than to an exam. In other words, it's not sufficient that you be good at algorithms. You also need to get a panel of interviewers with whom you gel well. What interviewers you get at a company, is not in your hands. You can try a few things: 1. Do mock interviews with experienced interviewers. Get used to being judged. Repeatedly. 2. Don't interview with the agony of getting a job. Just go to solve the problems. Listen to this: [VIDEO] 3. Interview at companies that bias towards take-home tests. If you haven't already, then try triplebyte.com. They can help you find such companies. -- I make these observations based on my experience running a successful bootcamp for interview prep ( [LINK] ) and also being an early engineer and a hiring manager (Director of engineering) at Box prior to that."},{"id":10622332,"author":"js2","month":"2015-11","story":"What I Learned From Blowing An Interview","stance":"rec","why":"Bryan Cranston's advice to aspiring actors, applied to job interviews.","quote":"Thanks for the tip.","text":"\"Bryan Cranston's Advice to Aspiring Actors\" [VIDEO] Thanks for the tip. I hadn't seen this video before."},{"id":20375267,"author":"kentrado","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: Why are hiring processes flawed and testing random standards?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"make them jump like monkeys with impunity.","text":"They only get as picky as their options. Remote companies get exponentially more candidates so they can make them jump like monkeys with impunity. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["N1pIYI5JQLE",1],["oFeoS41xe7w",1],["iYJKd0rkKss",1],["xtpgkX588nM",1]]},"5uljtqyBLxI":{"wy":"HN treats Cliff Click's talks as an excellent source on JVMs, garbage collection and hardware-assisted Java.","q":{"x":"Anything from Cliff Click is quite good.","i":5519772,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5519772,"author":"qznc","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cliff Click has deep understanding of compilers, VMs, and CPUs.","quote":"Anything from Cliff Click is quite good.","text":"Probably this one: [LINK] Anything from Cliff Click is quite good. This guy has a very deep understanding of compilers, virtual-machines, CPUs, and their interactions. A JVM does that? [LINK] Java on a 1000 Cores [VIDEO]"},{"id":886814,"author":"bokchoi","month":"2009-10","story":"JOP: A Tiny Java Processor Core for FPGA","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk by Cliff Click on Azul hardware and JIT versus bytecode CPUs.","quote":"Here's an excellent google tech talk by Cliff Click of Azul talking about their hardware.","text":"Here's an excellent google tech talk by Cliff Click of Azul talking about their hardware. Among the firehose of information in that talk, he mentions that the hardware guys wanted to add directly execute JVM bytecode. He told them not to bother since he could JIT it better and they wouldn't be able to support all the bytecodes anyway. I'd love to know more about why that is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45991954,"author":"hayley-patton","month":"2025-11","story":"Implementation of a Java Processor on a FPGA (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Cliff Click on Azul's hardware-assisted Java CPU with GC barriers","quote":"Cliff Click gave an excellent talk on it","text":"> What I can imagine is a purpose-built CPU that would make the JIT's job a lot easier and faster than compiling for x86 or ARM. Such a machine wouldn't execute raw Java bytecode, rather, something a tiny bit more low-level. This is approximately exactly what Azul Systems did, doing a bog-standard RISC with hardware GC barriers and transactional memory. Cliff Click gave an excellent talk on it [0] and makes your argument around 20:14. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23777030,"author":"mcbridematt","month":"2020-07","story":"Java Optimized Processor (2005)","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on Azul's multicore Java hardware and JIT target.","quote":"there is an interesting tech talk about it","text":"Azul Systems (who today are mainly a JVM vendor) started by building large multicore boxes (Vega) to do Java workloads - there is an interesting tech talk about it: [VIDEO] (2009) They built a \"really good JIT target\" rather than direct bytecode for reasons explained in the video. Curious to hear from anyone who ever used one..."},{"id":9187106,"author":"Alupis","month":"2015-03","story":"Peter Lawrey Describes Petabyte JVMs","stance":"rec","why":"Cliff Click's great talks on JVM development and scaling.","quote":"Cliff Click from Azul has done some great Google Tech Talks about JVM development and scaling JVM's out to really big systems.","text":"There are some interesting challenging when scaling this large with any system. Peter referenced some of Azul System's stuff and their custom GC. Cliff Click from Azul has done some great Google Tech Talks about JVM development and scaling JVM's out to really big systems.[1][2] [1] A JVM Does That? - [LINK] [2] Java on a 1000 Cores - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29660309,"author":"hayley-patton","month":"2021-12","story":"Does the Bronze Garbage Collector Make Rust Easier to Use?","stance":"reference","why":"Click's result doubling stack allocations via escape detection.","quote":"Cliff Click reported roughly doubling the number of stack-allocated objects by using escape detection rather than analysis [3].","text":"Note that \"short lived objects\" and \"stack allocated objects\" aren't exactly the same set. Common Lisp has a dynamic-extent declaration, which can be used to stack allocate [1]. Go and Java implementations perform escape analysis. There can have some wins if stack allocatability is tracked at runtime, rather than compile-time [2]; Cliff Click reported roughly doubling the number of stack-allocated objects by using escape detection rather than analysis [3]. At least the Inkscape vector editor uses the Boehm garbage collector [4]. There are several programming language implementations which use Boehm and C, but one wonders if it is still a \"C codebase\" then. [1] [LINK] [2] Henry Baker, CONS should not CONS its arguments [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":8661999,"author":"nickik","month":"2014-11","story":"Java Garbage Collection Distilled (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Click talks about Intel and Sun's lack of interest.","quote":"See Cliff Click talk about intel and sun.","text":"I have not suggested that they put C4 or the orginal passless as a standard. While they did focuse on a perticular market segment the performance and paustime numbers they showed could easly have been usful for tons and tons of other markets. While C4 does currently require OS enhancements, they could make it without it, it would just be slower. However the problem that Azul has tried to overcome with the OS could have been much more powerful attacked with the full force of oracle/sun behind it. They once put some linux patches but it was never there focus and it never went upstream. At a big company this technogoly could have had a bigger impact, they could have talked to microsoft, apple and the linux team. I know that Azul also tried to strike up a conversation with Intel but in there words \"it was like moving a mountain\". And im not suggesting Sun/Oracle should have done this for the greater good of all humans. They would would have created a gigantig benefit for java products over the competition C# and C++ in the server space. I rember talks by Cliff Click that mentioned that they were really suprised that Sun did not have more intresst in them, considering some simple things that Sun could have to get huge increses in some of the high end server systems they were selling (am searching for the refernce ...). Edit: See Cliff Click talk about intel and sun. [VIDEO] t=54m33s He mentions that the sun shareholders should put there exutive staff into prison."},{"id":8541447,"author":"wahern","month":"2014-10","story":"What sort of allocation rates can servers handle?","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation covering the basics of Azul's pauseless collector.","quote":"All the basics are discernible from this presentation","text":"I don't think that there's anything magical about C4. All the basics are discernible from this presentation: [VIDEO] Azul originally started out as a hardware company. A pauseless collector is easy as long as you have transactional memory (at a minimum hardware which supports strong LL/SC, allowing you to build the rest in software). No commodity CPUs support this, then or now, so Azul's first product was a specialized CPU and memory architecture with real transactional memory semantics. But nobody wants to buy specialized hardware these days. So they ended up having to emulate transactional memory on x86. That means _emulating_ the memory architecture of their original CPU. Again, there's no secret sauce to do doing this. Things like QEMU are free--which is to say, many people know how to do it. It just takes a lot of programmer hours to build it, and many, many more to optimize it. Long story short, Azul's collector is truly pauseless, but its transactional throughput in the vast majority of workloads sucks because it's an emulator (for the JVM) built atop another emulator (for their original memory architecture). And while you can speed up emulating the JVM using JIT techniques, that doesn't work for emulating a transactional memory architecture. There are endless tweaks you can make, including playing around with memory page tables, etc, but at the end up the day you're still stuck emulating the semantics you need, causing huge latencies relative to your clock speed, whic"},{"id":864435,"author":"jeremyw","month":"2009-10","story":"An Interview with David Moon and Daniel Weinreb","stance":"reference","why":"Click on hardware read barriers transforming GC performance.","quote":"Intel could implement a hardware read-barrier and radically change the GC performance picture for commodity systems.","text":"As Click discusses in his Google tech talk ( [VIDEO] ) Intel could implement a hardware read-barrier and radically change the GC performance picture for commodity systems."}],"r":[["uL2D3qzHtqY",2],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["Uooh0Y9fC_M",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["43XaZEn2aLc",1]]},"Hhy7JUinlu0":{"wy":"HN cites the scene as a model of clear, decisive communication and of leaders acting fast to survive, and recommends the film as the best take on 2008.","q":{"x":"There is a very fine example of this in cinema","i":31059840,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31059840,"author":"unfocussed_mike","month":"2022-04","story":"Be Less Technical","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fine example of explaining what matters while eliding detail.","quote":"There is a very fine example of this in cinema","text":"Right. You can't hide the detail with a wave of the hand, you can definitely overcommunicate detail that is unnecessary, but the art of it is finding a way to explain the bit that matters in a way that makes it clear to the users that you're eliding detail that isn't important, without misleading them. There is a very fine example of this in cinema -- the senior partners meeting in Margin Call, where Zachary Quinto's character has to explain why the firm needs to sell all of a particular asset class: [VIDEO] One day I will use the line Jeremy Irons uses in this clip. I don't want to spoil it by quoting it."},{"id":21118110,"author":"abvdasker","month":"2019-09","story":"WeWork says will file to withdraw IPO","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Boardroom scene capturing the 2008 crisis and a convincing CEO.","quote":"what a fantastic film.","text":"Oh man, what a fantastic film. It's a been a while since I saw Margin Call, but I still feel like it's one of the films that best captured the 2008 financial crisis. Jeremy Irons' portrayal of a Goldman CEO-like character — I think he's the one who delivers the line you quoted — remains probably the most convincing portrayal of that type of person I can remember. The wonderful board room scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42365709,"author":"silvestrov","month":"2024-12","story":"Pat Gelsinger was wrong for Intel","stance":"rec","why":"Scene where leaders decide to act immediately on an expensive sell-off to survive.","quote":"The movie \"Margin Call\" has an excellent scene where this happends","text":"The movie \"Margin Call\" has an excellent scene where this happends: [VIDEO] It is clear how it is setup that \"to survive we must act now, not in a week, and we must do this expensive sell-off\" The whole movie is very well worth watching: [LINK]"},{"id":33104369,"author":"photochemsyn","month":"2022-10","story":"Charles Mitchell and the 1929 stock crash","stance":"rec","why":"Best cinematic take on the 2008 financial crisis.","quote":"Probably the best cinematic take on that is in the movie Margin Call","text":"Yes indeed. Probably the best cinematic take on that is in the movie Margin Call - here's the senior partners meeting: [VIDEO] Essentially, the dissolution of Glass-Steagall allowed investment banks to create complex financial packages, each one constructed out of dozens if not hundreds of home mortgages. Each individual mortgage was graded but the packages were built out of a mixture of low-grade and high-grade mortgages. Since trading (gambling) with these packages was very lucrative, the investment banks worked with shady mortgage brokers to increase the number of packages by pushing adjustable-rate mortgages on gullible people who probably were not that familiar with compound interest calcululations. These unlikely-to-succeed mortgages were mixed in with the high-qulaity mortages to create a wide variety of highly leveraged financial products, and then bets were placed on the outcomes of trading in these products (derivative markets, synthetic CDO squared nonsense, etc.). Once people realized that many of these mortgages were not going to be paid off, then the avalanche of collapse began. Glass-Steagall, if retained, would have made all of that impossible."},{"id":16952047,"author":"chiph","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: Turning 40 soon – seeking personal and professional life advice","stance":"rec","why":"Movie recommendation about the 2008 crisis.","quote":"Movie recommendations: Margin Call","text":"Start of the Great Recession. S&P 500 went from 1400 to 800 in six months. I lost 40% of my retirement investment and it was ... distressing to say the least. Movie recommendations: Margin Call [VIDEO] The Big Short [LINK]"},{"id":39753571,"author":"onetimeuse92304","month":"2024-03","story":"Dead Air on the Incident Call","stance":"reference","why":"Margin Call scene showing a manager asking questions as a learner to gather information.","quote":"My ideal is this scene from Margin Call","text":"Simple concept, the author is overthinking it. I have been \"problem manager\" for many large outages. I use the term \"problem manager\" to remind people that an outage is something you manage just like any other kind of project, except on much shorter time scales. Everything you learned about project management applies to dealing with outages. > Sometimes an investigator needs to go silent for a while to chase down a hunch, or collect some data, or research some question. As long as such a silence is negotiated in advance, with a specific time to reconvene, it can serve a crucial purpose. I call this functional dead air. Hey, if you are the kind of project manager that talks and does not listen to your team... that's a problem. My ideal stance on those occasions is to present myself as somebody who \"wants to be educated about the issue\". I think it is more helpful and creates less stress. As I am asking questions I am trying to not seem to be interrogating them but instead emphasise I am a noob on the topic but need to learn quickly. My ideal is this scene from Margin Call: [VIDEO] This usually is actually true, btw. There is no single way to do it right but as a manager it is your job to maintain good information flow between you and your reports and on an outage, your reports are essentially everybody involved."},{"id":34863213,"author":"NegativeK","month":"2023-02","story":"Revenue is easy, profit is harder","stance":"reference","why":"Scene quote about making a living in finance.","quote":"There are three ways to make a living in this business: be first, be smarter, or cheat.","text":"I'm reminded of the scene in Margin Call[1]: \"There are three ways to make a living in this business: be first, be smarter, or cheat.\" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24270430,"author":"nicbou","month":"2020-08","story":"How to Write in Plain English","stance":"reference","why":"Example of speaking clearly and decisively.","quote":"I can only think of this scene right now.","text":"There is a place and a time for culture. Your electric bill is not one of them. I disagree with your last paragraph. Speaking clearly and decisively is a good thing, and people will usually appreciate it. People become famous for speaking clearly. I remember some general made the rounds for his plain, no-nonsense COVID-19 address. I wish I could find it again. I can only think of this scene right now. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35119941,"author":"belter","month":"2023-03","story":"Circle’s stablecoin banked at SVB and guess what happened next","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Willing to bet a dinner on it? I say it's a small club and this meeting is probably happening now...I think young Spock will change Yellen mind...All through logic... - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["0fKBhvDjuy0",1],["qS4H6PEcCCA",1],["PfYYraMgiBA",1],["qhbuKbxJsk8",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["8AHCfZTRGiI",1],["MesrrYyuoa4",1],["SC2eSujzrUY",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["lI7T650RTT8",1],["j4kI2h3iotA",1],["8bZh5LMaSmE",1]]},"f_0QlhYlS8g":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best introduction to Factor and concatenative programming, with commenters saying it impressed them with the engineering.","q":{"x":"It absolutely blew my mind.","i":18128634,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":46430008,"author":"fud101","month":"2025-12","story":"Ask HN: Why did Python fail to evolve?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Factor demo that blew Python out of the water","quote":"a contemporary language to python3 that completely blew Python out of the water.","text":"It's funny because 3 dropped in 2008, the same year Slava was demonstrating Factor in google tech talks which was a contemporary language to python3 that completely blew Python out of the water. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18128634,"author":"xelxebar","month":"2018-10","story":"Concatenative language (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tech talk on Factor with top-notch engineering","quote":"It absolutely blew my mind.","text":"I just stumbled across this Google Tech Talk on [factor][0] last night! It absolutely blew my mind. The engineering looks to be top notch, and the video is 9 years old to boot! [VIDEO] Anyway, really want to give Factor a try for some personal projects. Would love to hear anecdotes from people who futz with this stuff. [0]: [LINK]"},{"id":45076408,"author":"johnisgood","month":"2025-08","story":"The V Programming Language","stance":"rec","why":"Slava Pestov's 2008 talk on Factor","quote":"Here is his talk on Factor","text":"I agree. I remember his talk on Factor. I wish people gave enough support back then so he could work on it full-time. Here is his talk on Factor: [VIDEO] , back from 2008."},{"id":22068894,"author":"benhoyt","month":"2020-01","story":"Factor: A Practical Stack Languge","stance":"rec","why":"Factor's creator shows off the language","quote":"a Google tech talk video of Slava Pestov (Factor's creator) showing off the language here","text":"As someone who built Forth compilers back in the day, I really like this language. There's a nice overview of Factor as a paper here: [LINK] and a Google tech talk video of Slava Pestov (Factor's creator) showing off the language here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7082090,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2014-01","story":"K, by Arthur Whitney (2005)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by Factor's creator Slava Pestov","quote":"just in case here's a very enjoyable talk, not about, but by Slava","text":"I see what you did there. ps: just in case here's a very enjoyable talk, not about, but by Slava [VIDEO] Beautiful and impressive."},{"id":45137299,"author":"johnisgood","month":"2025-09","story":"Evolving the OCaml Programming Language (2025) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Factor implements much of itself as libraries","quote":"Large chunks of functionality are not part of the core language, they are in just as library","text":"Your comment does not make much sense even if it is true. Factor (Forth-like language) implements even its own \":\" (defines a word, i.e. a function) using the language itself, it is not builtin, same with \"if\", and so forth. Thus, \"MEMO:\" or locals[1] (\"::\") being implemented as a library does not mean it is a bad thing, on the contrary, in the case of Factor, it makes it quite powerful. The object system is entirely implemented in Factor, too. \"Large chunks of functionality are not part of the core language, they are in just as library\".[2] And to compare OCaml's type system to Python's is straight out absurd. [1] Locals are entirely implemented in Factor, too, which is only about ~500 lines of code. It is not part of the core language, and on top of that, there is no performance penalty whatsoever! [2] See more here: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":39298560,"author":"zoltar","month":"2024-02","story":"Thinking Forth: A Language and Philosophy for Solving Problems (2004) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Factor's quotations handle stack management well","quote":"Props to that whole community for their effort.","text":"There was always something I liked about Forth when I didn't have to do much stack-thinking. One of the things that felt forth-y to me when I first encountered it was threaded code in Clojure (or any other point-free kind of idiom for that matter). [LINK] I'll sometimes abuse Python's compose to do similar things I won't send out for code review. Maybe I really should just try to live in Coconut instead of silly stuff like: import chess import chess.svg from IPython.display import SVG, display from functools import reduce def rcompose(*fs): return reduce(lambda f, g: lambda *xs, **ys: g(f(*xs, **ys)), fs) inline_board = rcompose( chess.Board, chess.svg.board, SVG, display) Factor's quotations dealing with stack management weirdness is super nice, too. [LINK] It still has a bit more stack juggling than I like, though. That could just be a \"me\" problem. Props to that whole community for their effort. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8327405,"author":"runeks","month":"2014-09","story":"Pipes and Filters","stance":"reference","why":"Introduction to Factor, a language similar to pipes and filters","quote":"I recently came across a language called Factor that works very similar, if not identical, to this.","text":"I recently came across a language called Factor that works very similar, if not identical, to this. Here's a video about it: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lpu-3UF_b48",1],["5bQhGS8V6dQ",1],["2-oBNh0UkQc",1],["sCZJblyT_XM",1],["Mfsnlbd-4xQ",1],["NVEgyJCTee4",1],["vuIlsN32WaE",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["1AjhFZVfB9c",1],["iG9CE55wbtY",1],["V-1RhQ1uuQ4",1],["R7EEoWg6Ekk",1],["v7Mt0GYHU9A",1],["1PGm8LslEb4",1],["FY_NtO7SIrY",1],["z8MVKianh54",1]]},"gxAaO2rsdIs":{"wy":"HN pointed to it during 2020 as a digestible, well-made illustration of epidemic dynamics and interventions.","q":{"x":"3blue1brown's video on the topic is super digestible and informative [1].","i":22902446,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22905289,"author":"kaunta","month":"2020-04","story":"Building a command line tool to simulate the spread of an infection","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers social distancing, central locations, isolation, quarantine scenarios","quote":"My inspiration for writing this came from watching 3Blue1Brown's YouTube video","text":"Garrett, thank you. * * * My inspiration for writing this came from watching 3Blue1Brown's YouTube video, \"Simulating an epidemic\": [VIDEO] His simulations cover several scenarios: - Simulating social distancing. - Having people travel to a central location (e.g. a grocery store). - What if the government is able to identify and isolate cases? - Simulating multiple communities with travel, that put quarantine measures in place. - What happens when people get tired of social distancing. * * * Thanks for sharing your code. I see that you are using gnuplot to visualize the output of the script. Have you considered using the csv module ( [LINK] ) to output the data, instead of using the print() function? That way, you wouldn't have to manually add in spaces around the data and stringify the numeric data. >>> import csv, sys >>> writer = csv.writer(sys.stdout, delimiter=\" \") >>> writer.writerow([\"#\", \"T\", \"S\", \"I\", \"R\"]) # T S I R In the script, usage would look something like this: import csv import sys ... writer = csv.writer(sys.stdout, delimiter=\" \") writer.writerow([\"#\", \"T\", \"S\", \"I\", \"R\"]) writer.writerow([T, S, I, R]) while I > 1000: ... writer.writerow([T, S, I, R])"},{"id":22716520,"author":"foobarbecue","month":"2020-03","story":"Japan set to ban entry from the U.S. as early as next week","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Simulates travel restrictions and their limited effectiveness","quote":"Part of 3 Blue 1 Brown's excellent latest video simulates travel restrictions","text":"Part of 3 Blue 1 Brown's excellent latest video simulates travel restrictions and suggests they're not very effective: [VIDEO] . However, that was only for cutting travel between all communities by a factor of 4, rather than completely banning travel between two particular communities / countries."},{"id":27003558,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2021-05","story":"India’s second wave of Covid-19 feels nothing like its first","stance":"rec","why":"How an epidemic spreads","quote":"3Blue1Brown made a fascinating video about how an epidemic spreads","text":"Dynamics of the virus spread is quite interesting. 3Blue1Brown made a fascinating video about how an epidemic spreads: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25978900,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2021-01","story":"Contra Weyl on Technocracy","stance":"rec","why":"Nice explanation of more complicated epidemic models, with caveats","quote":"For a nice explanation of more complicated models, there is a video of 3Blue1Brown","text":"> Technocrats used complicated mathematical models to determine that mass vaccination would create a \"herd immunity\" to disease. The SIR model is very simple [LINK] At the beginning of the covid19 epidemic everyone and their dog was writing one and publishing a demo version. It was interesting/scary to modify the parameters and see how thing changed. Also, try to fix misinformation in the models, like a 50% death rate. Anyway, a SIR model can be programed in 20 lines of codes, and is pretty straightforward. Adding a vaccine is like 5 additional lines at most. Also, the main idea is very simple to understand. (Someone should write a tutorial of SIR in Excel/GoogleSheets, it's interesting to see the graphics.) The most simple models just assume that everyone meets everyone randomly. It's more complicated if you want to consider a more realistic model of how people move. For a decision about lockdown you need the complex model, but to understand herd immunity the simple model is good enough. For a nice explanation of more complicated models, there is a video of 3Blue1Brown: \"Simulating an epidemic\" [VIDEO] Note that he explains that the models in this video are not 100% accurate, some number are chosen to get nice graphics, they are only useful to get some insight but not get quantitative predictions."},{"id":24927795,"author":"tmabraham","month":"2020-10","story":"Manim: Animation engine for explanatory math videos","stance":"rec","why":"Nice video about epidemics made with manim","quote":"He also had a nice video about epidemics/pandemics","text":"BTW, he's currently co-teaching a course on Julia with some really great lectures on computational thinking: [LINK] He also had a nice video about epidemics/pandemics: [VIDEO] All using manim for visualizations."},{"id":22902446,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2020-04","story":"Covid-19 Antibody Seroprevalence in Santa Clara County, California","stance":"rec","why":"Digestible, informative explanation of the SIR model","quote":"3blue1brown's video on the topic is super digestible and informative [1].","text":"> ...noticeable reduction in the spread? I believe the SIR model [0] answers those questions. 3blue1brown's video on the topic is super digestible and informative [1]. In short, the discovery of a prophylactic if not a therapeutic treatment are our best immediate bets, absent which, quarantines and lockdowns will have to persist to curb the spread of a disease as infectious as covid-19 whilst we patiently wait for an effective vaccine against it [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22711544,"author":"gilbetron","month":"2020-03","story":"FDA grants emergency use authorization for 5-13 minute Covid-19 test","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrative simulation of disease spread","quote":"this is illustrative and well done that shows many aspects of disease spread","text":"If we can test and isolate, we can manage how the disease progresses through society, with a (very very slight, now) chance of stopping it. While not scientific, this is illustrative and well done that shows many aspects of disease spread: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22727266,"author":"mcsb4","month":"2020-03","story":"Austria to make basic face masks compulsory in supermarkets","stance":"rec","why":"Simulations of epidemic spreading","quote":"There is a pretty good video for simulations of the epidemic spreading","text":"Homemade masks made of a double layer of tea towel cloth are not only protecting others but also protect yourself nearly as effectively as N95 masks. The original research was made by PHE - Public Health England and the results are: B atrophaeus - ~1um : Surgical mask 96.35% filtering Double layer tea towel 96.71% filtering Bacteriophage MS2 - 23nm : Surgical mask 89.52% filtering Tea towel one layer 72.46% filtering The full paper is on researchgate, title: \"Testing the Efficacy of Homemade Masks: Would They Protect in an Influenza Pandemic?\" Also every super market should have the use of hand sanitizer obligatory at the entrance. There is a pretty good video for simulations of the epidemic spreading [1] - it is not going to stop until we either have a vaccine or ~60% of the population have antibodies. So the choice is to sit the next 12-18months at home or we fight the transmission of the virus. Masks cost nothing compared to the economic disaster that we are running into. The virus spreads via mouth and nose and secondary via the hands. So just stop it at the source. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38690525,"author":"salimmadjd","month":"2023-12","story":"3Blue1Brown Calculus Blog Series","stance":"reference","why":"Modeling showing lockdowns flatten rather than stop spread","quote":"3b1b did an interesting video on the epidemic modeling [0] that shaped my thinking a bit on the lockdown","text":"OT - 3b1b did an interesting video on the epidemic modeling [0] that shaped my thinking a bit on the lockdown and caused me to lose a few friends to argue against the prolonged lockdowns. I believe he used the same technique deployed by major media institutions and health organizations. Basically the interesting conclusion was unless you apply a very rigorous lockdown (CCP style) the virus ultimately spreads. All you’re doing is spreading the time. As in “flattening the curve”. As some might recall the initial lockdown was proposed as a way to flatten the curve and allow the medical system to cope better by having a more gradual influx of patients. Which makes me think everyone promoting the initial lockdown understood its limitations and its objectives. However, for what ever reason “control demolition” of the economy to make Trump lose the election or who knows why it turned into a long term lockdown. I know this is a controversial topic, but it shouldn’t be. This is a clear case that the science of pandemic modeling suggested one path and for political reasons we were pushed to follow a course that caused a lot of damage, some irreversible, to the society. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36218508,"author":"leobg","month":"2023-06","story":"Did lockdowns work? The verdict on Covid restrictions","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation supporting social distancing","quote":"And here’s 3Blue1Brown running a simulation","text":"I don’t get it. How can social distancing NOT help during a pandemic? Quote from the book 1491: “One reason is that Indians were fresh territory for many plagues, not just one. Smallpox, typhoid, bubonic plague, influenza, mumps, measles, whooping cough—all rained down on the Americas in the century after Columbus. (Cholera, malaria, and scarlet fever came later.) Having little experience with epidemic diseases, Indians had no knowledge of how to combat them. In contrast, Europeans were well versed in the brutal logic of quarantine. They boarded up houses in which plague appeared and fled to the countryside.” And here’s 3Blue1Brown running a simulation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23954957,"author":"andreareina","month":"2020-07","story":"What's the point of a Covid-19 test that takes 19 days for results?","stance":"reference","why":"Agent-based simulation showing mitigation sensitivity","quote":"this agent-based simulation suggest that mitigations like quarantine are highly sensitive to how thoroughly they are applied","text":"Not exactly a citation[1], but this agent-based simulation suggest that mitigations like quarantine are highly sensitive to how thoroughly they are applied: [VIDEO] [1] I'm putting this firmly in the \"all models are wrong, but some models are useful\" camp"},{"id":23054014,"author":"ilogik","month":"2020-05","story":"Why SARS-CoV-2 Bluetooth contact tracing apps are a tremendously stupid idea","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation of isolating contacts to stop exponential growth","quote":"if everyone has the app, you won't have an exponential growth.","text":"the whole point of the app is to isolate people that have been in contact with positive cases before they become contagious. if everyone has the app, you won't have an exponential growth. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22811499,"author":"michaelt","month":"2020-04","story":"We’re working on 1M Covid-19 testing capacity per day","stance":"reference","why":"Central locations increase spread of virus","quote":"some simulations show [1] if lots of people from an otherwise-socially-distancing population all go to one central location, that increases the R0","text":"Basically, some simulations show [1] if lots of people from an otherwise-socially-distancing population all go to one central location, that increases the R0 (i.e. spread) of the virus. This is a problem for anything that draws together lots of people from a large area into the same place - trade shows, sports events, music festivals, cinemas, conventions, busses and trains, food stores, beaches, universities and schools. We almost certainly haven't hit on the most optimal solution - in my country we closed schools, yet keeping crowded metro trains running? - but it wouldn't have been wise to wait for more data to come in before taking any action. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22945628,"author":"salimmadjd","month":"2020-04","story":"The epidemiologist behind Sweden’s controversial coronavirus strategy","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation showing flattened curve with same total infections","quote":"I had watched the simulation of covid19 infection by 1blue3brown[1]","text":"There is a lengthier interview by UnHeard with Professor Johan Giesecke (another experienced epidemiologist) of Sweden on YouTube [0]. It’s worthwhile listening to their perspective. One of the comments he made was that no lockdown would work in democratic countries and the real test will be fatality rates between Sweden and others in 1 year. He believed it’ll be very little difference between those countries that have implement the lockdown and those that haven’t I had watched the simulation of covid19 infection by 1blue3brown[1] and some of his modeling appeared to me (maybe I misunderstood them) to confirm Swede’s point of view. Ultimately the same number of people got infected albeit in a “flatter” rate. However one counter point by Johan Giesecke was that these flattening the curve is motivated by the assumption of a static capacity of ER rooms to deal with this pandemic and he pointed out they have increased capacity. Overall I found listening to his side very informative, at least it gave me a different view of looking at from their view. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22974284,"author":"shawnz","month":"2020-04","story":"A Third Solution","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis showing fast quarantining is best mitigation","quote":"3blue1brown on YouTube did an analysis which similarly shows that fast quarantining is the best way to mitigate the virus","text":"> screening everyone every day (which is very different from simply doing \"more testing\"). That seems like the logical conclusion of \"more testing\" to me. If we could, why wouldn't we? 3blue1brown on YouTube did an analysis which similarly shows that fast quarantining is the best way to mitigate the virus: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23031955,"author":"thu2111","month":"2020-04","story":"Flatten the Curve of Armchair Epidemiology","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's pretty apparent the writers don't understand epidemiology themselves. What is this field, really? It's not medicine. Its most famous practitioners often don't have any medical training or background, e.g. Prof Ferguson who did his PhD in theoretical physics. The assumptions it uses are of the level of medical complexity that a small child can understand - essentially that people who spend time close to each other infect each other. The models don't take as input the molecular biology or DNA of a virus that's being simulated. Arguably it's not science. A basic characteristic of science is that it tells us something new about the world, something that can be proven true. Experiments are fundamental to science for that reason. Epidemiology is about the development of models. Models aren't science because they cannot ever tell us something truly new or unexpected: a model is ultimately just a rendering of its author's assumptions. When someone puts some formulas into R and copies the results into a paper, the model is acting as a kind of fancy PowerPoint. It's useful to illustrate the conclusions that follow from the assumptions, but it no more lets us discover new things than PowerPoint does. (n.b. Under this definition, a lot of what gets published as computer science also isn't science but rather engineering. \"Data science\" on the other hand is a tautology. That's a fine conclusion and one I'll happily defend.) It often gets described as mathematics. It's not mathematics "},{"id":22727438,"author":"CHsurfer","month":"2020-03","story":"Austria to make basic face masks compulsory in supermarkets","stance":"reference","why":"Central gathering places drive disease spread","quote":"central markets, places where people in the region congregate, are major factors in the the rate of disease spread","text":"This seems like such a no-brainer. 3Blue 1Brown did a video on epidemic simulation, and central markets, places where people in the region congregate, are major factors in the the rate of disease spread. Limiting the transfer coefficient in these places has the key to reducing this impact, assuming you can't prevent people from going there. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zxRgfBXn6Mg",1],["nM9f0W2KD5s",1],["rhgwIhB58PA",1],["oIG9ztQw2Gc",1],["C4VHfmiwuv4",1],["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["-pKR212H5vQ",1],["swQbA4zub20",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["rWRbTnE1PEM",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["3qln2u1Vr2E",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1],["jcKRGpMiVTw",1]]},"9yjZpBq1XBE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible, engrossing lesson in queueing theory and systems design, even for people with no interest in Disney.","q":{"x":"If you ever wanted a feature-length explanation of queueing theory and how ignoring it makes everything worse, well... here you go","i":32088049,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":41906724,"author":"eterm","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: What's the best documentary you've watched this year?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gripping documentary, accessible without Disney knowledge.","quote":"is an incredible documentary that I watched this year.","text":"It was made a couple of years ago, but Defuncland's \"Disney's FastPass: A complicated history\" is an incredible documentary that I watched this year. You don't need to know too much about Disney for it to absorb you in. [Defunctland]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32088049,"author":"kmeisthax","month":"2022-07","story":"DoorDash and pizza arbitrage (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feature-length queueing theory lesson via Disney FastPass.","quote":"If you ever wanted a feature-length explanation of queueing theory and how ignoring it makes everything worse, well... here you go","text":"People who do not understand the role of balking and price information act at their own peril. This economic backpressure is something that executives hate - it keeps line from going up - but people who actually do the work love, because it keeps workloads manageable. If you ever wanted a feature-length explanation of queueing theory and how ignoring it makes everything worse, well... here you go: [VIDEO] . The context is Disney parks and Fastpass, but the lessons are applicable to basically any business. Everything is a queue."},{"id":29372272,"author":"alborzb","month":"2021-11","story":"Today’s Disneyland is a price gouge, not a magical experience","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive and statistical model of FastPass history.","quote":"I highly recommend this video from Defunct Land","text":"For anyone interested in the Fast App discussion from this article, I highly recommend this video from Defunct Land -- [VIDEO] The video not only does a deep dive into fast pass, but also does a statistical model break down to reveal that the modern implementation of fast pass is actually not what you might expect and serves to increase wait times for rides!"},{"id":39084640,"author":"duped","month":"2024-01","story":"Elevator Expert on How to Move 10k People Up a 118-Floor Skyscraper [video]","stance":"rec","why":"History and simulation of Disney FastPass systems.","quote":"yes this is an 1:45 long video on the history and a simulation of Disney parks, but its worth the payoff.","text":"> Concentrated harm and distributed benefit is a recipe to have a few vociferous complainers and many silent beneficiaries A fascinating example of this with queuing humans is the Disney Fastpass system (1). I think the real design goal to avoid \"complainers\" is to try and optimize the wait time for your resource to avoid a bimodal distribution and try and spread it out for the average rider, rather than make wait times worse for the majority of people but better for the people who game the system. (1) [VIDEO] , yes this is an 1:45 long video on the history and a simulation of Disney parks, but its worth the payoff."},{"id":32240635,"author":"rawgabbit","month":"2022-07","story":"Shamelessness as a strategy (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Long documentary on Disneyland queueing tweaks.","quote":"Here is a TWO hour long documentary on Youtube that talked all the machinations/tweaks Disneyland made to queueing.","text":"I don't get it either. My understanding is that with a queue, we are trading our time to get what we want. Is he saying instead of trading time, we should fork out cash to jump the queue? Here is a TWO hour long documentary on Youtube that talked all the machinations/tweaks Disneyland made to queueing. TLDR, you have to stay at a Disney Hotel to really jump the queue, buy some crazy app, and make reservations months in advance. It is really stupid. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32240642,"author":"jdmichal","month":"2022-07","story":"America’s favorite family outings are increasingly out of reach","stance":"rec","why":"Evolution and engineering of FastPass and Genie+.","quote":"there's a really good video someone did on the evolution and systems engineering behind the Genie+ / FastPass system","text":"Uh, so... That's basically Disney World right now. You must reserve your visit to the park, and there's a paid system (Genie+) to compete for ride times without waiting in line. And there's a really good video someone did on the evolution and systems engineering behind the Genie+ / FastPass system: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29373133,"author":"ekhaliul","month":"2021-11","story":"Today’s Disneyland is a price gouge, not a magical experience","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of Disney's attempts to control lines.","quote":"There is a nice analysis of everything that Disney tried as far as controlling lines","text":"After I took my kids day after thanksgiving for the first time last Friday, I was wondering why this problem simply exists. And how is it possible to pay such big amount of money for outdated experiences that we had. There is a nice analysis of everything that Disney tried as far as controlling lines: [VIDEO] As a person that came from USSR, I see that many of the issues are created by removing money as a control mechanism of supply and demand. If you pay entrance fee and then remove fine tuning for each ride, the result is long lines. My personal opinion is that pay-per-ride is a much better model although I can see how many may disagree. From my childhood in a country where money did not have much meaning, I learned that all-you-can eat mode for a price of entry will be filled with schemes, long lines, and tricks to game the system."},{"id":39042200,"author":"JoelEinbinder","month":"2024-01","story":"Queues don't fix overload (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of complicated real-world queue design.","quote":"I think of how complicated Disney's fast pass system got","text":"Real life queues can be scary too. I think of how complicated Disney's fast pass system got [VIDEO] . Luckily with software it is way easier to get more servers than it is to build more theme park rides."}],"r":[["oV9rvDllKEg",1],["Tn7egDQ9lPg",1],["9syvZr-9xwk",1],["GiYO1TObNz8",1],["zkbPdEHEyEI",1],["I6xwMIUPHss",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1]]},"P3AyI_u66Bw":{"wy":"The default reference in Python performance threads for why removing the GIL is hard.","q":{"x":"I think Larry Hasting's first Gilectomy talk was extremely insightful","i":15010476,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15010476,"author":"gshulegaard","month":"2017-08","story":"Let's Remove the Global Interpreter Lock","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insightful on the GIL and performance when removing it.","quote":"I think Larry Hasting's first Gilectomy talk was extremely insightful","text":"I feel like the GIL is, at this point, Python's most infamous attribute. For a long time I thought it was also the biggest flaw with Python...but over time I care less and less about it. I think the first thing to realize is that single-threaded performance is often significantly better with the GIL than without it. I think Larry Hasting's first Gilectomy talk was extremely insightful (about the GIL in general and about performance when removing the GIL): [VIDEO] I am not sure I would, personally, trade single-threaded performance for enabling multi-threaded applications. I view Python as a high-level rapid prototyping language that is well suited for business logic and glue code. And for that type of workload I would value single-threaded performance over support for multi-threading. Even now, a year later, the Gilectomy project is still slightly off performance-wise (although it looks really really close :) ): [LINK] As noted elsewhere, multi-processing offers adequate parallelization for this type of logic. Also, coroutines and async libraries such as gevent and asyncio offer easily approachable event loops for maximizing single-threaded resource utilization. It's true that multi-processing is not a replacement for multi-threading. There definitely are tasks and workloads where multi-processing and its inherent overhead make it unsuitable as a solution. But for those tasks, I question whether or not Python itself (as an interpreted, dynamically typed language) is suitable."},{"id":36644580,"author":"santiagobasulto","month":"2023-07","story":"Meta pledges Three-Year sponsorship for Python if GIL removal is accepted","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on why removing the GIL is hard.","quote":"There's a great talk by David Beazley","text":"There's a great talk by David Beazley (I don't remember which one) that he basically explains that removing the GIL isn't difficult at all. After all, it's just one lock. The issue is all the libraries and packages that were built around it. It's now been YEARS of building on top of the GIL. So anyways, PEP 703 is a a nice effort, but I doubt we (everyday mortals) can enjoy it. Meta, and big companies with a specialized team might be able to exploit it by making sure their entire stack is GIL-free. EDIT: My bad, the talk is from Larry Hastings: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18118903,"author":"zbentley","month":"2018-10","story":"Questions","stance":"rec","why":"Pointer on the Gilectomy for questions about GIL removal.","quote":"Or search “gilectomy” on LWN.","text":"[VIDEO] Or search “gilectomy” on LWN. Or check out stackless etc.; it turns out removing the GIL technically is historically one of the easier parts of removing the GIL entirely. It sounds like your main problem is with python’s GC model. Reference counting doesn’t have to be thread-unsafe, but in scripting/interpreted-ish languages that want their threading story to involve seamless (I.e. no special syntax unless you want it, it’s on you not to blow off your foot) sharing of state between threads at will, like Python and Ruby, a GIL or equivalent is the norm. Sometimes it’s not as intrusive as python’s, but it does seem like a necessary (or at least very likely) implementation pattern for languages that want to provide that seamlessness. You can have thread-safe reference counted GC in a traditional scripting language, but that tends to come with a much less automatic threading/concurrency API. Perl 5 is an example of that category, and it is implemented in C."},{"id":13826874,"author":"pfranz","month":"2017-03","story":"CPython internals: A ten-hour codewalk through the Python interpreter (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"30-minute talk on removing Python's GIL.","quote":"It's 30m, but worth it if you're interested.","text":"Larry Hastings - Removing Python's GIL: The Gilectomy - PyCon 2016 [VIDEO] (I'm pretty sure this is the video I'm thinking of) It's 30m, but worth it if you're interested. Not sure what progress has been made since then."},{"id":12027144,"author":"tedmiston","month":"2016-07","story":"Why is Python slow","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the Gilectomy project to remove Python's GIL.","quote":"See Larry Hastings' talk \"Removing Python's GIL: The Gilectomy\" from PyCon 2016.","text":"See Larry Hastings' talk \"Removing Python's GIL: The Gilectomy\" from PyCon 2016. [VIDEO] As well as his earlier talk \"Python's Infamous GIL\". [LINK] The GIL is a simple design that provides serious benefit to Python (end user) developers. There are plenty of options for working around this for parallelism, such as the built-in module multiprocessing."},{"id":21426563,"author":"the_why_of_y","month":"2019-11","story":"Python adopts a 12-month release cycle","stance":"reference","why":"Atomic reference counting reduces single-threaded performance.","quote":"there was a PyCon talk about that.","text":"In addition to the points other people raised, \"simply\" removing the GIL by changing the current reference-counting GC to do atomic reference updates will also substantially reduce single threaded performance; there was a PyCon talk about that. [VIDEO] See also Swift for another language whose implementation has performance issues caused by ubiquitous atomic reference counting."},{"id":13564635,"author":"gshulegaard","month":"2017-02","story":"The multiprocessing module in Python","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for C extensions blocking GIL removal.","quote":"this guaranteed thread safety has led to the creation of many non-thread safe Python C extensions","text":"Just to add more context for others... C extensions called from Python are still subject to the GIL of the calling interpreter[1]. In fact, this guaranteed thread safety has led to the creation of many non-thread safe Python C extensions which is now a critical blocker to efforts to removing the GIL[2], although there are also a number of other issues as well. As noted in the StackOverflow, you can release the GIL but there are some tricks to doing so safely[3]. But threads of a C extensions library are not necessarily pinned to a single core by the GIL like regular Python byte code. Although many performance oriented libraries like numpy[4] forsake multi-threading by default in many cases and rely on parallelization by the user (programmer)[5]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":13481648,"author":"xapata","month":"2017-01","story":"Problems I Have with Python","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on benefits of the GIL.","quote":"I've never been bothered by the GIL, but I have benefitted from it [1].","text":"Python has made some trade-offs that you dislike. You complain about the negative consequences without comparing those against the benefits. One of the major factors in speed is efficient memory layout. Contrast a Python list with a NumPy array. To achieve speedier loops and vectorized arithmetic [0], the array gives up dynamic typing and dynamic sizing. In most applications, I would gladly give up some compute speed to gain some programming productivity. I love duck-typing. Formal typing has some impressive examples, but in the projects I've worked on has reduced my productivity. Perhaps because data are so often serialized to simple formats or written to databases that discard the best tools of formal typing. Anecdotal evidence, for sure. I've never been bothered by the GIL, but I have benefitted from it [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12052184,"author":"gshulegaard","month":"2016-07","story":"Why is Python slow","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for Gilectomy performance issues.","quote":"There is some work being done by the Gilectomy project to try and meet this bar","text":"The BDFL (Benevolent Dictator For Life) Guido Von Rossum himself put forth the idea that he would consider a patch removing the GIL in 2007 [1]: > \"... I'd welcome a set of patches into Py3k only if the performance for a single-threaded program (and for a multi-threaded but I/O-bound program) does not decrease.\" Unfortunately, experiments thus far have not succeeded to meet these requirements. There is some work being done by the Gilectomy project to try and meet this bar as well as some other requirements currently though [2]. But it is currently grappling with the afore-discovered performance issues that come with removing the GIL. Also at PyCon 2016, Guido himself mentions the Gilectomy project and it's potential consideration (if it works) for Python 3.6+ [3]. So when you say Python leaders declared the GIL a \"non-issue\", I think you are oversimplifying the actual reality of what removing the GIL means and why leaders (like Guido) have been reluctant to invest resources pursuing. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":12052214,"author":"gshulegaard","month":"2016-07","story":"Why is Python slow","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the Gilectomy project source.","quote":"If the Gilectomy [2] project succeeds, Guido has mentioned he would consider it for Python3.6+","text":"To be fair, the GIL wasn't included because it was a simple threading model (AFAIK). It was included because it was simple to implement and it was/is fast(er) (than removing it)[1][2]. If the Gilectomy [2] project succeeds, Guido has mentioned he would consider it for Python3.6+ [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":12052252,"author":"gshulegaard","month":"2016-07","story":"Why is Python slow","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for Gilectomy project's performance issues.","quote":"There is some work being done by the Gilectomy project to try and meet this bar","text":"To be fair, the GIL was included (AFAIK) because it was simple to implement. Also, Guido has said (2007) [1] that he would welcome a patch to remove the GIL if...: > \"... I'd welcome a set of patches into Py3k only if the performance for a single-threaded program (and for a multi-threaded but I/O-bound program) does not decrease.\" Unfortunately, experiments thus far have not succeeded to meet these requirements. There is some work being done by the Gilectomy project to try and meet this bar as well as some other requirements currently though [2]. But it is currently grappling with the afore-discovered performance issues that come with removing the GIL. Also at PyCon 2016, Guido himself mentions the Gilectomy project and it's potential consideration (if it works) for Python 3.6+ [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"}],"r":[["Obt-vMVdM8s",1],["yQ16_YxLbB8",1]]},"-FENubmZGj8":{"wy":"HN commenters send it to anyone who thinks innocent people have nothing to fear from answering police questions.","q":{"x":"this is a very important video for everyone to watch","i":29413027,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29413027,"author":"dotBen","month":"2021-12","story":"Former Ubiquiti employee charged with stealing data and extorting company","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Essential viewing on US policing, especially for newcomers.","quote":"this is a very important video for everyone to watch","text":"Yup, this is a very important video for everyone to watch, but especially anyone moving to America who doesn't understand the differences to policing in this country vs others. If you have watched this video, you might want to review this highly anticipated follow up several years later: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22633368,"author":"keanzu","month":"2020-03","story":"Body Camera Once Again Catches an NYPD Officer Planting Drugs in Someone's Car","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Law professor explains why talking to police is dangerous even when innocent.","quote":"watch \"You Have the Right to Remain Innocent\" by James Duane.","text":"If anyone reads the parent here and doesn't agree then watch \"You Have the Right to Remain Innocent\" by James Duane. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34453416,"author":"the_af","month":"2023-01","story":"What not to write on your security clearance form (1988)","stance":"rec","why":"Follow-up showing how volunteering information to police can incriminate innocent people.","quote":"this follow up video by Duane specifically addresses your scenario","text":"To my other comment, let me add that this follow up video by Duane specifically addresses your scenario: the concerned parents of a missing girl unwisely spoke to the cops, without knowing they were suspects in the case (because the cops regularly suspect this, \"9 out of 10 times, the caller is the one who committed the crime\" as one cop put it). In this case, the cops suspected the father. After they found the body of the daughter, which had drowned, they didn't tell the father (because he was a suspect but they didn't want to read him his rights) and claimed to be still looking for her in order to continue incriminating her father. His mistake: volunteering to talk to the cops without a lawyer. Watch it here, I've picked the right timestamp for you: [VIDEO] By volunteering to talk to the cops, you may also end up providing information that -- according to the cops -- \"only the killer could have known\". Oops! Even worse, if the interview wasn't taped (and in some cases, even if it was!) the cops can claim, either mistakenly or on purpose, that you provided self-incriminating evidence even if you didn't. Duane provides examples of how, even if your answers are taped but the cops' questions aren't , seemingly innocent answers can get you in trouble. Example: \"I swear I've never touched a gun in my life\" (cops: \"we didn't mention the murder weapon, how did he know it was a gun? We didn't ask this. Suspicious!\")."},{"id":28026057,"author":"robomartin","month":"2021-08","story":"FBI Seized $900K from Safe Deposit Box on 'Pure Conjecture,' Federal Judge Says","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on how police interactions can be used against you.","quote":"that is a presentation worth watching [0]","text":"Yes, that is a presentation worth watching [0] and a follow-up [1]. Even as someone who is pro law enforcement, I have to keep in mind that their job does not always align with protecting you. In other words, if they pull you over for speeding, the relationship isn't the same as the case where you might be asking a cop for directions or if you are engaging with them because you were a victim of a crime. In the first case they will use their training and knowledge against you to the extent necessary given the circumstances, the the other two cases they will be helpful and supportive. I do not vilify police officers --the individuals-- for this. It is their job. This is what they do. They would not be doing the very difficult work they do every day if they did not, for example, gather evidence and use what suspects say as evidence, etc. My problem is with asymmetry of the law as it pertains to lying. When a politician gets on TV and lies, and lies and lies --for years!--, suffering no consequences whatsoever, well, I just don't think that's right. Thinking of this as an evolutionary process, if the fitness function responds positively to lies, the net result will be evolving towards a condition where lies are a normal part of how one gets ahead or survives. And this is precisely what we have. Some might say: Well, politicians would not be able to say much on so many subjects if the absolute veracity of their statement was a requirement. That's fine. I'd rather they shut the fuc"},{"id":48560692,"author":"iwhalen","month":"2026-06","story":"Never talk to the police","stance":"reference","why":"Duane qualifies his advice for traffic stops.","quote":"James Duane (the law professor from the video in the blog) himself makes a qualifier about traffic stops","text":"A lot of discussion in this thread about traffic stops. James Duane (the law professor from the video in the blog) himself makes a qualifier about traffic stops: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42709532,"author":"sigmoid10","month":"2025-01","story":"Nevada court shuts down police use of federal loophole for civil forfeiture","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"He mentions it in this talk for example: [VIDEO] It also seems like he succeeded, because the original and all reuploads except for some ultra low-quality copies are gone from youtube."},{"id":28372903,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2021-08","story":"Surveilance bill rushed through Australian parliament in 24 hours","stance":"reference","why":"Condensed version of the book's message to demand a lawyer.","quote":"Here's a youtube of more or less the book","text":"I'm reading this book \"You have the right to remain innocent\" [1] And it brings up amazing cases where the system is definitely not limiting it's powers and it's leading to false convictions. Here's a youtube of more or less the book: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] EDIT: errm actually that YT video is actually a little scant on details. The TLDR is say nothing except make an absolute demand for your 6th amendment rights (a lawyer). And don't be soft about it like \"maybe I can get a lawyer?\" ... say \"I have a right to a lawyer and will not answer anything without first speaking to my lawyer\" People might be thinking \"I thought it was the 5th ?\" Ironically , the same conservatives that have been trumpeting the 2nd have concurrently been undermining the 5th and now can be used against you as evidence of guilt."},{"id":18113883,"author":"cheeko1234","month":"2018-10","story":"New Zealand travellers refusing digital search now face $5k Customs fine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I might be wrong on this but according to this updated video[0] by James Duane, things have changed considerably due to Obama and a recent Supreme Court decision and even using the 5th improperly can be used against you. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33287333,"author":"belter","month":"2022-10","story":"Team Interpol: Metaverse Police","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"No worries, Facebook compliance team will ban them soon. Expect an appeal on HN for help one day...but remember, you should not talk to the Police... [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["mf5otGNbkuc",1],["RuOvn4UqznU",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["nv7-YM4wno8",1],["iG9CE55wbtY",1],["eDJrQBwJpqk",1],["uTdyb-RWNKo",1]]},"6RhtiPefVzM":{"wy":"HN commenters link it repeatedly as a clear, number-based rebuttal to the claim that EVs are worse for the climate than gas cars.","q":{"x":"This video crunches the numbers pretty thoroughly","i":24853094,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":32461755,"author":"theluketaylor","month":"2022-08","story":"Electric vehicles are way more energy-efficient than internal combustion","stance":"rec","why":"Good breakdown of build energy versus lifetime emissions","quote":"good breakdown","text":"Yes, it takes more energy to build a BEV than an ICE car. It's not massively different and the lifetime emissions of a BEV are still lower than an ICE car even on a pretty dirty grid. good breakdown: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27147035,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2021-05","story":"$365B wiped off cryptocurrency market after Tesla stops accepting Bitcoin","stance":"rec","why":"Math on lifetime carbon footprint of EV versus ICE","quote":"Engineering Explained did a good video doing the math behind the carbon footprints of ICE versus EV over the life of the car","text":"Nobody thinks that EVs have a zero carbon footprint. But over the lifetime of the car, they have a lower footprint than ICE. Yes, they have a higher footprint than ICE to manufacture, but even if you get your power from coal, over the long term their carbon footprint is lower. And if your power comes from a renewable source, then the break-even point is less than two years. Engineering Explained did a good video doing the math behind the carbon footprints of ICE versus EV over the life of the car: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20776111,"author":"bennyelv","month":"2019-08","story":"Singapore Says Musk's Electric Cars Are About 'Lifestyle,' Not Climate","stance":"rec","why":"Easily digestible calculation showing EVs repay manufacturing deficit early","quote":"An easily digestable version of this calculation is here","text":"Citation needed. The literature I've seen suggests that an electric vehicle easily makes back any deficit quite early in its life. An easily digestable version of this calculation is here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20097539,"author":"PopeDotNinja","month":"2019-06","story":"If Seeing the World Helps Ruin It, Should We Stay Home?","stance":"rec","why":"Liked video on carbon costs with sources provided","quote":"Here's a video I liked on the carbon costs associated w/ gas & electric cars.","text":"Here's a video I liked on the carbon costs associated w/ gas & electric cars. Check out video's sources for further reading. [VIDEO] -- \"Are Electric Cars Worse For The Environment? Myth Busted\" -- Engineering Explained"},{"id":18477887,"author":"rfeather","month":"2018-11","story":"Oil Demand for Cars Is Already Falling","stance":"rec","why":"Explains EV versus gas emissions depending on location","quote":"does a decent job explaining though I feel he leaves a few parts out for the sake of clarity","text":"The article doesn't talk about greenhouse gas emissions, so the power grid and fuel source isn't really relevant to the point, especially since oil is an extremely small part of the energy mixture in most places.[1] If they were talking about greenhouse gases, then the answer seems to be that it depends on where you live and how/what you drive, but an electric vehicle almost always produces less emissions than an equivalent gas powered one. [2] does a decent job explaining though I feel he leaves a few parts out for the sake of clarity. For example, driving a P100D isn't the equivalent of driving the average car. It's more like driving a sports sedan (like a BMW M5), so those sorts of vehicles would probably be a more fair comparison. In the same vane, the whole US car fleet includes lots of SUVs and other trucks, which if there were equivalent electric models, would obviously not go as far as a Leaf. It would be great if there was a US calculator that worked like [3] does for the UK so that we could do the same sort of comparison based on the mix where we live. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":37644521,"author":"jwestbury","month":"2023-09","story":"Earth’s average 2023 temperature is now likely to reach 1.5 °C of warming","stance":"reference","why":"Rebuts claim by showing how quickly EVs overtake ICE emissions","quote":"demonstrating how quickly EVs overtake ICE vehicles in total emissions, including during manufacture","text":"Stop parroting this nonsense. Here's an Engineering Explained video from five years ago demonstrating how quickly EVs overtake ICE vehicles in total emissions, including during manufacture: [VIDEO] Here's a peer-reviewed study which explicitly looks at lifetime emissions including gathering of raw materials, and finds that EVs have far lower lifetime emissions: [LINK] Here are the results of a Reuters analysis which also looks at total lifecycle emissions, including resource extraction: [LINK]"},{"id":36723402,"author":"jwestbury","month":"2023-07","story":"Electric Vehicles for Everyone? The Impossible Dream","stance":"reference","why":"Detailed analysis of EV payback versus keeping an old ICE car","quote":"it would take less than five years (on average) for the electric car to have less emissions than the old, ICE vehicle","text":"Engineering Explained went into this in some detail in a video about five years ago[0]. The key takeaway: If two people were driving the same car, and one sold their car and bought an electric car whilst the other kept driving the old car, it would take less than five years (on average) for the electric car to have less emissions than the old, ICE vehicle. There are certainly some other moral questions here, involving treatment of labour in places where, e.g., we mine for lithium, but those are a bit harder to quantify. The quantifiable aspects are pretty clear, and electric cars are the clear winner. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30727508,"author":"kemiller","month":"2022-03","story":"US schools can subscribe to an electric bus fleet at lower prices than diesel","stance":"reference","why":"Shows BEVs beat best gas cars even on coal power","quote":"Engineering Explained did the math","text":"Engineering Explained did the math: [VIDEO] Spoiler: Well-to-wheels, BEV cars are better than the best gas cars (top end prius) even if the source electricity is 100% coal. The best is still not driving, but it's a step in the right direction."},{"id":28944982,"author":"masswerk","month":"2021-10","story":"Tesla's German plant hits snag as public consultation repeated","stance":"reference","why":"Used as source for battery production emissions offset time","quote":"","text":"Regarding the credibility of said statement, also mind that – depending on estimates and your local mix of energy production – the rather high CO2 footprint of long-range battery production is only offset after 3.5 – 15 years of average use [0]. Where average use is US millage, with millages being typically lower in Europe. So Tesla Europe will not be saving the world. What it really does, is relocating the source of emissions. [0] Compare [VIDEO] and studies referred to in the description."},{"id":26444190,"author":"kevan","month":"2021-03","story":"Vintage technology and why some people still use it","stance":"reference","why":"Aggregates source studies on manufacturing and operating emissions","quote":"this[1] video aggregated some source studies.","text":"For anyone looking to research that, this[1] video aggregated some source studies. tl;dw: estimates are between 2-17 metric tons of CO2 emissions to produce a new car (SUVs are more). And during operation an average car emits 5.2 metric tons of CO2 per yer. For a concrete example, a 2021 Toyota Camry Hybrid at 52mpg emits 170g/mile of CO2[2]. A 2000 Camry 4cl at 24mpg emits 340g/mile[3]. Assuming you drive an average 13,500 miles/year[4] the break-even point for CO2 is about 4.5 years. If you have the 2000 V6 Camry at 20mpg then the break-even is closer to 2.5 years. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":26091906,"author":"npsimons","month":"2021-02","story":"Elon Musk wants clean power. But Tesla's carrying Bitcoin's dirty baggage","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that breakeven varies by location but EVs win","quote":"","text":"Going to need to see numbers to back these claims; what I've seen says otherwise, granted the breakeven point is different depending on where you live: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24853094,"author":"Diederich","month":"2020-10","story":"Tesla Earning Result 3Q 2020 [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Thorough number crunching refuting claim EVs are worse","quote":"This video crunches the numbers pretty thoroughly","text":"> And electric cars are actually responsible for even more emissions - not from burning fuel, obviously, but just manufacturing it releases more carbon dioxide than an average gas-powered car will in its 25 years lifecycle. I don't believe this is true. This video crunches the numbers pretty thoroughly: [VIDEO] \"The main takeaway from this video: Electric cars certainly aren't any worse for the environment than gasoline cars regardless of where your energy comes from, and in the vast majority of cases, they are vastly superior to gasoline cars.\""},{"id":21996022,"author":"ed_balls","month":"2020-01","story":"US emissions fell 2.1% in 2019","stance":"reference","why":"Average electric car breaks even on emissions after 4.8 years","quote":"average electric car is in the green after 4.8 years","text":"[VIDEO] There are other videos on the subject in this channel (battery recycling, EV vs ICE efficiency). According to the video, average electric car is in the green after 4.8 years. If you already own a Prius that could be true, but a Dodge Ram is unlikely."},{"id":18600958,"author":"rohit2412","month":"2018-12","story":"China ‘Is the Only One in the Race’ to Make Electric Buses, Taxis and Trucks","stance":"reference","why":"State by state emissions breakdown disputing commenter's conclusion","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure why this guy doing a state by state breakdown comes to a different conclusion than you have. [VIDEO] I can find you more sources that say the same thing he does."},{"id":18389687,"author":"ed_balls","month":"2018-11","story":"What can I do about climate change?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for emissions depending on electricity source","quote":"","text":"I depends on you location and driving in the city e.g. Prius can have less total CO2 emission than Tesla S if the electricity comes from fossil fuels. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18474133,"author":"guntars","month":"2018-11","story":"Oil Demand for Cars Is Already Falling","stance":"reference","why":"Source for manufacturing footprint of gas vehicles","quote":"","text":"Producing a gas vehicle is equivalent to two years of average driving as far as carbon footprint is concerned, according to this one guy on YouTube ( [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["66YRCjkxIcg",1],["7gtog_gOaGQ",1],["giQ8xEWjnBs",1],["thnXzUFJnfQ",1],["uWUHkCgslNE",1],["wE5G1kTndI4",1]]},"FjHJ7FmV0M4":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining, accessible way to get a feel for light and electric fields.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend you watch this video of Feynman explaining light","i":43134269,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43134269,"author":"kypro","month":"2025-02","story":"Richard Feynman's blackboard at the time of his death (1988)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman explaining light in a fun, entertaining way","quote":"I highly recommend you watch this video of Feynman explaining light","text":"I'm sure a lot of people here have already seen this, but for those who haven't I highly recommend you watch this video of Feynman explaining light, [VIDEO] He had an amazing ability to make physics fun and entertaining. I could listen to him talk all day."},{"id":24231979,"author":"RichardCA","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: What is a science fact that blew your mind when you learned it?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman explains the strange nature of light and electromagnetic radiation","quote":"This is one of my favorite PBS clips from the 80's in which Richard Feynman explains how strange the nature of light","text":"Let's try to work the problem in a way that avoids pedantry, just for a moment. This is one of my favorite PBS clips from the 80's in which Richard Feynman explains how strange the nature of light and by extension all of electromagnetic radiation. [VIDEO] If you listen carefully, you notice that he never talks about wave/particle duality (because he wasn't giving a classroom lecture). What he does talk about is an electric field that waves slosh around in, and that something \"elaborate and complicated\" is going on at a deeper level. That's what I'm on about, if you were pressed to explain it so that a bright child would get it, how would you do that?"},{"id":30865393,"author":"martyvis","month":"2022-03","story":"How is portable AM radio possible?","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman on all radio waves being present at every point","quote":"Richard Feynman gets very excited about this very fact","text":"Richard Feynman gets very excited about this very fact, that at any point it has ALL of the radio waves, amongst other electromagnetic radiation, that just has to be tuned in or focussed on. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20342099,"author":"Jach","month":"2019-07","story":"The Backbone of VHF Amateur Radio May Be Under Threat","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman gives laymen a taste of the electric field","quote":"A favorite Feynman vid to give laymen a taste","text":"The potential is obvious once you really start to grasp the reality of the electric field and its properties. (A favorite Feynman vid to give laymen a taste: [VIDEO] ) I don't see how any worthwhile possibilities can be realized at the amateur level however given just two of the restrictions I pointed out in a sibling thread..."},{"id":17994808,"author":"Quekid5","month":"2018-09","story":"The regress argument against Cartesian skepticism (2012) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on how we experience light versus what it is","quote":"The vast majority of light is effectively invisible to you.","text":"I don't think that's what the poster meant. I think it was probably more along the lines of how you experience e.g. 'light' vs. what it actually is : [VIDEO] (The vast majority of light is effectively invisible to you.)"},{"id":26323593,"author":"p1mrx","month":"2021-03","story":"Physicists have filmed the oscillation of a time crystal","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh, but you can see past events while wielding (or not wielding) a time crystal. Vision is far out: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["P76Vbsk_3J0",1],["dCuZkaaou0Q",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["495nCzxM9PI",1],["KZokQov_aH0",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["Q1lL-hXO27Q",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1]]},"MFRWDuduuSw":{"wy":"HN recommends Herb Gross as an unusually clear teacher whose explanations make calculus click for people who struggled elsewhere.","q":{"x":"The way he explains things just clicked, after numerous failed attempts to learn from other sources.","i":33594152,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33594152,"author":"mtreis86","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Do you recall any book or course that made a topic finally click?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Herb Gross's explanations made calculus finally click","quote":"The way he explains things just clicked, after numerous failed attempts to learn from other sources.","text":"I recently learned calculus from the Herb Gross series from 1974. The way he explains things just clicked, after numerous failed attempts to learn from other sources. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18253935,"author":"avinassh","month":"2018-10","story":"Calculus Made Easy (1910)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Old black-and-white lectures covering basics and derivations by hand","quote":"Watch the first lecture by yourself [1] and you will immediately realise how good are these.","text":"This is amazing! The fonts are easy on eyes and page renders beautifully. If you are looking for vidoes, then check lectures by Herber Gross [0] on Youtube. These were recorded in 70s. They are in black & white, gives a feeling of watching some old beautifully shot movie. He goes into basics and gives you a taste of all derivations, by hand. Watch the first lecture by yourself [1] and you will immediately realise how good are these. On a similar note, any similar resources like the one submitted, but for Linear Algebra? I am aware of Gilbert Strang's book [2] and vidoes [3], but I find them advanced for a beginner. [0] - [LINK] [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [LINK]"},{"id":44912398,"author":"jackdoe","month":"2025-08","story":"Death and What Comes Next (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Herb Gross is particularly good for struggling with calculus","quote":"If you struggle with calculus, I found Herb Gross to be partucularly good","text":"Ignoring the moral question in the story, many people are satisfied by saying \"haha funny, the cat is both dead and alive\" and they just move on. Reading Pratchett for almost 30 years, it has fueled quite an interested in quantum mechanics in me, he subtly introduces QM teasers in almost every book I read. Particularly Mort. As I dropped out of highschool, I didnt have the luck to properly study physics, but in my 30s, I my existential crisis hit, as they say, a man dies twice, and the stupid half dead cat wouldn't leave me alone. And one night the omnipotent youtube algorithm recommended Susskind's lectures to me, then Feynman, Shankar, .. it just kept going. Now I watch and re-watch them but take screenshots and send them to claude when I am confused (which is still most of the time). If you are a layman like me and just want to know what is up with the cat, I encourage you to watch Sean Carroll's lectures (dont miss the Q&A videos) and it will for sure allow you to ask at least better questions. I love that cat so much. --- Sean Carroll: [LINK] Richard Feynman: [LINK] and [LINK] Leonard Susskind: [LINK] Ramamurti Shankar: [LINK] Barton Zwiebach: [LINK] Carl Bender: [LINK] If you struggle with calculus, I found Herb Gross to be partucularly good Herb Gross: [VIDEO] Of course watch 3blue1brown, alphaphoenix, styropyro etc as well"},{"id":14164611,"author":"lunchladydoris","month":"2017-04","story":"Calculus Made Easy (1914) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Herb Gross's calculus course, taught by someone who loves the subject","quote":"Clearly this is a man who loves his subject and loves introducing it to others.","text":"I just had a look at the first video in the first course on YouTube [0] and was delighted to see Herb Gross responding in the comments. Clearly this is a man who loves his subject and loves introducing it to others. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19880052,"author":"rofo1","month":"2019-05","story":"The Beauty of Calculus [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There are many, many ways to study calculus, so take this as a preference. For example, this wouldn't be my first choice.","text":"[VIDEO] There are many, many ways to study calculus, so take this as a preference. For example, this wouldn't be my first choice. In general, I find the \"Definition. Axiom. Theorem.\" approach very dry and just doesn't fit reality. Nobody ever discovered mathematics like this. One of the best books I've read is Gausses \"Disquisitiones Arithmeticae\" rather than any modern Theory of Numbers book. I've had more insight in sums manipulations (and various little tricks, some of them not even justified in modern mathematics) from Euler's letters than any other book on this subject. I don't know what happened in the meantime. It sure doesn't look like 18/19 century books were so dry like the ones today."}],"r":[["UEAMfLPZZhE",1],["cQP8WApzIQQ",1],["oeYBdghaIjc",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["-kFOXP026eE",1],["HI09kat_GeI",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1],["pyX8kQ-JzHI",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["vT1JzLTH4G4",1],["2Op3QLzMgSY",1],["KuG_CeEZV6w",1]]},"B965f8AcNbw":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an engaging, high-quality introduction to the Bronze Age Collapse and praise the channel generally.","q":{"x":"is also a great introduction","i":26608708,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":48861842,"author":"antondd","month":"2026-07","story":"Late Bronze Age Collapse","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Episode on the Bronze Age collapse from a top-quality channel.","quote":"The whole channel is top quality if you want to ruminate on other civilisations that came and went.","text":"Here is a youtube video he did on it: [VIDEO] The whole channel is top quality if you want to ruminate on other civilisations that came and went."},{"id":39900534,"author":"badbart14","month":"2024-04","story":"Mycenaean Warriors and the Sea-Peoples (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Engaging episode on Sea Peoples as symptom and cause of collapse.","quote":"the whole episode is extremely engaging and educational.","text":"Highly recommend the Fall of Civilizations podcast episode on the Bronze Age Collapse to learn more about the Sea-Peoples ( [VIDEO] ) . They discuss how the Sea-Peoples may be a symptom and a cause of the bronze age collapse, and the whole episode is extremely engaging and educational. (As all the episodes of this podcast are, cannot recommend it enough)"},{"id":26608708,"author":"paulintrognon","month":"2021-03","story":"Bronze Age mining sites received deliveries of pre-processed foods","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great introduction to the Bronze Age Collapse.","quote":"is also a great introduction","text":"The Fall Of Civilization's Podcast on this subject is also a great introduction [VIDEO] (Generally speaking I really recommand this podcast, it has been recommanded on HN before, which is how I first heard of it)"},{"id":35704825,"author":"nobody9999","month":"2023-04","story":"Shell admits 1.5C climate goal means immediate end to fossil fuel growth","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the Bronze Age Collapse reference point.","quote":"","text":">Cities have fallen to disasters throughout history (easy example is Pompeii). Cultures vanish. The only reason anyone's still around to talk about it is because natural disasters, wars, and human errors have largely been localized. Climate change won't be. The \"Bronze Age Collapse\"[0] wasn't global, but it certainly wasn't \"localized\" in the sense that (I think) you're using it. Yes, I'm picking a nit there. That said, you're otherwise spot on. And that's not breaking news either, and it's been discussed ad infinitum, Fall of Civilizations [1] brings your point into a pretty clear focus. I highly recommend it! [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["ciStnd9Y2ak",1]]},"FihU5JxmnBg":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a talk that changed how people debug, worth rewatching periodically.","q":{"x":"changed how I debug problems. I try to watch it once every year or two.","i":32737058,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32737058,"author":"seanc","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: How do you get better at debugging/finding a solution?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Debugging by forming and falsifying hypotheses while writing everything down","quote":"changed how I debug problems. I try to watch it once every year or two.","text":"This talk, \"Debugging with the Scientific Method\" changed how I debug problems. I try to watch it once every year or two. tl;dr, when debugging people naturally form a hypothesis about what is going wrong and then set about gathering evidence to support or refute that hypothesis. When you do so unconciously you are very prone to biases of all sorts, most importantly confirmation bias. When you do so mindfully, and Write It All Down then you are much more likely to a) come up with an evidence gathering exercise which will usefully falsify your hypothesis, and b) respect the gathered evidence, reject the now false hypothesis, and move on to a new one. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35855487,"author":"simon_acca","month":"2023-05","story":"Rich Hickey: Design in Practice","stance":"rec","why":"Halloway talks on applying the scientific method in programming","quote":"Stu Halloway (also a member of the Clojure core team) has a couple of great talks on using the scientific method in programming","text":"Stu Halloway (also a member of the Clojure core team) has a couple of great talks on using the scientific method in programming: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":17932647,"author":"grierson","month":"2018-09","story":"REPL + Debugger = REPLugger: a pleasant and scalable live code editor","stance":"rec","why":"Halloway talk on using the REPL to debug","quote":"Stuart Halloway from the Clojure community has some great a talks using the REPL to debug.","text":"Stuart Halloway from the Clojure community has some great a talks using the REPL to debug. Links: Debugging with the Scientific Method - Stuart Halloway ( [VIDEO] ) REPL DEBUGGING: NO STACKTRACE REQUIRED ( [LINK] ) scope-capture ( [LINK] )"},{"id":10666399,"author":"chrislloyd","month":"2015-12","story":"Metabase: Why we picked Clojure","stance":"rec","why":"Backstory on why Clojure errors are bad, useful in any language","quote":"This talk is a good backstory as to why the errors are so bad, and useful no matter what language you use","text":"I think your criticisms are definitely valid. The learning curve is steep (intentionally so) but definitely worth it. Your programs will become more reliable and you'll get more done as a consequence. Please push through! The error messages are terrible, and there are a lot of ongoing conversations about them at the moment in the Clojure community[1]. The Clojure[script] toolchain does tend to be all-in but there's work happening now to break that up — stay tuned. StackOverflow and the Clojure Slack channel also full of people who know how to fix issues :) Stick with it, when you'll have an \"ah-ha!\" moment soon enough! [1]: This talk is a good backstory as to why the errors are so bad, and useful no matter what language you use [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IOiZatlZtGU",1],["VESMU7JfVHU",1]]},"7CgXvIuZR40":{"wy":"HN commenters rate it a great GDQ watch and point to its feats, such as streaming video and Skype through controller ports, as inspiring hacks.","q":{"x":"TASBot which always makes a great GDQ watch","i":31623780,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31623780,"author":"zer01","month":"2022-06","story":"Super Mario Bros. 3: “Game End Glitch” in 00:00.216","stance":"strong_rec","why":"TASBot at GDQ, a great watch that inspired the commenter","quote":"TASBot which always makes a great GDQ watch","text":"I absolutely love speed runs and think they’re a great expression of the hacker mentality. To take a subtle bug in something like a physics engine and bend it to your will to eek out another 5s save off the world record is absolutely fascinating. Not to mention things like TASBot which always makes a great GDQ watch [VIDEO] and got me interested in speed running in the first place!"},{"id":21547696,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-11","story":"NaN Gates and Flip FLOPS","stance":"strong_rec","why":"TASbot streaming Skype to consoles through controller ports","quote":"I recommend this[2] video of TASbot streaming Skype realtime","text":"LOL... from \"Reverse emulating the NES to give it SUPER POWERS!\"[1] (which is insa^Wbizza^Wa very creative hack): \"The cartridge is the dual of the Nintendo.\" [1] [LINK] edit: for a very impressive variation on the SMW \"joke\" in [1], I recommend this[2] video of TASbot streaming Skype realtime to [2xNES, SNES+SuperGameBoy] through the controller ports . [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43065923,"author":"gmueckl","month":"2025-02","story":"Diablo hackers uncovered a speedrun scandal","stance":"rec","why":"Video player injected into Zelda via controller inputs","quote":"injecting a video player into Zelda on SNES and streaming the video through the controller port","text":"He was the public figure for TASBot and TASVideos for a while (see [LINK] ). TASBot is really a beautiful idea to help visualize what tool assisted speedruns are doing via the controller button lights in TASBot's hands. I think DwangoAC and TASBot represented the TAS community at multiple events, not just GDQ. Tool assisted speedruns are remarkable to me because creating the authoring tools is hacking in the classic sense. And then there's the sheer insanity of exploiting broken game logic to gain arbitrary code execution solely via controller inputs. The most elaborate demonstration of this that I know of was given at AGDQ 2017, eventually injecting a video player into Zelda on SNES and streaming the video through the controller port: [VIDEO] Ars Technica went into a little more detail about that feat: [LINK]"},{"id":30895387,"author":"Dylan16807","month":"2022-04","story":"Portal for Apple II (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Assuming you're fine with using a $1 chip to make a TCP<->controller adapter, then you can make it work on SNES. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29094871,"author":"memco","month":"2021-11","story":"Zig monthly, October 2021: Games, gamedev, Elixir, tools and more","stance":"reference","why":"Consoles linked via exploits into a VOIP setup","quote":"a series of TASes was presented in 2017 wherein multiple different hardware systems were linked together","text":"I'm quite amazed how resilient games are to this. Like how SMB1 for the NES is capable of things like the minus worlds where you're more or less playing levels that don't exist because the game doesn't expect you to be reading memory past a certain point, but as long as the values are valid, it can still use it to some degree. There's more extreme situations like Super Mario Land 2 for the Game boy where it's possible for the game to present it's own memory on the screen and if you understand how it works you can do things like ask the game to execute the ending sequence [0]. At it's most extreme a series of TASes was presented in 2017 wherein multiple different hardware systems were linked together via RCEs in different games to make a VOIP streaming setup from a bunch of consoles that don't actually have internet connections [1]. I'm sure there's all kinds of reasons this kind of UB is undesirable, and could be hazardous in some environments, but people have also done some really cool stuff with it! [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"NUQu-OPrzUc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a rigorous clinical look at ADHD, including the view that it has no benefits and that external prompts help.","q":{"x":"Educate yourself by watching this","i":31741808,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31741808,"author":"terracatta","month":"2022-06","story":"Association between ADHD medication and depression: A 10-year follow-up","stance":"rec","why":"Explains ADHD as an executive function disorder","quote":"Educate yourself by watching this","text":"> Every child I know diagnosed with ADHD had parents who didn't want to deal with them You must not know many parents then. Parents I know that have children with ADHD recognize their children are struggling beyond simple hyperactivity. These are children that are markedly behind their peers in childhood milestones regardless of their family upbringing, education, and socio-economic status. These are children that have a deficiency in the executive function of their brains where hyperactivity is one of many symptoms, and is not even necessarily the most worrying. These are children that struggle with simple tasks that other children do not. Parents of these children are no less loving, caring, or capable than parents without ADHD children. Parents should not be shamed for using effective medications (like MDH) so their children can have positive outcomes in their development and adult-life. > What we really need is a strong emphasis on family development, courses built around it and support groups ADHD is generally a disorder that you are born with. No amount of family development can prevent the disease. Educate yourself by watching this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13665348,"author":"indolering","month":"2017-02","story":"Large imaging study confirms brain differences in ADHD","stance":"rec","why":"Cognitive psychology of ADHD beyond brain scans","quote":"Brain scans are fun, but cognitive psychology is where the real work gets done","text":"> The way I describe it to most people is very simple. If you give a ADD/ADHD child Ritalin, Adderol, etc, they will calm down. You give those drugs to a normal child and they get super hyperactive. No they don't, ~25% won't feel anything. Most will become more focused. Methylphenidate and Methamphetamine /sound/ the same, but the former is better at \"stimulating\" certain brain structures : ) > Inversely, you give most people a stimulant such as caffeine and their heart beat increases and they perk up. I've always been very careful about coffee in the morning or teas as it is very much a calming downer. Sometimes I will drink a coffee to really calm my (always thinking too much) brain and let me focus, but can't do it if I'm the least bit tired as I'll be more inclined to doze off mid day. Yeah, the \"ADHD people react oppositely to stimulants\" stuff is folklore. Go drink a quad shot latte and tell me caffeine actually makes you tired. > I'm happy to see more actual science that shows this is a real thing, not something made up by an impatient child or child who had lots of trouble paying attention in school. Brain scans are fun, but cognitive psychology is where the real work gets done: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6469362,"author":"shadeless","month":"2013-09","story":"Procrastination should be solved by lighting fires, not filling buckets","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley on frequent prompts for accountability with ADHD","quote":"frequent prompts are one effective way to keep someone with ADHD accountable and on track","text":"It sounds to me like you may have ADHD. Dr. Russell Barkley mentions in one of his speeches[1] that frequent prompts are one effective way to keep someone with ADHD accountable and on track. You may want to watch this shorter version[2] and see if you recognize yourself (that's doesn't proof anything of course, but it's a start). [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":5479611,"author":"mortdeus","month":"2013-04","story":"How I Used ADD to Build Great Businesses","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley lecture arguing ADHD has no benefits","quote":"there is nothing beneficial about ADHD","text":"As somebody who has ADHD (Please start using the correct acronym.) I feel it is my obligation to make it absolutely clear to everyone that there is nothing beneficial about ADHD. If we are successful with ADHD, then we would only be more successful if we didnt have ADHD. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RIctzAQOe44",1]]},"OMPfEXIlTVE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a funny, smart and well-constructed talk on abstraction, duplication and object-oriented design.","q":{"x":"as a talk it is fantastic - funny, smart, well put together.","i":42191154,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42191154,"author":"crabmusket","month":"2024-11","story":"99 Bottles of OOP now available in Python","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short intro to Sandi Metz's style; funny, smart, well constructed","quote":"as a talk it is fantastic - funny, smart, well put together.","text":"I'd like to mention that despite OOP being in the title, I thought this book had a lot to teach that isn't specific to OOP architecture or OOP languages. Really, I think the star of the show is TDD and refactoring. For a short intro to Sandi's style and approach, I always recommend this 35min talk: [VIDEO] Aside from any specifically educational content, as a talk it is fantastic - funny, smart, well put together."},{"id":11099584,"author":"buren","month":"2016-02","story":"Write code that is easy to delete, not easy to extend","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sandi Metz on wrong abstractions versus duplication","quote":"Sandi Metz has an excellent talk touching on this topic.","text":"Couldn't agree more. Sandi Metz has an excellent talk touching on this topic. Developers exaggerated willingness to keep things DRY and elaborates a bit on why: it's one of the easiest things for a not-so-experienced developer to identify and one of the easiest things to teach. Edit: One of the best quotes from that talk is (paraphrased): \"The wrong abstraction is a lot more expensive than duplicated code\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":13311595,"author":"newtscamander","month":"2017-01","story":"Classes Considered Harmful [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains shared behavior without duplication and common mistakes","quote":"it explains how shared behavior works without code duplication and why people tend to mess it up.","text":"The premise is the introduction is wrong. \"so before we have written a single program in our language, before we know whether shared behaviour will be important in the applications that will be written in it\" read the POODR book, or watch this [VIDEO] it explains how shared behavior works without code duplication and why people tend to mess it up."},{"id":39666701,"author":"ValentinTrinque","month":"2024-03","story":"Maybe Functions","stance":"reference","why":"Metz talk on null objects","quote":"As Sandy Metz is used to say","text":"You do have another solution that can lower the amount of conditions: Null Objects. These don’t fit every use cases, but they can allow you to express what’s missing, or not defined, or empty, and avoid nil pointers dereference or conditions to check the state. As Sandy Metz is used to say « Nothing is Something »[0] 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19418700,"author":"profalseidol","month":"2019-03","story":"Alan Kay on the Meaning of “Object-Oriented Programming” (2003)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about messages in Ruby","quote":"Here's one talk about 'message' in Ruby","text":"Here's one talk about 'message' in Ruby: RailsConf 2015 - Nothing is Something [VIDEO] But to me, this simply looks like Functional Programming done in an OOP mess."}]},"XKu_SEDAykw":{"wy":"Cited as a realistic, well-run example of a technical interview, both for candidates preparing and interviewers calibrating.","q":{"x":"two of the suggestions in this excellent video from Google showing an example of a great technical interview","i":24325067,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24325067,"author":"MarkMc","month":"2020-08","story":"Most common mistakes during interviews","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent example of a great technical interview with clarifying questions.","quote":"two of the suggestions in this excellent video from Google showing an example of a great technical interview","text":"'Ask clarifying questions' and 'think out loud' are also two of the suggestions in this excellent video from Google showing an example of a great technical interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19933633,"author":"sonnyblarney","month":"2019-05","story":"I turned my interview task for Google into a startup","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Example Google whiteboard interview, praised as excellent.","quote":"Here is an example of a Google white boarding interview, it's excellent in many ways","text":"Whether or not 'everyone' wants to work there is not hugely material. They, and others that do the white-boarding, are 'the best'. They have loads of talent, operational efficiency, ship tons of great products with great innovations. White-boarding done appropriately is an excellent means to develop an understanding of people's aptitudes in a short period of time. Here is an example of a Google white boarding interview, it's excellent in many ways: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19933791,"author":"sonnyblarney","month":"2019-05","story":"I turned my interview task for Google into a startup","stance":"rec","why":"Shows chances for candidates to shine technically and in communication.","quote":"This example [1] from Google is really quite good.","text":"\"I personally don't recommend those kinds of companies \" You wouldn't recommend Google, Microsoft, Facebook and a host of the best companies in the world because they do 'white board interviews'? You're not only working against the data here, you're working against reason: asking people to solve problems that they may encounter on the job is an excellent means of measuring a number of things, even beyond their ability to solve this problem. There are still quite a lot of 'senior engineers' who actually might be valuable in some specific circumstances, who can't walk through problems or write decent code at all. This example [1] from Google is really quite good. If you watch carefully you'll see how many elements and opportunities there are for a 'good developer' to shine both technically, and also in terms of communication. And also for 'ok developer' to just do an ok job. They're not asking bizarre or impossible to solve questions that you'd have to study for either. \"That can be, especially for junior engineers.\" No, it's good to weed out 'senior engineers' who think that their experience and 'good relationships' entitle them to a job. \"I don't want to burn myself out doing unpaid work\" - an on-site bit of coding is not 'unpaid work' - please don't take this angle that your (or mine, or anyone else's) bit of quick hacky coding is 'work' that could be useful really in any way. This is different from a '2 hour but really 2 day' take home test which is ridiculous. \"And yet I'v"},{"id":19081174,"author":"sonnyblarney","month":"2019-02","story":"A Humility Training Exercise for Technical Interviewers","stance":"rec","why":"Nice Google interview example with open-ended questions.","quote":"Google has a nice interview example [1]","text":"Very good points, agree with it. However - I don't think this is about 'ego' or even 'humility' - I think those are not the right words. It's a lack of contextual understanding both in 'self awareness' and also the interviewers plight. I think the premise can be taught. Also, I think interviews can be structured to find qualities independant of background. + Questions that don't measure a person's ability to 'memorize algorithms' are a good start. + Allowing devs to pick their language of expression, i.e. sometimes they are more comfortable in one lang than another. + Don't get syntax/code structure confused with the abstract problem if that's what one is going for. Google has a nice interview example [1] + Open ended questions with many possible turns allow for a 'good' thinker to just go a lot further, and be more impressive while at the same time allowing junior devs to still walk through and complete something. The Google example is again good here. + Time/on the spot - one of the worst issues. Personally I'm about 50/50. Sometimes 'in the flow' sometimes 'not', but surely just given a little bit fo time, I'd be fine on most things. For this reason, giving interviewees an intro to the problems, and giving them as much time as they want to think about them before the interview starts, might be worthwhile as well. 'Let us know when you want to go over a solution'. This could work well for pedantic things such as 'here's some code, find some bugs' or 'how would you structure"},{"id":15473289,"author":"bhuga","month":"2017-10","story":"Ask HN: How do you conduct coding interviews?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Google's interview prep material.","quote":"Google sends you stuff like .","text":"I just went through a round of interviews and came away with a better opinion of these \"toy\" interviews than I started with[1], so I'll play devil's advocate here: these questions have a place. The main reason to use these questions is that they're studyable. Google and Facebook both put out content that walk you through how to solve problems like this. When you get an interview, you can delay it as long as you want, they can tell you the sort of problems to study: go read \"Cracking the Coding Interview\". Facebook sends you a link to it and a video of the author working a problem. Google sends you stuff like [VIDEO] . There are thousands of toy questions, and it's not feasible to memorize them all. The correct _and expected_ thing to do is study up for the category. Now that your candidates have in theory studied, you'll get a studied, \"best\" performance for each candidate, and interview outcomes can be compared in a very low-bias, apples-to-apples way. I can't imagine the success criteria for a pairing being clearer than for toys. I think the potential for toy question interviews to be managed in a low-bias way is the main reason they're used. In comparison, how do you ensure that your exercise in go is exactly as hard as your exercise in Java? Or do they need to know go, or some other language? Or that they didn't spend longer on the take home than you asked? Or 100 other factors of your particular exercise? It's really hard to let every candidate shine on a level playing f"}]},"Y0Oa4Lp5fLE":{"wy":"Recommended in game-theory and cooperation threads, with the whole lecture series endorsed.","q":{"x":"This entire lecture series on Human Behavioral Biology is worth watching from the beginning","i":13635824,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":26123177,"author":"jonas_kgomo","month":"2021-02","story":"Microbial ecosystems in the mouth and gut are linked to many ills","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sapolsky course covering behavioral evolution and toxoplasma.","quote":"Professor Robert Sapolsky[1] has an amazing course on Behavorial Evolution.","text":"Besides the gut flora being capable of some neurological effects it has demonstrated to be psychoactive. Professor Robert Sapolsky[1] has an amazing course on Behavorial Evolution. The toxoplasmic gondii comes from the rat -> cat -> gut -> brain, it is profound that side effects in the brain are chemically constructed from the stomach. Considering that most animals do not even have a brain,but usually have a stomach, it is evident that the gut is underestimated in the evolutionary tree. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13635824,"author":"philipov","month":"2017-02","story":"Understanding Agent Cooperation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sapolsky on tit-for-tat strategies arising in animals, such as vampire bats.","quote":"This entire lecture series on Human Behavioral Biology is worth watching from the beginning","text":"> Looking at the long history of evolution, I'd say no. This entire lecture series on Human Behavioral Biology is worth watching from the beginning, but I've linked to a moment where Sapolsky describes tit-for-tat strategies arising in animals. First example: Vampire Bats. []: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23207413,"author":"remolueoend","month":"2020-05","story":"Iterated Prisoners Dilemma Strategies Dominate Any Evolutionary Opponent (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Sapolsky lecture on behavioral evolution and tit for tat.","quote":"There is a great lecture from Robert Sapolsky/Stanford [2010]","text":"There is a great lecture from Robert Sapolsky/Stanford [2010] about behavioral evolution / prisoner's dilemma / tit for tat strategy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12827864,"author":"spacehacker","month":"2016-10","story":"Strategies of Human Mating (2006) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think the tournament vs. pair-bonding spectrum [1] might be very insightful with regards to this debate. The observation is basically that animals have different manifestations of sexual dimorphism and that there are very clear behavioral patterns that correlate with them. In some species, e.g. gorillas, males are big and muscular and you reliably get a lot of aggression and males that care little about their offspring. In tournament species also only few alpha males have reproductive success. On the other hand, in other species, e.g. bonobos, there is very little sexual dimorphism, little aggression, females select partners that display parenting behavior and most males reproduce. It turns out that humans are somewhere in between tournament and pair-bonding species and this is probably root of a lot of confusion. The rules are not as clear as in the two extremes, and in addition there is a lot of variability in behavior anyway because the generality of the neocortex basically allows us to adopt arbitrary rules. Some cultures are more puritanical, some are less. Some males get more excited about parenting and cute animals, while others are rather focused on achieving high status and thus fare well with traditional gender roles to achieve that. But what status means is itself variable, e.g. high status can include the appeal to some egalitarian paternal parenting meme. A lot of the results, e.g. gender-specific appreciation of good jobs, high incomes might reflect both cultu"}],"r":[["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["9crggox5rbc",1]]},"_160oMzblY8":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as the best ground-up, non-hype introduction for people who want to understand what a blockchain actually is.","q":{"x":"It explains from ground up without getting complicated","i":15504655,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15504655,"author":"andreasklinger","month":"2017-10","story":"Blockchains explained visually (part 1 of 2)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ground-up blockchain explanation that demystifies it for non-technical people.","quote":"It explains from ground up without getting complicated","text":"My favorite: [VIDEO] It explains from ground up without getting complicated - it basically demystifies the whole thing completely i give this to everyone - even to non technical people"},{"id":15107689,"author":"dave7","month":"2017-08","story":"Storj: Not a Dropbox Killer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Basic visual introduction to blockchain.","quote":"this video is a fantastic introduction","text":"This is much more basic than academic level, for which you could maybe start with the original Satoshi Nakamoto Bitcoin white paper? - but this video is a fantastic introduction [VIDEO]"},{"id":16310897,"author":"phn","month":"2018-02","story":"Ask HN: Would like to learn blockchain tech, where do I start? What to build?","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to what a blockchain actually is.","quote":"this video is a really good intro","text":"If you're really wondering what a blockchain actually is, this video is a really good intro: [VIDEO] If you want something a bit more hands-on, I'm currently following this course: [LINK] I have only made it through the first section, and it does a really good job of explaining the basics of Ethereum and what a smart contract actually is."},{"id":45260372,"author":"glaucon","month":"2025-09","story":"Show HN: Demochain, a toy blockchain network that runs on the browser","stance":"reference","why":"Visual blockchain demo cited as a model for explainer videos.","quote":"Something like these two videos, only based on your tool, would be very good","text":"Great looking tool, thanks. Looking through your \"Futures\" on the README I do think ... \"Add videos demonstrating common concepts and attacks (such as race attack, finney attack etc).\" ... would certainly make it accessible to a wider audience which, given the amount of effort you've put in, seems like a good idea. Something like these two videos, only based on your tool, would be very good - to [VIDEO] (Part 1), [LINK] (Part 2) ."},{"id":15400703,"author":"agreen","month":"2017-10","story":"Ask HN: Resources to learn blockchain and smart contracts?","stance":"reference","why":"Blockchain demo included in a learning resource list.","quote":"","text":"Intro: 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [VIDEO] 5. [LINK] 6. [LINK] Build Your Own Blockchain: 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] Resource List for an Advanced Understanding (Multidisciplinary Approach): 1. [LINK] --- Additionally, here is some advice from Balaji Srinivasan (source: [LINK] ): 1. First, I'd master database and cryptography fundamentals, with something like Dan Boneh's course for the latter 2. Then, I'd read the Bitcoin.org and Ethereum documentation 3. Then, you should write short Python or Go scripts to do things like printing out the blockchain, formatting and signing a transaction by hand, managing private keys, that type of thing. This will give you a feel for the raw data structures. 4. After doing all this, I'd take a look at application libraries like Ryan Shea and Muneeb Ali's new blockstack.org The reason I'd do it in this order is that you would learn fundamentals first before proceeding to the cutting edge."},{"id":14435643,"author":"fergazen","month":"2017-05","story":"Blockchains from the ground up: Part 1","stance":"reference","why":"Visual demo of blockchain as a data structure.","quote":"is an algorithm for detecting if one blockchain disagrees with other blockchains","text":"In every post i've made it has been crystal clear I'm referring to \"blockchain the data-structure\"... The \"consensus component\" is an algorithm for detecting if one blockchain disagrees with other blockchains, and ignoreing it if it does. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["D38dcArwCNc",1]]},"uYNzqgU7na4":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining, short explanation whenever British place names or their linguistic history come up.","q":{"x":"The Map Men on YouTube did a short (8min) video on why pronunciation is hard and a guide","i":39989395,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41718797,"author":"happymellon","month":"2024-10","story":"The other British invasion: how UK lingo conquered the US","stance":"rec","why":"Shows multiple language roots behind English place names.","quote":"The video helps it makes sense.","text":"Hopefully Map Men might help with this. [VIDEO] TLDW; We have multiple different root languages in English city names, and you are only thinking one dimensionally, with the Roman Latin root. Cirencester is a good example, pronounced how you might expect while further north the town names get more germanic, and eventually Scandinavian. The video helps it makes sense."},{"id":41932347,"author":"Maakuth","month":"2024-10","story":"An Illustrated Guide to Maritime Signal Flags","stance":"rec","why":"Fun clip on British place name pronunciation drift.","quote":"Here's a fun clip about it, focusing on British place names","text":"Yes, yes it is. My understanding is that it used to be spoken in a way closer to the written form, but it has drifted. And then there are all the loan words that retain at least some of their original pronunciation. Here's a fun clip about it, focusing on British place names: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39989395,"author":"helsinkiandrew","month":"2024-04","story":"British Placename Mapper","stance":"rec","why":"Short video on why British place names are hard to pronounce, with a guide.","quote":"The Map Men on YouTube did a short (8min) video on why pronunciation is hard and a guide","text":"> Just needs a pronunciation guide now The Map Men on YouTube did a short (8min) video on why pronunciation is hard and a guide: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35948247,"author":"happymellon","month":"2023-05","story":"Why are so many young Americans adopting fake British accents?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why British place names reflect many invading languages.","quote":"Map men explain it best.","text":"It's due to being invaded by many different counties over the years, and each of them only invade a portion. Map men explain it best. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27922947,"author":"verytrivial","month":"2021-07","story":"4.6B-year-old meteorite found","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining explanation of how British place names are pronounced.","quote":"Ref. for entertaining additions.","text":"Off-central-topic, but regarding where this was found, if you find yourself discussing this offline, \"Loughborough\" is pronounced \"LUFF-burro\" or \"LUFF-burrah\". Ref. [VIDEO] for entertaining additions."},{"id":39989656,"author":"ubutler","month":"2024-04","story":"British Placename Mapper","stance":"reference","why":"Map Men episode showing Viking influence via place names.","quote":"by plotting British place names of Viking origins, it is possible to see the true extent of their invasion","text":"This reminds me of an episode of Map Men where they offhandedly point out that by plotting British place names of Viking origins, it is possible to see the true extent of their invasion: [VIDEO] . In fact, the map they show in that episode ( [LINK] ) looks strikingly similar to this map I've found on Wikipedia of Celtic, Danish and English territory in 878: [LINK]"}],"r":[["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["st571DYYTR8",1],["g3Ud6mHdhlQ",1],["xnWKz7Cthkk",1],["vaCVh2SAZY4",1],["GJCF-Ufapu8",1],["OXS8ljif9b8",1],["3lXv3Tt4x20",1]]},"-2ZTmuX3cog":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever a company shuts down a service and wipes user content, as an entertaining case for digital preservation.","q":{"x":"gave a fantastically entertaining talk about how they saved Geocities, Yahoo! Video, and Friendster","i":7871762,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7871762,"author":"aroman","month":"2014-06","story":"ArchiveTeam rescues Justin.tv videos","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining talk on how Archive Team saved Geocities, Yahoo! Video and Friendster.","quote":"gave a fantastically entertaining talk about how they saved Geocities, Yahoo! Video, and Friendster","text":"I love the ArchiveTeam. Jason Scott, the leader of the project, gave a fantastically entertaining talk about how they saved Geocities, Yahoo! Video, and Friendster — using a Distributed Preservation of Service Attack. Definitely worth a watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11439762,"author":"ggreer","month":"2016-04","story":"In the Future, We Will Photograph Everything and Look at Nothing","stance":"rec","why":"Jason Scott explains why even mundane archived data becomes historically valuable.","quote":"In it, he explains why this data is important.","text":"Often, this supposedly redundant data is useful in ways current society can't fathom. Millions of photos of the Mona Lisa taken over decades won't tell you much new about the Mona Lisa. But it will tell you a lot about changes in camera technology over that time. Also, the long tail is long . There are whole cultures and subcultures that would be completely forgotten if not for archives. At Defcon 19, Jason Scott gave a talk titled Archive Team: A Distributed Presentation of Service Attack[1]. In it, he explains why this data is important. As you rightly point out, the vast majority of it is boring and useless. It matters to no one but the author and their friends. But some of it is weird or funny, and a tiny bit of it is pure historical gold. The early days of usenet, for example, contain tons of interesting conversations involving people who are now prominent. Thanks to these archives, we can get a better picture of these people's lives than in earlier times. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7827399,"author":"chroma","month":"2014-05","story":"Justin.tv ending archiving, deleting all archived videos after June 14","stance":"rec","why":"Jason Scott explains why companies deleting user content destroys historical evidence.","quote":"Jason Scott gave an excellent talk about this failure mode[1]","text":"A few Defcons ago, Jason Scott gave an excellent talk about this failure mode[1]. Basically, companies host user-generated content for free. Then years later, they destroy it with little or even no notice. Sturgeon's law applies; most of the deleted content is crap. But a lot of it matters to the people who made it. More importantly, a small fraction of it is stuff that future historians would kill for. Collectively, companies that engage in this behavior are burning historical evidence. 1. Archive Team: A Distributed Preservation of Service Attack [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["et8xNAc2ic8",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["4Z2uzEM0ugY",1],["nLF0n9SACd4",1],["g6bOwQdCJrc",1],["hBpetDxIEMU",1]]},"2nBPN-MKefA":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a cartoonish but thorough explanation of the monetary system, though some critics dispute its claims.","q":{"x":"is a cartoonish but thorough explanation for anyone who has the time","i":32361563,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":22534673,"author":"jonplackett","month":"2020-03","story":"Payments on mortgages to be suspended across Italy after coronavirus outbreak","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on how the monetary system works.","quote":"Anyone who wants to understand this insanity, I recommend MONEY AS DEBT","text":"Anyone who wants to understand this insanity, I recommend MONEY AS DEBT [VIDEO] The first time I saw it I thought it was a luny conspiracy theory, but NO. It's all true."},{"id":22574206,"author":"scirocco","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: Book recommendations for understanding financial systems?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changes how you view financial markets.","quote":"since then you will never take financial markets seriously","text":"This is a good start, \"Money as debt\". Saw it in university and since then you will never take financial markets seriously. And all the \"stimulus\" we see is just pushing the can forward. You will start to see through a lot of lies. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32361563,"author":"zugi","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: “government is printing money” - what does it mean in practice?","stance":"rec","why":"Cartoonish but thorough explanation of debt-based money.","quote":"is a cartoonish but thorough explanation for anyone who has the time","text":"> Which in practice means there's literally never enough money in circulation to pay off the 'debt'. I always wondered why so many people were in so much debt, and was really shocked when I came to the understanding that we've designed our monetary system to trap people in debt. [VIDEO] is a cartoonish but thorough explanation for anyone who has the time. This system really is insane. I mean, I thought Bitcoin was dumb because it's based on nothing, but our national government-based monetary systems being based on debt are even worse."},{"id":30827607,"author":"OrlandoHakim","month":"2022-03","story":"What is money, anyway?","stance":"rec","why":"Important documentary on money.","quote":"I am surprised no one has shared these two important documentary videos on money","text":"I am surprised no one has shared these two important documentary videos on money: Money as Debt: [VIDEO] Principles for Dealing with the Changing World Order by Ray Dalio: [LINK]"},{"id":30329255,"author":"ur-whale","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: What real world problem does cryptocurrency solve?","stance":"reference","why":"Personal anecdote: the video that came with the introduction to bitcoin.","quote":"","text":"> Early(ish) adopter here(circa 2013): initially it was a way for nerds who didn’t have street connections to buy drugs. You are making assumptions. It might have been your motivation back then. It most certainly wasn't the motivation for many people I know in the space. I was introduced to bitcoin by someone who was also promoting this: [VIDEO] and the interest I developed for it was both because the tech. was interesting and new and it resonated with my political sensibilities. Strictly nothing to do with drugs."}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["K1WrHH-WtaA",1],["5HbYScltf1c",1]]},"94ed4v_T6YM":{"wy":"HN points to it as a short, approachable starting point on warp drive physics from a channel commenters like.","q":{"x":"For a shorter 10 minute video, see this PBS Spacetime video","i":29469302,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":36522607,"author":"pedro_hab","month":"2023-06","story":"Monster gravitational waves spotted for first time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the Alcubierre drive and why spacetime moves, not the ship","quote":"The channel below has quite a few other videos on the subject. I love it.","text":"Mathematically yes, actually probably not. I'd suggest looking into Alcubierre Warp Drive, cool story on why the guy came up with it and shows how to wrap spacetime around a spaceship to make it go faster than light. The ship wouldn't go faster than light because it wouldn't move at all, the spacetime around it would. The channel below has quite a few other videos on the subject. I love it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29469302,"author":"LegitShady","month":"2021-12","story":"Numerics applied to Casimir geometry generates intersection with Alcubierre warp","stance":"rec","why":"Short 10 minute overview of Alcubierre drive feasibility","quote":"For a shorter 10 minute video, see this PBS Spacetime video","text":"The issue seems to be that the alcubierre drive requires things like external negative energy which are 'theoretically possible' but have never been detected and probably should have been already if they existed. Also, even if they worked, the negative energy requirements is something like the mass energy of jupiter on the high end or the moon on the low end. This is an hour long interview Dr Miguel Alcubierre who wrote the paper describing the Alcubierre drive. [LINK] For a shorter 10 minute video, see this PBS Spacetime video [VIDEO]"},{"id":25231631,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-11","story":"Warp Drive News","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting episode on warp drives in a short physics series","quote":"they have an episode that would be a good place to start on warp drives","text":"There's an episode of PBS Space Time about this, \"The Speed of Light is NOT About Light\" [1], which comes pretty close to making the same points you are making. It might be useful to watch this earlier episode, \"Are Space and Time an Illusion?\" [2], first. There is a later episode (or maybe a couple of them) that go into how interactions with the Higgs field make it so things with mass travel slowed than the speed of causality. To tie back to the warp drive news, they have an episode that would be a good place to start on warp drives, \"Is The Alcubierre Warp Drive Possible?\" [3]. There's also \"Superluminal Time Travel + Time Warp Challenge Answer\" [4], and \"Will Wormholes Allow Fast Interstellar Travel?\" [5]. A non-warp unknown physics drive that comes up a lot also got an episode, \"The EM Drive: Fact or Fantasy?\" [6]. There is at least one episode covering interstellar travel without using any unknown physics, \"5 REAL Possibilities for Interstellar Travel\" [7]. The scope of the series is basically anything vaguely connected to astronomy, cosmology, and quantum mechanics, and the episodes are all pretty short (5-12 minutes), so it is a great thing to watch when you've got a short wait for something. Here's the series home page [8], and their YouTube channel [9]. Also on the PBS streaming app, for those who like to get their physics on their big screen TVs. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK] [8] [LINK] [9] [LINK]"}],"r":[["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["RkTvDjhImwo",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["4d0EGIt1SPc",1],["p45T7U5oi9Q",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HF-9Dy6iB_4",1],["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1],["XheAMrS8Q1c",1]]},"HrUy1Vn2KdI":{"wy":"It is one of the few long, candid interviews with the famously reclusive McCarthy, and commenters share it whenever he comes up.","q":{"x":"This is my favourite interview with him so I will take this post as an excuse to share it.","i":45449852,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45449852,"author":"polytely","month":"2025-10","story":"Cormac McCarthy's personal library","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Commenter's favourite interview with McCarthy","quote":"This is my favourite interview with him so I will take this post as an excuse to share it.","text":"Thanks for posting, he was a really interesting guy. This is my favourite interview with him so I will take this post as an excuse to share it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39527848,"author":"slibhb","month":"2024-02","story":"Cormac McCarthy's Sideline: Freelance copy editor","stance":"rec","why":"McCarthy among scientists at Santa Fe Institute","quote":"Worth a watch if you're interested in McCarthy","text":"Worth a watch if you're interested in McCarthy: [VIDEO] The gist is that he liked smart people. He was good friends with Murray Gell-Mann and others at The Santa Fe Institute. I absolutely think he \"liked science,\" I don't think he was cynical about it, though I do see where you could pick that up in his writing. According to Gell-Mann, McCarthy knew a lot about 20th century physics...not in terms of math but in a \"history of ideas\" sense, i.e. where major physicists stood on various questions."},{"id":36316254,"author":"AJRF","month":"2023-06","story":"Cormac McCarthy, Novelist of a Darker America, Is Dead at 89","stance":"rec","why":"A nice interview with McCarthy","quote":"This is a nice interview;","text":"God I will miss his writing. I finished (and adored) The Passenger a few weeks ago. Rest In Piece. This is a nice interview; [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5d6SEQQbwtU",1],["o0fG_lnVhHw",1]]},"Npd_xDuNi9k":{"wy":"Recommended as the accessible explanation to share with non-techies, family and politicians.","q":{"x":"I'd highly reccomended his video \"What is right to repair? An introduction for curious people.\" as it clears up many misconceptions about right to repair","i":26998169,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26706638,"author":"throwhelp41890","month":"2021-04","story":"Let's Get Right to Repair Passed – Louis Rossmann","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Simple, profanity-free explanation shareable with politicians and family","quote":"Go forth and share.","text":"\"Many people have asked for a simple, concise, non-profanity laden video that summarizes the issue that is shareable to politicians and family members. Here you go. No profanity, no technobabble, just a basic explanation. Go forth and share. Thank you!\" -- Louis Rossmann What is Right to repair? An intro: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26998169,"author":"operator-name","month":"2021-04","story":"It's your device, you should be able to repair it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clears up misconceptions about right to repair","quote":"I'd highly reccomended his video \"What is right to repair? An introduction for curious people.\" as it clears up many misconceptions about right to repair","text":"I'd highly reccomended his video \"What is right to repair? An introduction for curious people.\" as it clears up many misconceptions about right to repair: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26642993,"author":"dataflow","month":"2021-03","story":"Louis Rossmann crowdfunding initiative & investing 2 years for Right to Repair","stance":"rec","why":"Non-technical pitch for right to repair","quote":"Pitch for non-techies","text":"Yes! Spread the word too. Also, if you know a professional that might be able to help him out with the legal/politics/etc., consider reaching out. He's a sharp guy but it's still a tough task and he is going to need all the help he can get. And so does our planet. Donation link: [LINK] Pitch for non-techies: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23285296,"author":"skdd8","month":"2020-05","story":"Hacker Mods Old Calculator to Access the Internet, Casio Files DMCA Complaint","stance":"rec","why":"Rallying cry for right to repair","quote":"Fight for your right to repair","text":"What in the actual fuck! I laughed so hard at this video just to watch in disgust how the DCMA and it's lackey GH would take down a repo that has nothing to do with Casio other than the name perhaps. Where is the freedom to tinker? If I modify my Toyota Corolla to take a Ferrari engine would Ferrari or Toyota come to my house and take my car away? Fight for your right to repair: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40532570,"author":"londons_explore","month":"2024-05","story":"EU countries must implement right to repair laws within two years","stance":"reference","why":"Introduces the most vocal right-to-repair advocate","quote":"The most vocal person behind the right to repair movement","text":"The most vocal person behind the right to repair movement: [VIDEO] Oh, and he runs a professional repair shop..."},{"id":25926440,"author":"ClumsyPilot","month":"2021-01","story":"IKEA buys 11,000 acres of U.S. forest to keep it from being developed","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as background on unrepairable products","quote":"deliberate efforts by compabies like Apple and Sonor to make their equipment unrepairable.","text":"Buying new stuff in the west is now cheaper than repairing old items - this is a combination of outsourcing production, loosing skills, and deliberate efforts by compabies like Apple and Sonor to make their equipment unrepairable. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NeopkvAP-ag",1]]},"clnozSXyF4k":{"wy":"HN points to it as a demo of how routinely mundane TV scenes use green screen set replacement, even on TV budgets.","q":{"x":"This is an amazing demo reel of effects shots used in \"mundane\" TV shows","i":41584276,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41584276,"author":"xsmasher","month":"2024-09","story":"Why wordfreq will not be updated","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demo reel of effects faking locations in mundane TV shows.","quote":"This is an amazing demo reel of effects shots used in \"mundane\" TV shows","text":"This is an amazing demo reel of effects shots used in \"mundane\" TV shows - comedies and produce procedurals. - for faking locations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39417851,"author":"e_y_","month":"2024-02","story":"Jeffrey Katzenberg Says A.I. Will Eliminate 90% of Artist Jobs on Animated Films","stance":"rec","why":"Example of green screen set replacement on a TV budget.","quote":"is a great example of green screen set replacement being used for things as ordinary as a city street","text":"Even today, there's good and bad uses of CG, usually coming down to schedule and creative direction. The average quality has gone up substantially, though. As you pointed out, there's all kinds of ways to use CGI and VFX more subtly that most people would never realize. [VIDEO] is a great example of green screen set replacement being used for things as ordinary as a city street, 15 years ago on a TV series budget. I expect the use of AI will be similarly varied in the beginning. It could be used just for background details. It could be used in a hybrid fashion where artists design the characters with reference poses, a storyboard, and maybe even some keyframes, and the AI fills in the gaps. And of course some studios will just be sloppy and use AI to generate larger pieces without fixing the glitches and inconsistencies. People are sometimes dismissive of AI based on the \"fire and forget\" prompting modes but don't consider AI-assisted tools or human-assisted AI like inpainting."},{"id":15333291,"author":"manigandham","month":"2017-09","story":"Why did science fiction stop moving forward?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows hidden CG in ordinary TV shows.","quote":"Here's a good video, from 2009","text":"You only notice the bad CGI - most TV shows, even a normal sitcom, have a ton of CG that you would never realize unless you saw the raw shots. It's what the audience expects and demands today. Things are getting more efficient but quality level has risen even faster. Here's a good video, from 2009: [VIDEO] And 2012: [LINK]"},{"id":6453983,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2013-09","story":"Google Recently Made A Silent Shift To A New Search Algorithm, “Hummingbird”","stance":"reference","why":"Shows ordinary TV shows heavily using chroma key compositing.","quote":"It's now normal for standard-issue comedies and dramas to be chroma keyed, comped, etc","text":"Oh, it goes a lot deeper than that my friend. It's now normal for standard-issue comedies and dramas to be chroma keyed, comped, etc: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["Erp8IAUouus",1],["wrwgIjBUYVc",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1],["996OiexHze0",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1]]},"e08kOj2kISU":{"wy":"A favorite tech talk on HN for showing that hardware knowledge and generalist skills beat simply adding machines.","q":{"x":"Humour, serious technical insight and a good reminder of why being a generalist is an advantage.","i":12638364,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12638364,"author":"anoother","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Humorous, technically insightful talk on optimizing Python","quote":"Humour, serious technical insight and a good reminder of why being a generalist is an advantage.","text":"\"How to Speed up a Python Program 114,000 times.\" - [VIDEO] Humour, serious technical insight and a good reminder of why being a generalist is an advantage."},{"id":24766844,"author":"lkbm","month":"2020-10","story":"14,000x Speedup (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows hardware knowledge beats adding machines","quote":"It's an hour-long talk, but quite enjoyable.","text":"I thought this was going to be \"How to Speed up a Python Program 114,000 times\"[0]. It's an hour-long talk, but quite enjoyable. In the example in the talk, it was less about knowing cs algorithms and more about knowing the details of how modern computer architecture works, but the lesson is the same: sometimes the background knowledge of a cs degree is useful. He also has the same point about optimizing our code rather than just adding more and more machines. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13130579,"author":"gravypod","month":"2016-12","story":"Talks that changed the way I think about programming","stance":"rec","why":"Explains pipelining and cache effects on speed","quote":"There is a great talk about this [0]","text":"This will technically only work on pipelined architectures of which all major CPU architectures are one. You will be able to see a significant boost to performance given the following are all true: * You have no inter dependancy of information across the lines * All your data is in the cache * You don't have instructions generated between the two lines that will require some sort of operand forwarding Basically, if you already have the stuff in the cache, the calculation is invarent to the last calculation, and you have a processor capable of it you'll see a large speed improvement. There is a great talk about this [0] (for the exact moment go to [1]). If you're interested further I'd very much recommend finding some sort of GOOD computer architecture class. Luckily now we have a fantastic resource to locate these such courses [2]. This seems like dark magic and many programmers refuse to come to terms with it's existence. Check out `dreta`'s comment to see an example. Now I do agree with dreta, this is VERY much architecture dependant but most architectures would benifit from this sort of optimization. Pretty much all modern Intel and AMD CPU's caches are large enough, they support a big enough cache, and they also all have pipelines under the hood. Intel started this in the pentium series with a 5-stage-pipeline (which I find very funny due to the GHz wars that came from that time period desipte the pipeline being probably the pargest performance boon in most cases that you"},{"id":31272649,"author":"lmc","month":"2022-05","story":"JavaScript Containers","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture on speeding up a Python algorithm 100000x","quote":"I suspect it's this one","text":"> I remember I recently saw a lecture by an industry professional who took a Python algorithm and improved it 100 000x, in parts. That is, in stages he explained how to utilize the hardware on that particular machine, to improve the algorithm so much that it was hard to believe. Anyone have a link? I suspect it's this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8904092,"author":"dmoney","month":"2015-01","story":"The Cost of Scalability in Graph Processing","stance":"reference","why":"Example of scaling in by optimizing a Python program","quote":"An example of \"scaling in\" is this talk, \"How to speed up a Python program 114,000 times\"","text":"An example of \"scaling in\" is this talk, \"How to speed up a Python program 114,000 times\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bvlzZnhZhrc",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["4t1K66dMhWk",1],["j6ow-UemzBc",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["an5abNFba4Q",1],["4s9IjkMAmns",1]]},"4t1K66dMhWk":{"wy":"It is the standard citation in HN arguments over Rust for game development, the borrow checker and ECS designs, as a skeptical view from an experienced game developer.","q":{"x":"A more detailed description of his opinions are laid out here where he goes into much more detail","i":22220205,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":40172604,"author":"colund","month":"2024-04","story":"Leaving Rust gamedev after 3 years","stance":"rec","why":"Blow's perspectives on Rust game development and its reliance on arrays.","quote":"He adds interesting perspectives to the discussio","text":"I find that Jonathan Blow ranting about Rust game development here [VIDEO] . He adds interesting perspectives to the discussio, how the language makes the Rust game developer resort to arrays and their so called Rust point of views."},{"id":32718378,"author":"hertzrat","month":"2022-09","story":"Unreal Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Blow explains why Rust misses hard game problems.","quote":"learned a lot from the various ways people try to solve them","text":"These discussions always remind me of some talks by Jonathan blow where he argues rust doesn’t solve the hard problems he needed it to. There are podcast episodes with better discussions, but the main one people link to seems to be this I haven’t used rust, but I sympathize with his problems with pointers and learned a lot from the various ways people try to solve them [VIDEO]"},{"id":22220205,"author":"gravypod","month":"2020-02","story":"Jonathan Blow on Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed opinions from experience porting The Witness.","quote":"A more detailed description of his opinions are laid out here where he goes into much more detail","text":"Pointers living through a render pass aren't the main use case he is interested in. It's a problem space that many engineers are familiar with so it's easy to talk about. Game developers have some very domain-specific problems that are less common outside of game engines. A more detailed description of his opinions are laid out here where he goes into much more detail about his experiences porting The Witness to mobile (I think): [VIDEO]"},{"id":46315517,"author":"matthewkayin","month":"2025-12","story":"Jonathan Blow has spent the past decade designing 1,400 puzzles","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as Blow's stated reason for disliking Rust: borrow checker slows iteration.","quote":"he has said that he doesn't like Rust because he feels that satisfying the borrow-checker slows down iteration time","text":"> What features in Jai make it superior to C++ for writing games specifically? I know that Jonathan Blow can be abrasive and one-sided in his talks on programming, but I think we should be open-minded about Jai. Yes, he is making this language because he doesn't like C++, but you make it sound like he is hating on C++ just for the sake of it. I mean, is it really so hard to imagine that someone might not like something about C++? There are plenty of people who think we could have a better systems language, which is why we have seen languages like Rust, Zig, and Odin pop up. In Blow's case, he has said that he doesn't like Rust because he feels that satisfying the borrow-checker slows down iteration time[1], which is important especially in the early stages of game development when you are still experimenting with mechanics are where requirements and architecture are still very much subject to change. As far as what Jai offers, it seems his focus is on making a simple but powerful language (contrary to C++'s ever-growing bag-of-tricks), with fast compile times (less than 3 seconds on a full build of his new game), better build and dependency management (no more cmake), and powerful meta-programming features. In a talk on the language[2], he demos how he is able to use the language's meta-programming features to develop powerful code-analyzing and memory-analyzing tools. These tools, in particular, hint at his philosophy: lots of ideas in programming like RAII, garbage collecto"},{"id":44873809,"author":"bakugo","month":"2025-08","story":"Undefined Behavior in C and C++ (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant example of game patterns conflicting with the borrow checker.","quote":"a lot of patterns that are commonly used in game development are fundamentally at odds with the borrow checker","text":"There's also the fact that a lot of patterns that are commonly used in game development are fundamentally at odds with the borrow checker. Relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39008185,"author":"pengaru","month":"2024-01","story":"Show HN: A minimal working Rust / SDL2 / WASM browser game","stance":"reference","why":"Blow's response to a keynote about ECS and the borrow checker.","quote":"Blow actually responded to the keynote you linked","text":"ECS can be implemented in any language, it's orthogonal to my comment re: why Rust for gamedev? ECS is such an obvious concept, it's akin to file descriptors and PIDs on *NIX; an indirection table for handles, :slowclap:. There's nothing Rust-unique about it. The amount of hype I see surrounding ECS like it's some kind of groundbreaking amazing thing, often muddled into \"Rust for gamedev\" conversations, causes me to question everything said in such pro-Rust discussions. Blow actually responded to the keynote you linked [0]. What's still unclear to me is what's the advantage that Rust is bringing to the table? Is game development velocity higher than in C++/C/C#? The ecosystem is obviously less mature, and the borrow-checker is an obvious source of friction... How do we separate the hype from the facts? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37122787,"author":"zengid","month":"2023-08","story":"I built a garbage collector for a language that doesn't need one","stance":"reference","why":"Critique that index-based approaches hack around the borrow checker.","quote":"isn't just a hack to get around the borrow checker as people like Jonathan Blow have famously critiqued","text":"This is really impressive work and I applaud the author for building something that I'm sure lots of folks will find interesting and possibly very useful, but I want to say that I think there's a reason reference semantics (using and sharing heap allocations) are hard in Rust: It's because references are just plain hard to use safely. Rust tries to make the gnarly things awkward, but what is the easy path? If you look at the Hylo (formerly Val as of 3 days ago) language [0] that folks like Dave Abrahams have been working on, they're outright saying to just not use reference semantics if you can avoid it. In this talk at CppCon about Value Semantics, Dave argues that we should be decoupling object graphs from access to objects [1]. That's right folks, using `usize` indexes into collections, or adjacency lists, isn't just a hack to get around the borrow checker as people like Jonathan Blow have famously critiqued [2], it may just be the right way of doing things . [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31856501,"author":"anonymoushn","month":"2022-06","story":"How safe is Zig?","stance":"reference","why":"Circumventing the borrow checker brings back the bugs it prevents.","quote":"it sounds like you are circumventing the borrow checker and then experiencing some of the classes of bugs it was supposed to prevent.","text":"Right, it sounds like you are circumventing the borrow checker and then experiencing some of the classes of bugs it was supposed to prevent. And this seems common: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29197551,"author":"arkush","month":"2021-11","story":"The Rust compiler has gotten faster again","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The hvasilev's comment got flagged and I could not reply to it anymore, so I'll reply here (sorry) and copy-paste the hvasilev's comment verbatim below, for the sake of commenting on it's claims: --- Reality is not on the side of this language. 11 year old, has a very low adoption with virtually no jobs associated. ( [LINK] ) On the other hand if you search for \"Rust\" in the latest \"Who wants to be hired?\" thread, you will see it is quite popular with unemployed people. The reality is that the language has a lot of friction, the ergonomics are bad, the syntax is heavy and some poor decision making has been made there for a systems-level programming language. There are a lot of ideological traps in this industry and many people that fall for them. Why people are interested in ideologies and cults is beyond me. [ A screenshot: [LINK] ] --- <rant> Now my comments on the issues mentioned in the hvasilev's comment. > Reality is not on the side of this language. 11 year old, has a very low adoption with virtually no jobs associated. ( [LINK] ) Tiobe index is shit. The most flattering thing I've read about it states that it (poorly) depicts quantity of educational materials available online for particular programming language. Unfortunate naming of programming languages after letters of alphabet, symbols (++, #) and real-life stuff (like islands) doesn't help this rating either. That said, Rust isn't that popular and isn't growing much according to other better language ratings: 1. "},{"id":28868904,"author":"modernerd","month":"2021-10","story":"Hands-On Rust: Effective Learning Through 2D Game Development and Play","stance":"reference","why":"Blow on friction and complexity for indie developers.","quote":"Even 10% friction more than I ever had would have killed me.","text":"On why ECS gets in the way for him: > Because it is far more complicated, thus takes far more work, than what you actually need to do. That work has a large opportunity cost. [LINK] To me this has more nuance than, “you ain't gonna need it”. I don't think “concrete implementations” would do all that much to strengthen his argument that unnecessary complexity gets in the way of shipping for indie devs: > Even 10% friction more than I ever had would have killed me. I wouldn't have been able to make the things I had. Even 5% more friction would have been really bad. [VIDEO] And… > I have, several times, built games where I barely managed to finish. … I've just experienced that too many times to increase friction. I need to decrease friction. [VIDEO] And again, in relation to entities rather than Rust's borrow checker: > If you are trying to focus on the way your entities are set up, you are mis-directing your effort and that's going to make it harder. Try to solve the problem that makes your game interesting. What is it about the gameplay that makes it interesting … that users can see? Focus on that, solve those problems. [LINK] On what he uses instead of ECS/components in his engines: > One struct per entity type, with a base struct that is common to all of them. [LINK]"},{"id":26088398,"author":"hectorlorenzo","month":"2021-02","story":"Are we game yet? – A guide to the Rust game development ecosystem","stance":"reference","why":"Counter-rant to a Rust game development talk.","quote":"Counter-rant from Jonathan Blow","text":"I just recently came across these two videos. Using Rust For Game Development by Catherine West: [LINK] Counter-rant from Jonathan Blow: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22222469,"author":"_pmf_","month":"2020-02","story":"Jonathan Blow on Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Indexes amount to manual memory management circumventing the borrow checker.","quote":"Jon Blow addresses this in some earlier rant here","text":"Jon Blow addresses this in some earlier rant here: [VIDEO] His point being that now you're effectively implementing a form of manual memory management to circumvent the borrow checker."},{"id":19170737,"author":"platz","month":"2019-02","story":"Entity–component–system (ECS) back and forth","stance":"reference","why":"Blow's response noting synchronization is still needed for array allocations.","quote":"One of his points is that you still have to solve synchronization for allocations and deletions from the arrays.","text":"And the response by Jonathan blow [VIDEO] One of his points is that you still have to solve synchronization for allocations and deletions from the arrays."},{"id":18190389,"author":"wokwokwok","month":"2018-10","story":"Rust has a static “garbage collector”","stance":"reference","why":"Jonathan Blow's video on indexes versus the borrow checker.","quote":"This was extensively discussed on reddit and a few other places","text":"This was extensively discussed on reddit and a few other places (not HN afaik, or I couldn't find the thread), after the video^ Jonathan Blow posted on the topic. I think the tldr; is, opinions vary. Using indexes manually doesn't by-pass the borrow checker, its just a different, manual, memory allocation and management strategy. It preserves memory-safety, but, it's questionable if you're better or worse off in terms of correctness of application logic when you use it. ...you're probably better off using the borrow checker (that's why it exists) or an abstraction in a crate to deal with this sort of problem, regardless of whatever implementation strategy it uses internally (unsafe, this, etc). [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17994398,"author":"kbwt","month":"2018-09","story":"You can’t “turn off the borrow checker” in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Blow argues manual memory management effectively turns off the borrow checker.","quote":"Jonathan Blow's video about how doing your own memory management is effectively turning off the borrow checker","text":"I thought this was going to be a response to Jonathan Blow's video about how doing your own memory management is effectively turning off the borrow checker: [VIDEO] The takeaway being that the borrow checker doesn't magically prevent the use-after-free class of bugs. Although you will never experience a segmentation fault in safe Rust, the bug is still there and your program keeps running in an invalid state. The symptoms are changed, but no less dangerous. To make the problem even more obvious, think of allocating a large array to be used as a heap and handing out indices to implement your own malloc. You have bounds checking to prevent indexing outside the bounds of the heap, but it doesn't really help when the elements have logically different lifetimes and occupy different parts of the array. I don't think this is a contrived example either. A less obvious version of this can easily creep into large or complex systems, as evidenced by the Entity Component System in Rust example."}],"r":[["bvlzZnhZhrc",1],["e08kOj2kISU",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["j6ow-UemzBc",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1],["l4dCJJFuMsE",1]]},"IYO3tOqDISE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a quick video version of the classic essay on specialization and economic interdependence.","q":{"x":"It's really about specialization and the interdependency of everything in the economy","i":22329650,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24494721,"author":"pieno","month":"2020-09","story":"On Modern Web Applications Stability","stance":"rec","why":"Short movie on the collaboration needed to produce a pencil.","quote":"(6:33 in total; it’s a bit “dreamy” sometimes but still worth a watch, you can skip the first minute)","text":"Reminds me of this movie illustration the complexity and human collaboration required to produce a pencil: [VIDEO] (6:33 in total; it’s a bit “dreamy” sometimes but still worth a watch, you can skip the first minute). Basically entire human civilization is based on collaboration, learning from and relying on each other (among other things). The process kickstarted with developments in agriculture, freeing up Human Resources to work on other things. We’ve been perfecting this for a few thousand years, of which we’ve been automating things using software for only 50 years (being generous here). Give it some time - with the current rate of development we may not need another thousand years to improve software development to near perfection as well ;-) (Or more pessimistically: there’s still a lot of hunger in this world so even after a few thousand years we’ve not even perfected the most basic of human needs - doesn’t bode well for something as fancy as the typical glorified beans counter that is most enterprise software...) Disclaimer: This video is typically used as a pro free markets argument to illustrate the level of cooperation required to produce even the simplest things, arguing that such collaboration is only possible by free actors in a free market - that’s not the point here but still interesting tangent to think about in this context."},{"id":22329650,"author":"CWuestefeld","month":"2020-02","story":"Brown: color is weird [video]","stance":"rec","why":"About specialization and interdependency in making a pencil.","quote":"It's really about specialization and the interdependency of everything in the economy","text":"Or, how to make a pencil. [VIDEO] (not the same guy at all, but your comment reminded me of this. It's really about specialization and the interdependency of everything in the economy)"},{"id":13933204,"author":"flyingfences","month":"2017-03","story":"Most items of clothing have complicated international journeys","stance":"rec","why":"Video adaptation of I, Pencil for those who do not want to read.","quote":"There's even a video adaptation available for those who don't want to read at all.","text":"For those looking for an even shorter read, Leonard Read's I, Pencil gets the basic point across pretty quickly. There's even a video adaptation available for those who don't want to read at all. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29119209,"author":"syshum","month":"2021-11","story":"World food prices reach new peak since July 2011","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">>Okay, but presumably a smart government would decide that all jobs involved in the production and transportation of foodstuffs are essential. I know my country's government did. That is the folly of this way of thinking, all jobs are interlinked, you can not just extract jobs from the market and not have an impact. The person that cooks breakfast for the truck driver that picks up the food from the warehouse that stored the food that came from the factory that made the food from ingredients from a farm field is just as \"essential\" as ever other person in the chain. As is the IT worker that works on a helpdesk for the credit card processor that service the restaurant that truck driver above stops at every morning As the manufacture of the monitor stand that the IT worker uses to hold up their monitor to enable them to service the Restaurant of the truck driver... and on and on we go down the chain.. Look up I, Pencil[1] for a better understanding of this Government just pulling out people they claim are \"not essential\" to food production or other essential things is foolish because all jobs are essential. Period. non-essential jobs do not exist, and any government claiming there is, and claiming they are smart enough to make that determination should never be trusted with anything. >I haven't seen any evidence that anyone tried to implement Central Planning The very nature of choosing who can work, who cant, what is jobs are essential, what jobs are not is Central planning ["},{"id":21847413,"author":"eddieh","month":"2019-12","story":"Adam Smith and the Pin Factory","stance":"reference","why":"2012 CEI movie version of I, Pencil.","quote":"And in 2012 CEI made a movie version","text":"It should remind you of I, Pencil since it is essentially the modern telling of Smith's pin factory. Milton Friedman described the pencil in his Free to Choose series: [LINK] And in 2012 CEI made a movie version: [VIDEO] I know the rules here are such that we don't talk about voting up/down, but I have no idea why you were voted down. Your post is on-topic and informative. I didn't know their was a book version or the CEI movie version, I had only watched the Free to Choose series. Thanks for sharing."}]},"RzWB5jL5RX0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a good starting point on practical Enigma cryptanalysis with modern hardware.","q":{"x":"would be a good place to start.","i":28466834,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":40268609,"author":"H8crilA","month":"2024-05","story":"Interview with Marian Rejewski, the first person to crack Enigma (1974) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Practical ciphertext-only cryptanalysis of Enigma","quote":"made a good video on practical cryptanalysis of the Enigma using modern hardware","text":"AFAIK the Germans kept improving both the Enigma itself and the procedures for using it. For example there was some specific weakness that the Polish attacks used, but at some point Germans figured it out and \"patched\" it. By the end of the war they could change the \"encryption key\" even more often than once a day, whereas before the war they could reuse the same key for many months. The Poles didn't break all Enigma setups till 1939 because one didn't have to it yet to effectively collect intelligence. Also because they hadn't existed yet :) Enigma was actually pretty good, but it had a major design flaw: insufficient diffusion. The YouTube channel Computerphile made a good video on practical cryptanalysis of the Enigma using modern hardware: [VIDEO] . In short, if you know what you're doing you can do ciphertext-only attacks given long enough messages and minimal knowledge about the distribution of the plaintext (their example relies only on the fact that the plaintext is in English)."},{"id":31184045,"author":"gregsadetsky","month":"2022-04","story":"How did the Enigma Machine work? (2021) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Feasibility of cracking Enigma with modern compute","quote":"This is also a good companion video","text":"Super interesting, thanks! This is also a good companion video -- [VIDEO] -- about the feasibility of cracking Enigma \"in 2021\" i.e. using computational power available to us today. TLDW & AFAIK: it's crackable (just as it was back then, using a bag of approaches & heuristics), but you still couldn't plain brute force an encrypted message today if you didn't have any portion of the plain text, the Enigma was using 10 plugboard pairs, etc. Also see [LINK]"},{"id":28466834,"author":"whiskeytuesday","month":"2021-09","story":"The Scandalous History of the Last Rotor Cipher Machine","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on cracking Enigma","quote":"would be a good place to start.","text":"I would think that the recent computerphile video \"Cracking Enigma In 2021\" would be a good place to start. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36485405,"author":"aquova","month":"2023-06","story":"P1030680: Unbroken Enigma message (U534, 01 May 1945)","stance":"reference","why":"Enigma can be cracked quickly on a modern computer","quote":"With some clever tricks, and the advantage of hindsight, it can be done reasonably quickly on a modern computer.","text":"An episode of Computerphile attempted to answer the question - [VIDEO] . TL;DW: With some clever tricks, and the advantage of hindsight, it can be done reasonably quickly on a modern computer."}],"r":[["_3loq22TxSc",1],["8--5LwHRhjk",1],["C7JQ7Rpwn2k",1],["MQufJBVvAtY",1],["2tdiKTSdE9Y",1],["wGt4XwBbCvA",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1]]},"imhUmLtlZpw":{"wy":"HN recommends it in discussions of China's surveillance and social-credit systems for explaining Chinese attitudes toward the state.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend Martin Jacques' Ted talk [0] on this very subject from over 7 years ago.","i":17576890,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18996522,"author":"abhiminator","month":"2019-01","story":"For now at least, China's citizens are embracing social-credit systems","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how Chinese view the state as part of the family","quote":"I highly recommend watching Martin Jacques' TED talk [0] on this subject.","text":"Nice article. I highly recommend watching Martin Jacques' TED talk [0] on this subject. It's interesting how people in China view the State as an intimate part of their family instead of something of an invasion, in contrast to societies in the West; which, I suspect, may be because of their collectivist roots. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17576890,"author":"abhiminator","month":"2018-07","story":"Inside China’s surveillance state","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why Chinese see a strong state as central to national progress","quote":"I highly recommend Martin Jacques' Ted talk [0] on this very subject from over 7 years ago.","text":"\"China starts from a different point of view — that a strong empowered state is necessary, in order to drag the nation forward.\" I highly recommend Martin Jacques' Ted talk [0] on this very subject from over 7 years ago. I believe this aspect of Chinese culture -- of treating the state as being a BIG part of your daily life -- is super important to grasp if one wishes to get a sense of Chinese thinking and way-of-life. It's also a starting point if one wishes to imagine how a future China might turn out to be. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19234039,"author":"PakG1","month":"2019-02","story":"China’s surveillance state has created at least four billionaires","stance":"reference","why":"Cited loosely in a discussion of cultural worldview differences","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure we actually disagree on many points. We do disagree on some points. For example, what you view as an abusive relationship, I think is more simply a completely different worldview as to what are considered acceptable norms in society. But it's easy for you to look at the relationship and think it's abusive because of your foundational worldview about what is normal and what is OK, especially considering the likely difference between power distance index values between yourself and Chinese culture. edit: This reminds me of a business case about cross-cultural work I had to learn for my MBA. It is amazing how much a real-life company failed in China only simply because lack of cultural understanding led to immense distrust between the two parties, even though both parties really actually wanted to work with each other. But their cultural ignorance could only lead them to both believe that they couldn't trust each other, as they were completely misunderstanding each other's messages and signals. I can see that happening in general when analyzing another person's society. The point of the case was that cultural misunderstanding tends to lead to negative assumptions, rather than benefit of the doubt. :endedit I am definitely mostly saying that I've learned that the world has a lot more nuance than I realized when I was younger, and that we'd all do well if we realized that. It allows for better dialogue among people, especially with those who are different from us. For"},{"id":18817065,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-01","story":"China lands Chang'e-4 on far side of Moon","stance":"reference","why":"China sees itself as a civilization state","quote":"The People's Republic of China doesn't think of itself as a \"Nation State.","text":"The legend \"Chang'e flies to the moon\" (嫦娥奔月) lies behind the Mid-Autumn Festival (featuring mooncakes, as it were). Point is, the names of Chinese space missions are every bit as carefully steeped in tradition as, say, Mercury/Gemini/Apollo. The People's Republic of China doesn't think of itself as a \"Nation State.\" Instead, it thinks of itself as a \"civilization state.\" A part of PRC's self appointed charge is to prevent the marginalization of Chinese civilization. [VIDEO] When I was working in Houston, it was a regular occurrence for me to meet yet another Chinese born employee of whatever corporation I was in, who would soon after ask me if I could read Chinese characters, then say it was a \"shame\" I couldn't. I'm not even Chinese, so I find this a bit parochial. I'm aware of the importance of Chinese characters in the culture of East Asia, but still, there's something a bit too self important about it. Now, many years later, I'm married to a Chinese woman. Even she gives me this guff! It's like they're trying to match the attitude level of the French. (EDIT: If you know of something wonderful more people should know about, most of the time, you should try to share it as a direct experience. That way, you're more likely to get across the magic and you're less likely to come across as a lecture.)"},{"id":16216902,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-01","story":"In-N-Out managers earn triple the industry average","stance":"reference","why":"Frames China as a civilization-state rather than a nation-state","quote":"","text":"I would have left my wife standing on the porch When you'd made an arrangement to meet them at a certain time? Man, to me that's just cold. your wife no doubt picked up from China’s strong nationality One thing that I picked up from dating then marrying my wife, is that China is waaaay more diverse and varied within its borders than most people in the US imagine. The basic philosophy towards friendship between the north and south varies, and even that is an oversimplification. One province over, the dining habits are waaay different from where she grew up. She comes from a Mandarin speaking region in the south, where before I'd heard that the south speaks Cantonese, but even there it's more complex than I'd thought. Turns out the real traditional languages are two entirely different dialects I'd never even heard of. China's not so much a nation-state, as a \"civilization-state.\" [VIDEO] and authoritarian paradigm. Maybe there's something to that."}]},"uGlzRt-FYto":{"wy":"Cited in debates over Elm's direction and open source health, with some praising its points about metrics and others finding its tone toward unhappy users hard to watch.","q":{"x":"he raises some interesting points about how measuring projects by Github activity is strongly biased","i":17845990,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19234672,"author":"ssivark","month":"2019-02","story":"Ask HN: What do productive people do on weekends?","stance":"rec","why":"Food for thought on what success means","quote":"I really like the talk by Evan Czaplicki (Elm creator/designer) on","text":"Agreed; I think it's important to realize that a naive bias for action/productivity is not always the best thing. It's imperative to cultivate a good sense of what is worth doing, otherwise one will just produce a lot that will be irrelevant at best or garbage at worst. There are many \"productive\" people, but a question worth asking is how many of those actions result in lasting value, however you choose to qualify that. A lot of competent and productive people leave lasting negative value, due to misdirected efforts. For some definitions, spending a weekend building sand castles with kids might have more lasting value than working on a coding project. Don't let more visible metrics of productivity (eg: number of Github projects, HN points, etc.) cloud your judgement. If you want to step back and think about this matter (one productive use of the weekend ;-)), I really like the talk by Evan Czaplicki (Elm creator/designer) on \"What is success?\" which might provide some food for thought [1]. To clarify, doing only things that are worth doing doesn't imply holding back from producing/practicing because you're not yet good enough. I was referring more to the direction than the quantum in that direction. See what Ira Glass said about taste and practice [2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":17845990,"author":"ssivark","month":"2018-08","story":"Methodologies for measuring open source project health","stance":"rec","why":"Measuring project success beyond GitHub activity","quote":"he raises some interesting points about how measuring projects by Github activity is strongly biased","text":"Interesting observation by Aaron Swartz. That could happen because the barrier to entry for adding to a text blurb is much lower than that for contributing to a code base — maybe because we practice the former for a decade in school. For code, ease of access (edit ability) is non-obvious and therefore, an important axis along which to evaluate open source projects. So, funnily enough, I agree with you because the data you quoted for Wikipedia is much less likely to apply to code :-) Btw, I recently came across a talk by Evan Czaplicki (creator of Elm lang) on “What is success?” for an open source project like Elm, and he raises some interesting points about how measuring projects by Github activity is strongly biased by a model of how the larger Javascript community works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17600824,"author":"antouank","month":"2018-07","story":"Our Use of Haskell and Elm","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Elm's release pace and priorities","quote":"Maybe you should watch Evan's talks from various Elm conferences","text":"Why is the pace of releases an important metric for using a programming language? Focus on code quality, and maintenance simplicity. If anything, being able to use the same version for x years and having it be consistently better than any JS library/framework I used, means that the work done is of high quality. Maybe you should watch Evan's talks from various Elm conferences ( search on youtube, for example [VIDEO] ) He addresses that concern that some people have, and why it has the pace it has. As I understand it, it's better to do things right and take as long as it needs, rather than hurry and ship fancy things just to ship them. Elm is perfectly production ready as is, and beats any JS stack for me. Both in code quality and maintenance cost."},{"id":21398396,"author":"dragonsh","month":"2019-10","story":"Thank You, Guido","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about building a humble, welcoming language community","quote":"I recently watched a talk by creator of Elm language","text":"Language, its community and development model is a reflection of its creator and his philosophy. So Without meeting the creator Guido, through language and its community I can see is very humble and care about things besides solving computer science problems. May be this is one of the reasons of success of Python and its community which is open, humble, friendly and inclusive. In my startup we have been using Python as one of the primary development language, and thanks to its community and Guido, we will continue to do so in time to come. I recently watched a talk by creator of Elm language [1] [2] who is trying to create similar community like Python, I will try it besides lisp, since I like the ethos of Python like community and obviously PEP-20 [3]. From my whole team thanks to Guido, and Python community. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":18900519,"author":"cttet","month":"2019-01","story":"IBM releases Elm-powered app","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in Elm ports debate","quote":"","text":"> You're saying a codebase of 5% JavaScript demands more JavaScript experience than a codebase of 100% JavaScript. A codebase of 100% Javascript does not usually mean 100% written by the app developer. If you are coding in react for some basic app, like some basic crud with react using existing backend with Firebase, it almost only require you to learn the some basics of react and Javascript syntax, and fill in the template. You can also use other modules by just follow their documentation when you need some extra functionality. However in Elm Ports we are required to pass async messages for everything which is much harder. I am OK with the boilerplate for the JSON encoder and stuff, but I have to wrap around my head for how to write a port for lots of basic web APIs. Port is hard even for people that familiar with Javascript. There are plenty of experienced Javascript programmers willing to dive into the source code of Elm to write native modules to avoid ports even. [LINK] In addition, even the 5% of Javascript will eventually leads to the full Javascript stack, where Elm, without an official recommend JS stack, feels more like additional choice as a part of the JS fatigue. Beginners ends up looking up on browserfy/gulp/webpack etc and figuring out a way to integrate Elm in. > Seems like the argument is Elm isn't ideal if you're not a programmer I program daily for machine learning, and I have used many programming languages. But Javascript is not the language that I want t"},{"id":34746997,"author":"PragmaticPulp","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: What Happened to Elm?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Evan doubled down by dedicating the opening of his next conference talk to mocking the users who were unhappy about the changes","text":"> This is FUD. For any normal web app you don't need that functionality, just use the built in \"ports\" concept to interface with JS stuff. The change was handled extremely poorly. I only knew one team using Elm in production at the time. They went from being huge proponents of Elm to recommending against it after the 0.18/0.19 changes and drama upended a lot of their work. There may have been a \"right\" way to do things under the new version, but breaking major functionality in a minor release without warning is a sign that you don't really care about your users. Changes like this require discussion, warning, and long transition periods. It was bad enough that they got it wrong, but then Evan doubled down by dedicating the opening of his next conference talk to mocking the users who were unhappy about the changes: [VIDEO] The part where he mocks a commenter for asking \"Is Evan killing Elm's momentum?\" is weirdly prescient, given that these events ultimately did mark the slowdown of the Elm community. Elm development came to a near halt about a year later. > And it being open source means nothing stops you from forking it and adding your own native modules. Reason no one has done it is it's no need, and doing thinga that way would break the promises the compiler makes about no runtime bugs. I disagree. Maintaining an in-house fork of a compiler just to accomplish what could be done with past versions isn't a reasonable suggestion. The reason people weren't maintaining in-house "},{"id":34749448,"author":"richbell","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: What Happened to Elm?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Wow, that was hard to watch.","text":"> It was bad enough that they got it wrong, but then Evan doubled down by dedicating the opening of his next conference talk to mocking the users who were unhappy about the changes: [VIDEO] > The part where he mocks a commenter for asking \"Is Evan killing Elm's momentum?\" is weirdly prescient, given that these events ultimately did mark the slowdown of the Elm community. Elm development came to a near halt about a year later. Wow, that was hard to watch. He takes people being passionate about Elm and discussing potential issues and mocks them. If I was a proponent of Elm that felt uneasy about its direction at the time, this would have made it easy to walk away from."},{"id":22824899,"author":"salimmadjd","month":"2020-04","story":"Why I’m Leaving Elm","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He spent the opening part of his European talk mocking it","text":"I believe Elm took some major missteps. Despite doing so many things right with the initial designs (and still one of the best designed front-end experiences) it never grew much beyond the early adopters and it basically remained a fun hobby language. I criticized the basic lack of communication and the infrequent updates in r/elm (I believed these infrequent updates were going to kill the momentum of Elm). I was concerned that it made the language seem stagnant. Immediately, after posting it was clear it was a major concern by others as well. So what did Evan do with that criticism? He spent the opening part of his European talk mocking it [0]. I didn’t take it personally, but it illustrated the kind of mindset Evan has. A good leader would have asked questions about why many feel that way and would have instituted a process for more frequent updates. It became clear to me, outside of being brilliant at designing Elm he is not the person to take it to the next level of adoption. If it’s not moderately adopted it won’t make sense recommending it for any production project, even if it does many things right. To me it feels like (sadly) Elm will just be a hobby project despite what it could have been. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["kc9HwsxE1OY",1],["39iKLwlUqBo",1],["Qi1Yry33TQE",1]]},"xuCn8ux2gbs":{"wy":"HN commenters list it among favorite long-form, info-dense YouTube videos and as a representative piece of Wurtz's quirky creativity.","q":{"x":"history of the entire world, i guess","i":29524609,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41914569,"author":"purgedreality","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite YouTube video, ever?","stance":"rec","why":"Included in a personal list of favorite YouTube videos.","quote":"History of the entire world, I guess -","text":"Journey to the End of Time - [LINK] Veritasium on Thermite reaction - [LINK] Problems of a Shared Office - [LINK] History of the entire world, I guess - [VIDEO] Colin Furze Underground Activities - [LINK] Future of Reasoning - [LINK] Sex in Trek - [LINK]"},{"id":29524609,"author":"Isinlor","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Long-form info-dense videos on YouTube you would recommend to anyone?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among recommended long-form info-dense videos.","quote":"history of the entire world, i guess","text":"Everything from 3 blue 1 brown: [LINK] Visualization of Quantum Physics (Quantum Mechanics): [LINK] history of the entire world, i guess: [VIDEO] TIMELAPSE OF THE FUTURE: A Journey to the End of Time (4K): [LINK] Humanity was born way ahead of its time. The reason is grabby aliens. and Grabby aliens: when we'll meet them, how big they are, and other predictions [LINK] [LINK] Everyday Astronaut videos (he has 90 min documentary on Soviet rocket engine): [LINK] Cool Worlds - a lot of 25-35 min very nice astronomy explanations: [LINK] 'A Universe From Nothing' by Lawrence Krauss: [LINK]"},{"id":17203065,"author":"IanCal","month":"2018-06","story":"Ask HN: Which YouTube channels do you watch regularly?","stance":"rec","why":"Bill Wurtz ranges from absurd short clips to this history video.","quote":"Bill Wurtz - Ranging from absurd 5 second clips to, well, this","text":"smartereveryday I love (particularly the enthusiasm), numberphile too. Anything (not a channel name, just anything) by Tom Scott, his recent gameshow has been pretty good as it's like Only Connect but I've got a better chance of answering the questions. Huge backlog of things too and as he's got more popular he's got more access to interesting places and things. Just seems like a genuinely nice guy explaining things he finds interesting. Probably worth starting with \"things you might not know\" or \"amazing places\". CollegeHumor - can be hit and miss but the ratio is good enough for me. They did a series of a gameshow called \"Umm actually\" which was great. Gus Johnson - never quite sure where I am on things going too far on these but it keeps me laughing and coming back. exurb1a ClarkeAndDawe - teaching me about Australian politics, one fake interview at a time. What got me into it: [LINK] Bill Wurtz - Ranging from absurd 5 second clips to, well, this: [VIDEO] CasuallyExplained"},{"id":43620238,"author":"ben_w","month":"2025-04","story":"UK Effort to Keep Apple Encryption Fight Secret Is Blocked","stance":"reference","why":"Link appears in a long comment on encryption and secrecy.","quote":"","text":"Unfortunately, I don't see there is any healthy middle ground with even existing tech, let alone future tech. -- From the perspective of tech, secrets are mandatory and impossible: Mandatory for the functioning of identification, of logging in with a secure password that remains secure. The modern world would just stop functioning if passwords were not secure, if online orders or banking could be intercepted by criminals, and there's no way to limit encryption to \"just the people who need it\", because that's approximately everyone on approximately all economically relevant websites. Impossible, because surveillance tech is already powerful, and also improving so fast. Drones with telephoto lenses to watch you type your password, or duplicate every key on your physical keyring. WIFI used as wall-penetrating radar. Laser microphones to hear your conversation. Side-channel attacks from Van Eck phreaking onwards. The attacks are increasingly affordable, I have to assume at this point that organised crime uses them. (For future tech, I think we're only a few years from \"smart dust\" that's actually dust-sized). -- From politics, it's no better: We all know about our own secrets, the importance of keeping them. Many of us are familiar with the lessons of history, where governments use secret police to engage in covert ops against a political, ideological, or social opponents and dissidents — even the term \"secret police\" is a shorthand for authoritarian and totalitarian regimes. And"},{"id":32636767,"author":"adhesive_wombat","month":"2022-08","story":"How Can We Create a Manual for Civilization? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote linking the video for the river valley landowner point.","quote":"","text":"Presumably it caused some kind of problem back then too, so that's why they put it in. Also, it's bad for the guy who owns all the land in that sweet dank river valley near you[1] if the people who do the work on that land start to covet his things in particular. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38420720,"author":"marci","month":"2023-11","story":"MonadGPT – What would have happened if ChatGPT was invented in the 17th century?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"History of the human race, i guess","text":"It's an LLM, \"pretending\" and \"truly beleiving\" are the same, or rather don't exist. It's like, when you use prompts like : \"You're an helpful assistant\", is it believing, pretending, or beleiving to be pretending to be an helpful assistant? It's as funny as disconcerting to see intent and will attributed to probabiltities. Feels sometimes like we're close to making a religion out of this. History of the human race, i guess ( [VIDEO] for the ref)."},{"id":37790215,"author":"Tuna-Fish","month":"2023-10","story":"A prehistoric cosmic airburst preceded the advent of agriculture in the Levant","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"my first thought was Bill Wurtz saying \"you could make a religion out of this","text":"Funny, my first thought was Bill Wurtz saying \"you could make a religion out of this\"[1], and my second thought was that if it actually happened, they probably made a whole bunch of religions about it. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uD4izuDMUQA",2],["_ArVh3Cj9rw",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["oAVjF_7ensg",1],["p7bzE1E5PMY",1],["l3whaviTqqg",1],["7ImvlS8PLIo",1]]},"McLq1hEq3UY":{"wy":"HN recommends Boyd's lectures as a fantastic, often funny follow-up to linear algebra and as a step in machine learning study paths.","q":{"x":"These lectures are fantastic after you've mastered the basics of linear algebra","i":31422240,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31422240,"author":"natly","month":"2022-05","story":"The big six matrix factorizations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic, often funny convex optimization lectures by an experienced lecturer.","quote":"These lectures are fantastic after you've mastered the basics of linear algebra","text":"These lectures are fantastic after you've mastered the basics of linear algebra [VIDEO] (convex optimization, by a very experienced and often funny lecturer)"},{"id":29972998,"author":"hwers","month":"2022-01","story":"Matrix Multiplication","stance":"rec","why":"Boyd's lectures give an intuitive way to think about matrix multiplication.","quote":"Steven Boyds astounding convex optimization lectures","text":"This is my favorite way to think about it as well. I think I learned it first in one of Steven Boyds astounding convex optimization lectures [VIDEO]"},{"id":9311298,"author":"zxcvvcxz","month":"2015-04","story":"The Importance of Mathematics Courses in Computer Science Education","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A practical math topic I think all computer science (and engineering, really) students should take is convex optimization. Often a problem in engineering or computer science can be phrased as minimizing a cost function over some data and/or variable constraints. Take SVM for example, or sparse signal denoising, or template matching. And far too often, people will apply \"advanced state of the art techniques\" to solve these problems, e.g. neural networks of some exotic kind, graphical models, etc. And while these are great techniques, they need not be applied to convex problems of a reasonable size. In the examples I listed, I can almost guarantee that a knowledge of how to phrase the model as a convex optimization problem and throw it into an interior point solver is going to be much more effective than running stochastic gradient descent on some user-defined model with way too many (or too little) parameters. Because when you say a problem is convex, what you are saying is that it can be solved globally in polynomial time (usually on the order of ~20 least-squares problems of size corresponding to number of variables and constraints). There is a sophisticated convergence theory as well. And if you understand the mathematical theory, you get to use this mature technology to solve exactly a whole class of problems you wouldn't think you could. It's also worth mentioning that a lot of very hard non-convex problems can be approximated and solved well using these methods, and this"},{"id":2646280,"author":"wcsun","month":"2011-06","story":"Ask HN: How do I start with Machine Learning?","stance":"reference","why":"Convex optimization course lectures as a step in an ML learning path.","quote":"Then, you can try to finish EE364 and watch the videos.","text":"First, finish lectures by Professor Gilbert Strang. [LINK] To my memory, session notes of CS229 is good enough for understanding SVM and gaussian distributions. Also watch youtube videos. [LINK] [LINK] If you just want to use the libraries, you can stop here. If you want to know more, read chapters 1-3 of nonlinear programming by Professor Dimitri Bertsekas before convex optimization. [LINK] Then, you can try to finish EE364 and watch the videos. [LINK] [VIDEO] If you want to roll your own algorithms, you have to know some optimization tools. [LINK] And there is some statistics knowledge you have to fill in. I used these: [LINK] [LINK] R is used in the courses."}]},"zaXKQ70q4KQ":{"wy":"Recommended as one of the most accessible introductions to PCR and its history.","q":{"x":"It's one of the most accessible videos on PCR I've ever come across","i":42546649,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42546649,"author":"wenc","month":"2024-12","story":"Intolerable Genius: Berkeley's Most Controversial Nobel Laureate (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accessible intro to PCR and the history of Kary Mullis.","quote":"It's one of the most accessible videos on PCR I've ever come across","text":"Veritassium did a recent video on Kary Mullis and PCR. It's one of the most accessible videos on PCR I've ever come across (I have no background in biology), and also an interesting history of Kary Mullis. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42654440,"author":"fhe","month":"2025-01","story":"Ask HN: Books about people who did hard things","stance":"rec","why":"Short video version of the history of PCR's invention.","quote":"for a short video version of this history, see","text":"Making PCR: A Story of Biotechnology (goodreads link: [LINK] ) for a short video version of this history, see [VIDEO] from Veritasium. reasons for recommendation: - it's an example in the biological science, to complement the heavy representation of examples from computer science and entrepreneurship in this thread - the main character, Kary Mullis, is colorful and controversial. Not a likable individual, but nevertheless had traits (mostly an unreasonable obsession) that enabled him to make such a discovery - the discovery of high temperature tolerant enzymes predated Mullis' insight by some two decades, and it played a key role in making PCR practical and widely applicable. this is a pattern I have seen often in major inventions, which were made possible by prior discoveries (often decades old) which lay dormant until someone put everything together. This process of re-discovering the pieces and making connections is also where I think machine learning could be particularly helpful. In fact this is my main motivation for picking up this book (by online reviews, not a particularly well-written one)."},{"id":47900335,"author":"bahador","month":"2026-04","story":"PCR Is a (Surprisingly) Near-Optimal Technology","stance":"reference","why":"Covers taq polymerase and the origin of PCR.","quote":"veritasium did a video of about it not too long ago","text":"one of the coolest parts about the original inception was the application of taq polymerase. veritasium did a video of about it not too long ago. a friend of mine and a man whom i very much respect was mullis's lab manager at the time. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ybcvlxivscw",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["2S0k12uZR14",1]]},"zyQwAhppWj8":{"wy":"Recommended as a documentary on the hardware shortage told from the perspective of consumer-facing companies, and cited in threads on RAM prices and ownership.","q":{"x":"GN did a documentary on the situation from the perspective of consumer-facing companies.","i":48383596,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48798662,"author":"willdr","month":"2026-07","story":"It's not about physical vs. digital games, it's about ownership","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Costs driving manufacturers out and PC components out of reach.","quote":"Watch the GamersNexus video, The Collapse of Personal Computing","text":"Unfortunately the AI bubble means we are witnessing the death of PC gaming. Watch the GamersNexus video, The Collapse of Personal Computing: [VIDEO] Costs are driving manufacturers out of business. Essential components for PCs are becoming out of reach for the average consumer. You will own nothing and be happy."},{"id":48383596,"author":"brnaftr361","month":"2026-06","story":"32GB of DDR5 now costs $375 – AI shortage continues to squeeze PC building","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on the hardware market crunch from consumer-facing companies' view.","quote":"GN did a documentary on the situation from the perspective of consumer-facing companies.","text":"GN did a documentary on the situation from the perspective of consumer-facing companies. Seems pretty dire for them, and it's hard to see the long-range consequences, but the idea of consumers being priced out isn't too far out, which to me is a little alarming. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48304071,"author":"jauntywundrkind","month":"2026-05","story":"Jensen Huang Just Told Every CEO Hiding Behind AI Layoffs to Shut Up","stance":"reference","why":"Stream of Jensen Huang quotes about the PC market.","quote":"there's quite the stream of quotes from Jensen relishing in fucking over everyone except the titanscalers","text":"Watching Jensen stick his junk in it and say again and again and again the PC market is going to be crushed forever and eve and ever and him being delighted about it feels barely any better. It's way way longer a video than feels useful, but there's quite the stream of quotes from Jensen relishing in fucking over everyone except the titanscalers, with gleefully predictions it will destroy every other form of computing forever. [VIDEO]"}]},"34rdxDgpaaA":{"wy":"HN points to it as a good, detailed podcast account whenever the Goiania incident or lost radioactive sources come up.","q":{"x":"Goes into detail day by day about the events","i":34637072,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34637072,"author":"Pxtl","month":"2023-02","story":"Goiânia Accident","stance":"rec","why":"Day by day account of the event, celebrating heroes.","quote":"Goes into detail day by day about the events","text":"There's a pretty good Well There's Your Problem episode about it (dirtbag-left podcast-with-slides about industrial disasters). Goes into detail day by day about the events, including celebrating some of the heroes of the story who probably saved countless lives. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34572708,"author":"smcl","month":"2023-01","story":"Australians Scour Desert for Dangerous Radioactive Capsule Smaller Than a Penny","stance":"rec","why":"Good episode on the Goiania accident.","quote":"There’s a good Well There’s Your Problem episode about this","text":"There’s a good Well There’s Your Problem episode about this, if people would rather see a video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29147503,"author":"occz","month":"2021-11","story":"The Goiânia Accident (1987)","stance":"reference","why":"Podcast episode covering the Goiania incident.","quote":"The podcast Well There's Your Problem has an episode covering this incident","text":"The podcast Well There's Your Problem has an episode covering this incident: [VIDEO] As it turns out, when you have highly radioactive stuff in your buildings, you should probably clean that radioactive stuff out when abandoning said building."}],"r":[["I8LbkfSSR58",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["kb-m2fasdDY",1],["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["PbmJkMhmrVI",1]]},"4KuVvb9DTaU":{"wy":"Cited in MacBook keyboard and repairability threads for its clear teardown of what a replacement involves.","q":{"x":"Its eye opening seeing a keyboard replacement being performed","i":18159373,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18159373,"author":"ru999gol","month":"2018-10","story":"Apple’s repair kill switch hasn’t been activated yet","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a keyboard replacement being performed, revealing the labor involved","quote":"Its eye opening seeing a keyboard replacement being performed","text":"Apple intentionally builds their keyboards in such a way that it is incredible difficult to replace, the amount of skilled labor required doesn't make it profitable to just replace the keyboard. Its eye opening seeing a keyboard replacement being performed: [VIDEO] Who is to blame for letting it get so far? I think its not just Apples fault, its governments fault not regulating them and consumers fault for still buying and even defending their practices (which you can very well observe in this thread)."},{"id":16922810,"author":"ballenf","month":"2018-04","story":"Don’t buy the MacBook Pros even on sale, in my opinion","stance":"rec","why":"Deep teardowns and clear explanations of MacBook issues","quote":"I appreciate his deep teardowns and clear explanations","text":"Louis Rossmann has examined these issues in detail on his electronics repair YouTube channel: [VIDEO] (I disagree with the level of his vitriol against Apple, but I appreciate his deep teardowns and clear explanations. He just doesn't seem to understand how valuable the Apple software is vs. Windows to those who need or appreciate osx.)"},{"id":19503721,"author":"onepremise","month":"2019-03","story":"Appl Still Hasn’t Fixd Its MacBook Kyboad Problm","stance":"reference","why":"Covers MacBook keyboard problems and difficulty of self-repair","quote":"louis Rossman did an episode on the macbook keyboard problems","text":"louis Rossman did an episode on the macbook keyboard problems: [VIDEO] It's insane what a mac user must go through even to attempt to repair this themselves. Another reason it's important to support right to repair, [LINK] . There's also a lawsuit for the keyboard defects: [LINK]"},{"id":18158475,"author":"shawn","month":"2018-10","story":"Apple’s repair kill switch hasn’t been activated yet","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates MacBook keyboards being impossible to repair","quote":"Keyboards, on the other hand, are now impossible to repair.","text":"Keyboards, on the other hand, are now impossible to repair. [VIDEO] Interesting. I can edit my comment after it was vouched. Hmm. I could exploit this loophole. But I won't – yet – because I care about HN."},{"id":16833038,"author":"verisimilitude","month":"2018-04","story":"Apple Sued an iPhone Repair Shop Owner in Norway and Lost","stance":"reference","why":"Failing MacBook keyboards and costly top case repairs","quote":"His comments about the keyboard in the current MacBook Pros are particularly apt","text":"His comments about the keyboard in the current MacBook Pros are particularly apt: [VIDEO] So, even a person holding 1 (2, 3!) genuine Apple keyboard(s) in their hands likely still couldn't do the repair -- they'd have to get a new top case. Out of warranty, I've heard it is a $500 repair. That seems to be a direct consequence of ignoring repairability (the maintainability ship sailed long ago)."},{"id":16923321,"author":"shabble","month":"2018-04","story":"Don’t buy the MacBook Pros even on sale, in my opinion","stance":"reference","why":"Explains what a MacBook keyboard replacement involves","quote":"talks a bit about what's involved in an actual keyboard replacement","text":"If it's just a keycap (and maybe even the butterfly mechanism) that shouldn't be too bad. [VIDEO] (from about 5:20) talks a bit about what's involved in an actual keyboard replacement, if you ignore some of the histrionics. I think from what I've heard that for actual replacements, Apple will swap out the whole 'top case' subassembly, which could be a 4-500$ part if it's not covered under warranty."}],"r":[["XopSDJq6w8E",1],["-6rWqJhDv7M",1]]},"783ccP__No8":{"wy":"HN recommends it for understanding the original motivation behind GraphQL before judging whether it fits your problem.","q":{"x":"Listen to what the developers tell which problems they had to solve when they invented GraphQL.","i":31287231,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31287231,"author":"realPubkey","month":"2022-05","story":"GraphQL Is a Trap?","stance":"rec","why":"Developers describe problems that motivated GraphQL","quote":"Listen to what the developers tell which problems they had to solve when they invented GraphQL.","text":"Watch this Honeypot documentation about GraphQL. Listen to what the developers tell which problems they had to solve when they invented GraphQL. You likely do not have any of these problems. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23916121,"author":"typingmonkey","month":"2020-07","story":"GraphQL Query Generator","stance":"rec","why":"Shows problems GraphQL's creators solved, to judge fit","quote":"I recommend to watch the documentation about the facebook people that invented GraphQL","text":"I recommend to watch the documentation about the facebook people that invented GraphQL [1]. Listen careful to which problems they solved with graphql, then think about the problems you have to solve for your application. Most of the time GraphQL is not worth its tradeoffs. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21739710,"author":"namelosw","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Why GraphQL APIs but no Datalog APIs?","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on creators moving from REST to GraphQL","quote":"There's a documentary[0] for GraphQL","text":"> It is unclear that the total impact on performance would be significantly negative compared to the typical GraphQL API. Some of the problems also apply to GraphQL, people just need to constrain the query complexity in the end. But yeah, it would bite Datalog more if not designed well enough, and it's harder to tackle Datalog than to GraphQL. Other thoughts on this topic: I love GraphQL but I would love Datalog more if the toolchain is as good as GraphQL. There's a spectrum on API of simplicity and power -- on the left, there is HTTP/REST which could be seen as a primitive/naive way of describing API it's not consumer-driven at all. On the right there's Datalog which is a powerful way of describing API, it can maximize the flexibility of both the server and the client. Things like 'map' and 'filter' became well-known to average programmers not very long time ago, though the elite programmers already knew them well half a centry ago. Datalog is definitely a very good idea for API, but I'm afraid it wound't be as popular as GraphQL if Facebook chose this route instead. That being said, the industry always adopts things in a very passive manner, usually because the current solution is too painful to use, or almost couldn't solve the problem on new problem scale. There's a documentary[0] for GraphQL, the creators had enough of REST, they ended up with inventing GraphQL. It originally was to solving their data fetching problems, in the meantime it would solve others too. Meanwhil"},{"id":21364477,"author":"siscia","month":"2019-10","story":"Why Graphiti?","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter plans to watch it to understand GraphQL's rationale","quote":"","text":"So we invented a query language (GraphQL) to solve an orchestration problem? Sorry but I don't buy this reason. Especially not to the question: \"Why we don't use SQL as a query language?\" especially because REST was around way earlier than GraphQL. Your reasoning could be great to answer the question: \"Why we like GraphQL where there are a lot of microservices?\" BTW, there is nothing inherent in the language in itself, if each microservices would be speaking SQL, you would simply need to optmize your SQL query , decompose your optimized SQL query creating simpler queries one for each microservice, send each of those queries to the relative microservices, compute back the final result. If you replace \"microservices\" with \"table\" here you get the standard way an SQL engine works :) Please, it is not that I am against GraphQL, but it honestly seems like some engineer at facebook was annoyed by SQL, and decide to re-invent it. And that we are all following him or she just because they worked at facebook, which seems unwise. Maybe the use of types? But what is the difference between a type and something that belong to a table? Anyway, I am looking now at [GraphQL: The Documentary (Official Release)][1] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20476571,"author":"rwieruch","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: Is GraphQL still relevant?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for GraphQL's origin at Facebook","quote":"","text":"1) I think GraphQL is more relevant than ever. Facebook ran into the same problems like Netflix, Airbnb and Twitter and thus came up with GraphQL [0]. 2) The most popular one I know is GitHub's GraphQL API [1]. I made the difficult decision to rely on their API to teach GraphQL in my free book [2]. So far, there were no breaking changes for me and people love it to learn GraphQL from a client's perspective. 3)I would use it if I want to have no under-/overfetching, declarative data fetching, microservice API aggregation, ... [3] 4) Using it in personal projects and one client of mine is using it in one application to aggregate multiple microservices (each one speaking GraphQL) into one GraphQL API gateway. So far, everything works like expected. We didn't hit any rate limiting and caching issues because of the N+1 problem yet. But we are prepared for them :) [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"}]},"8LGDM9exlZw":{"wy":"HN points to it as a deep dive into the surveillance features of North Korea's OS and the extent of that state's digital tracking.","q":{"x":"A deep dive into the surveillance features of North Korea's operating system","i":29318626,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":31185345,"author":"MaxLeiter","month":"2022-04","story":"North Koreans are jailbreaking phones to access forbidden media","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how extensive and complex North Korea's digital tracking is.","quote":"The depths to which North Korea goes to track and monitor its citizens is a lot more complex than I thought","text":"If you found this article interesting and are curious for examples of North Korea’s extensive digital surveillance, I recommend watching “Florian Grunow, Niklaus Schiess: Lifting the Fog on Red Star OS”: [VIDEO] The depths to which North Korea goes to track and monitor its citizens is a lot more complex than I thought (and this video is from 6 years ago, so they’ve probably only improved their surveillance)"},{"id":29318442,"author":"freeflight","month":"2021-11","story":"Fun with Red Star OS","stance":"rec","why":"CCC 2015 talk on Red Star OS, relevant and entertaining.","quote":"A bit older but quite relevant and entertaining","text":"A bit older but quite relevant and entertaining; \"Florian Grunow, Niklaus Schiess: Lifting the Fog on Red Star OS\", from the 2015 Chaos Computer Congress. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29318626,"author":"pndy","month":"2021-11","story":"Fun with Red Star OS","stance":"rec","why":"More detailed deep dive into Red Star OS surveillance features.","quote":"A deep dive into the surveillance features of North Korea's operating system","text":"[VIDEO] - Florian Grunow and Niklaus Schiess, Lifting the Fog on Red Star OS - A deep dive into the surveillance features of North Korea's operating system The bit more detailed view, I'd say"},{"id":29318638,"author":"pndy","month":"2021-11","story":"Fun with Red Star OS","stance":"rec","why":"More detailed deep dive into Red Star OS surveillance features.","quote":"A deep dive into the surveillance features of North Korea's operating system","text":"Florian Grunow and Niklaus Schiess, Lifting the Fog on Red Star OS - A deep dive into the surveillance features of North Korea's operating system [1] [1] - [VIDEO] The bit more detailed view, I'd say"},{"id":23407552,"author":"DavidPeiffer","month":"2020-06","story":"German city wants to ditch Microsoft for Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Talk revealing Red Star OS file watermarking for tracking.","quote":"They appear to have a watermarking system allowing them to track how files move between people.","text":"Interesting note on Red Star OS: They appear to have a watermarking system allowing them to track how files move between people. [VIDEO] Previous discussion: [LINK]"}],"r":[["wtV5ev6813I",1],["lKQ0yaEJjok",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1]]},"CVxeuwlvf8w":{"wy":"Recommended alongside the original paper and Unix history threads as an engaging account of the typesetter hack.","q":{"x":"explained the brilliant and exiting history of this paper and its recreation, as well as the history of Phototypesetters.","i":10508925,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":40958398,"author":"jgalt212","month":"2024-07","story":"The Birth of Standard Error (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Computerphile video on the 202 typesetter jailbreak with Professor Brailsford.","quote":"staring the inimitable Professor Brailsford","text":"> As one might expect, Bell Labs didn't use the paper tape input tangentially related. The Great 202 Jailbreak - Computerphile [VIDEO] staring the inimitable Professor Brailsford."},{"id":10508925,"author":"ATsch","month":"2015-11","story":"Experience with the Mergenthaler Linotron 202 Phototypesetter (1980) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Interview about the paper's history and recreation","quote":"Computerphile interviewed one of the writers of this paper in a series of videos","text":"Computerphile interviewed one of the writers of this paper in a series of videos, who explained the brilliant and exiting history of this paper and its recreation, as well as the history of Phototypesetters. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK]"},{"id":25179358,"author":"leoc","month":"2020-11","story":"The Birth of Unix with Brian Kernighan","stance":"reference","why":"Computerphile explains the hacking of the Linotron 202 typesetter at Bell Labs.","quote":"about the hacking of the typesetting machine, a Mergenthaler Linotron 202","text":"0:16:40 For those who haven't seen it before, there's a Computerphile video [VIDEO] (related video: [LINK] ) about the hacking of the typesetting machine, a Mergenthaler Linotron 202. 0:24:31 The photo must be the one at [LINK] (probably the webpage, on Dennis Ritchie's old website, that made it famous recently)."}],"r":[["XvwNKpDUkiE",1],["a1zDuOPkMSw",1],["g2tMcMQqSbA",1]]},"E4tYpXVTjxA":{"wy":"Brought up as an early example of context-aware touch sensing and as part of Disney Research's wider interaction prototypes.","q":{"x":"Basically the idea is to make devices aware of the physical context of user interactions.","i":4403553,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":39159402,"author":"jorvi","month":"2024-01","story":"Disney unveils the HoloTile floor","stance":"rec","why":"Turns the world into a touchscreen.","quote":"Their Disney Research Hub channel is a goldmine.","text":"Disney researches / develops some really cool stuff. Their Disney Research Hub channel is a goldmine. One of my favorites is Touché. The world becomes your touchscreen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8433814,"author":"smtddr","month":"2014-10","story":"Fluid Actuators from Disney Research Make Soft, Safe Robot Arms","stance":"rec","why":"Another Disney Research project on touch interaction.","quote":"If you liked this, check out another project from Disney labs","text":"If you liked this, check out another project from Disney labs called \"Touché\" -- [VIDEO] For some reason, the girl's reactions to the 2 bzzzt-wrong sounds @ 2:55 are hilarious."},{"id":45284074,"author":"jorvi","month":"2025-09","story":"Meta Ray-Ban Display","stance":"reference","why":"Prior Disney prototype of wristband gesture sensing.","quote":"Disney Research has had a prototype on gesture detection via wristband electric sensing tech since 2012","text":"Disney is about to have a serious talk with Facebook. Disney Research has had a prototype on gesture detection via wristband electric sensing tech since 2012 : [VIDEO]"},{"id":4403553,"author":"Gigablah","month":"2012-08","story":"Show HN: A multi-touch document manipulation system","stance":"reference","why":"Swept frequency capacitive sensing gives context-aware touch.","quote":"Basically the idea is to make devices aware of the physical context of user interactions.","text":"The \"false touch\" problem is solvable: \"Swept Frequency Capacitive Sensing\" [VIDEO] Basically the idea is to make devices aware of the physical context of user interactions."},{"id":39160489,"author":"Etheryte","month":"2024-01","story":"Disney unveils the HoloTile floor","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"you can have it make a loud sound if your child puts their hand in the bowl","text":"This is really cool tech, but the first application concept is hilarious [0]. If you hook a cereal bowl up to this device, you can have it make a loud sound if your child puts their hand in the bowl as opposed to using a spoon? That's simultaneously funny and utterly dystopian. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["dw_mRaIHb-M",1]]},"IE7j1SgUpKs":{"wy":"HN people share it as a look at the custom backend and hardware behind a large sci-fi larp.","q":{"x":"Here's a video I found years ago by one of the volunteers talking about the work they put into the electronics.","i":47296693,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47296693,"author":"hifikuno","month":"2026-03","story":"The Collective Ambition Behind Odysseus, a Game-Changing Sci-Fi Larp","stance":"rec","why":"Volunteer talks about electronics work behind Odysseus","quote":"Here's a video I found years ago by one of the volunteers talking about the work they put into the electronics.","text":"I love Odysseus so much. On one hand I think it sounds amazing and would love the opportunity to play it one day. On the other, from a technical accomplishment of volunteer work it is amazing! Just the IT work alone. Here's a video I found years ago by one of the volunteers talking about the work they put into the electronics. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28359718,"author":"trynewideas","month":"2021-08","story":"Empty Epsilon: a fully open-source spaceship bridge simulator game","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on custom backend tech for Odysseus LARP","quote":"got a good talk at RopeCon","text":"There's an active forum for bridge sims that's the de facto community space for EmptyEpsilon: [LINK] There's a Discord that assembles online games run by United Stellar Navy (it's officially an role-playing community, but the EmptyEpsilon games aren't heavy on it): [LINK] They organized a few 50+ player games last year, which have some videos on YouTube. A bit chaotic without context, but if you want to see the upper scope of gameplay: [LINK] EmptyEpsilon also has traction as a LARP platform, thanks in parts to being pretty straightforward to modify in source, using Lua to script scenarios, and having a rudimentary optional HTTP API to trigger scenario events, manipulate game state, and control player stations. A Finnish group used EE in their large 2019 LARP event Odysseus: [LINK] The custom backend for the tech, which included several systems and physical interfaces outside of EE that interfaced with the game, got a good talk at RopeCon: [VIDEO] A French LARP scene has an active fork with features more geared toward that usage: [LINK]"},{"id":34986853,"author":"starkparker","month":"2023-03","story":"Thorium – A Starship Bridge Simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Describes Odysseus LARP hardware puzzles representing ship systems","quote":"Odysseus LARP paired that functionality with a bunch of RPi Zero W-powered hardware puzzles to represent ship systems,[1]","text":"EmptyEpsilon has support for a bunch of DMX flavors as well as Hue lights and custom control surfaces, as well as an optional exposable API that can run Lua scripts on game state. Odysseus LARP paired that functionality with a bunch of RPi Zero W-powered hardware puzzles to represent ship systems,[1] so Engineers had to physically tinker with things in order to get systems back up and running after they broke. That included things like going through crawlspaces and ladders to access certain components, digging through a printed manual when computer systems were \"down\". 1: [VIDEO]"}]},"LNjJTgXujno":{"wy":"HN points to it as a source for Norvig's 'unreasonable effectiveness of data' argument and for his views on image search and the semantic web.","q":{"x":"the startup school video that quote comes from is worth watching","i":8510953,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8510953,"author":"brandonb","month":"2014-10","story":"Ask HN: Is the semantic web still a thing?","stance":"rec","why":"Source of Norvig's semantic web quote; worth watching.","quote":"the startup school video that quote comes from is worth watching","text":"Peter Norvig put it best: \"The semantic web is the future of the web, and always will be.\" (For what it's worth, the startup school video that quote comes from is worth watching: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":2808897,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2011-07","story":"Ask HN: What are the best videos to watch about building startups?","stance":"rec","why":"One entry in a list of startup talks.","quote":"- (Peter Norvig at Startup School '08)","text":"[LINK] - (Steve Blank / Eric Ries - part 1) [LINK] - (Steve Blank / Eric Ries - part 2) [LINK] - (Steve Blank / Eric Ries - part 3) [LINK] - (Steve Blank at the Startup Lessons Learned conference) [LINK] - (Steve Blank: The Democratization of Entrepreneurship) [LINK] - (Alexander Osterwalder speaking @ Google) [LINK] - (Eric Ries speaking @ Google) [LINK] - (pg at Startup School '08) [VIDEO] - (Peter Norvig at Startup School '08)"},{"id":4706161,"author":"marshallp","month":"2012-10","story":"How Long Will Programmers Be So Well-Paid?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the idea that data beats programming.","quote":"programming is slow and data is agile, as talked about by Peter Norvig in his startup school 2008 speech","text":"Programming bubble will pop. Automated Machine learning is taking over. Even at google, Jeff Dean has recently stated that his Perceptual AI project is aimed at reducing feature engineering in their machine learning projects. In plain terms this means eliminating the need for programming in their search engines and other machine learning focused projects like google now and google goggles (the mobile app, not google glass). The only programmers left will be to manage cloud computing infrastructure and front end programmers. As things like siri and google now take off, front end programmers will also be in less demand. Microsoft, Apple, and Amazon are also increasingly focusing on machine learning. Automated analysis of text will take over the role of sql databases as demonstrated in IBM watson. In manufacturing, the role of programming also diminishing as vision guided robots like Baxter by Rodney Brooks's team take off. All this is a good thing though, programming is slow and data is agile, as talked about by Peter Norvig in his startup school 2008 speech [VIDEO] Startups should aim at doing as little programming as possible and leaving it to data crunching."},{"id":3123897,"author":"apu","month":"2011-10","story":"Peter Norvig talks how search is evolving, thanks to AI algorithms","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig goes into more detail on image search.","quote":"Norvig's talk at Startup School '08 went into more details on this","text":"Norvig's talk at Startup School '08 went into more details on this: [VIDEO] They do use SIFT (or at least a variant thereof) for finding and describing interest points, but by itself, there is no geometric matching in SIFT. There are various competing approaches on how do it, although in many cases, you can get very good results even without it. (It's very slow to do geometric matching so people often skip that step, or only apply it to the best matches.) Landmark detection is a recent \"hot topic\" in computer vision, and given a large enough dataset, it essentially works now for the most part."},{"id":533298,"author":"presty","month":"2009-03","story":"Official Google Research Blog: The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Data","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig talk on same theme of data effectiveness.","quote":"It's very Norvig-esq ( )","text":"This line of thought follows previous posts on the same subject. It's very Norvig-esq ( [VIDEO] ), but there's also [LINK] and also Chris Anderson and Wired's flame bait [LINK] (that month's wired was dedicated to this subject) And like someone at the previous discussions has said, this is the base of the scientific method, not it's death"}],"r":[["q7K0vRUKXKc",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["ZTidn2dBYbY",1],["3wMKoSRbGVs",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1],["GLQyj-kBRdo",1]]},"OWJCfOvochA":{"wy":"HN points to it both as an approachable quantum computing intro and as an example of explaining one concept at multiple levels.","q":{"x":"I found it easy to comprehend. It starts explaining to a child & works its way up to a doctorate level.","i":19163125,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":23033284,"author":"stewdio","month":"2020-04","story":"Show HN: Quantum JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Helped commenter get started learning about physical quantum computers.","quote":"These two videos in particular helped me get started","text":"I’ve begun to put together useful resources for learning more here: [LINK] I’d like to really flesh out that page; I’ve now come across so many folks that are amazing at explaining this stuff... It’s just a time crunch to juggle all this as you might imagine ;) These two videos in particular helped me get started: 1. What is a physical quantum computer? [VIDEO] 2. How might one compute using it? [LINK]"},{"id":19163125,"author":"mattferderer","month":"2019-02","story":"Quantum Computing, Capabilities and Limits: An Interview with Scott Aaronson","stance":"rec","why":"Explains quantum computing from child to doctorate level.","quote":"I found it easy to comprehend. It starts explaining to a child & works its way up to a doctorate level.","text":"Try this video - [VIDEO] I found it easy to comprehend. It starts explaining to a child & works its way up to a doctorate level."},{"id":29248466,"author":"Jun8","month":"2021-11","story":"Scott Aaronson, when reached for comment, said","stance":"reference","why":"Still left the commenter with no understanding of quantum computers.","quote":"even after watching this video","text":"Here are few thoughts on Aaranson's comments: 1) I have absolutely zero understanding of quantum computers (even after watching this video: [VIDEO] ), though I like to read up on quantum physics. However, it seems to me that I've lived through many \"breakthroughs\" in this technology. For example, here's an excerpt from a NYT article from 4/28/1998: [LINK] : The discovery has touched off a wave of excitement among physicists and computer scientists and is leading dozens of research centers worldwide to embark on similar experiments heralding the advent of an era of so-called quantum computers, specialized machines that may one day prove thousands or even millions of times faster than today's most powerful supercomputers. It seems quantum computers that can kick ass is always 10-15 years away. 2) Here's a writeup from Pano Kanelos, President of the University of Austin of University of Austin, where he explains the motivation: [LINK] . Others can debate the anti-wokeness, but this part is pretty much fact: Over the last three decades, the cost of a degree from a four-year private college has nearly doubled; the cost of a degree from a public university has nearly tripled. Colleges in the US are insanely expensive! If the new UoA starts fixing that I'm all of it, but I'll believe it when I see their tuition rates and first batch of graduates. This is the first question listed here (along with the high cost of healthcare in the US): [LINK] 3) The blog post he mentions is interest"},{"id":21418482,"author":"mattmcknight","month":"2019-11","story":"Stop Explaining Black Box Models for High Stakes Decisions","stance":"reference","why":"Example of explaining a concept at multiple levels.","quote":"The series of videos from Wired comes to mind where a concept is explained at multiple levels.","text":"\"sparsity is a useful measure of interpretability, since humans can handle at most 7±2 cognitive entities at once \" If you are going to limit models to 9 features, or 9 combinations of features, or 9 rules, models are not going to work as well. This just seems like a very weak argument to introduce. \"A black box model is either a function that is too complicated for any human to comprehend, or a function that is proprietary\" These seem like two very different things. A false equivalence is then made with many arguments against proprietary models being made as if they apply to complex models. \"It is a myth that there is necessarily a trade-off between accuracy and interpretability....However, this is often not true\" It's a bit of a straw man to suggest that there is \"necessarily\" a trade-off, as there are surely cases where there is not. One could say that there is \"often\" a trade-off between accuracy and interpretability. I don't think there are many people out there with the naive view that one should never do feature engineering. \"If the explanation was completely faithful to what the original model computes, the explanation would equal the original model\" This is just nonsensical to me. The idea is not to be completely faithful, but to raise things up to another level of abstraction. The series of videos from Wired comes to mind where a concept is explained at multiple levels. [VIDEO] \"Black box models are often not compatible in situations where information outside the da"},{"id":18806029,"author":"brownbat","month":"2019-01","story":"The Tsetlin Machine outperforms neural networks","stance":"reference","why":"Same concept explained at five difficulty levels.","quote":"Here's an example of someone explaining something to five different levels of audiences.","text":"What constitutes reasonable? Here's an example of someone explaining something to five different levels of audiences. [VIDEO] None of these approaches are prima facie \"unreasonable.\" But if you get the wrong level it won't feel useful. I don't think it's crazy to have open teaching discussions on HN, I think you lay out a fair hope that someone can break something down here. But I'm just not sure which level you're looking for. Precision and simplicity are often competing constraints."},{"id":29247698,"author":"vilhelm_s","month":"2021-11","story":"IBM unveils 127-qubit quantum processor","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"seems to have an old quantum computer as a piece of office decoration","text":"One of the IBM scientists seems to have an old quantum computer as a piece of office decoration, so I guess there's some hope to preserve them: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["F_Riqjdh2oM",1]]},"QUcTsFe1PVs":{"wy":"HN recommends it to fans of ancient music as a rare chance to hear an attempt at 4,000-year-old song.","q":{"x":"If you like this video, you will probably also like Peter Pringle's The Epic Of Gilgamesh In Sumerian","i":40245440,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40237842,"author":"danans","month":"2024-05","story":"How did Ancient Greek music sound?","stance":"rec","why":"Gilgamesh sung with a reconstructed oud.","quote":"If you like this kind of stuff, you should listen to Peter Pringle singing the Epic of Gilgamesh while playing a reconstructed oud.","text":"If you like this kind of stuff, you should listen to Peter Pringle singing the Epic of Gilgamesh while playing a reconstructed oud. While the melodies/rhythms he uses are his creation, the words are from the epic itself which was itself 2000 years old by the time of Ancient Greece. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40245440,"author":"ZeljkoS","month":"2024-05","story":"How did Ancient Greek music sound?","stance":"rec","why":"Fans of ancient music will like it.","quote":"If you like this video, you will probably also like Peter Pringle's The Epic Of Gilgamesh In Sumerian","text":"If you like this video, you will probably also like Peter Pringle's The Epic Of Gilgamesh In Sumerian: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32637442,"author":"kibwen","month":"2022-08","story":"Hear the World’s Oldest Known Song, “Hurrian Hymn No. 6” Written 3400 Years Ago","stance":"rec","why":"Opening lines of Gilgamesh sung with reconstructed Sumerian instrument.","quote":"Peter Pringle singing the opening lines of The Epic of Gilgamesh (written 4,000 years ago)","text":"In a somewhat similar vein, here's Peter Pringle singing the opening lines of The Epic of Gilgamesh (written 4,000 years ago), using our best guess at Sumerian pronunciation and playing a modern recreation of an instrument that the Sumerians would have had, as an attempt to guess what ancient Sumerian music might have sounded like: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46318988,"author":"ZeWaka","month":"2025-12","story":"Classical statues were not painted horribly","stance":"reference","why":"Pringle researches Sumerian music and makes replica instruments.","quote":"Peter Pringle, who researches Sumerian music and even makes replica instruments for the songs","text":"Hijacking this to point out Peter Pringle, who researches Sumerian music and even makes replica instruments for the songs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29084312,"author":"forgotpwd16","month":"2021-11","story":"How to Read “Gilgamesh” (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier popular video of Pringle singing an excerpt.","quote":"a continuation to a very popular video made years ago where he sung an excerpt","text":"Here's the first tablet in original language sung by Peter Pringle (with help from prof. A. R. George for pronunciation) accompanied with music instruments available at the time: [LINK] , a continuation to a very popular video made years ago where he sung an excerpt: [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["wIC-iGDTU40",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["M4AhABManTw",1],["qL0BB2PRY7k",1],["uzkD5SeuwzM",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["dqwpQarrDwk",1]]},"TRL7o2kPqw0":{"wy":"HN commenters use it to put radiation exposure and risk into perspective, including everyday sources like flights and smoking.","q":{"x":"There was a great video by the Veritasium guy on it","i":20062298,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20062298,"author":"erentz","month":"2019-05","story":"They Fictionalize Nuclear Disasters Like Chernobyl Because They Kill So Few","stance":"rec","why":"Explains radiation risks, notably the danger of cigarettes.","quote":"There was a great video by the Veritasium guy on it","text":"Radiation is definitely creepy and poorly understood. There was a great video by the Veritasium guy on it: [VIDEO] Punch line, you definitely wanna stay away from ciggarettes if you don't like radiation. There's also this crazy woman (I think she's German?) who likes to go play with radiation sources. She has the most dead pan voice that barely changes except to possibly register some excitement when she finds a particularly hot source of radiation. She has a bunch of videos exploring Chernobyl. It's quite interesting for example to watch her pick apart dirt to find the single hot particle: [LINK]"},{"id":9803900,"author":"junto","month":"2015-06","story":"A Walk Around Chernobyl [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Reveals which people are most exposed to radiation.","quote":"The answer shocked me","text":"One of his other videos describes the people who are most exposed to radiation. The answer shocked me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27383582,"author":"dharmab","month":"2021-06","story":"United Airlines will buy 15 planes from Boom Supersonic","stance":"reference","why":"Source for radiation exposure comparisons during flights.","quote":"The amount of radiation you receive during a regular flight is quite small","text":"The amount of radiation you receive during a regular flight is quite small, and more than you would receive on a supersonic flight: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Ra_0DgnJ1uQ",1],["GZ5XN_mJE8Y",1],["ciStnd9Y2ak",1],["fiD24uEvY1U",1],["hC3VTgIPoGU",1]]},"TiXINuf5nbI":{"wy":"HN recommends it in threads about sheep-counting systems as the famous song that explains them.","q":{"x":"I'll again recommend the Youtube video of Jake Thackray singing 'Molly Metcalfe'","i":33897609,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33897609,"author":"nkurz","month":"2022-12","story":"Yan Tan Tethera","stance":"rec","why":"Jake Thackray singing about the sheep-counting system","quote":"I'll again recommend the Youtube video of Jake Thackray singing 'Molly Metcalfe'","text":"It's fair game, since it didn't get much discussion, but if you click on 'past' in the list of links below the title, you'll see a couple times this has already been posted. But since it's here again, I'll again recommend the Youtube video of Jake Thackray singing 'Molly Metcalfe': [VIDEO] Anyone grow up somewhere where this style of counting is still in use? Or one like it? Or did these die out in the last generation?"},{"id":33932892,"author":"I_complete_me","month":"2022-12","story":"Yan tan tethera pethera pimp – an old system for counting sheep","stance":"rec","why":"Song containing the sheep-counting words","quote":"Wonderful stuff.","text":"Here's Jake Thackray singing a song with this in it. Wonderful stuff. [VIDEO] . Edit: Just noticed this is referenced in the article. Sorry."},{"id":29866529,"author":"nkurz","month":"2022-01","story":"Yan tan tethera: A Celtic sheep-counting system","stance":"rec","why":"Famous folk song opening with an explanation of the counting system","quote":"Have you seen the famous folk song that starts with an explanation of these?","text":"Have you seen the famous folk song that starts with an explanation of these? Jake Thackray 'Molly Metcalfe' [VIDEO]"},{"id":42025651,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-11","story":"Optimizers need a rethink","stance":"reference","why":"Bonus link on traditional counting","quote":"","text":"in qua talibus Indorum fruimur bis quinque figuris : [LINK] (our own convention; the fox has Hox, and the lion too, but they'd probably count in octal. For those keeping score, compare The House of Asterion : \"The original says fourteen, but there is ample reason to infer that, as used by Asterion, this numeral stands for infinite\") Lagniappe: [VIDEO] EDIT: while we're at it, maybe I should specify units as orbits of Sol III?"},{"id":21610502,"author":"nkurz","month":"2019-11","story":"Counting in the wrong language","stance":"reference","why":"Song featuring the Swaledale counting rhyme","quote":"","text":"Depending on what you are counting, sometimes \"wrong\" can be historically \"right\": In Swaledale, in the North Riding of Yorkshire, sheep farmers used to, and some still do; count their sheep in a very curious fashion. Instead of One, Two, Three, Four... they go, thus: (1)Yan, (2)Tan, (3)Tether, (4)Mether, (5)Pip; (6)Ezar, (7)Sezar, (8)Acker, (9)Conter, (10)Dick; (11)Yan-a-Dick, (12)Tan-a-Dick, (13)Tether-a-Dick, (14)Mether-a-Dick, (15)Bumfit; (16)Yan-a-Bum, (17)Tan-a-Bum, (18)Tether-a-Bum, (19)Mether-a-Bum, (20)Jigget. Having reached Twenty, they then take a stone [or make a mark upon the ground or a piece of wood, thus the term 'a score' or 'twenty'] representing the Twenty sheep that they have counted; and if they possess more than Twenty sheep, they go for another twenty: Yan, Tan, Tether, Mether, Pip... Another Twenty, another stone. Yan, Tan, Tether, Mether, Pip... Again,Twenty, again another stone. Jake Thackray 'Molly Metcalfe' 1971 [VIDEO]"},{"id":5109223,"author":"robotmay","month":"2013-01","story":"I Support the WebKit Monoculture","stance":"reference","why":"Song explaining the traditional northern English sheep-counting system","quote":"a great explanation and song by the late Jake Thackeray","text":"Oh absolutely, if they updated it to be based on modern farming then everyone would have significantly less acreage :D But you'll find that with most imperial measurements; they're based on physical objects or systems of measuring which make less sense today. One of my favourites being the traditional way of counting sheep in the north of England: [LINK] (and here's a great explanation and song by the late Jake Thackeray: [VIDEO] )."}],"r":[["fbhPIK-oBvA",1],["ljPFZrRD3J8",1],["FBaVwwuErmU",1],["HLNhPMQnWu4",1]]},"YHLU0Uej1WA":{"wy":"Commenters bring it up in nuclear-fear and WWIII threads as an unnervingly realistic piece of fictional news coverage.","q":{"x":"I recommend waiting till nighttime and turning up the volume for extra effect.","i":30493759,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30542713,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2022-03","story":"Threads (1984) – Documentary style account of a nuclear holocaust and its effect","stance":"rec","why":"Well-done fictional news-style film of conflict escalating to nuclear war","quote":"There's a pretty well-done War of the Worlds-style film where a local conflict escalates in to nuclear war","text":"There's a pretty well-done War of the Worlds-style film where a local conflict escalates in to nuclear war at [VIDEO]"},{"id":30493759,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Is anyone else genuinely scared that this is the start of WW3?","stance":"rec","why":"Chilling fake BBC nuclear attack broadcast","quote":"I recommend waiting till nighttime and turning up the volume for extra effect.","text":"Thanks to HN user eswat, you can: [VIDEO] I recommend waiting till nighttime and turning up the volume for extra effect."},{"id":44564668,"author":"JoshTriplett","month":"2025-07","story":"Ask HN: Why isn’t Hollywood producing WWIII films in these perilous times?","stance":"reference","why":"Detailed fake BBC breaking news special about nuclear war","quote":"A very detailed fake BBC \"Breaking News\" special was produced a while back.","text":"A very detailed fake BBC \"Breaking News\" special was produced a while back. The original seems to have disappeared, likely because people were sending it around without clear labeling as being intentionally fake, but here's one copy of it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30526221,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: How do you cope with possible nuclear threat?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the ending is 11 out of 10","text":"By watching this on repeat: [VIDEO] Though that’s probably not a super useful coping mechanism. Still, the ending is 11 out of 10."}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["Ot4qdCs54ZE",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["REni8Oi1QJQ",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["oHlpmxLTxpw",1],["r-TuGAHR78w",1],["0lvUByM-fZk",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["42QuXLucH3Q",1],["PYylPRX6z4Q",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["NxvQPzrg2Wg",1],["fCn8zs912OE",1],["Ggz_gd--UO0",1],["Ox5P7QyL774",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1]]},"d-YcVLq98Ew":{"wy":"Recommended in orbital data center threads as a sober explanation of why the idea doesn't work out economically or thermally.","q":{"x":"The great Scott Manley did a video about that","i":46275152,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46275152,"author":"freefaler","month":"2025-12","story":"Nvidia-backed Starcloud trains first AI model in space, orbital data centers","stance":"rec","why":"Argues orbital data centers don't make sense even with Starship","quote":"The great Scott Manley did a video about that","text":"The great Scott Manley did a video about that: [VIDEO] basically it doesn't make sense with current technologiesa and even with Starship's proposed specs/price it won't be profitable. Check it out..."},{"id":46282842,"author":"Ataraxic","month":"2025-12","story":"Economics of Orbital vs. Terrestrial Data Centers","stance":"rec","why":"Cooling and mass are the biggest problems for orbital data centers","quote":"cooling is likely the biggest problem","text":"Scott Manley had a video about this last year. [VIDEO] The short of it is that cooling is likely the biggest problem, given you will need to pump the heat to the backside and radiate it away, and the amount of mass you will need to dedicate to cooling works against deployments and increases the cost per unit significantly. Not to mention, the idea of these huge deployments runs into potential space debris issues. Whenever one of these ventures actually manages to launch a proof of concept, I think we'll be able to quickly discern if there is actually a near-future here."},{"id":43978062,"author":"shantara","month":"2025-05","story":"Starcloud","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why orbital data centers are a bad idea","quote":"Scott Manley has published a video a few months ago explaining why putting data centers in space is an absolutely terrible idea.","text":"Scott Manley has published a video a few months ago explaining why putting data centers in space is an absolutely terrible idea. Lumen Orbit, the company mentioned, is a former name of Starcloud. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41553577,"author":"tourmalinetaco","month":"2024-09","story":"Data center emissions probably 662% higher than big tech claims","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you want to see exactly the type of solar panels needed for a data center (and why they’re impractical in even ideal situations) then check out this mockup of a proposed space data center ran off solar: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HUEvRyemKSg",1],["ND2TeNfcmKA",1]]},"fPKMmJpAGWc":{"wy":"Recommended in Elixir and machine learning threads as the introduction to what Nx makes possible.","q":{"x":"I recommend you watching José’s talk introducing Elixir Nx","i":26743910,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26743910,"author":"bfm","month":"2021-04","story":"Axon: A library for creating neural networks in Elixir","stance":"rec","why":"Talk introducing Elixir Nx","quote":"I recommend you watching José’s talk introducing Elixir Nx","text":"If you have not been following the work José and Sean have been doing lately, I recommend you watching José’s talk introducing Elixir Nx [VIDEO]"},{"id":26366696,"author":"rmetzler","month":"2021-03","story":"Caramel: An OCaml for the Erlang VM","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what Nx makes possible currently","quote":"in case you wonder what's possible currently.","text":"Lots of people mention Nx and of course I want to do the same but with a link to a youtube video, in case you wonder what's possible currently. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27731937,"author":"Rich_Morin","month":"2021-07","story":"Ray: A Distributed Framework for Emerging AI Applications","stance":"reference","why":"Intro to AI capabilities coming to Elixir","quote":"See \"Introducing Nx\" ( ) for an intro.","text":"Although it's early days, José Valim and some other folks are working on adding AI-related capabilities to Elixir and the (Erlang) BEAM. See \"Introducing Nx\" ( [VIDEO] ) for an intro. Given that they already have a robust Actor model as a base to work from, it occurs to me that they may be able to use some of Ray's ideas as they go along..."}]},"fwCz8Ris79g":{"wy":"Linked as the quickest way to grasp the rocket mass heater concept in threads on heating and thermal storage.","q":{"x":"Here's a quick 3 minute video that explains the concept","i":26168188,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26162257,"author":"mikeytown2","month":"2021-02","story":"What went wrong with the Texas power grid?","stance":"rec","why":"Short video explaining the rocket mass heater concept.","quote":"3m video explains the concept; longer videos will better explain it","text":"Best fireplace in terms of efficiency is a rocket mass heater. It burns the wood gas in a secondary burn chamber and then routes the exhaust through a lot of thermal mass. One 2 hour fire a day will usually heat 2000 sq feet of house. 3m video explains the concept; longer videos will better explain it [VIDEO]"},{"id":26168188,"author":"mikeytown2","month":"2021-02","story":"What went wrong with the Texas power grid?","stance":"rec","why":"Quick three-minute explanation of the rocket mass heater.","quote":"Here's a quick 3 minute video that explains the concept","text":"You can do a hybrid approach with the hot metal burn chamber for wood gas and earthen materials to slowly radiate the heat. This is called a rocket mass heater. The exhaust temperature is usually below 100C and often smokeless (a complete burn). Using a fan to extract heat from the final vertical stack (pipe around the exhaust with air running by) can give you an exhaust temperature of around 40C. Here's a quick 3 minute video that explains the concept; there's other YouTube videos that go into greater details if you wish to research this topic more. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18149933,"author":"michaelbuckbee","month":"2018-10","story":"Heat Storage Hypocausts: Air Heating in the Middle Ages (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a full rocket mass heater and its efficiency.","quote":"Mass rocket stoves are _incredibly_ efficient.","text":"I wonder what the efficiency is compared to a modern \"rocket stove\" build [1] Mass rocket stoves are _incredibly_ efficient. So much so that I've always wondered why more similar designs weren't used in times when the primary heat sources were wood, etc. 1 - [VIDEO] (skip to 1:50 if you want to see a full one and not the ms paint diagrams)"},{"id":33475412,"author":"zdragnar","month":"2022-11","story":"A sand battery could transform clean energy","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as residential example of thermal mass heating.","quote":"","text":"A few things: Clay weighs significantly more than sand. For the same volume of material, clay will store significantly more thermal energy. The big question is- is the hot air on a closed loop or an open loop? I don't know of too many fans comfortable cycling 600C air, which means that you need an open loop of air (fresh air > fan > heater > sand > exhaust). If the sand isn't drawing heat off of the hot air fast enough, the heat won't actually \"sink\" in through the sand. If we are talking a closed loop of air (fan > heater > sand > fan) then the sand will get fully heated. My reference to \"residential\" is the aformentioned rocket stove mass heater- see [LINK] or [VIDEO] and [LINK] if you prefer videos. These types of things typically stay warm for about 24 hours, so they're burned once a day, maybe once every other day depending on how the house is insulated. Since these are all wood fired, they must be open loop, and sand is almost universally acknowledged as a terrible idea to use for thermal storage."},{"id":32624419,"author":"mikeytown2","month":"2022-08","story":"Home Heating Cost Comparison","stance":"reference","why":"Quick intro to rocket mass heaters and cost of heating.","quote":"Quick Video about the rocket mass heater.","text":"Quick Video about the rocket mass heater. I'm guessing the cost to heat with wood, would be even lower if your house was built around this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29724274,"author":"tomohawk","month":"2021-12","story":"Oven Stoves and Heat Walls (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Shown as an efficient modern replacement for old stoves.","quote":"The rocket mass heater is the modern replacement for this.","text":"The rocket mass heater is the modern replacement for this. [VIDEO] Uses about 1/10th the fuel, and fuel can be wood unusable in other stoves. Very little exhaust / smoke. Super efficient."}],"r":[["yvFKh_3evC8",1],["ooOL4T-BAg0",1],["2AecAXinars",1],["U_X5uR7VC4M",1],["ewsGmhAjjjI",1],["HmL2l-bcuUQ",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["o4MwTvtyrUQ",1]]},"gmn7tjSNyAA":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in threads about indoor air quality, CO2 and houseplants as a cheap, low-tech approach.","q":{"x":"There is also a Ted talk about taking this to an extreme, which does seem to anecdotally work","i":48949889,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":12470620,"author":"internaut","month":"2016-09","story":"Air pollution a risk factor for diabetes","stance":"rec","why":"Researcher on indoor plants and air quality","quote":"Indian researcher describes side affects of indoor plants on health esp. air quality.","text":"From this thread \"How Trees Calm Us Down\": [LINK] Japanese researcher describes (with control group) the interesting affects of walking in a forest. [LINK] Indian researcher describes side affects of indoor plants on health esp. air quality. [VIDEO] I'm incorporating some of these concepts into my tiny house design e.g. a (truly ingenious design using mirrors!) terrarium and an hinoki ofuro, together with natural daylighting. A poster called mantesso offered up this interesting paper: Here's the original study from NASA, Interior Landscape plants for indoor air pollution abatement : [LINK]"},{"id":12054586,"author":"internaut","month":"2016-07","story":"How Trees Calm Us Down","stance":"rec","why":"Researcher on indoor plants improving air quality","quote":"Indian researcher describes side affects of indoor plants on health esp. air quality.","text":"If you want to know more you may be interested in these: Japanese researcher describes (with control group) the interesting affects of walking in a forest. [LINK] Indian researcher describes side affects of indoor plants on health esp. air quality. [VIDEO] I'm incorporating some of these concepts into my tiny house design e.g. a terrarium and an hinoki ofuro, together with natural daylighting."},{"id":12179377,"author":"patrickk","month":"2016-07","story":"Mercedes-Benz shows off the first fully electric heavy urban transport truck","stance":"rec","why":"More environmentally friendly option than air purifiers","quote":"Another, more environmentally friendly option","text":"When I flew into Heathrow last year, I was amazed by how visible the pollution was- over central London, there were no clouds (there were clouds around the outskirts) but this vague, blue haze was quite noticeable. Although compared to Shanghai, London is really clean, if you live in Shanghai you keep your windows permanently shut and run an air purifier 24/7: [LINK] > I'd recommend anyone living in a city to invest in a good air purifier from BlueAir or IQAir (if you can afford it). Another, more environmentally friendly option: [VIDEO] Also basil plants are a natural mosquito repellent, might not be an issue in the UK though."},{"id":48949889,"author":"schainks","month":"2026-07","story":"CO2 overload, detected in human blood, suggests toxic atmosphere within 50 years","stance":"reference","why":"Takes houseplants to an extreme that anecdotally works","quote":"There is also a Ted talk about taking this to an extreme, which does seem to anecdotally work","text":"Uh, do you mean house plants? There is also a Ted talk about taking this to an extreme, which does seem to anecdotally work: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38075165,"author":"diimdeep","month":"2023-10","story":"Global CO2 Levels","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I meant high tech gadgets, but sure How to grow fresh air | Kamal Meattle [VIDEO] [LINK] On a second thought, indoor DIY growbox is probably can be considered as such device."},{"id":18192703,"author":"nelsonic","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: Which plants can be planted indoors and easily maintained?","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among relevant videos on indoor plants","quote":"Relevant YouTube videos on this topic","text":"Here is the list of house/indoor plants which promote cognitive function in order of ease of maintenance: Areca Plam (Chrysalidocarpus lutescens AKA Dypsis lutescens) [LINK] Mother-in-law's Tongue / Snake Plant (Sansevieria trifasciata) [LINK] Money Plant (Epipremnum aureum) [LINK] Boston Fern (Nephrolepis Exaltata) [LINK] Peace Lilly (Spathiphyllum) [LINK] Gerbera Daisy (Gerbera jamesonii) [LINK] Spider plant (Chlorophytum comosum) [LINK] English Ivy (Hedera helix) [LINK] Take the list of plants to your local plant nursery to see how many of them they have in stock. Buying them already established is much cheaper/easier than growing them from seed/cuttings. According to Allen et Al 2015 (see link below), having pure/fresh air in your working environment significantly improves cognitive function. i.e. having indoor plants makes you smarter! > Associations of Cognitive Function Scores with Carbon Dioxide, Ventilation, and Volatile Organic Compound Exposures in Office Workers: A Controlled Exposure Study of Green and Conventional Office Environments ~ [LINK] > Conclusion : Office workers had significantly improved cognitive function scores when working in Green and Green+ environments compared with scores obtained when working in a Conventional environment. > We’ve been researching this for: “home” [LINK] Relevant YouTube videos on this topic: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["PTUY16CkS-k",1],["np_ylvc8Zj8",1],["T71ibcZAX3I",1],["RxabLA7UQ9k",1],["Kxryv2XrnqM",1],["aJ4TJ4-kkDw",1],["tw-rxtwhzcY",1]]},"leFuF-zoVzA":{"wy":"HN cites it as proof that a full iPhone can be assembled from sourced parts, in right-to-repair and Shenzhen hardware ecosystem discussions.","q":{"x":"You may enjoy this video where a guy goes to Shenzen and tries to build an iPhone 6s from parts.","i":17572875,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":25029635,"author":"Darkphibre","month":"2020-11","story":"Review: A Counterfeit, $100 iPhone X","stance":"rec","why":"Building an iPhone 6S from parts at Huaqiangbei markets.","quote":"I popped in to bring up this neat video I came across a few years back","text":"You addressed the first question, but I popped in to bring up this neat video I came across a few years back regarding the second question. How about this guy, who \"built a like-new (but really refurbished) iPhone 6S 16GB entirely from parts purchased from the public cell phone parts markets in Huaqiangbei.\"? :D [LINK] Making-of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17572875,"author":"Consultant32452","month":"2018-07","story":"Review: A Counterfeit, $100 iPhone X","stance":"rec","why":"Builds an iPhone 6s from Shenzhen parts for about $300.","quote":"You may enjoy this video where a guy goes to Shenzen and tries to build an iPhone 6s from parts.","text":"You may enjoy this video where a guy goes to Shenzen and tries to build an iPhone 6s from parts. He claims it cost $300. Technically he spent a little over $600 because he broke a lot of parts and bought the wrong parts in the process, but if everything went perfectly it would be about $300. I think this was created back when the 6s was the current model. $300 claim [LINK] build video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35299614,"author":"BoorishBears","month":"2023-03","story":"We Ran a Phone Check at a Y Combinator Event in SF. Here’s How It Went","stance":"reference","why":"Building an iPhone piece by piece from a Shenzhen market.","quote":"building your own iPhone piece by piece just walking around a market","text":"The short answer is, yes they do. It's true that Apple has paired hardware on newer phones, and that combined with Find My has really taken a huge bite out of how many iPhones are stolen, but Shenzhen is Shenzhen. It is a mecca of technology, and you will find things that we'd generally otherwise consider impossible, like building your own iPhone piece by piece just walking around a market: [VIDEO] So even if they can't take a given part as-is, they can always find something useful. We'd think \"the logic board is paired so it's useless\": they'd think \"ICs X,Y, and Z on the logic board commonly fail and now we got spares\". There's been more than one case of stolen stuff ending up in Shenzhen: [LINK]"},{"id":26484819,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2021-03","story":"Right to Repair Gaining Momentum","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I do think it's fair for Apple to say, \"Hey, we can't ensure this device is properly repaired unless we are the ones to repair it.\" Maybe some sort of \"Apple-certified repair place\" program... I think that exists today. I don't think Apple should be forced to sell raw parts; it's not part of their business model. You're free to buy more modular hardware... things you can easily repair. That's a selling point, right? But Apple should be free to do business however they want to do business. You're free not to buy Apple products, but you shouldn't be free, as a consumer, to insist they make schematics and parts available. You can choose to support companies that sell parts if you want! (= EDIT: [VIDEO] - seems about as good as you can hope for. Pretty sure Apple isn't keen on \"DIY\" for their brand."},{"id":20246962,"author":"aurizon","month":"2019-06","story":"Support for right-to-repair laws slowly grows","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is not so easy. The Surface Books are made with a number of snap together modules, via fine cable runs and multi-pin connectors. Each part is made with care and subjected to detailed tests, that include a term of power-on burn in for the major parts with infra-red smart vision that looks for any hot spots (that might lead to failure). Then they are assembled with in progress tests at some points until the final productis made and it is tested after they add the SSD with all the operating system installed. There are assorted areas partitioned on the SSD that might hold an OS recovery partition, as well as other software provided by others as choice ware (since so many people like this so called bloatware not installed). Then it will be placed on a burn-in rack at a higher temperatrure than room temp and some sort of test program will be run on a recurrent basis. Those that pass are packed and shipped, those that fail are analyzed for faults and any faulty part replaced and it is re-tested. A lemon with recurrent faults will probably be gutted to find the part with an enduring fault. Once in the field, it is rare for part level test and repair to be done. Those boards are marked as scrap with dye?? and sold in the scrap boards auction in schenzen. Buyers buy these boards and perform some analysis of them, or simply remove certain high value parts and sell them to other repris shops. Often many parts are tossed because these parts lack detailed information on them abd are n"},{"id":18577127,"author":"srcmap","month":"2018-12","story":"Chip wars: China, America and silicon supremacy","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the hardware ecosystem supporting Shenzhen prototyping.","quote":"Take a look at this video","text":"Slightly different Point of view: HW needs infrastructure. It needs Packages, materials, PCB houses etc ideally all near by, with good transportation networks, etc. That was what ShenZhen was good at. It took very low cost labors and supported government and a lot time to build up. Take a look at this video [VIDEO] on how to build IPhone from parts bought in ShenZhen public market to see the supported systems needed for HW. You can imagine how much easier and faster to prototype/build HW (drone, robots, etc) in ShenZhen as compare to anywhere else in the world (for now). As the labor cost goes up in ShenZhen / China, the significant advantages are gone. That's why China is trying to move up via Tech/IP, etc. Same development processes happened in Japan, Korea, Taiwan for the past 40-50 years. All of them start with unskilled Textile -> toy assembly -> Electonic PCB assembly -> PCB Design -> Simple Asic / Systems-> Complex ASIC / Complex Systems. As they move up the food chain, they all \"clone / steal / borrow / learn\" ip from more developed nations. It is also repeating the process 150 years ago where UK invented the Train/Rail system and US just scaled/improved to much bigger system. History just keep repeating."},{"id":14930212,"author":"franciscop","month":"2017-08","story":"Why Apple and other tech companies are fighting to keep devices hard to repair","stance":"reference","why":"Building a full iPhone from sourced components.","quote":"you can make a full iPhone by buying each of the components if you know where to find them","text":"On the other hand you can make a full iPhone by buying each of the components if you know where to find them (disclaimer: I've met Scott before): [VIDEO]"},{"id":14101803,"author":"justboxing","month":"2017-04","story":"How I Made My Own iPhone in China [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Creator's comment on the cost of the build.","quote":"I'd say it's probably around $300 worth of parts in the actual phone.","text":"Yep. In the video comments, he says he spent well over $1000. Justifies the $300 price tag by stating that he didn't need the $700+ worth of parts and tools he purchased. > Strange Parts 4 hours ago > Thanks for subscribing! I spent well over $1000, but a lot of that was parts and tools I didn't end up needing. I'd say it's probably around $300 worth of parts in the actual phone.﻿ Source: Direct link to comment: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xzYgiOC9cj4",1]]},"x6AOwDttBsc":{"wy":"Recommended as a detailed, level-headed look at the technical challenges Meta faces in VR.","q":{"x":"plenty of really fascinating and intellectual-curiosity-satisfying information exists out there about the metaverse? For instance, this video","i":32714960,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32714960,"author":"johnfn","month":"2022-09","story":"Meta’s metaverse hype leads to real-world backlash","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating, intellectually satisfying look at metaverse tech.","quote":"plenty of really fascinating and intellectual-curiosity-satisfying information exists out there about the metaverse? For instance, this video","text":"Why are we deluged with such low quality, low interest, low curiosity articles - about some screenshot of Mark Zuckerberg's avatar of all things - when plenty of really fascinating and intellectual-curiosity-satisfying information exists out there about the metaverse? For instance, this video: [VIDEO] Especially on Hacker News, of all places! This article is the equivalent of celebrity tabloids. (This comment is probably not written with the best tone, but I am upset. PR mishaps aside, the metaverse is extremely technically interesting. Why HN skips all of that and just does reruns of Zuckerberg's avatar or whatever is beyond me.)"},{"id":31949152,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2022-07","story":"Meta slashes hiring plans, girds for 'fierce' headwinds","stance":"rec","why":"Hands-on with Meta's prototype headsets and technical challenges in detail.","quote":"there's an hour long episode of Adam Savage's Tested series where they get to try the prototypes and go into even more detail","text":"> it just isn't happening with dorky headsets and probably won't until the tech matures enough to get there Zuckerberg is well aware of the technical challenges of VR headsets and they're putting a lot of effort into getting the headsets where they need to be. Seems like they're helping push the technology much further than any other company right now. Here's a 30 minute video where he and Michael Abrash go into the technical challenges, and what they've been doing to address those issues, as well as showing off prototype devices they've been working on[1]. And there's an hour long episode of Adam Savage's Tested series where they get to try the prototypes and go into even more detail[2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36256565,"author":"lhl","month":"2023-06","story":"Here’s what Mark Zuckerberg thinks about Apple’s Vision Pro headset","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Meta's lab prototypes.","quote":"Tested had a good demo video of what they have in the labs last year","text":"While I think Apple throwing their hat in the ring with a super-high end device is great for the market and should light a fire under Meta (and everyone else's butt), I think that the idea that Apple has a super-advanced technology lead isn't totally true for those that have been paying close attention to the XR space. I mean yes, Apple is the best hardware company in the world, and they've assembled/polished best in class hardware and most importantly, have been willing to commit to the volumes for production for bleeding edge parts (eg, companies like eMagin have been demoing 4K micro OLED display for years [1] but no one's ever committed for an $X billion order - already we're seeing the \"Apple\" effect though as suppliers now have customers to chase [2]). Meta's FRL has been working on pinch-and-release click for years with a haptic EMG controller that while bulkier, I think would actually be a more satisfying experience: [LINK] Meta has also been working on next-gen displays (focused on varifocal displays/fixing vergence acommodation conflict) for years. I still think that Doug Lanman's 2020 EIS Plenary presentation is one of the best technical talks of recent memory, and highly recommend watching it to anyone interested in displays: [LINK] - Tested had a good demo video of what they have in the labs last year: [VIDEO] and you can read about their DeepFocus work from back in 2018: [LINK] or see their Half Dome prototypes going back the same time period: [LINK] You can go "},{"id":35137742,"author":"megaman821","month":"2023-03","story":"Tim Cook bets on Apple’s mixed-reality headset to secure his legacy","stance":"reference","why":"Tour of Reality Labs showing VR challenges.","quote":"it seems that they realize the challenges ahead","text":"I don't think anyone is going to crack VR in the short term. Watching this tour of Facebook's Reality Labs, [VIDEO] , it seems that they realize the challenges ahead. Looking at these challenges (resolution, brightness, eye tracking, form factor) is there an area where Apple is going to be a generation or more ahead? I think the world will be waiting another decade or so before VR gets anything like phone penetration in the market."},{"id":33167027,"author":"KVFinn","month":"2022-10","story":"Meta Quest Pro","stance":"reference","why":"Hands-on impressions of Quest Pro.","quote":"Tested hands on impressions","text":"Tested hands on impressions: [VIDEO] Funny thing about the $1500 price point. If the Quest Pro could replace my triple monitors, $1500 is a bargain. It's basically a laptop with 3 monitors that appear out of thin air whenever you want. Amazing. To do this it needs to be comfortable enough to wear all day and it needs to be high enough resolution to stare at text all day. It's at least plausible the Pro is comfortable enough for some based on early impressions. But the displays are not cutting it. I don't think it needs to equal the DPI of a monitor at human viewing distance, but 2160x2160 is still way to low. Maybe 3k would do it with super sampling. Oh and the Quest Pro would need to run any Android app, or else it would only be useful tethered to my computer and lose a lot of the appeal. So if the Quest Pro can't handle this use case, $1500 seems crazy expensive. A luxury. They have to hope they have a killer game or app that somehow gets people in. During the height of the pandemic I would have almost paid $1500 if this had a really good 'virtual couch' experience where you can hang out and watch Netflix with friends, passively socializing in a way that felt comparable to the real thing. People love doing this in but it's always so janky."},{"id":33357963,"author":"megaman821","month":"2022-10","story":"Meta stock price drops more than 20%","stance":"reference","why":"Meta prototypes show clear goals but a decade away.","quote":"They seem pretty clear-headed on where they need to get to","text":"I was watching [VIDEO] about Meta's VR prototypes. They seem pretty clear-headed on where they need to get to, but a lot of it seems like a decade away. At double the price-point of Quest Pro, Apple may solve some of VR's issues (battery-life, speed, ergonomics and looks) but the screen-tech just doesn't exist yet."},{"id":32270923,"author":"gpm","month":"2022-07","story":"My newsletter is making $2k per month with 7k subscribers – AMA","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Facebook's VR R&D.","quote":"facebook is probably going to in the future given the R&D they've been showing off","text":"First you need VR goggles that you can reasonably work in... These guys are making a go at that: [LINK] And facebook is probably going to in the future given the R&D they've been showing off: [VIDEO] But having used the current generations of headsets (Valve Index, Quest 2), they just aren't quite there yet."}],"r":[["Bd2bbHoVQSM",1],["Fiu9GSOmt8E",1],["qPb_0JZ6-Rc",1]]},"yF2KgQGmUic":{"wy":"HN points to it as the video explanation of how RAID-Z expansion works without block pointer rewrite.","q":{"x":"For those that like videos more than text, there is a youtube video from last year [1] that explain the feature","i":30265945,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37194166,"author":"allanjude","month":"2023-08","story":"OpenZFS – Incremental add of disks to existing RAIDZ","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation detailing how RAID-Z expansion works without block pointer rewrite.","quote":"See this presentation","text":"That is not how this will work. The reason the parity ratio stays the same, is that all of the references to the data are by DVA (Data Virtual Address, effectively the LBA within the RAID-Z vdev). So the data will occupy the same amount of space and parity as it did before. All stripes in RAID-Z are dynamic, so if your stripe is 5 wide and your array is 6 wide, the 2nd stripe will start on the last disk and wrap around. So if your 5x10 TB disks are 90% full, after the expansion they will contain the same 5.4 TB of data and 3.6 TB of parity, and the pool will now be 10 TB bigger. New writes, will be 4+2 instead, but the old data won't change (they is how this feature is able to work without needing block-pointer rewrite). See this presentation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30265945,"author":"mrighele","month":"2022-02","story":"Raid-Z Expansion Feature for ZFS Goes Live","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the RAID-Z expansion feature in video form.","quote":"For those that like videos more than text, there is a youtube video from last year [1] that explain the feature","text":"For those that like videos more than text, there is a youtube video from last year [1] that explain the feature (unless it's changed since ,but it seems not to be the case). One downside that I see of this approach, if I understand it correctly, is that the data already present on disk will not take advantage of the extra disk per slice. For example, if I have a raidz of 4 disks (so 25% of space \"wasted\"), and add another disk, new data will be distributed on 5 disks (so 20% of space \"wasted\") but the old data will keep using stripes of 4 blocks, they will just be reshuffled between the disks. Do I understand it correctly ? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29368228,"author":"vermaden","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: What are the best-designed things you've ever used?","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation on RAID-Z expansion allowing growing ZFS pools on the fly.","quote":"There is RAIDZ Expansion available. Happens on the fly.","text":"> AFAIK ZFS pools can't easily be resized > you generally have to recreate them Not anymore. There is RAIDZ Expansion available. Happens on the fly. - [VIDEO] - [LINK]"}]},"zT58pzY41wA":{"wy":"Cited in aviation-accident threads as a compact analysis of whether whole-aircraft parachutes work, with the track record described as impressive.","q":{"x":"AVweb did an interesting video a couple of years ago","i":32800779,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32800779,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2022-09","story":"Cirrus Vision Jet Pilot Pulls Chute in Florida","stance":"rec","why":"Analyzes whether Cirrus parachutes saved lives; concludes generally good.","quote":"AVweb did an interesting video a couple of years ago","text":"AVweb did an interesting video a couple of years ago, \"Are Planes With Parachutes Really Safer?\": > The Cirrus line of aircraft have been flying for 20 years and although most people in aviation know they have full aircraft parachutes, it's fair to ask how effective these have been. With more than 90 uses of the so-called CAPS, has the system really saved lives? In this video, AVweb's Paul Bertorelli analyzes the record. * [VIDEO] Concludes that parachutes a generally a good thing, and even though the Cirrus aircraft fleet has grown from 5000 to 7000 airplanes over the last ten years (12m30s), the anual fatality numbers have stay the same and so have overall annual accidents. The first few years didn't see much improvement, probably because the training didn't emphasize it as much, but since ~2013 the advice is generally \" if you're experiencing issues just pull the 'chute \". There is a cost to fixing the aircraft afterwards, but it will be you paying it and not your widow or estate."},{"id":27144621,"author":"jrockway","month":"2021-05","story":"Midair Collision over Denver","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of Cirrus CAPS safety record with footage.","quote":"Here's an analysis of the CAPS system's safety record","text":"Here's an analysis of the CAPS system's safety record: [VIDEO] (contains some good footage of an SR22 parachuting into the ocean, and into the ground). This is a good overview on the survivability of water landings: [LINK] . I haven't watched the video recently but I think even a 172 that cartwheels after landing has a pretty good survivability rate, something like 90%. Parachute is a nice to have, of course."},{"id":44538820,"author":"maxbond","month":"2025-07","story":"Preliminary report into Air India crash released","stance":"reference","why":"Precedent for whole-aircraft parachutes with impressive track record.","quote":"The system has a very impressive track record.","text":"There's precedent. [LINK] [30s] [VIDEO] [15m] The Cirrus system is deployed by rockets, allowing it to function at a very low altitude. They say that you should deploy it no matter what altitude you are at, and it will add at least some friction. The system has a very impressive track record. However, at this altitude, with an airplane this heavy, you might have to put the rockets on the plane to decelerate enough to save lives."},{"id":37241413,"author":"maxbond","month":"2023-08","story":"Wagner leader Yevgeny Prigozhin listed as passenger on plane which crashed","stance":"reference","why":"Video essay on the Cirrus parachute system.","quote":"14m video essay about the system","text":"Perhaps some private planes (Cirrus) are better equipped to survive losing a wing, since they have rocket-deployed parachutes. Somewhat boring video of the parachute in action (\"boring\" being a synonym for \"an amazing triumph of safety engineering\"): [LINK] 14m video essay about the system: [VIDEO] (Reporting says this was an Embraer, this is strictly a tangent.)"}],"r":[["j4H9LL7A-nQ",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["MejbOFk7H6c",1],["qWKz3psejb0",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["QvW61K2s0tA",1],["LWGJA9i18Co",1],["o1Fyn_h6LKU",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["-VsmF9m_Nt8",1],["Wpzvaqypav8",1],["9q3uNcJh4pc",1],["cIcbAMO6sxo",1]]},"4Z75A_JMBx4":{"wy":"HN cites it in climate and diet debates as a contrarian perspective that cattle on managed pasture can be part of the solution.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend Joel Salatin's works - he has a really interesting perspective.","i":17208346,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17208346,"author":"cwkoss","month":"2018-06","story":"Avoiding meat and dairy is ‘single biggest way’ to reduce your impact on Earth","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Salatin's perspective on cows and climate via rotational grazing","quote":"Highly recommend Joel Salatin's works - he has a really interesting perspective.","text":"Highly recommend Joel Salatin's works - he has a really interesting perspective. Here's a talk of his on Cows and Climate Change [VIDEO] From my recollected (watched that ~6 mo ago), the amount of land needed to grow food for his herd is less than the amount of land that would be needed to purchase it elsewhere, because the rotation of the animals keeps his fields fertilized and trimmed to the optimum length for fast growth. Plus, by rotating in chickens, he's double dipping a bit. There are some people doing some interesting research into using herd animals to prep beds for annual vegetables: they let the animal graze as the seeds are starting to germinate, animals eat everything green. The germinating seeds are then able to fill the void left behind faster than anything else, because the farmer timed it properly."},{"id":30833208,"author":"ComradePhil","month":"2022-03","story":"Our soil is dying! - We and our governments need to act!","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Look at these videos for a completely different view of soil protection","text":"Direct link to the video for those on mobile: [LINK] The problem they mention is the soil being exposed to direct sun and the solution they propose is that farming should be done under tree shade. That sounds absolutely baseless and nonsensical... and counter-productive. Look at these videos for a completely different view of soil protection: - [LINK] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":14907796,"author":"shanev","month":"2017-08","story":"Impossible Foods has raised $75M for its plant-based burgers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">>potentially (and most likely) more land needed to produce equal amounts of meat >Please explain this in more detail. Sure! Plants are much less nutrient dense that animals. So for them to create fake meat that's equally nutrient dense as real meat, their farms have to be adequately large. You need a ton of plants to achieve the density of meat. Animals on land however are already nutrient dense. The only physical space they need is enough room to graze. >>and sequesters carbon/methane as a side effect >I have not heard of a farming practice that sequesters methane released from ruminants. Can you provide more information? Scientists and farmers in this area can do a much better job explaining it than I [1], [2], [3]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["WB2-2j9e4co",1],["Z0oFJ2jbkDI",1],["0olpSN6_TCc",1]]},"tI3kkk2JdoI":{"wy":"Recommended in urban planning threads as a clear account of suburban municipal finance.","q":{"x":"Everyone that wants to know more about this owes it to themselves to watch this strong towns video","i":39015714,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39015714,"author":"wing-_-nuts","month":"2024-01","story":"US Cities Could Verge on Becoming Ghost Towns by 2100, Study Finds","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains suburban finances as a growth-dependent scheme","quote":"Everyone that wants to know more about this owes it to themselves to watch this strong towns video","text":"Everyone that wants to know more about this owes it to themselves to watch this strong towns video It's a grim situation that doesn't even require decline, just the end of growth: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48374304,"author":"scoofy","month":"2026-06","story":"Americans don't know how to fight AI. So they're fighting data centers","stance":"reference","why":"Strong Towns on municipal finance and suburban insolvency","quote":"The entire point of the last 10 years of Strong Towns was talking about municipal finance, infrastructure costs, and the insolvency of the American suburban town.","text":"The reason these data centers are getting built in place people don’t want them is because the towns are broke. The city councils know it, but the residents don’t. The entire point of the last 10 years of Strong Towns was talking about municipal finance, infrastructure costs, and the insolvency of the American suburban town. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39697402,"author":"currydove","month":"2024-03","story":"Mass timber is great, but it will not solve the housing shortage","stance":"reference","why":"Quick video on cost of spread-out infrastructure","quote":"here's a video I could quickly find about the same topic from the same source","text":"I don't even know where to begin here. Of course we have the land to spread people out, but it's insanely expensive on everyone (especially those who are spread out) over time to sustain it. [1] If you don't have time to read - here's a video I could quickly find about the same topic from the same source - [VIDEO] You have a lot of straw man arguments that but the one that I'll focus on the most is \"Our leaders just FORCE us to flock to cities cpz it's cheaper on infrastructure\" - prove it. 1. [LINK]"}]},"tbcy_ZxXLl8":{"wy":"Recommended as a worthwhile, full-length statement of the free software philosophy, especially around the zero marginal cost of knowledge.","q":{"x":"I promise every second is worth watching.","i":5191428,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5191428,"author":"clicks","month":"2013-02","story":"The Elsevier boycott one year on","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moglen on free software and zero marginal cost of knowledge.","quote":"I promise every second is worth watching.","text":"Have you read Eben Moglen's writings per chance? Your thoughts? If you haven't looked into his work, here's something (in line with topic) to get started on: [VIDEO] -- I'd be curious to hear your thoughts. It's 1 hour 11 minutes, but I promise every second is worth watching. Moglen is an excellent communicator. Near 8 minutes in, he says this: This is the introduction to the free software movement. This is the purpose of the free software movement. This is the aim not only of the free software movement, but of a large number of the other things we are doing that arise from the fact that the digital revolution means that knowledge no longer has a non-zero marginal cost, that when you have the first copy of any significant representation of knowledge – whatever the fixed cost of the production of that representation may have been – you have as many additional copies, everywhere, as you need, without any significant additional costs. The non-zero marginal cost quality of all the things we digitize, which – in the society we are now building – is everything we value, because we digitize everything we can value, right down to how we fit in our jeans, right? In the world where we digitize everything of value and everything of value has been digitized, a moral question of significance arises: When you can provide to everybody everything that you value, at the cost of providing it to any one body, what is the morality of excluding people who cannot afford to pay? If you could make a"},{"id":36544582,"author":"teddyh","month":"2023-07","story":"YouTube is testing a more aggressive approach against ad blockers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Free and Open Software: Paradigm for a New Intellectual Commons , 2009-03-13","text":"“Most of you have browsers, and most of those browsers show you advertisements, and I’m very puzzled about why. The advertisements are annoying most of the time, they slow you down, they injure the concentration that you bring to whatever task it is that you’re doing, and there’s no reason why they show you advertisements; my browser doesn’t show me any advertisements. I don’t see any ads when I read The New York Times, or go to wherever it is that you are happy going to, because my browser has Adblock in it, and that pretty much ends the story. Even in this town, many of you, indeed, I would guess, most of you are probably using the Firefox browser. That means you’re two clicks away from not having any advertising on the net anymore. All you need to do is google “Adblock Plus”, and say “I’m feeling lucky”, [Laughter] thank you very much. Now you know why my friends – and they are my friends – at the Mozilla Foundation are paid tens of millions of dollars every year by Google – basically, not to bundle Adblock Plus into the default distribution of Firefox. But you also know why all the talk about advertising supported models on the web is just talk, and why it is that in the end, all of those models are fated not to work. Because in digital media, when you give people knowledge, you can’t force them to take advertising, because digital media are filterable – that’s the beauty of them.” — Eben Moglen, Free and Open Software: Paradigm for a New Intellectual Commons , 2009-03-13"},{"id":13062643,"author":"balupton","month":"2016-11","story":"Time Well Spent: From attention tech to time well lived tech (Dec 16 Refresh)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Richard Stallman and Eben Molgen for the past 30 years","text":"I'm super skeptical of this. They have been around since Jan 2015. And include Google not in attention companies but in platform companies. This is a strange divide considering Google makes YouTube, and which business is just to intercept as much information as possible, to build a profile of you, to target your attention to advertising and enough value that you don't mind. This is then continued by their recommendation of AdBlock which blocks ads (but not Google's by default, because Google pays them), and not tracking. Their message seems somewhat to what we have already been hearing from Richard Stallman and Eben Molgen for the past 30 years — [VIDEO] — Aral Balkan from Indie for the past 5-10 years — [LINK] — but packaged up for consumers, blaming tech. However their delivery seems more at targeting consumers, with the illusion of targeting designers, by blaming those dam malicious tech folk because it is not the consumer's job to accept responsibility of how they spend their time - and instead, it is up to companies to stop wasting the irresponsible and perhaps addicted consumers attention, despite the consumer not wanting to support non-free business models, probably hence why this movement hasn't taken off in its 2 years. The video seems reminiscent of the rap video by another person, who's message was to consumers, hey there was a time before tech invaded our attention, you can put your phone away you know, perhaps do that. This version seems to be perhaps aimed at ch"}]},"4Wrc4fHSCpw":{"wy":"HN cites it in asteroid threads for how well-tracked large asteroids are and how hard deflection is.","q":{"x":"Veritasium video with Dave Jewitt of UCLA explains a lot about what were doing about asteroids.","i":31633057,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31633057,"author":"ourmandave","month":"2022-06","story":"Algorithm detected over a hundred asteroids after studying old telescope data","stance":"rec","why":"Explains a lot about what's being done about asteroids","quote":"Veritasium video with Dave Jewitt of UCLA explains a lot about what were doing about asteroids.","text":"How old is the data? You can only forecast an asteroid's future path so far, given the snapshot of data you have. Veritasium video with Dave Jewitt of UCLA explains a lot about what were doing about asteroids. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37138686,"author":"deepakg","month":"2023-08","story":"Asteroid ZTm0038 with a >3% impact probability","stance":"reference","why":"Compares devastation from different asteroid sizes","quote":"This video has comparisons of devastation that would be caused by different sizes (around 7:50 mark) of asteroids","text":"This video has comparisons of devastation that would be caused by different sizes (around 7:50 mark) of asteroids: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37139221,"author":"mcint","month":"2023-08","story":"Asteroid ZTm0038 with a >3% impact probability","stance":"reference","why":"Passive-consumption summary video","quote":"","text":"VSauce with packages summary for passive consumption. [VIDEO] And jacquesm's replies to my other comments, [LINK]"},{"id":30022582,"author":"netsec_burn","month":"2022-01","story":"Starlink satellites hindering detection of near-Earth asteroids, study finds","stance":"reference","why":"Source for all large asteroids being detected","quote":"We've already detected all asteroids with that mass in our solar system","text":"We've already detected all asteroids with that mass in our solar system [1]. There are smaller ones that won't end life on Earth that are still concerning, but the quandary is we have almost nothing to do even if we detected a threat from an asteroid. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27535961,"author":"umvi","month":"2021-06","story":"What happened in the impact crater the day it did in the dinos (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for how hard detecting and preventing impacts is","quote":"It's unbelievably hard even detecting an incoming asteroid let alone prevent it.","text":"Yeah because it's pretty much impossible. It's unbelievably hard even detecting an incoming asteroid let alone prevent it. See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26618098,"author":"ruph123","month":"2021-03","story":"NASA mission to Venus in 1978 may have detected phosphine, a gas related to life","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for inability to divert asteroids","quote":"","text":"> But I think the point I was making stands. There are certainly innovations that sounds illogical but turn out to be very successful. Just because something sounds like a bad idea doesn't mean it is, it just means it hasn't worked yet. No because your point was about horses being replaced by cars, which is what people (and probably horses) were skeptical about at that time. In the same way IBM was saying that computers will never appear in homes, etc. But these devices were already there. The misconceptions were about predicting demand and miniaturization. This is completely different from terraforming. You are comparing \"oh these devices sure never will be popular enough to make this other thing obsolete\" with \"lets nuke the Mars to create an atmosphere so we can live on it\" (always winter, yeay).[0] Your argument is bad because by saying \"technologies were laughed at in the past\" every technology that sounds laughable should be taken seriously? No. This is the same trap that Theranos investors had fallen in. It is all a fantasy. We humans are very bad at estimating what is possible and what not. E.g. our inability to divert asteroids [1]. That is already a hard problem and currently not possible to do. I am not against crazy technological ideas, for example the space elevator had a surprisingly good feasibility to being realized before we polluted our planet's atmosphere with junk. Terraforming Mars is in the Theranos land of crazy ideas though. > Just like I also didn't t"},{"id":25960081,"author":"willis936","month":"2021-01","story":"The Uncensored Guide to ‘Oumuamua, Aliens, and That Harvard Astronomer","stance":"reference","why":"Shows planet-killer asteroids are easiest to spot","quote":"Asteroids large enough to be considered planet-killers are the easiest ones to spot.","text":"Actually, we do. Asteroids large enough to be considered planet-killers are the easiest ones to spot. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["SC2eSujzrUY",1],["5eW6Eagr9XA",1],["nmgFG7PUHfo",1]]},"7d9juPsv1QU":{"wy":"HN points to it as proof that the Physically Based Rendering book won an Oscar and for the speech crediting Knuth's literate programming.","q":{"x":"In their receiving speech they give a shout out to Donald Knuth for pioneering literate programming.","i":10030510,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10030510,"author":"tinco","month":"2015-08","story":"Show HN: A toy ray tracer in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Actors introduce the book award; speech credits Knuth.","quote":"In their receiving speech they give a shout out to Donald Knuth for pioneering literate programming.","text":"I didn't know that Physically Based Rendering was introduced as a book, and that book is a literate program (as in, you can compile the book and get a physically based renderer as a binary) that was so influential that it received an academy award(!). There's a video of two famous actors announcing them, being professionals they explain exactly what the book is about convincingly despite not having a clue what they are talking about: [VIDEO] In their receiving speech they give a shout out to Donald Knuth for pioneering literate programming."},{"id":30717971,"author":"svat","month":"2022-03","story":"Physically Based Rendering: From theory to implementation (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Ceremony where Matt Pharr thanks Knuth at 2:12.","quote":"at 2:12 Matt Pharr comes up and says","text":"When Knuth came up with the term/idea of \"literate programming\" and the idea of programs as works of literature, in the paper ( [LINK] ) he joked (I think?) that he hoped that, just as there are Pulitzer prizes for literature, there may one day be Pulitzer prizes awarded to computer programs. Well, reality is even stranger than imagination: no computer program has won a Pulitzer prize, but this computer program won an Oscar! Here's a video of the awards ceremony, where Kristen Bell and Michael B. Jordan introduce the recipients, and at 2:12 Matt Pharr comes up and says \"needless to say, when we started writing a book we never expected an Academy Award would be the end result of that effort\" and thanks Donald Knuth for literate programming: [VIDEO] Knuth has repeated the joke/remark ( [LINK] ): > “This book has deservedly won an Academy Award. I believe it should also be nominated for a Pulitzer Prize.”"},{"id":26289701,"author":"svat","month":"2021-02","story":"Noweb – A Simple, Extensible Tool for Literate Programming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"One of the problems with literate programming is that everyone who wants to write literate programs seems to want to write their own literate programming system. This was the conclusion of a short-lived (running to 5 issues) column of literate programs in the Communications of the ACM (1987–1990), by Christopher J. Van Wyk: > Unfortunately, no one has yet volunteered to write a program using another’s system for literate programming. A fair conclusion from my mail would be that one must write one’s own system before one can write a literate program, and that makes me wonder how widespread literate programming is or will ever become. This column will continue only if I hear from people who use literate-programming systems that they have not designed themselves. And it did not continue. Since then though, it appears that Noweb (and more recently, org-babel, and somewhere in between the Leo editor) is among the literate-programming systems that have been the most successful at getting others to use them! Separately, something amusing: When Donald Knuth came up with \"literate programming\" (partly because it had been suggested to him, by Tony Hoare IIRC, that he ought to publish as a book the source of the TeX program he was rewriting, so he was led to solve the problem of exposition) and the idea of programs as literature, he made a joke (or maybe he was half-serious, hard to say): > Perhaps we will even one day find Pulitzer prizes awarded to computer programs. ( [LINK] ) That d"},{"id":13301166,"author":"taeric","month":"2017-01","story":"A Paper Algorithm Notation","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as proof the book won an Oscar.","quote":"It won a oscar.[1]","text":"I can't claim you are wrong. But this is so non-intuitive to me that it hurts. I get why \"covered ground\" for text books would be easier to do. But many textbooks should be pushing the field, as well. Right? And I fully note that the book I linked to is exceptional. It won a oscar.[1] And was basically required reading at pixar, if I recall. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7776868,"author":"corysama","month":"2014-05","story":"Questions for Donald Knuth","stance":"reference","why":"Acceptance speech where authors thank Knuth for literate programming.","quote":"In the acceptance speech, the writers thanked Knuth for the concept.","text":"A literate program, Physically Based Rendering, recently became the first book to win an Academy Award for it's technical contributions to the film industry. In the acceptance speech, the writers thanked Knuth for the concept. [VIDEO] The book can be compiled into the production-quality renderer [LINK]"}],"r":[["8bZh5LMaSmE",1],["2Op3QLzMgSY",1]]},"7jPKEyM44GU":{"wy":"HN cites it as the concrete demo behind discussions of Japan's TRON project and early smart-home ideas.","q":{"x":"the video is more informative than this article.","i":37724640,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37724640,"author":"rwmj","month":"2023-10","story":"The Tron Project: How Japan Almost Ruled IT","stance":"rec","why":"More informative than the article about the intelligent house.","quote":"the video is more informative than this article.","text":"A video about the intelligent house: [VIDEO] Edit: Sorry, it seems this was already posted. Still, the video is more informative than this article."},{"id":49384560,"author":"haunter","month":"2026-08","story":"Japan tried to build an operating system for the world, the US intervened","stance":"reference","why":"TRON house tech demo built in Tokyo in 1989.","quote":"There was a whole TRON house tech demo built in Tokyo back in 1989","text":"TRON wasn’t just an OS but a whole integrated system, it was meant to be used with different parts working together. There was a whole TRON house tech demo built in Tokyo back in 1989 [VIDEO] If you want to try here is the BTRON 4.5 media, should work in modern VMs [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":31557785,"author":"kkfx","month":"2022-05","story":"Unity Systems Home Manager Support","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"IBM have invented touchscreens with a dedicated pen (I ignore the name) around late '60s, decades ago we have seen [VIDEO] the point? Well, it's simple: 99% of today high tech came from PUBLIC or at least publicly founded research from after WWII to first '80s ALL THE REST is just popularisation, ingenerisation and added crap to ensure some private party profits carefully designed not to give any power to users. It's about time we came back to a public research for public progress..."}],"r":[["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["jAhiMCSvtCc",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["AvEqfg2sIH0",1]]},"MBVmnKuBocc":{"wy":"Cited in energy transition threads for ballpark numbers on mineral demand, though some commenters criticize its assumptions.","q":{"x":"Long video, but informative.","i":37437091,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37437091,"author":"SinParadise","month":"2023-09","story":"Our climate change debates are out of date","stance":"rec","why":"Long but informative video on mineral limits for renewables.","quote":"Long video, but informative.","text":"[VIDEO] Long video, but informative. TLDR: We don't have enough minerals (including reserves) to completely switch our energy from ff to renewables, assuming our current energy consumption flatline, and our renewable technology efficiency and mineral consumption remain the same."},{"id":36804234,"author":"MSFT_Edging","month":"2023-07","story":"We Will Never Run Out of Resources","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote source for mineral reserve shortfall claim.","quote":"We have about 20% of mineral reserves required for a full green energy conversion.[1]","text":"While obviously an opinion piece,these kinds of deranged articles are dangerous IMO. Between the two authors, one is a spokesman for a free-market thinktank, the other is a pop scientist known for writing books on the debunked parallel universe theories. This kind of \"oh we'll figure it out\" kind of attitude is why every major nation's plan for climate change includes the imaginary technology of carbon capture. We have about 20% of mineral reserves required for a full green energy conversion.[1] These type of think tank grifters(I'm sorry I cannot think of a better term for these people) have a single goal, to throw us all over the cliff of climate change while enabling their patrons to extract as much value from us on the way down. Due to this prevailing \"we'll figure it out\" opinion, every major country in the world has increased their carbon emissions, continues to destroy environments, and overall has done very little to counter pollution, emissions, and fossil fuel usage as a whole. Oh you're going to mine at the bottom of the ocean? Sure, that sounds about as likely as Musk actually sending himself to Mars. But here's the thing, we didn't figure it out! The polar vortex jet stream has been interrupted, we've broken an extremely important meteorological system due to the endless search for growth, and these people want us to do more growing! more profit! At what cost? We still pretend like carbon capture will save us, that telling the average joe to recycle will save us,"},{"id":34271757,"author":"daniel1969","month":"2023-01","story":"Does the world have enough lithium to move to electric vehicles?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains lithium shortage problem with data.","quote":"Here is the presentation given by prof. Simon Michaux where the problem is explained in more detail and supported with data","text":"There might be enough lithium for EVs, but lithium is needed not only for EV batteries. Overall, there is no enough lithium to phase out fossil fuels. Not even for the first generation of technology devices. Here is the presentation given by prof. Simon Michaux where the problem is explained in more detail and supported with data: [VIDEO] Lithium is not the only problem, other minerals are too. And, just like the original article says - the pace of extraction."},{"id":33058971,"author":"TheLoafOfBread","month":"2022-10","story":"Toyota CEO talks about why he isn’t all-in on EVs","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on copper shortage limiting EVs.","quote":"Assoc Prof Simon Michaux on shortage of copper","text":"Toyota is probably only company who understands that poor people can't afford BEVs and will be left behind. And introducing another EV Hummer or Cybertruck for 100k USD won't really help them to get affordable EV. I am convinced that BEV are not the future, because there is not enough of material, especially there is not enough copper. Assoc Prof Simon Michaux on shortage of copper [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":33240521,"author":"evilos","month":"2022-10","story":"Germany extends lifetime of all 3 remaining nuclear plants","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in argument; link is incidental.","quote":"","text":"1) It is dangerous yes. That is the nature of the energy being so concentrated. But this concentration is a blessing because you don't need to mind nearly as much material, and it is far far easier to keep an eye on the waste. Where does the waste from coal/gas go? Into the air. It costs way more to try to contain the harms of those substances because they are the opposite of energy dense. Nuclear waste is such a tiny tiny amount that we just keep it on site. It's solid. It's not going to leak out of its containers. It just sits in concrete casks on site. Even better, it still has 98% of the energy in it so you don't really want to get rid of it. It can be used in breeder reactors to extract more energy. I quote this too much, but all the nuclear waste the US has ever generated would sit in a single football field, 10 yards high. 2) Nuclear is capital intensive AND the only energy generation that is forced to pre fund its own decommissioning and cleanup. The increased operating costs of nuclear plants is largely due to intentional mismanagement b/c of politics. For example, in France they force nuclear plants to stop outputting power when renewables are generating. They prioritize renewables because that's what politics dictates. They also mandated a cap on power allowed to be generated by nuclear plants, forcing the closure of perfectly good and already paid for plants, so they could buy more renewables. These privatized energy markets don't want stable cheap energy because "},{"id":33342332,"author":"ephbit","month":"2022-10","story":"Apple calls on global supply chain to decarbonize by 2030","stance":"reference","why":"Numbers on decarbonization limits at minute 40.","quote":"Here are some numbers at minute 40","text":"Easy one. Apple pays a premium (to their suppliers) compared to what other non-luxury-hipster-feelgood companies can, thus buying up those (more or less) green capacities (which are scarce). Which leaves those other companies less of these green capacities. Which makes their production less green. Don't get me wrong, it's totally ok they do that. But it shouldn't lead people to think, that since Apple/Tesla succeeds, humanity doesn't need to drastically change its way of doing things. Sustainable ressources are scarce. One company stocks up on them -> others are pushed to less green sources or they have to reduce output (which they try to avoid obviously). Yeah, sure, there's also positive effects such as economies of scale improving things with sustainable business when there's more demand for it. But, in my opinion, the mining and sourcing of raw materials and the supply of renewable energy is in a pretty inelastic situation right now because of physical constraints. This inelasticity means: one players buys up more, others are pushed elsewhere. > ... falling prey to the fallacy that decarbonization is impossible. Oh it is possible. It's not possible as fast as most politicians make it appear. Here are some numbers at minute 40: \"Assoc Prof Simon Michaux - The quantity of metals required to manufacture just one generation of renewable technology to phase out fossil fuels\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":32741933,"author":"TheLoafOfBread","month":"2022-09","story":"The energy historian who says rapid decarbonization is a fantasy","stance":"reference","why":"Longer presentation on copper shortage.","quote":"Here is a longer presentation from same author from this year about lack of copper","text":"Here is a longer presentation from same author from this year about lack of copper to even start with renewables as planned [VIDEO]"},{"id":32548140,"author":"s-lambert","month":"2022-08","story":"I Choose Optimism","stance":"reference","why":"Estimate of metals needed to switch to renewables.","quote":"Here's a link to a talk from an associate professor doing an estimation of how much metals are needed to switch to renewable energy","text":"Solar energy isn't free, it takes a lot of metals just to turn solar energy into electrical energy (including batteries for storage) and they don't last forever. Here's a link to a talk from an associate professor doing an estimation of how much metals are needed to switch to renewable energy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32560443,"author":"dimtion","month":"2022-08","story":"EV shipping is set to blow internal combustion engines out of the water","stance":"reference","why":"Ballpark analysis of material needs for battery replacement of fossil fuels.","quote":"This presentation does ballpark analysis of the problem","text":"On the flip side, the amount of materials, metals, and energy necessary to produce enough batteries to replace current fossil fuel usages, are order of magnitude larger than the current world production and known reserves. This presentation does ballpark analysis of the problem: [VIDEO] Even if the numbers are off by an order of magnitude, them problem remains worrisome and difficult to solve at scale."},{"id":32612074,"author":"TheLoafOfBread","month":"2022-08","story":"California votes to ban new gas car sales by 2035","stance":"reference","why":"Seminar on metal shortages for renewable tech.","quote":"Exploration for more at required volumes will be difficult, with this seminar addressing these issues.","text":"I don't believe that BEV economics will improve. It could actually get worse as we are running out of material, especially copper The quantity of metal required to make just one generation of renewable tech units to replace fossil fuels, is much larger than first thought. Current mining production of these metals is not even close to meeting demand. Current reported mineral reserves are also not enough in size. Most concerning is copper as one of the flagged shortfalls. Exploration for more at required volumes will be difficult, with this seminar addressing these issues. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32645328,"author":"elihu","month":"2022-08","story":"The Mining of Minerals and the Limits to Growth (2021) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Longer talk on mineral limits; commenter critiques assumptions.","quote":"There's also this much longer talk he gave that I watched recently","text":"There's also this much longer talk he gave that I watched recently: [VIDEO] The premise is that we don't have enough minerals to transition to a decarbonized economy if we just do the most obvious, naive thing. For some reason, he assumes we need a battery capable of storing the world's energy use for 4 weeks, and then shows that there's no way we can manufacture that amount of lithium ion batteries. His analysis also assumes pretty heavy use of hydrogen for ground transport to get around the limitations of batteries, despite being rather energy-inefficient by comparison. I'm actually kind of optimistic: his result just means we can't do things that don't scale, but we could do other things instead. Rather than building huge chemical batteries for grid storage, we can use pumped hydro and expand our power grids so we can move energy between continents and buffer out renewable energy variability and day/night cycles. Instead of using hydrogen fuel cells or huge batteries for ground transport, we can electrify our major highways so trucks only need small batteries to get from the freeway to their delivery/pickup point. (We could also rely on trains a lot more than we do.) Instead of building billions of brand new cars, we can convert many of the cars we already have to electric if they're still in good shape and worth the effort. We can stop using so much copper in everything, and use aluminum instead where it makes sense."},{"id":37437256,"author":"SinParadise","month":"2023-09","story":"Our climate change debates are out of date","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Are we going realistically find enough undiscovered lithium reserves that is the same size as our known reserves as of 2022","text":"Based on his calculations from a video a year ago. He projects that we would need 940M tons of lithium, while 2022 reported reserves is 95M tons.[0] Are we going realistically find enough undiscovered lithium reserves that is the same size as our known reserves as of 2022, even assuming that he is off by a factor of 4? [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"aVnRQRdePp4":{"wy":"HN links it as an expert teardown perspective on Tesla engineering, used to back up claims about motors and electronics quality.","q":{"x":"this video, which is very interesting","i":19150161,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19150161,"author":"ravedave5","month":"2019-02","story":"Amazon, GM in talks to invest in electric pickup truck maker Rivian: sources","stance":"rec","why":"Sandy Munro's deep dive into Tesla","quote":"this video, which is very interesting","text":"I really hope Rivian makes it. They seem to have the right idea working from the top of the market down like Tesla. They could probably survive on only high-end low volume sales for quite a while. I hope Tesla gives them time and room to grow before introducing their own truck. Also - There was a video with Sandy Munro on it and he couldn't talk about Rivian so he's either working with them or someone who wants to invest in them. So it seems like they are getting the right brains working on their problems. Edit - this video, which is very interesting - [VIDEO]"},{"id":21667390,"author":"zaroth","month":"2019-11","story":"Burned-Out Flash Trips Up Older Teslas","stance":"reference","why":"Munro discussing Model 3 electronics quality","quote":"Here he is talking about the Model 3","text":"> He had negative things to say about the quality of the rest of their electrical components... That is simply false. Here he is talking about the Model 3: [VIDEO] Their margins are better than their competitors because Tesla is vertically integrated, allowing them to do things like reduce the number of harnesses by 40%, and design solutions like the \"SuperBottle\" which Munro once said is his favorite part of the Model 3: [LINK] And there's a world of difference between after-market upgrades (of which there are plenty for Tesla) and the manufacturer taking their latest generation technology and manufacturing and designing it so that they can slot it into late-model cars, all while constantly shipping firmware upgrades to their entire fleet which make the cars better in every possible way from speed, handling, range, efficiency, user experience, safety, entertainment, etc."},{"id":20662725,"author":"Geee","month":"2019-08","story":"Electric motor design claims remarkable improvements","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Halbach array magnets in Tesla motors","quote":"Tesla uses Halbach array magnets in their motors.","text":"Tesla uses Halbach array magnets in their motors. It's explained here (this guy discovered it by accident): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_yMMTVVJI4c",1]]},"eakKfY5aHmY":{"wy":"Cited as a benchmark for leaderless swarm behavior that robots and drones could aim to match.","q":{"x":"when drones can do this, drones will be cool","i":9116788,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":31640922,"author":"pflanze","month":"2022-06","story":"The mesmerising murmurations of Europe’s starlings","stance":"rec","why":"Higher-quality starling murmuration recording","quote":"One with higher quality (not the same recording)","text":"One with higher quality (not the same recording): [VIDEO] . Youtube also refers to some other nice recordings from that one. (There's also a video with intermediate quality, and otherwise distracting parts, but amazing formations too: [LINK] / [LINK] ) Sadly I haven't found one with (just) original sound (no music)."},{"id":17420829,"author":"jbob2000","month":"2018-06","story":"Colorado Lawmakers Seek to Make Flying Drones Over Wildfires a Felony","stance":"reference","why":"Example of swarming behavior drones could mimic","quote":"I'm waiting for someone to develop drones that swarm like starlings","text":"If you manage to develop a drone swarm system that drops things, the last thing it will be used for is fighting fires. I'm waiting for someone to develop drones that swarm like starlings ( [VIDEO] ). You could take down an entire battlegroup with a couple thousand suicide drones."},{"id":9116788,"author":"sounds","month":"2015-02","story":"A girl who gets gifts from birds","stance":"reference","why":"Benchmark for impressive drone swarm behavior","quote":"when drones can do this, drones will be cool","text":"As someone who has worked a LOT with drones of all form factors (quad copters, propeller drones, battery and dead dinosaur-fueled), when drones can do this, drones will be cool: [VIDEO] Context: [LINK]"},{"id":6464759,"author":"lutusp","month":"2013-09","story":"Professor Develops 'Brain' for Robots","stance":"reference","why":"Starlings flying without a leader as an example","quote":"watch a flight of a thousand starlings maneuver adeptly without collisions","text":"> But why is there a \"leader\" to begin with? In swarms of birds the older birds do have some specific roles, but real \"leaders\" seem to me a brittle approach. I agree, and it seems nature agrees. Anyone who think a leader is required should watch a flight of a thousand starlings maneuver adeptly without collisions, a swarm of bees or ants carry out their duties without any command hierarchy, or a modern government rob the people blind with nary a collision between the overwhelming number of redundant departments. [VIDEO] (starlings in flight)"}]},"hKcOkWzj0_s":{"wy":"Cited as evidence for OCaml's readability benefits and for hiring in less popular languages.","q":{"x":"People who are nervous about hiring people in a relatively unpopular language should watch this video[1]","i":6389125,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6389125,"author":"gnuvince","month":"2013-09","story":"Where my mouth is","stance":"rec","why":"Minsky on hiring for a relatively unpopular language, at 1:01:00.","quote":"People who are nervous about hiring people in a relatively unpopular language should watch this video[1]","text":"People who are nervous about hiring people in a relatively unpopular language should watch this video[1] at the 1:01.00 mark by Yaron Minsky. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16298440,"author":"bufferoverflow","month":"2018-02","story":"A.I. Has Arrived in Investing, Humans Are Still Dominating","stance":"reference","why":"Jane Street tech talk on using Caml in automated trading.","quote":"Jane Street, for instance, has a tech talk on how they take advantage of Caml in automated trading.","text":"That's just not true. There are whole companies based on algorithmic trading. Jane Street, for instance, has a tech talk on how they take advantage of Caml in automated trading. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13260021,"author":"imaginenore","month":"2016-12","story":"LSTM Neural Networks for Time Series Prediction","stance":"reference","why":"Jane Street talk as evidence that firms openly discuss their trading tech.","quote":"Jane Street is a company that does it, and they are quite open about their tech approach","text":"You most certainly can, just not with such naïve approach. Jane Street is a company that does it, and they are quite open about their tech approach (just not about their specific models): [VIDEO] Jane Street went from zero to trading $1 trillion in volume in just 15 years."},{"id":7952454,"author":"hoggle","month":"2014-06","story":"Reading Lamport, again ","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant talk on how OCaml gets used at Jane Street.","quote":"Relevant video on how OCaml gets used at Jane Street","text":"Jane Street is one of the few companies which are betting heavily on OCaml, it makes sense to go all functional in the high frequency trading world I guess. It's purportedly been C/C++ for most of that industry, probably not the best toolset for keeping error rates low in a complex environment. Fascinating to read how far beyond language decisions they need to think to shield themselves against outages (and competitors?). I did a little bit of experimentation with OCaml by playing with bitcoin exchange APIs during the China boom some months ago, but I unfortunately didn't push further to a point where I'd be using Jane Street's open source libraries. It still was very interesting and IMHO totally worth it if not just for the functional programming practice. Relevant video on how OCaml gets used at Jane Street: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6383237,"author":"wtetzner","month":"2013-09","story":"Clojure for the Brave and True – Functional Programming","stance":"reference","why":"Minsky on why OCaml made trading code more readable and understandable.","quote":"Yaron Minsky explained that one of the main benefits of it was that people could read and understand the code much better","text":"Jane Street Capital uses OCaml for their trading system. Yaron Minsky explained that one of the main benefits of it was that people could read and understand the code much better than the other languages they tried (including C#). Yaron's talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5110332,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2013-01","story":"Quantopian Brings Algorithmic Trading To The Masses","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for point that market making and arbitrage are the pros' bread and butter.","quote":"market making and arbitrage is the bread and butter of the pros, not signal trading.","text":"> We (I work for Quantopian) will surely support other order types when we support live trading. I suspect they won't be true limit orders, but stop orders (true limit orders wouldn't slip). Also, for a passive trading algorithm its important to have level II data (the order book), or the algorithm is flying blind. Re 1, its not the size of the data set which prevents overfitting (larger data sets actually make it more likely), but the use of it when developing/training the algorithm. Repeated testing and tweaking the algorithm will overfit, unless one is careful to maintain the complexity of the algorithm. Anyway, best of luck to your users! But they should be warned that active trading on signals (price prediction) is the hard way to algorithmic profit. That's why market making and arbitrage is the bread and butter of the pros, not signal trading.[1] 1. said as much here somewhere in the first 20:00 [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["aFwVf5a3pZM",1]]},"kXpzp4RDGJI":{"wy":"Linked as a vivid visualization of ATP synthase as a rotary molecular machine, often in discussions of protein machines and cellular energy.","q":{"x":"has a pretty cool visualization of how ATP synthase uses rotation","i":30491237,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30491237,"author":"whoisburbansky","month":"2022-02","story":"Discovery of ultrafast myosin, its amino acid sequence, and structural features","stance":"rec","why":"Cool visualization of ATP synthase rotating to make ATP.","quote":"has a pretty cool visualization of how ATP synthase uses rotation","text":"Professor Lue from Harvard has a pretty cool visualization of how ATP synthase uses rotation and the energy produced through cellular respiration to produce ATP at [VIDEO] , slightly more visceral than digging through a paper for a layperson."},{"id":30491450,"author":"dopylitty","month":"2022-02","story":"Discovery of ultrafast myosin, its amino acid sequence, and structural features","stance":"rec","why":"ATP synthase shown as tiny motors/pumps inside mitochondria.","quote":"And then you get into the other protein machines like the ATP synthase in the mitochondria [2]","text":"Motor proteins are so incredibly cool. Like many others I was blown away by the 3D animated Inner Life of the Cell[1] showing these proteins physically walking around in the cell. Previously I had this idea that cells were just blobs of jelly and somehow they interacted with each-other to produce macro phenomena like muscle movement. And then you get into the other protein machines like the ATP synthase in the mitochondria [2] (also linked below) which are actually little motors/pumps. And we all have billions of these inside use spinning away. It's just amazing. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43849682,"author":"kragen","month":"2025-04","story":"Reversible computing with mechanical links and pivots","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It seems like you've misremembered the situation somewhat. Merkle developed several of his families of mechanical logic, including this one, in order to answer some criticisms of Drexler's earliest mechanical nanotechnology proposals. Specifically: 1. Chemists were concerned that rod logic knobs touching each other would form chemical bonds and remain stuck together, rather than disengaging for the next clock cycle. (Macroscopic metal parts usually don't work this way, though \"cold welding\" is a thing, especially in space.) So this proposal‚ like some earlier ones like Merkle's buckling-spring logic, avoids any contact between unconnected parts of the mechanism, whether sliding or coming into and out of contact. 2. Someone calculated the power density of one of Drexler's early proposals and found that it exceeded the power density of high explosives during detonation, which obviously poses significant challenges for mechanism durability. You could just run them many orders of magnitude slower, but Merkle tackled the issue instead by designing reversible logic families which can dissipate arbitrarily little power per logic operation, only dissipating energy to erase stored bits. So, there's nothing preventing this kind of mechanism from scaling down to single atoms, and we already have working mechanisms like the atomic force microscope which demonstrate that even intermittent single-atom contact can work mechanically in just the way you'd expect it to from your macroscopic in"},{"id":30357939,"author":"nosianu","month":"2022-02","story":"Citizen sleuths exposed pollution from a century-old Michigan factory (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Proteins are machines, pretty much literally.","text":"Chemicals don't need to chemically react. Especially for biology, merely existing is enough. Lots of biological regulation is done by influencing three-dimensional shapes of the huge highly complex structures called proteins. Organic chemistry especially is a science of complex three dimensional structures and its dynamic changes, at least as important as actually altering the chemical structure. Often they are not static but have moving parts, because not all bonds restrict movement of the atoms connected through that bond in relation to the other part. Many have what's call binding sites - other molecules fitting to those sites won't be altered or alter the chemical structure of the protein. They alter the shape . Proteins are machines, pretty much literally. For example, there are channel proteins, and a molecule docking on a binding site of such a protein might open or close that channel for certain molecules. The variety of those molecular machinery (really, literally) is huge. You can influence machines not just by breaking them down, you can just throw a spanner between their gears. Things that matter are not just shape and size, but also polarity of the parts. Polar components of a molecule influence polar components of proteins if they can get close enough, which influence the shape. Binding sites, as only one example: Description: [LINK] Images: [LINK] Example - 22 second movie - for a protein-machine whose function it is to move stuff around: [LINK] Example for ion"},{"id":27814619,"author":"jcims","month":"2021-07","story":"Temozolomide Is Explosive","stance":"reference","why":"More detailed animation and narration of ATP synthesis, slower paced.","quote":"This animation and associated narration is much more detailed, but a bit slow","text":"This is an animation and cursory explanation of ATP synthesis, with suitably dramatic music: [LINK] This animation and associated narration is much more detailed, but a bit slow for something you haven't vetted your interest in yet :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":27413360,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2021-06","story":"Modern cities modelled as “super-cells” rather than multicellular organisms","stance":"reference","why":"ATP synthase animation, likened to a hydroelectric turbine.","quote":"I think of it as a hydroelectric power plant.","text":"ATP synthase! [VIDEO] [LINK] I think of it as a hydroelectric power plant. Hydrogen atoms are water, the electrochemical gradient is gravity and the enzyme is the turbine."}],"r":[["y-uuk4Pr2i8",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1]]},"rKnDgT73v8s":{"wy":"Used as the primary source for what Go's creators originally promised, such as fast compiles, systems-language framing, safety and generics.","q":{"x":"A good starting point is their FAQ page and also the tech talk at youtube","i":934736,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":934736,"author":"ypavan","month":"2009-11","story":"Google Go: A New Programming Language That’s Python Meets C++","stance":"rec","why":"Go announcement talk covering goals and design","quote":"A good starting point is their FAQ page and also the tech talk at youtube","text":"From the Tech Talk PDF, it is mentioned that Robert Griesemer, Ken Thompson, and Rob Pike started the project in late 2007. Ian Lance Taylor and Russ Cox joined in 2008. And the goal, as they mention, seems to have the efficiency of a statically-typed compiled language with the ease of programming of a dynamic language but with - Safety: type-safe and memory-safe. Good support for concurrency and communication. Efficient, latency-free garbage collection. High-speed compilation. They are aiming at a lightweight type system with no implicit conversions and no more 0x80ULL. A good starting point is their FAQ page and also the tech talk at youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49130399,"author":"win311fwg","month":"2026-08","story":"Golang proposal: container/: generic collection types","stance":"reference","why":"Generics were expected eventually per announcement","quote":"In short: not yet","text":"> But looking at how adamant Go used to be on the whole \"No Generics\" stance What \"no generics\" stance? Ian Lance Taylor started working on generics before anyone outside of Google had even heard of Go and when the rest of the world found out about Go it was understood that they would come someday. \"In short: not yet\" — [VIDEO]"},{"id":49130449,"author":"win311fwg","month":"2026-08","story":"Golang proposal: container/: generic collection types","stance":"reference","why":"Announcement showed generics not yet figured out","quote":"","text":"Does it? It was only preventable by not releasing Go, which isn't exactly the greatest of solutions. As you know from when Go was first broadcast to the world, they hadn't figured out how to make generics fit[1]. It is not like they weren't trying. Ian Lance Taylor is regarded for beginning work on generics before anyone outside of Google had even heard of Go. \"In short: not yet\" What was surprising is that after Go was released as an open source project, nobody across the whole wide world ever came up with a workable solution. You can sense the optimism in that original announcement that later turned to \"Go might never get generics\" dispair. As you'll recall, it finally took convincing Philip Wadler (of Haskell and Java generics fame) to help. Without that lucky break, which almost certainly never would have happened if Go was still some basement project used by nobody, Go probably still wouldn't have generics. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":49132382,"author":"win311fwg","month":"2026-08","story":"Golang proposal: container/: generic collection types","stance":"reference","why":"Announcement said generics would come","quote":"First they said they would","text":"Huh? First they said they would: [VIDEO] After a decade of nobody coming up with a solution that did turn to believing that Go would never be able to add generics. That is true. The air of defeat did become quite strong. But the Haskell guy did eventually came along to share his expertise and the rest is history."},{"id":49149760,"author":"win311fwg","month":"2026-08","story":"Go 1.27 Interactive Tour","stance":"reference","why":"Announcement showed generics were always expected","quote":"Everyone else knows that they were always expected to be included at some point","text":"> Am I the only one who’s absolutely shocked that Go finally is embracing generics? Yes. Everyone else knows that they were always expected to be included at some point, as told when Go was first announced to the world: [VIDEO] > So what changed? Philip Wadler, of Haskell fame, decided to help? This was well publicized on HN at the time. > I’m not buying the “it took us 20 years to understand how to do it correctly” argument If I recall correctly, Pike suggested that they never did come to that understanding without the outside help. I can understand why you are surprised that it took 20 years for someone with the right expertise to show up. You can get the sense in the above announcement that even the Go team thought that open sourcing the project would attract the right talent far, far earlier. But, now that we have hindsight, we can see that all the experts on HN who could have been the missing positive contributor were too busy complaining that Go didn't have generics. There wasn't enough time left for them to lend a hand. There are only so many hours in the day."},{"id":44679326,"author":"phire","month":"2025-07","story":"There is no memory safety without thread safety","stance":"reference","why":"Original announcement listed memory safety as an explicit goal","quote":"It's critical that the language be type-safe and that it be memory-safe.","text":"Keep in mind that the 2012 presentations dates to 10 months after Rust's first release, and its version of \"Memory Safety\" was collecting quite a bit of attention. I'd argue the definition was already changing by this point. It's also possible that Go was already discovering their version of \"Memory Safety\" just wasn't safe enough. If you go back to the original 2009 announcement talk, \"Memory Safety\" is listed as an explicit goal, with no carveouts: \"Safety is critical. It's critical that the language be type-safe and that it be memory-safe.\" \"It is important that a program not be able to derive a bad address and just use it; That a program that compiles is type-safe and memory-safe. That is a critical part of making robust software, and that's just fundamental.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":44691425,"author":"phire","month":"2025-07","story":"There is no memory safety without thread safety","stance":"reference","why":"Pike's definition of memory-safe in announcement","quote":"The program should not be able to derive a bad address and just use it","text":"True, \"have a garbage collector\" was never the formal definition, it was more \"automatic memory management\". But this predates the work on Rust's ownership system and while there were theories of static automatic memory management, all practical examples of automatic memory management were some form of garbage collection. If you go to the original 2009 announcement presentation for Go [1], not only is \"memory-safety\" listed as a primary goal, but Pike provides the definition of memory-safe that they are using, which is: \"The program should not be able to derive a bad address and just use it\" Which Go mostly achieves with a combination of garbage collection and not allowing pointer arithmetic. The source of Go's failure is concurrency, which has a knock-on effect that invalidates memory safety. Note that stated goal from 2009 is \"good support for concurrency\", not \"concurrent-safe\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43682000,"author":"9rx","month":"2025-04","story":"Problems with Go channels (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Generics acknowledged as desired feature from day one","quote":"From day one it was explicitly called out as a feature Go should have","text":"> The only things I can think of which Go did that seemed blatantly wrong in that era would be the lack of generics and proper enums. Typescript added \"improper\" enums several years later. Which is especially interesting as it is the one feature in Typescript that doesn't map directly to Javascript. I'm not sure even that one was the settled science back then. Generics were well established at that time, but that one didn't escape the Go authors. From day one it was explicitly called out as a feature Go should have, but that they hadn't figured out how to integrate it. [VIDEO] Despite Taylor's admirably insistent efforts to find a solution (with 8+ proposals to his name, dating back to before Go was released to the public!), it ultimately required convincing an outside expert to lend a hand. > I know others find the type system lacking It may be lacking by today's standards where types are all the rage, but at the time we should also remember that it was the case that types simply weren't cool. People had grown tired of \"doing XML sit-ups\" and had fully embraced dynamic languages in reaction to that. Go was built in that time, for that time, explicitly intending to be a language that felt like a dynamic language but with the performance advantages of a static language. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32627195,"author":"hsn915","month":"2022-08","story":"I like Odin","stance":"reference","why":"Pike framing Go as a systems language at announcement","quote":"When Go was first announced ~2009, Rob Pike explicitly framed it as a systems language","text":"When Go was first announced ~2009, Rob Pike explicitly framed it as a systems language [VIDEO] I would not say it's the same as Java or C#. The crucial difference is that it compiles to a native executable binary file, not something that needs a virtual machine."},{"id":9663839,"author":"legutierr","month":"2015-06","story":"Three months of Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Pike discusses compile time as a design goal","quote":"compilation time was one of the primary metric that Rob Pike et al were optimizing for","text":"If I remember properly, when Go was first developed, compilation time was one of the primary metric that Rob Pike et al were optimizing for, and drove major aspects of its design. It shouldn't be surprising that Go blows other systems out of the water in this regard. Here he is talking about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8213268,"author":"andralex","month":"2014-08","story":"D for the Win","stance":"reference","why":"Go announced as a systems programming language","quote":"Go has been announced as a systems programming language","text":"Go has been announced as a systems programming language: [VIDEO] . After some backlash the slogan has been changed quietly."},{"id":7009753,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2014-01","story":"Using Rust for an Undergraduate OS Course","stance":"reference","why":"Go announced as systems language for things like web servers","quote":"And it's a systems language in the sense that we intend it to be used to write things like web servers","text":"> it seems initially it was meant to be those kind of \"systems\" From the announcement talk: \"And it's a systems language in the sense that we intend it to be used to write things like web servers\" [VIDEO] I don't think that Google ever wanted to develop a new OS kernel."},{"id":5203932,"author":"dexen","month":"2013-02","story":"Google Go: The Good, the Bad, and the Meh","stance":"reference","why":"Go's fast compile times","quote":"Go compiles crazy fast","text":"For performance of compilation . Go compiles crazy fast -- think two seconds for complete compiler and runtime on a notebook. This provides for rapid hack-compile-debug iterations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4517076,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2012-09","story":"Will Go be the new go-to programming language?","stance":"reference","why":"Item in a personal list of Go talks","quote":"","text":"The \"Talks and Videos\" page ( [LINK] ) is outdated as well. Here's my personal list: Why Learn Go? [LINK] Short interview with Rob Pike, OSCON 2012 Get Started with Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand A Tour of Go [LINK] Russ Cox Go Concurrency Patterns [LINK] Rob Pike, Google I/O 2012 Goroutines, Channels Meet the Go Team [LINK] Panel (Pike, Thompson, Griesemer, Gerrand, Fitzpatrick, Simons), Google I/O 2012 Computing Map Tiles with Go on App Engine [LINK] Chris Broadfoot, Andrew Gerrand, Google I/O 2012 Go in Production [LINK] Gustavo Niemeyer, Google I/O 2012 Go: Code that grows with grace [LINK] (Contains some audio glitches) Andrew Gerrand Interview with Rob Pike [LINK] Rob Pike, GOTO Conference Lexical Scanning in Go [LINK] Rob Pike Cuddle, A Go AppEngine Demo [LINK] Andrew Gerrand Building Integrated Apps on Google's Cloud Platform [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, Google Developer Day Japan 2011 Real World Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, I/O BootCamp 2011 Go: it's that simple?! [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, OSDC 2011 Practical Go Programming [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, FOSDEM 2011 Writing Web Apps in Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, Rob Pike, Google I/O 2011 Go Programming [LINK] Rob Pike, Google I/O 2010 Another Go at Language Design [LINK] Rob Pike, Stanford University Public Static Void [LINK] Rob Pike, OSCON 2010 The Go Programming Language [VIDEO] Rob Pike, Google Tech Talk"},{"id":1993573,"author":"devrim","month":"2010-12","story":"OOP practiced backwards is \"POO\"","stance":"reference","why":"Rob Pike explains problems of OOP","quote":"problems of OOP is explained in Go Language video by Rob Pike*","text":"thinking OOP as a solution may lead to an article like this whereas it merely is a vehicle. you may or may not use it. problems of OOP is explained in Go Language video by Rob Pike*, however very intellectually stated arguments in this article are mainly false. [VIDEO]"},{"id":939620,"author":"allenbrunson","month":"2009-11","story":"Why I'm not very excited about Go","stance":"reference","why":"Rob Pike talk, interesting but syntax concerns","quote":"a lot of it is really interesting","text":"programming languages influence how you think about programming to such a degree that it's difficult to reason objectively about them. it's rare for me to watch long videos, because they are information-poor for the time investment, but i watched rob pike talk for an hour about go this evening. a lot of it is really interesting, but a lot of things about the syntax made me think: ow, that's going to be painful for me if it becomes successful. rob's talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5821150,"author":"JeanPierre","month":"2013-06","story":"Replacing Clever Code with Unremarkable Code in Go","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Rob Pike mentions in that type safety is of high importance for Golang","text":"I would like to add on to this: Generics may not be the correct solution, but I would really like a way to make collections which are general enough to be used by any datatype. As of now, I cannot do that without doing the typical `interface{}` approach. The built-in data structures + channels are able to handle this, but if I just want a set of elements, what do I do? Make a `map[foo] bool`? If I see such a piece of code in an unfamiliar code base, I have no idea whether to test for key existence or whether I have to test whether the bool is true. A generic set would leave me puzzled and type unsafe[1]: What types could possibly exist in this set? A set of foos is not that hard to comprehend, and is in fact very much more straightforward to understand than a generic set, which in unfamiliar code may contain anything, or a mapping from foos to bools. [1]: Rob Pike mentions in [VIDEO] that type safety is of high importance for Golang, but how does one achieve that if all the different datatypes I implement/need use `interface {}` where I have to cast all values afterwards? That seems very type unsafe, from where I stand."}]},"ua5aOFi-DKs":{"wy":"Cited as a mathematician's debunking of numerical claims of 2020 election fraud.","q":{"x":"has analyzed some of the numerical allegations of fraud","i":25553156,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25553156,"author":"nitrogen","month":"2020-12","story":"California Public Utilities Commission fired director who exposed missing $200M","stance":"rec","why":"Mathematician analyzes numerical claims of 2020 election fraud.","quote":"has analyzed some of the numerical allegations of fraud","text":"I am replying for the sake of the hidden sibling comment: I don't watch any major media coverage of politics, but a mathematician and comedian I follow on YouTube (from a different country who presumably has no horse in the race) has analyzed some of the numerical allegations of fraud: - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":41126132,"author":"nick238","month":"2024-08","story":"Suspicious data pattern in recent Venezuelan election","stance":"reference","why":"Debunks a flawed statistical claim about 2020 US election results.","quote":"the amount of data that every county in every US state produces makes systematic fraud pretty much impossible.","text":"There were some pretty shit analysises of the 2020 US elections that Matt Parker covered with videos like \"Why do Biden's votes not follow Benford's law?\"[1] and \"Why was Biden's win calculated to be 1 in 1,000,000,000,000,000?\"[2]. I don't know about other countries, but the amount of data that every county in every US state produces makes systematic fraud pretty much impossible. If there's literally only 3 numbers produced by the Venezuelan government, you need to be seriously incompetent to have detectable fraud because techniques like Benford's or Zipf's law need lots of individual numbers. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25666928,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2021-01","story":"U.S. Capitol Locked Down Amid Escalating Protests","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this is basic high school level statistics they can't even get right.","text":"> Can you link me to those majority of cases? [LINK] [LINK] And this third one just shows how much of a joke some of the claims in the lawsuits are: [VIDEO] Like, this is basic high school level statistics they can't even get right."}],"r":[["lyqt6u5_sHA",1],["etx0k1nLn78",1],["aokNwKx7gM8",1]]},"9T1vfsHYiKY":{"wy":"HN points to it as the origin of the Party Parrot emote and as the famous viral kakapo clip whenever kakapo come up.","q":{"x":"The video of that encounter is the origin of the Party Parrot emote which you might have seen on Slack.","i":32907502,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":42801841,"author":"bitwize","month":"2025-01","story":"Lonely sunfish in Japan gets cardboard human friends","stance":"reference","why":"Sirocco the kakapo mounting Mark Carwardine","quote":"Here he is mounting and having a go at Mark Carwardine","text":"Famously there is a kakapō named Sirocco who imprinted on humans to the point of being sexually attracted to them rather than to other kakapō. Which is both a problem because with only 244 individuals left, the species needs as many mating pairs as it can get; and a blessing because his international fame and fondness for humans makes him an effective \"spokesbird\" for conservation. Here he is mounting and having a go at Mark Carwardine: [VIDEO] \"You are being shagged by a rare parrot!\""},{"id":32907502,"author":"richdougherty","month":"2022-09","story":"Caring for the only known full kākāpō feather cloak in the world","stance":"reference","why":"Origin of the Party Parrot emote","quote":"The video of that encounter is the origin of the Party Parrot emote which you might have seen on Slack.","text":"You may be able to see a real-life kākāpō by booking to see Sirocco, New Zealand's official spokesbird for conservation. I saw him once and he's pretty cool! Not sure if he's touring at the moment. [LINK] It wasn't quite as exciting as when Stephen Fry's film crew met him. The video of that encounter is the origin of the Party Parrot emote which you might have seen on Slack. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":32907507,"author":"kemayo","month":"2022-09","story":"Caring for the only known full kākāpō feather cloak in the world","stance":"reference","why":"Viral clip of a kakapo attempting to mate with a cameraman","quote":"a video that has gone viral a few times of one attempting to mate with a documentary cameraman's head","text":"If you're like me, you may mostly recognize kākāpō from a video that has gone viral a few times of one attempting to mate with a documentary cameraman's head: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30693986,"author":"EdwardDiego","month":"2022-03","story":"Endurance: Shackleton's lost ship is found in Antarctic","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example footage of the kakapo","quote":"","text":"I understand that, my two favourite New Zealand explorers (Charlie Douglas and George Dobson), are both very obscure but very admirable IMO - and they're obscure because they were the furthest from glory hounds that you can get. If you can get a hold of this book[0], it's a very interesting read (well, for me, anyway, being a Kiwi and all, especially the chapter where the editor collated Charlie Douglas' diary writings on our native birds, and what they were like for eating, which features some of our now rarest birds, he was quite fond of the flavour of kākāpō[1][2], which is critically endangered now). This article might be easier to access than an out of print book.[3] But, so many explorers that were contemporaries of Shackleton, like Amundsen, Nansen, Byrd, Scott, etc. _were_ glory hounds. Does that negate their exploration? I mean, it often meant that they took stupid risks to be \"first\", fully agree there. But if we eliminate the glory-hound polar explorers, there's precious few left. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":11770218,"author":"amatix","month":"2016-05","story":"Today is Towel Day","stance":"reference","why":"Kakapo Sirocco's encounter during Last Chance to See filming","quote":"with a Kakapo named Sirocco and he then became quite the internet celebrity","text":"During the filming of the TV series of Last Chance to See (with Stephen Fry) this happened [VIDEO] with a Kakapo named Sirocco and he then became quite the internet celebrity: [LINK]"},{"id":46251254,"author":"swyx","month":"2025-12","story":"OpenAI are quietly adopting skills, now available in ChatGPT and Codex CLI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the :partyparrot: emoji being from an actual real life documentary","text":"and this is my excuse to talk about the :partyparrot: emoji being from an actual real life documentary [VIDEO]"},{"id":32909791,"author":"csunbird","month":"2022-09","story":"Caring for the only known full kākāpō feather cloak in the world","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"there is a meme video about those parrot species","text":"Also, there is a meme video about those parrot species, a male parrot mistakes a zoologist to be a female parrot, and attempts intercourse with him: [VIDEO] The shaking parrot head emote with rainbow coloring in Slack is from this video :) Edit: Realized that someone already posted the video here below in the comments."}]},"Bmz67ErIRa4":{"wy":"Used mostly as a punchline or example of cheesy, unrealistic Hollywood depictions of hacking and over-stylized tech UIs.","m":[{"id":17376350,"author":"needz","month":"2018-06","story":"Revisting Neuromancer After Three Decades","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"older movies about technology (e.g. Hackers[0]) stylize digital events.","text":"The book was released in 1984. Could it be that his descriptions of tech were just true to the times? I imagine his stylistic embellishments were also important in the same way older movies about technology (e.g. Hackers[0]) stylize digital events. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47460977,"author":"dijksterhuis","month":"2026-03","story":"Our Commitment to Windows Quality","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"when i hear bollocks like \"agentic UX\" i think of things like this","text":"when i hear bollocks like \"agentic UX\" i think of things like this [VIDEO] i feel like someone high up in microsoft probably has this pinned in a epic or something somewhere"},{"id":28907804,"author":"jl2718","month":"2021-10","story":"MorphCharts","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"When I hear about VR/AR in data science, this is what I think of","text":"These are great to look at for something like tornado data, specifically because you probably don’t care at all about what the data tells you. For a while I was a data science consultant and then chief data scientist at a major organization, and all throughout, the most common request was for an interface that looked like “Minority Report”. I don’t even remember what that was, but, in almost all cases, these visualizations are something like corporate edutainment. If you are looking for an answer to a question, it is usually a number. Visualization can be useful if you don’t know why you are looking at the data in the first place, and even then, there is a choice between a chart that is made for understanding the data, and a ‘visualization’ that is made for entertaining the viewer. When I hear about VR/AR in data science, this is what I think of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12548863,"author":"cmdrfred","month":"2016-09","story":"'Mr. Robot' may be fiction, but its hacking plots are all too real","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it wasn't much better than this","text":"I only watched a few episodes, but the whole \"She needs to learn to hack in 24 hours\" bullshit was a little too much for me. Everything she was doing could have been a script. There was 0 reason to have her punching in commands like that, and either way that's simply using Linux. 'python hack-it.py' would have sufficed. If you haven't seen it, it wasn't much better than this: [VIDEO] and at least that was entertaining."},{"id":12556227,"author":"touristtam","month":"2016-09","story":"'Mr. Robot' may be fiction, but its hacking plots are all too real","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Hacker (film) was painful to watch. So much cheesyness.","text":"> 'python hack-it.py' would have sufficed. If you haven't seen it, it wasn't much better than this: [VIDEO] and at least that was entertaining. Hacker (film) was painful to watch. So much cheesyness."}]},"GcqQ1ebBqkc":{"wy":"Cited for Amodei's claim that each model is profitable on its own, which commenters debate and dispute.","q":{"x":"Dario Amodei has said that their models actually have a good return, even when accounting for training costs [0].","i":46629713,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46852409,"author":"sothatsit","month":"2026-02","story":"Show HN: NanoClaw – \"Clawdbot\" in 500 lines of TS with Apple container isolation","stance":"reference","why":"Amodei on model returns covering training costs","quote":"Dario Amodei has said that their models actually have a good return, even when accounting for training costs [0].","text":"Dario Amodei has said that their models actually have a good return, even when accounting for training costs [0]. They lose money because of R&D, training the next bigger models, and I assume also investment in other areas like data centers. Sam Altman has made similar statements, and Chinese companies also often serve their models very cheaply. All of this makes me believe them when they say they are profitable on API usage. Usage on the plans is a bit more unknown. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46629713,"author":"sothatsit","month":"2026-01","story":"Furiosa: 3.5x efficiency over H100s","stance":"reference","why":"Amodei on model returns covering training costs","quote":"Dario Amodei has said that their models actually have a good return, even when accounting for training costs [0].","text":"Inference costs scale linearly with usage. R&D expenses do not. That's not to mention that Dario Amodei has said that their models actually have a good return, even when accounting for training costs [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45902241,"author":"jonas21","month":"2025-11","story":"Yann LeCun to depart Meta and launch AI startup focused on 'world models'","stance":"reference","why":"Amodei's claim that each model is profitable on its own","quote":"Dario Amodei from Anthropic has made the claim that if you looked at each model as a separate business, it would be profitable","text":"Dario Amodei from Anthropic has made the claim that if you looked at each model as a separate business, it would be profitable [1], i.e. each model brings in more revenue over its lifetime than the total of training + inference costs. It's only because you're simultaneously training the next generation of models, which are larger and more expensive to train, but aren't generating revenue yet, that the company as a whole loses money in a given year. Now, it's not like he opened up Anthropic's books for an audit, so you don't necessarily have to trust him. But you do need to believe that either (a) what he is saying is roughly true or (b) he is making the sort of fraudulent statements that could get you sent to prison. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47326299,"author":"Avshalom","month":"2026-03","story":"No, it doesn't cost Anthropic $5k per Claude Code user","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he doesn't actually say that each model has been profitable.","text":"[VIDEO] is on such but he doesn't actually say that each model has been profitable. He says \"You paid $100 million and then it made $200 million of revenue. There's some cost to inference with the model, but let's just assume in this cartoonish cartoon example that even if you add those two up, you're kind of in a good state. So, if every model was a company, the model is actually, in this example is actually profitable. What's going on is that at the same time\" importantly you'll notice that he's talking revenue, and assumes that inference is cheap enough/profitable enough that 100M + Inferance_Over_Lifetime < 200M"},{"id":45108456,"author":"Avshalom","month":"2025-09","story":"Anthropic raises $13B Series F","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he doesn't actually say that each model has been profitable.","text":"if you're referring to [VIDEO] he doesn't actually say that each model has been profitable. He says \"You paid $100 million and then it made $200 million of revenue. There's some cost to inference with the model, but let's just assume in this cartoonish cartoon example that even if you add those two up, you're kind of in a good state. So, if every model was a company, the model is actually, in this example is actually profitable. What's going on is that at the same time\" notice those are hypothetical numbers and he just asks you to assume that inference is (sufficiently) profitable. He doesn't actually say they made money by the EoL of some model."}]},"HRPDO63rI1E":{"wy":"HN commenters mostly reference it as shorthand for a reality that feels like a trap, often alongside The Truman Show, rather than recommending it.","m":[{"id":7500430,"author":"TempleOSV2","month":"2014-03","story":"Ask HN: I can't do this anymore, I need help","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter says reality resembles The Prisoner and Truman Show","quote":"My reality is some combination of and the Truman show.","text":"My reality is some combination of [VIDEO] and the Truman show. Get a job? Fuck that -- retarded. All media talks to me, I'm worth billions. God talks. I made His temple. I entertain God. ----- God says... the programming how_about_that Shakespeare heads_I_win_tails_you_lose tattle_tale Ghost Varoom gross I_veto_that California left_field Icarus recipe I'm_bored that's_all_folks God_smack theft ha grumble what's_the_plan stuff yuck quit you_don't_like_it thank_you_very_much quit I_am_not_amused ---- God says.... C:\\TAD\\Text\\PARADISE.TXT of waters deep, Hoarse murmur echoed to his words applause Through the infinite host; nor less for that The flaming Seraph fearless, though alone Encompassed round with foes, thus answered bold. O alienate from God, O Spirit accursed, Forsaken of all good! I see thy fall Determined, and thy hapless crew involved In this perfidious fraud, contagion spread Both of thy crime and punishment: Henceforth No more be troubled how to quit the yoke Of God's Messiah; those indulgent laws Will not be now"},{"id":7209022,"author":"TempleOSV2","month":"2014-02","story":"The importance of co-founder due diligence","stance":"reference","why":"Incoherent rambling with the video link at the end","quote":"","text":"I kill a person with my car, nothing happens. I death threat the president, nothing happens. I send emails, get no response. Nothing changes my download numbers. I am in CIA prison. God says... poison weigheth state diverse appointment Jove cries Julian wantonness weakly director burnest admission shortly skill conveyedst Ambrose Apostolic BUT cloyed flower discharge barred WARRANTY wait turning undo darksomely wroth regarded deafness abuse false Vindicianus worshipper Whom undergo My download numbers are like a limp wrist. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6430109,"author":"losethos","month":"2013-09","story":"The Story of Engineering Education in India","stance":"reference","why":"Incoherent comparison to Truman Show reality","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] The Truman Show reality. Truman learns there is something very wrong with reality. They tell him to get a job. He says, \"fuck-off\". God says... C:\\TAD\\Text\\QUIX.TXT our Casildea de Vandalia in beauty; and in addition to this you must promise, if you should survive this encounter and fall, to go to the city of El Toboso and present yourself before her on my behalf, that she deal with you according to her good pleasure; and if she leaves you free to do yours, you are in like manner to return and seek me out (for the trail of my mighty deeds will serve you as a guide to lead you to where I may be), and tell me what may have passed between you and her-conditions wh"},{"id":4467469,"author":"MusicOS","month":"2012-09","story":"Show HN: My Github résumé","stance":"reference","why":"Rambling comparison of reality to The Prisoner","quote":"I learned my reality is a joke like the 1960's the prisoner.","text":"CIA doesn't want other operating systems. CIA==KGB Duh! You think they would let another operating system exist! ROFLMAO Win8 You can have your starry eyed view of the US. Back in 2001 when I made SimStructure, the CIA made SimStructure. I learned my reality is a joke like the 1960's the prisoner. [VIDEO] I get no emails, except FBI."}]},"IKVFZ28ybQs":{"wy":"Cited in StarCraft and AI threads as a vivid example of superhuman micro and of machine speed advantage over humans.","q":{"x":"Zerglings vs Siege Tanks when controlled by AI with perfect micro.","i":15872739,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":47311909,"author":"L_226","month":"2026-03","story":"I'm Getting a Whiff of Iain Banks' Culture","stance":"reference","why":"Zergling swarm dodging tank shells.","quote":"Exactly the reference I was thinking of","text":"> We're talking about hundred unit zergling swarms perfectly dodging tank shells. Exactly the reference I was thinking of [VIDEO]"},{"id":20408844,"author":"pjspycha","month":"2019-07","story":"DeepMind’s StarCraft II agent will play anonymously on battle.net","stance":"reference","why":"Showcases what super APM can do.","quote":"that showcase what super APM can do","text":"It was also really unimpressive in my opinion, instead of being smarter and making broad decisions about tactics that are harnessed by some perfect unit composition knowledge, the AI won on a purely micro scale. There are videos from several microbots such as Automaton 2000: [VIDEO] that showcase what super APM can do. The DeepMind AI that beat some pro players did it by having better micro while generally having worse strategies. The DeepMind matches showed how better micro can turn a generally weaker unit composition into the winning unit composition just as that Automaton 2000 video did. To me it seemed that the DeepMind team figured out that the only way to beat competent players was to do what all those microbots do and pump APM into godly levels. They decided to limit APM, however like you said, just average APM and have the bots explode APM when needed. The real funny match was when MaNa beat Deepmind by completely breaking the AI with generally simple drop strategies and made Deepmind look very much inept."},{"id":18973079,"author":"knicholes","month":"2019-01","story":"DeepMind – StarCraft II Demonstration","stance":"reference","why":"Tank-vs-zergling outcome changes with super-human micro.","quote":"In this video, 20 tanks kill 100 zerglings and lose only two tanks.","text":"It makes a huge difference. In this video, 20 tanks kill 100 zerglings and lose only two tanks. With super-human micro, the results are... quite different. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18040431,"author":"thaniri","month":"2018-09","story":"Tencent's AI plays StarCraft II, and wins over 90 percent of the time","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates AI crushing humans in real-time games.","quote":"This video demonstrates why","text":"For anyone who actually plays SC2, or any real time game where human speed is a limitation, it should not be surprising that AI would crush human opponents. This video demonstrates why: [VIDEO] AI has crushed humans in Go, the most complicated (widely played) board game. I'll be impressed again by AI when it beats us in physical sport."},{"id":17693568,"author":"kenning","month":"2018-08","story":"OpenAI bots competing against Humans right now","stance":"reference","why":"Strategy only viable with superhuman reactions.","quote":"This is an example of a strategy that is not viable without superhuman reactions","text":"dota 2 is not as extreme about this as, say, starcraft. in starcraft, most \"strategies\" are heavily scripted build orders that almost never deviate from a handful of openings (similar to opening moves in chess) and a large amount of winning comes from the ability to quickly give orders to your troops (\"micro\"). This is an example of a strategy that is not viable without superhuman reactions: [VIDEO] if you directly attack 20 siege tanks with 100 zerglings you will only kill about two siege tanks, but an AI can kill all the siege tanks with some zerglings left over. There's some of this in dota, but there's a cap on the skill level for most playable characters that pros generally get \"close enough\" to, and beyond that the strategic depth comes from area control decisionmaking. Theres over 100 heroes and many of them have really weird abilities, like the possibility of creating a temporary wall (earthshaker) or the ability to teleport anywhere on the map every 20 seconds (furion). I could be wrong though, maybe the AI is winning games by playing heroes with long range and perfectly microing them to harass and prevent the other team from ever getting gold/xp."},{"id":17863786,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2018-08","story":"OpenAI’s Dota 2 defeat is still a win for artificial intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Superhuman mechanical skill of AI.","quote":"makes me think that a typical AI would beat a human.","text":"Frankly, superior reaction time and other mechanical skills in Dota2 (or Starcraft, or other games which are being tested) makes me think that a typical AI would beat a human. [VIDEO] That's the \"gloves come off\" AI. Granted, its a problem solved by raw, mechanical button-pushes at a rate beyond what is possible on a keyboard/mouse, but it demonstrates the greatest skills an AI or computer has. Of course, no one wants to see an AI beat a human using known methodologies. They want to see an AI win at the abstract game of \"a game of strategy\". From this perspective, I think the poker AI results over the past year or two have been far more interesting. AIs figuring out the Nash Equalibrium and playing bluffing games around it. Sure, its a bit mechanical and mathematically inclined, but generalizing and solving the bluffing game is IMO far more useful."},{"id":17554186,"author":"vkou","month":"2018-07","story":"Riot Games Approach to Anti-Cheat","stance":"reference","why":"Microbots punch above their weight.","quote":"Even moreso if you throw in an auto-scatter script.","text":"Yes, they start punching way above their weight against tanks, too. Even moreso if you throw in an auto-scatter script. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15872739,"author":"pythonaut_16","month":"2017-12","story":"Google's AlphaZero Beats Stockfish In 100-Game Match","stance":"reference","why":"Perfect micro lets AI win without strategy.","quote":"Zerglings vs Siege Tanks when controlled by AI with perfect micro.","text":"For human vs AI there will have to be APM rate limits otherwise the AI can win with no real strategy[0]. For AI vs AI it could be interesting to see what strategies develop with two opponents with the ability to perfectly microcontrol their units. [0]Zerglings vs Siege Tanks when controlled by AI with perfect micro. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15438569,"author":"bbradley406","month":"2017-10","story":"Facebook Quietly Enters StarCraft War for AI Bots","stance":"reference","why":"Units dance at max range and dodge hits.","quote":"it does have a certain elegance[1].","text":">The units basically just dance at maximum range, magically dodge hits, etc. While it certainly isn't fair to play against, it does have a certain elegance[1]. There's also the problem that even if it's AI vs AI, the races and units are balanced around reaction times of humans. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14971742,"author":"ktRolster","month":"2017-08","story":"DeepMind and Blizzard Open StarCraft II as an AI Research Environment","stance":"reference","why":"Example of computers winning through micro.","quote":"We already know that computers can have superior micro and beat humans at Starcraft through that(1).","text":"When Watson won at Jeopardy, one of its prime advantages was the faster reaction time at pushing the buzzer. The fairness of that has already been hashed out elsewhere, but..... We already know that computers can have superior micro and beat humans at Starcraft through that(1). Is DeepMind going to win by giving themselves a micro advantage that is beyond what reasonable humans can do? (1) [VIDEO] as one example"},{"id":14414420,"author":"literallycancer","month":"2017-05","story":"Google’s AlphaGo Defeats Chinese Go Master in Win for A.I","stance":"reference","why":"Zerglings dodging siege tank shots as an AI example.","quote":"zerglings doding siege tanks shots","text":"Yeah, I remember this vid with zerglings doding siege tanks shots: [VIDEO] The difference is quite drastic."},{"id":12875275,"author":"lanius","month":"2016-11","story":"DeepMind and Blizzard to release StarCraft II as an AI research environment","stance":"reference","why":"Example of insane AI micro.","quote":"I personally would love play against an AI with strong game sense as well as insane micro","text":"Unfortunate. I personally would love play against an AI with strong game sense as well as insane micro a la [VIDEO]"},{"id":12876492,"author":"trjordan","month":"2016-11","story":"DeepMind and Blizzard to release StarCraft II as an AI research environment","stance":"reference","why":"Infinite APM breaks unit counters.","quote":"Tanks counter zerglings, because they do splash damage to a bunch of them at once.","text":"A big part of Starcraft is that certain units counter other units. Tanks counter zerglings, because they do splash damage to a bunch of them at once. Here's what you can do with infinite APM: [VIDEO] It's just broken. It's not even the same game."},{"id":12876861,"author":"mmanfrin","month":"2016-11","story":"DeepMind and Blizzard to release StarCraft II as an AI research environment","stance":"reference","why":"Simple bots outdo humans at micro.","quote":"Really rudimentary AIs are already significantly beyond the skill of humans","text":"Really rudimentary AIs are already significantly beyond the skill of humans (in terms of micro, at least). [VIDEO]"},{"id":12877367,"author":"imaginenore","month":"2016-11","story":"DeepMind and Blizzard to release StarCraft II as an AI research environment","stance":"reference","why":"AI wins by simultaneous unit control.","quote":"because it can control all the units simultaneously.","text":"AI already beats humans at StarCraft, not because it's smarter, but because it can control all the units simultaneously. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":12895260,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2016-11","story":"Show HN: DeepRhyme (D-Prime) – Generating dope rhymes with machine learning","stance":"reference","why":"Context on bot weaknesses against human players.","quote":"","text":"It's possible but it's a lot more context-driven than you think with a human involved. The bots do great against each other with their strategies and muscle-memory stuff. Throw a human in the mix, they start noticing patterns in how the units are managed, what strategies are likely, etc. They've exploited these in the competitions to hilarious effect. Here's the main site on prior work & results at the competitions: [LINK] Here's a few examples of how the muscle-memory approach, esp if focused on unit vs unit, can fail against humans. \" In this example, Bakuryu (human) notices that Skynet's units will chase his zerglings if they are near, and proceeds to run around Skynet's base to distract Skynet long enough so that Bakuryu can make fying units to come attack Skynet's base. This type of behaviour is incredibly hard to detect in a bot, since it requires knowledge of the larger context of the game which may only have consequences 5 or more minutes in the future. \" (2013) Note: At this point, they also sucked at building and expansion strategies which surprised me since I thought a basic planner would be able to do that. The constraints between rate of expansion, where to place stuff, what units to keep/build, and so on get really hard. The other thing they weren't good at was switching strategies mid-game based on what opponents are doing. \" despite Djem5 (pro) making the bots look silly this year... they were able to defeat D-ranked, and even some C-ranked players. After the "},{"id":12498952,"author":"vinchuco","month":"2016-09","story":"Deep Reinforcement Learning to Play StarCraft","stance":"reference","why":"Shows machine speed advantage in StarCraft micro.","quote":"Brings to mind the power of high frequency trading","text":"That's incredible. The speed advantage here: [VIDEO] Brings to mind the power of high frequency trading"},{"id":12498972,"author":"danielvf","month":"2016-09","story":"Deep Reinforcement Learning to Play StarCraft","stance":"reference","why":"Zerglings dodging siege tanks via AI micro.","quote":"a hundred zergslings dodge seige tank cannons and destroy them","text":"And as cool as that is, this is even more terrifying, as a hundred zergslings dodge seige tank cannons and destroy them. [VIDEO] It's enough to make you scared for the future of humanity."},{"id":11325855,"author":"Monory","month":"2016-03","story":"DeepMind Challenges for StarCraft","stance":"reference","why":"Bot with unlimited APM dodges siege tank fire.","quote":"it beats people from a horribly disadvantageous positions","text":"Well, anyone who says that advantage in micromanagement is not a big thing should see \"Automaton 2000\" videos. Given that automaton is a map script and thus can have virtually unlimited APM, it beats people from a horribly disadvantageous positions (40 banelings vs 21 marine [1], or 100 zerglings vs 20 sieged tanks [2]). [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16882126,"author":"AmericanChopper","month":"2018-04","story":"StarCraft Micromanagement with Reinforcement Learning and Curriculum Transfer","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"boring to implement and boring to play against","text":"A 'perfectly' micro-ing AI is both boring to implement and boring to play against. Somebody made one for SC2 back when that was popular, take a look at these videos to see how uninteresting it would become after the first attempt. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"L3LbxDZRgA4":{"wy":"Quoted as a joke whenever sunshades, geoengineering or anti-solar policy come up.","m":[{"id":47494610,"author":"feurio","month":"2026-03","story":"US govt pays TotalEnergies nearly $1B to stop US offshore wind projects","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Since the beginning of time man has yearned to destroy the sun","text":"\"Since the beginning of time man has yearned to destroy the sun\" - Charles Montgomery Burns [VIDEO]"},{"id":39361665,"author":"a_e_k","month":"2024-02","story":"The likelihood of unilateral solar geoengineering","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Since the beginning of time, Man has yearned to destroy the sun. I will do the next best thing: block it out!","text":"\"Since the beginning of time, Man has yearned to destroy the sun. I will do the next best thing: block it out!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33205862,"author":"DocTomoe","month":"2022-10","story":"Cooling the Earth with a sunshade near the inner Lagrange point (2006)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And as always, the Simpsons have predicted it","text":"Friendly reminder that a private company with launch capability currently is massively increasing the space junk problem and about to trigger the Kessler scenario for a quick buck and internet access without cables, while at the same time ruining surface-based astronomy. So why stop there? And as always, the Simpsons have predicted it: [VIDEO]"}]},"LFrdqQZ8FFc":{"wy":"Cited as a joke whenever something is stacked or oddly named.","m":[{"id":38948191,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2024-01","story":"Spot Bitcoin ETF receives official approval from the SEC","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"What's the maximum levels of financial abstraction -- that can be stacked on top of one another?","text":">\"The historic approval paves the way for the first regulated exchange-traded product in the U.S. to give investors direct exposure to the price of Bitcoin without requiring them to buy it or worry about self-custody. Investors will buy shares in ETFs holding Bitcoin as its underlying asset .\" Interesting! Looks like we've paved the way for yet another abstracted financial instrument -- which sits on top of yet another abstracted financial instrument ... My only question, at this point in time, were I to have one, would be something like the following: What's the maximum levels of financial abstraction -- that can be stacked on top of one another? ? ??? Related: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35565892,"author":"perilunar","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: Alternatives to organizing code in files and folders?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Were you thinking of this Monty Python skit when you wrote that?","text":"> putting things inside of things Were you thinking of this Monty Python skit when you wrote that? [VIDEO]"},{"id":31790028,"author":"leohonexus","month":"2022-06","story":"International Institute of Not Doing Much","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The name reminds me of the Monty Python sketch - The Royal Society of Putting Things on Top of Other Things!","text":"The name reminds me of the Monty Python sketch - The Royal Society of Putting Things on Top of Other Things! [VIDEO]"},{"id":26282396,"author":"a3n","month":"2021-02","story":"Charter of the National Association of Wheeled Bin Washers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think these folks must share a building with The Royal Society for Putting Things on Top of Other Things.","text":"I think these folks must share a building with The Royal Society for Putting Things on Top of Other Things. [VIDEO]"}]},"LV0wTtiJygY":{"wy":"HN uses it purely as a joke reply about being out of touch with changing trends.","m":[{"id":23797896,"author":"DaiPlusPlus","month":"2020-07","story":"Amazon says email banning TikTok from employee phones was ‘sent in error’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It was just that for the past months every time someone forwarded me a stupid video I was supposed to like, it had a TikTok logo on it. So in my view this became the source of \"stupid videos people bother me with\". Not exactly a charming quality. This quote comes to mind: > I used to be with ‘it’, but then they changed what ‘it’ was. Now what I’m with isn’t ‘it’ anymore and what’s ‘it’ seems weird and scary. It’ll happen to you! [VIDEO]"},{"id":17450055,"author":"treis","month":"2018-07","story":"Ice Poseidon’s Lucrative, Stressful Life as a Live Streamer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That was a really interesting piece. All I can think of after reading it is Grandpa's quote from the Simpsons: I used to be with ‘it’, but then they changed what ‘it’ was. Now what I’m with isn’t ‘it’ anymore and what’s ‘it’ seems weird and scary. It’ll happen to you! [VIDEO]"},{"id":9310197,"author":"MichaelApproved","month":"2015-04","story":"How Beats by Dre played you like a fool","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I find this worrying, really. Are we so desperate to consume that we're willing to buy shit just for sake of buying? There's no need to worry, this isn't a sign that society is going downhill. Buying Beats for something other than sound as the primary purpose is no different than buying something purely for fashion sake, which is a centuries old human practice. This comment reminds me of how every generation finds the next generation's actions worrying. edit: Grandpa Simpson nailed it: \"I used to be with it, but then they changed what it was. Now what I'm with isn't it , and what's it seems weird and scary to me. It'll happen to you...\" [VIDEO]"}]},"LkqiDu1BQXY":{"wy":"Used as a joke or pop-culture reference in comments about futuristic technology and talking to computers.","m":[{"id":36952380,"author":"enraged_camel","month":"2023-08","story":"Origin of correlated isolated flat bands in LK99","stance":"reference","why":"Scene misremembered as computer searching chemistries","quote":"OK, I misremembered. Was thinking of this","text":">> If we have the ability to computationally determine these things without any experimental data needed, and we know we're looking for a specific band structure, wouldn't we just do an automated search of possible chemistries to find everything producing said band structure? Isn't this a plot point in that one Star Trek movie (episode?) where they go back in time and program a current-day computer to do this? edit: OK, I misremembered. Was thinking of this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43895666,"author":"1970-01-01","month":"2025-05","story":"I turned a 40 year old Apple Mouse into a speech to text button","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"We have Star Trek today.","text":"Exactly my first thought as well. [VIDEO] We're living in the 80s version of the future. We have Star Trek today."},{"id":21676586,"author":"furgooswft13","month":"2019-12","story":"Physicists Have Identified a Metal That Conducts Electricity But Not Heat (2017)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Using mostly his index fingers.","text":"[VIDEO] Using mostly his index fingers. Suck it vim users, your editor has nothing on MOLECULAR DESIGN for System 3.0"},{"id":10419804,"author":"QuercusMax","month":"2015-10","story":"Google’s growing problem: 50% of people do zero searches per day on mobile","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I feel like Scotty in Star Trek IV: The One with the Whales","text":"Disclaimer: I work for an Alphabet company (but not Google itself any more). I was a die-hard iPhone supporter since I got my 3G, but switched to Android when I started at Google back in June. One of the things I like best about Android is the search bar on the home screen - I use it constantly. Half the time what I'm looking for isn't a web page per se (directions, apps, email, etc), and having search integrated into the home screen is extremely convenient. The one thing I really do miss is being able to hold down the home button to activate Siri. Saying \"OK, Google\" is only successful for me about 50% of the time (and I have a very standard American accent) - when it doesn't work I feel like Scotty in Star Trek IV: The One with the Whales: [VIDEO]"}]},"RM0uvgHKZe8":{"wy":"HN commenters cite the clip critically as evidence of US sanctions policy and its human cost.","m":[{"id":38820766,"author":"ajsnigrutin","month":"2023-12","story":"Spain confirms nuclear power phase-out, extends renewable projects deadlines","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"So... are we going to count the wars russia started vs the wars US&allies started? Genocide is a strong word... how many dead people count as a genocide? Because if russia applies the same rules as US, 500k dead children are worth it: [VIDEO] ... in a war half a planet away from USA."},{"id":30782969,"author":"b215826","month":"2022-03","story":"Madeleine Albright has died","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Albright: \"I think this is a very hard choice, but the price, we think the price is worth it.","text":"Interviewer: \"We have heard that a half a million children have died. I mean that's more children than died in Hiroshima. You know, is the price worth it?\" Albright: \"I think this is a very hard choice, but the price, we think the price is worth it.\" This is from a 1996 interview with this woman [1] about the Iraq invasion. Good riddance to bad rubbish. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30783364,"author":"someotherperson","month":"2022-03","story":"Madeleine Albright has died","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"As a child, Albright was hoping I would die[0] so she would dance on mine. It's great that I survived, and I can dance on her grave on behalf of the half million children that didn't. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30783532,"author":"someotherperson","month":"2022-03","story":"Madeleine Albright has died","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"she gave her personal opinion that she personally supported it","text":"Knowing that people have died, she gave her personal opinion that she personally supported it[0]. That's a large part of the reason why she's disliked. It's because she advocates for genocide. If you replace \"Iraqis\" with \"Jews\" and have someone say that the deaths of half a million Jewish children is \"worth it\" nobody will be in this comment section trying to talk about honoring the deceased or suggesting everyone has good and bad sides. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29610537,"author":"American12143","month":"2021-12","story":"Hidden Pentagon records reveal patterns of failure in deadly airstrikes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here is Madeline Albright saying killing 500k+ kids in Iraq via sanctions was \"worth the price","text":"The body counts are not even comparable. In Iraq, ALONE, the U.S. is estimated to have killed 1M+ civilians, between the sanctions it pushed before the 2003 war and the war itself. Here is Madeline Albright saying killing 500k+ kids in Iraq via sanctions was \"worth the price\" (despite the fact that those sanctions achieved nothing in terms of real political goals): [VIDEO] Edit: but you can flag my comments if that makes you feel better"},{"id":29610679,"author":"American12143","month":"2021-12","story":"Hidden Pentagon records reveal patterns of failure in deadly airstrikes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Okay...and? I said that neither China nor Russia are good actors (when I said invasions, I meant active ones, but I get your point), however, the U.S. is worse. -The Russian invasion of both Ukraine and Georgia, summed together, had a civilian deathtoll of 4000+ -If we throw in the first/second Chechen war as well, we get a TOTAL civilian deathtoll of ~70k+ The U.S. killed 500k+ CHILDREN (not even accounting for adult civilians) with its Iraq sanctions (before the invasion), this is just ONE country of the MANY countries the U.S. has been a participant in destabilizing in the last two decades (and we're not even talking about Korea/Vietnam/supplying Saddam with chemical weapons/Gulf War/South American interventions) [VIDEO] If you want to look at it from a per capita standpoint, the U.S. has killed far, far more civilians than Russia has (not that this justifies even the death of a SINGLE civilian), but one must objectively come to the realization that the U.S. is the biggest force for civilian mass murder in the world. You can dress it up as \"it was for democracy and freedom\", but I don't think anyone buys that anymore. They don't even sell it to us as that anymore, now its for \"national security\", which is the same excuse Russia and China make as well."},{"id":27877058,"author":"pessimizer","month":"2021-07","story":"A reporter’s fight to expose Epstein’s crimes, and earn a living","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's a very difficult story to report on because 1) the people who own your institution may be involved on some level, 2) all legitimate sources of novel material information will have been destroyed, 3) interviews are worthless because everybody who we know about who would have anything interesting to say are also both implicated and endangered, and there are so many kooks claiming to know things that it would be impossible to sort them out of the people who we don't know about. It's bizarre that we even think that this story would have an outcome. A number of the completely arbitrary people we shower laurels on under crony capitalism like to have sex with runaway/endangered teenagers under completely safe and shielded circumstances. They disguised payment by parking money in a fake (investment fund/tax advisory structure/real estate partnership) with a guy who spent his entire life procuring for and protecting them. He probably kept blackmail material that got him out of the first prosecution, and lost control of that blackmail material before the next prosecution. Clinton's sanctions on Iraq killed a half million children. That's more important than the fact that he may have abused teenagers on a sex plane/island, and he still speaks at the Democratic convention. We know that he manipulated a young intern into sex while on the job, and suspect he may be a serial sex abuser/rapist, and we try to make his wife president. We all know about \"pussy-grabbing\" Trump. We need to g"},{"id":24304606,"author":"propagandist","month":"2020-08","story":"China Built a Vast New Infrastructure to Imprison Uighurs","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"America starves civilian populations outside formal states of war.","text":"1. America declared the war on false grounds, like most wars since WW2 2. It makes 0 difference morally whether you’re targeting domestic or foreign civilians 3. America starves civilian populations outside formal states of war. [VIDEO] . This is the visible tip of the iceberg"},{"id":20303153,"author":"stevenjohns","month":"2019-06","story":"An Open Letter to Google and Apple: Stop Hindering Iranian Entrepreneurs","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Pray tell: how did starving Iraqis to the point where 500,000 children under the age of five alone die from malnutrition[0] punish the Iraqi government? All it did was convert the secular military dictatorship into an extremist religious dictatorship and the effects of this punishment are still being felt today around the entire world[1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":18223569,"author":"prolikewh0a","month":"2018-10","story":"US Lawmakers Urge Canada to Snub China's Huawei in Telecoms","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":">BTW, slavery STILL exists in many of those parts of the world. We know it still exists and one example is at the fault of the USA there is now open slave trade in Libya [1]. We also still have legal slavery in our prison system as a law. 500,000 dead in Iraq due to illegal wars, laughed at and claimed as 'good' [2]. Cambodia, Laos, Iraq, Vietnam, Afghanistan, Korea, Libya, the list doesn't end! The world would likely be a much better place if the USA didn't have it's fingers in everything, everywhere, nearly every country, killing people in mass , all around the world for decades [3][4]. The first chapter, the preface even, of Noam Chomsky's 'Manufacturing Consent' goes into extraordinary detail about USA's history of genocide and atrocities all around the world, and it's all totally sourced in full. I suggest everyone read it. I've seen estimates between 6 million and 20 million killed by USA in wars since WW1, and that's only by war and conflict, most absolutely provoked by the USA. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":17385432,"author":"jacobr","month":"2018-06","story":"Hundreds of skulls reveal massive scale of human sacrifice in Aztec capital","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Then-Secretary of State Madeleine Albright calmly asserting that U.S. policy objectives were worth the sacrifice of half a million Iraqi children","text":"Regarding Civilization or savagery, which is this: Then-Secretary of State Madeleine Albright calmly asserting that U.S. policy objectives were worth the sacrifice of half a million Iraqi children: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5703084,"author":"calhoun137","month":"2013-05","story":"U.S. Secretly Obtains Two Months of A.P. Phone Records","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"What? If you didn't sound so serious I would think your comment is meant to be a parody. > Very little of this is actually true, at least with regards to what the U.S. does internally. The majority of the OP's comment is not about domestic US politics, it's about support of dictatorships abroad. I'm guessing you are not very familiar with the history of Latin america, you should read up on that before making comments like this again. By the way, the US backed ex-president of Guatemala was just found guilty of genocide[1], Hillary Clinton said Egypt's dictator Mubarak was a \"friend of her family\", and the US strongly backs the regime in Saudi Arabia which is one of the most repressive in the entire middle east. When Madeleine Albright was asked if she thought the death of half a million Iraqi children due to the sanctions was too high a price to pay, she said \"I think...the price is worth it\"[2]. > The U.S. government very rarely ignores the Constitution Are you aware that it was recently revealed that Henry Kissinger was recorded saying \"The illegal we do immediately, the unconstitutional takes a little longer\"[3] Or are you familiar with the regime of secret law that surrounds drone assassinations, do you think that just because the president can get his lawyer to write him a permission slip that it makes assassinating US citizens abroad constitutional? What about locking people up in Guantanamo bay without a trial? What about torture? What about the 4th amendment. Do you ev"}]},"Tj-KS2kfIr0":{"wy":"Used as evidence of prior art for slide-to-unlock in patent disputes involving Apple.","m":[{"id":18014998,"author":"close04","month":"2018-09","story":"Steve Jobs licensed Amazon’s one-click patent for $1M in one phone call","stance":"reference","why":"Slide to unlock used in commercial device since 2005","quote":"slide to unlock which was used at least since 2005 in a commercial device.","text":"Apple's problem isn't that the patents are stupid, it's that the inventions or ideas never belonged to them in the first place. In the vast majority of cases the idea or implementation already existed. Like the slide to unlock which was used at least since 2005 in a commercial device. [0][1] They also tried patenting a method to reduce power consumption of a chip by reducing voltage. Something that isn't just a law of nature (thus unpatentable) but was also used decades before ever since ICs were developed (thus \"prior art\"). One of the most controversial patents is the \"rectangle with rounded corners\". This was a design patent and these in general are usually not just very hard to enforce but also stupid in most cases. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4463457,"author":"jkn","month":"2012-09","story":"Apple asks court to ban Samsung’s Galaxy Note and Galaxy S III from US","stance":"reference","why":"Neonode prior art for swipe unlock","quote":"Well here is a video of the Neonode prior art","text":"Well here is a video of the Neonode prior art: [VIDEO] It's unlocked with a left to right swipe. Contrary to the iPhone there is no image moving with the finger. I don't think that difference is worth a patent. Moving things (i.e. images) around with the finger is a pretty obvious concept for a purely touch-based device. See for example the Microsoft Surface (now renamed to PixelSense) from 2008: [LINK]"},{"id":4200627,"author":"dataminer","month":"2012-07","story":"HTC wins swipe to unlock patent dispute against Apple","stance":"reference","why":"Neonode N1 showing slide to unlock before iPhone","quote":"shows slide to unlock feature predating the iphone","text":"Following video of Neonode N1 (cited in the article) shows slide to unlock feature predating the iphone [VIDEO]"},{"id":1163336,"author":"stse","month":"2010-03","story":"Apple Sues Nexus One Maker HTC","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Neonode N1 swipe unlock predating Apple","quote":"Neonode did this back in 2004/2005 with the N1.","text":"Also Neonode did this back in 2004/2005 with the N1. Apple's patent is a month old. Demo @ [VIDEO] Edit: from question to statement."}]},"UiOzGI4MqSU":{"wy":"Cited in debates about Windows design quality, sometimes agreeing and sometimes disputing the claim.","m":[{"id":39382449,"author":"gruturo","month":"2024-02","story":"Google enables OS upgrades for older PCs post-Windows 10 support cutoff","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on Microsoft having no taste","quote":"Jobs was absolutely right already back in 1995","text":"> Why remove such a good feature? To look more like Apple does? Fail fail fail Microsoft Imitating Apple, not understanding why Apple does some of these things (not that they're always right eh, far from it), thus fumbling really badly. My Apple Fan Card is long expired, I'm not even paying my dues (just a phone in the last 3 years) but Jobs was absolutely right already back in 1995: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38879342,"author":"WhereIsTheTruth","month":"2024-01","story":"Windows 11 struggling to escape the shadow of Windows 10","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"When you keep add bloat on top of bloat, fire your QA team, hire people not skilled enough for the task, protect your incompetent higher ups, at some point things start to fall apart, that's windows for you The sad part is as a result, windows 10 is viewed as \"good\".. society fell this low The everything app will certainly not be microsoft/windows based We still have a long way to go in the west.. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47201788,"author":"linguae","month":"2026-02","story":"The Windows 95 User Interface: A Case Study in Usability Engineering","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Steve Jobs is famous for his 1996 quote about Microsoft not having taste ( ). I disagree","text":"Steve Jobs is famous for his 1996 quote about Microsoft not having taste ( [VIDEO] ). I disagree; as much as I love the classic Mac OS and Jobs-era Mac OS X, and despite my feelings about Microsoft's monopolistic behavior, 1995-2000 Microsoft's user interfaces were quite tasteful, in my opinion, and this was Microsoft's most tasteful period. I have fond memories of Windows 95/NT 4/98/2000, Office 97, and Visual Basic 6. I even liked Internet Explorer 5. These were well-made products when it came to the user interface. Yes, Windows 95 crashed a lot, but so did Macintosh System 7. Things started going downhill, in my opinion, with the Windows XP \"Fisher-Price\" Luna interface and the Microsoft Office 2007 ribbon."}]},"VuDSz06BT2g":{"wy":"HN cites it as a real-world look at Huawei's lidar-based driver assistance when comparing against Tesla FSD.","q":{"x":"Here's the Out of Spec guys riding around on it in China for an hour","i":45149257,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46458670,"author":"lossolo","month":"2026-01","story":"BYD Sells 4.6M Vehicles in 2025, Meets Revised Sales Goal","stance":"reference","why":"Test comparing other makers' FSD to Tesla's.","quote":"","text":"> where is BYD at with FSD? I'm not sure about BYD, but other car makers have FSD that works like Tesla's FSD, and in some cases it even outperforms it. Here is a test from a few months ago: [VIDEO] > - The Chevy Corvette Stingray? Say what you want, but the high end ICE sports cars have an appeal of their own… The world is moving to EVs, ICE will mostly be collectors cars in 20 years in developed countries. As to Chinese sports cars Xiaomi SU7 Ultra: \"June 2025, an unmodified SU7 Ultra (with a maximum 1,139 kW (1,527 hp; 1,549 PS) power) lapped the Nürburgring in a hair under 7 minutes, 5 seconds. It is not only faster than the fastest Tesla Model S Plaid and Porsche Taycan versions, but also faster than a Rimac Nevera, one of the most high-end and expensive electric sportscars.\" U9 [LINK] is a supercar produced by BYD, fastest in the world. \"It achieved a maximum speed of 496.22 km/h (308.33 mph) at Germany’s ATP Automotive Testing Papenburg, making it the fastest car in the world and breaking the record previously held by the Bugatti Chiron Super Sport 300+ at 490.484 km/h (304.773 mph)\""},{"id":45149257,"author":"DennisP","month":"2025-09","story":"Tesla changes meaning of 'Full Self-Driving', gives up on promise of autonomy","stance":"reference","why":"One-hour ride in Huawei's lidar-based system in China.","quote":"Here's the Out of Spec guys riding around on it in China for an hour","text":"Huawei has a self-driving system that uses three lidars, which cost $250 each (plus vision, radar, and ultrasound). It appears to work about as well as FSD. Here's the Out of Spec guys riding around on it in China for an hour: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44628157,"author":"slaw","month":"2025-07","story":"How Tesla is proving doubters right on why its robotaxi service cannot scale","stance":"reference","why":"Review claiming Huawei beats Tesla, both level 2.","quote":"","text":"Huawei is better than Tesla according to 'Out of Spec'. Both are level 2. Waymo is level 4. Completely different animal [VIDEO]"},{"id":44378314,"author":"lossolo","month":"2025-06","story":"Tesla sales decline in Europe for fifth straight month as rivals gain ground","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Huawei driver assistance in China rivaling Tesla FSD.","quote":"","text":"> Tesla is the only one with consumer FSD technology. That's not true anymore, at least in China. [VIDEO]"}]},"XEL65gywwHQ":{"wy":"Posted as a joke whenever someone misunderstands what a tax write-off actually means.","m":[{"id":38386451,"author":"treve","month":"2023-11","story":"What has changed in CPU cores in M3 chips?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This reminds me of seinfeld scene","text":"This reminds me of seinfeld scene: [VIDEO] Anyway, for the bottom line of your company I think it rarely makes sense to frivolously spend more just to decrease your tax burden. There's smarter ways to spend money."},{"id":37872837,"author":"saulpw","month":"2023-10","story":"You shouldn't join Y Combinator","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It seems like VCs will often sell shares for a near-total-loss on their investment, for simplicity and relationship with founders, and they can write it off[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33121732,"author":"glofish","month":"2022-10","story":"$570M worth of Binance’s BNB token stolen","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Crypto losses always remind me of Kramer and Jerry talking about losses. - Not a problem. It is a write-off for them. - How is it a write-off? - All these big companies do it - they just write it off! - Write what off? You don't even know what a write-off is! - Maybe I don't but they do. And they are the ones writing it off! [VIDEO]"},{"id":27909109,"author":"woah","month":"2021-07","story":"Bezos donates $100M each to CNN contributor Van Jones and chef Jose Andres","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You don't even know what a write off is, do you?","text":"Kramer: It's a write off for them. Jerry: How is it a write off? Kramer: They just write it off. Jerry: Write it off of what? Kramer: They just write it off! Jerry: You don't even know what a write off is, do you? Kramer: No. Do you? Jerry: No I don't!! [VIDEO]"},{"id":23703739,"author":"sosborn","month":"2020-07","story":"Raspberry Pi 4 PCIe bridge “chip”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Broadcom very much is able to write off a ton as donations to charity If I donate $1000 dollars so that I can \"write it off,\" do I come out ahead financially? [VIDEO]"},{"id":21032556,"author":"wahern","month":"2019-09","story":"Too Much Dark Money in Almonds","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"People have similarly mistaken ideas about so-called write-offs","text":"> These donations are effectively free. A company or wealthy individual can either pay taxes or donate the money to a \"non-political\" think tank to promote policies they'd like to see. That's not how deductions work. A donation costs the donor much more than the tax reduction. All a deduction does is reduce your adjusted gross income, not literally offset the nominal sum you pay in taxes. Say my top marginal tax bracket is 20%. If I donate $100 then it's costing me precisely $100. If I don't donate then I'm on the hook for $20 ($100 * 20%) in tax. $100 > $20. This presumes the $100 is earned income, and that the donation is in fact tax deductible. If neither of those hold then the relation is $100 > $0. People have similarly mistaken ideas about so-called write-offs: [VIDEO]"}]},"Yf1o0TQzry8":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it mainly for Sutskever's statements that training data is far from exhausted.","q":{"x":"Ilya Sutskever from OpenAI saying that the data situation is good and there's more data to train on","i":35605038,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":44780223,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-08","story":"Persona vectors: Monitoring and controlling character traits in language models","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks for that good summary. > I see how Ilya was right There are still some things Ilya[0] (and Hinton[1]). The parts I'm quoting here are an example of \"that reddit comment\" that sounds right but is very wrong, and something we know is wrong (and have known it is wrong for hundreds of years!). Yet, it is also something we keep having to learn. It's both obvious and not obvious, but you can make models that are good at predicting things without understanding them. Let me break this down for some clarity. I'm using \"model\" in a broad and general sense. Not just ML models, any mathematical model, or even any mental model. By \"being good at predicting things\" I mean that it can make accurate predictions. The crux of it all is defining the \"understanding\" part. To do that, I need to explain a little bit about what a physicist actually does, and more precisely, metaphysics. People think they crunch numbers, but no, they are symbol manipulators. In physics you care about things like a Hamiltonian or Lagrangian, you care about the form of an equation. The reason for this is it creates a counterfactual model. F=ma (or F=dp/dt) is counterfactual. You can ask \"what if m was 10kg instead of 5kg\" after the fact and get the answer. But this isn't the only way to model things. If you look at the history of science (and this is the \"obvious\" part) you'll notice that they had working models but they were incorrect. We now know that the Ptolemaic model (geocentrism) is incorrect, but it did"},{"id":44156397,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-06","story":"How can AI researchers save energy? By going backward","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> What does it mean for computation to have a direction? Actually, all computation has a directionality! This is actually a subject that I get really excited about ^__^ Think about this, we have a function f, with input x, and output y. f(x) -> y We'd even use that notation! This is our direction. Now, the reverse actually gets a bit tricky. If the reverse is straightforward, our function has an inverse. But, it might not always. So our function f(x)=mx+b is invertible, because we can write x = (f(x)-b)/m (well... m can't be 0), which provides a unique solution. Every x corresponds to a unique f(x). But if instead, we have the function f(x) = x^2, this is not true! x = sqrt(f(x)), and here every f(x) corresponds to both x and -x! They are not unique. We can start adopting the language of images and preimages if you want to start heading down that route. There's a lot of interesting phenomena here and if you hadn't already guessed it, yes, this is related to the P=NP problem! An easy way to see this visually is to write down the computational circuit. Polylog actually has a really good video and will make the connection to P vs NP[0] In the context of machine learning, a Normalizing Flow would be invertible, while a diffusion model is reversible. A pet peeve of mine is that in ML people (I'm a ML researcher) call it the \"inverse problem\", such as GAN-Inversion, but that is a misnomer and we shouldn't propagate it... This also has to do with the naivety of these statements...[1"},{"id":44093480,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-05","story":"Chomsky on what ChatGPT is good for (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think many people are missing the core of what Chomsky is saying. It is often easy to miscommunicate and I think this is primarily what is happening. I think the analogy he gives here really helps emphasize what he's trying to say. If you're only going to read one part, I think it is this: | I mentioned insect navigation, which is an astonishing achievement. Insect scientists have made much progress in studying how it is achieved, though the neurophysiology, a very difficult matter, remains elusive, along with evolution of the systems. The same is true of the amazing feats of birds and sea turtles that travel thousands of miles and unerringly return to the place of origin. | Suppose Tom Jones, a proponent of engineering AI, comes along and says: “Your work has all been refuted. The problem is solved. Commercial airline pilots achieve the same or even better results all the time.” | If even bothering to respond, we’d laugh. | Take the case of the seafaring exploits of Polynesians, still alive among Indigenous tribes, using stars, wind, currents to land their canoes at a designated spot hundreds of miles away. This too has been the topic of much research to find out how they do it. Tom Jones has the answer: “Stop wasting your time; naval vessels do it all the time.” | Same response. It is easy to look at metrics of performance and call things solved. But there's much more depth to these problems than our abilities to solve some task. It's not about just the ability to do some"},{"id":35476858,"author":"lexandstuff","month":"2023-04","story":"Behind the curtain: what it feels like to work in AI right now","stance":"reference","why":"Sutskever says they are far from exhausting data.","quote":"he indicates that they aren't even close to tapping out of data.","text":"In this interview [1] with Ilya Sutskever, he indicates that they aren't even close to tapping out of data. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35604818,"author":"machdiamonds","month":"2023-04","story":"OpenAI’s CEO Says the Age of Giant AI Models Is Already Over","stance":"reference","why":"Sutskever says the data situation is still quite good.","quote":"Yeah, I would say the data situation is still quite good. There's still lots to go","text":"Ilya Sutskever (OpenAI Chief Scientist): \"Yeah, I would say the data situation is still quite good. There's still lots to go\" - [VIDEO] There was a rumor that they were going to use Whisper to transcribe YouTube videos and use that for training. Since it's multimodal, incorporating video frames alongside the transcriptions could significantly enhance its performance."},{"id":35605038,"author":"jstx1","month":"2023-04","story":"OpenAI’s CEO Says the Age of Giant AI Models Is Already Over","stance":"reference","why":"Sutskever says there is more data to train on.","quote":"Ilya Sutskever from OpenAI saying that the data situation is good and there's more data to train on","text":"Ilya Sutskever from OpenAI saying that the data situation is good and there's more data to train on - [VIDEO]"},{"id":44090740,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-05","story":"The \"AI 2027\" Scenario: How realistic is it?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I do say the same think about JKR, btw. And for the same things, because the content she writes. I think you focused on the fanfic part and not the part where I'm criticizing where they say their stuff is the most important to keep humanity alive and they're charging money for it. Meanwhile, you may notice in academia we publish papers to make them freely available, like on arXiv. If it is that important that people need to know, you make it available. The second person, Hinton, is not as good of an authority as you'd expect. Though I do understand why people take him seriously. Fwiw, his Nobel was wildly controversial. Be careful, prizes often have political components. I have degrees in both CS and physics (and am an ML researcher) and both communities thought it was really weird. I'll let you guess which community found it insulting. I want to remind you, in 2016 Hinton famously said[0] | Let me start by saying a few things that seem obvious. I think if you work as a radiologist, you're like the coyote that's already over the edge of the cliff, but hasn't yet looked down so doesn't realize there's no ground beneath him. People should stop training radiologists now. It's just completely obvious that within 5 years that deep learning is going to be better than radiologists because it's going to get a lot more experience. It might take 10 years, but we've got plenty of radiologists already. We're 10 years in now. Hinton has shown he's about as good at making predictions as Mu"},{"id":44092750,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-05","story":"Chomsky on what ChatGPT is good for (2023)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I see my peers at the top like Hinton[0] and Sutskever[1] making absurd claims.","text":"> The fact that we have figured out how to translate language into something a computer can \"understand\" should thrill linguists. I think they are really excited by this. There seems no deficiency of linguists using these machines. But I think it is important to distinguish the ability to understand language and translate it. Enough that you yourself put quotes around \"understanding\". This can often be a challenge for many translators, not knowing how to properly translate something because of underlying context. Our communication runs far deeper than the words we speak or write on a page. This is much of what linguistics is about, this depth. (Or at least that's what they've told me, since I'm not a linguist) This seems to be the distinction Chomsky is trying to make. > The main debate now is over the semantics of words like \"understanding\" and whether or not an LLM is conscious in the same way as a human being (it isn't). Exactly. Here, I'm on the side of Chomsky and I don't think there's much of a debate to be had. We have a long history of being able to make accurate predictions while erroneously understanding the underlying causal nature. My background is physics, and I moved into CS (degrees in both), working on ML. I see my peers at the top like Hinton[0] and Sutskever[1] making absurd claims. I call them absurd, because it is a mistake we've made over and over in the field of physics[2,3]. One of those lessons you learn again and again, because it is so easy to make t"}]},"_8fU2QG-lV0":{"wy":"HN cites it as a record of the CSGO gambling controversy and of undisclosed influencer ownership.","m":[{"id":14936693,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2017-08","story":"Anonymous LLC","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it's an example of the type of business an anonymous LLC would be used for","text":"It would've prevented [VIDEO] from getting caught. Not that that's a good thing, but it's an example of the type of business an anonymous LLC would be used for."},{"id":12034340,"author":"Mao_Zedang","month":"2016-07","story":"Popular YouTubers and Valve caught in underage gambling scandal","stance":"reference","why":"Video discussing the CSGO gambling controversy.","quote":"Video discussing the situation","text":"Video discussing the situation: [VIDEO] Counter-Strike player files suit against Valve over ‘illegal gambling’ surrounding CSGO [LINK] [LINK] Previous Discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":12079259,"author":"a_small_island","month":"2016-07","story":"Warner Bros Settles FTC Charges It Failed to Disclose It Paid Online Influencers","stance":"reference","why":"Example of undisclosed influencer ownership in CSGOLotto controversy.","quote":"caught up in a seemingly worse controversy of non disclosure and more","text":">\"Instead, according to the complaint, Warner Bros. instructed influencers to place the disclosures in the description box appearing below the video. Because Warner Bros. also required other information to be placed in that box, the vast majority of sponsorship disclosures appeared “below the fold,” visible only if consumers clicked on the “Show More” button in the description box. In addition, when influencers posted YouTube videos on Facebook or Twitter, the posting did not include the “Show More” button, making it even less likely that consumers would see the sponsorship disclosures.\" So a disclosure in the notes box below a video is not enough. I wonder what this will mean for the guys from CSGOLotto [0] who are caught up in a seemingly worse controversy of non disclosure and more. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14883093,"author":"meowface","month":"2017-07","story":"Timeline of the ChainCoin Scam – Massive Cryptocurrency Pump and Dump Scheme","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They created a Counter-Strike \"lottery\" website and uploaded a lot of videos of themselves winning big","text":"There was a not-dissimilar scam orchestrated by some popular Youtube channels last year, also mostly targeted at their own followers and fans: [VIDEO] They created a Counter-Strike \"lottery\" website and uploaded a lot of videos of themselves winning big, while never disclosing that they were the secret owners of the site. Of course, their supposed recorded lottery winnings were staged as a way to drive traffic and money to the site."}]},"_fV-C1Ag5sI":{"wy":"Cited, without much elaboration, by commenters who think mainstream media misinforms about Venezuela.","m":[{"id":18698101,"author":"lustysocietyorg","month":"2018-12","story":"Venezuela Forcibly Converts Pensions into Petro","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This spiteful article is probably part of the US war campaign against Venezuela. Leftist Debunks John Oliver's Venezuela Episode [VIDEO] A Brief History of U.S. Dirty Wars in Central America That Set the Stage for the Refugee Crisis [LINK] US Senator Rubio pushes for Venezuela to be placed on US state terrorism sponsors list – report [LINK] ‘Bolton is preparing plan for my assassination, with help from Bogota’ – Venezuela’s Maduro [LINK] Besides US agents are involved in the media secretly and openly (CNN, Fox News,... promoting the conspiracy and lie that should justify the publically well known war e.g. Vietnam, Yugoslavia, Iraq, Libya, Syria). What western media company is outraged regarding the situation of journalist Julian Assange ? The Guardian/Politico Psyop Against WikiLeaks [LINK] Besides: This comment might be my last comment on HN because I can not create replies because I am posting too fast according to HN."},{"id":17691620,"author":"zyxzevn","month":"2018-08","story":"Venezuela President Maduro 'survives drone attack'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The media dramatically misinforms us about Venezuela","text":"The media dramatically misinforms us about Venezuela: [VIDEO] And calls for war with \"humour\". I must be old, but it looks similar to me as the \"humour\" that Germany had before invading Poland."},{"id":17603603,"author":"gepi79","month":"2018-07","story":"Russian Hackers Reach US Utility Control Rooms, Homeland Security Officials Say","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Kosovo war. Iraq war. Libya war. Regime change in Ukraine. Syria war. Economic sanctions killing hundreds of thousands. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"aI0euMFAWF8":{"wy":"HN uses it purely as a joke reply, for example about flu seasons, big contracts or vaccines.","m":[{"id":47637246,"author":"latexr","month":"2026-04","story":"Scientists are working on \"everything vaccines\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fascinating. Looks like you’re right. However, disease and other stressors can disrupt that equilibrium. [LINK] Which means bats are the Mr Burns of the animal kingdom. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22321990,"author":"balls187","month":"2020-02","story":"Judge Halts Work on Microsoft’s JEDI Contract in Victory for Amazon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Right now, it hardly rates This is an underrated commentary. I'm continually amazed at what transpires in the public forum, leading me to believe that the US Government has been corrupt for quite some time, but never out in the open. Or, it could be like this [VIDEO]"},{"id":21169767,"author":"hardatwork","month":"2019-10","story":"Australia Just Had a Bad Flu Season. That May Be a Warning for the U.S.","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've never had the flu, and I'm curious to know why. I have a few theories: 1) I think not having kids might play a part, as every parent of young kids I know always seems to be catching stuff from them. Edit: maybe the stress of having young kids also plays a part. 2) I don't think it's the food I eat as I eat a pretty poor diet, but I do wonder if it might be the amount of food I eat as it's relatively little (I'm at the low end of the healthy BMI range), and scholar seems to have a few studies relating constant CR to a better immune system. 3) I'm a lifelong public transport user. One the one hand people say this might help build up the immune system but it doesn't quite fit with 1). 4) I avoid touching stuff like public door handles and taps. 5) Obligatory Simpsons explanation [VIDEO]"}]},"ccyu44rsaZo":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as evidence of how much social norms have shifted, given the audience's approval of jokes about domestic violence.","q":{"x":"EDIT: Really, watch the video, it actually applies to the discussion really well.","i":27716374,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30695288,"author":"JKCalhoun","month":"2022-03","story":"The undercover special agent who busted a dangerous biker gang","stance":"reference","why":"1967 TV clip with strong misogyny, Jerry Springer-like.","quote":"Warning, the misogyny is off the charts.","text":"You might enjoy this video from television, 1967: [VIDEO] Warning, the misogyny is off the charts. So weird to be watching something from 1967 and it feels like Jerry Springer."},{"id":27716374,"author":"jgilias","month":"2021-07","story":"When Orcs Were Real","stance":"reference","why":"Audience laughs at wife-beating talk in 1960s, showing shifting morals.","quote":"EDIT: Really, watch the video, it actually applies to the discussion really well.","text":"We really can not know this. We can only infer from cultures and religions in the relatively short timespan covered by written history. And even then, morality has changed significantly through time. A simple example - would you think that someone telling a story about someone else beating his wife to a pulp is something to laugh about? Here, check out how the audience reacts just a bit more than 50 years ago: [VIDEO] I think the only moral that may have been something close to universal throughout times would've been something like: \"Don't kill a member of in-group without a good reason if you're not the head honcho.\" Everything else? We have literally no idea. EDIT: Really, watch the video, it actually applies to the discussion really well. It's basically an argument about morals."},{"id":8456231,"author":"thesteamboat","month":"2014-10","story":"The Alice and Bob After Dinner Speech (1984)","stance":"reference","why":"1967 CBC clip showing audience approval of domestic violence jokes.","quote":"You should really watch the clip","text":"To be fair, the Woody Allen joke is also allegorically about domestic violence and this updated version only reinforced those references, so I don't think your reaction was off base. For even more context, around the same time as the Woody Allen stand-up Hunter S. Thompson gets confronted on CBC by a member of Hells Angels. It shows in stark contrast how much society has matured in 50 years (at least regarding public acceptance of domestic violence). You should really watch the clip, but I've again excerpted choice quotes from the Hells Angels member that the audience reacts favorably to. (At 3:24 in the video) > Junkie George is beating his old lady. Junkie George's dog bit him. To me this is a personal feud. If a guy wants to beat his wife and his dog bites him, that's between the three of 'em. [Audience laughter and applause] and a short time later (5:42 in the video): > To keep a woman in line you got to beat her like a rug once in a while. [Again, audience laughter and applause] [VIDEO]"}]},"jVhlJNJopOQ":{"wy":"Posted as a joke in threads about typefaces and typography.","m":[{"id":46826848,"author":"Apocryphon","month":"2026-01","story":"Buttered Crumpet, a custom typeface for Wallace and Gromit","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I feel like hipster typography is as much an intrinsic part of 2010s culture as cafes that have a barn aesthetic, or startups named after common nouns. SNL made a sketch about Papyrus almost ten years ago: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":39307685,"author":"esafak","month":"2024-02","story":"Web design inspiration catalog","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you are a designer and think about this all day, you might have reason to take offense. Here's a humorous look from the designer's side: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38038897,"author":"HenryBemis","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: Would you buy 'JOI' from bladerunner if it existed?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I assume this is a Blade Runner 2 thingie.. which I actively deny its existence, as the first (and ofc the only one) is one of the best sci-fi movies ever made, so there was never a need to make another one. So your question is moot because there is no JOI in Blade Runner (the good - orthodox - 1!) :) But because I am \"a generous god\" (\"300\" reference), for the fans of Ryan Gosling, you can always watch his SNL - Papyrus skit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37509804,"author":"irrational","month":"2023-09","story":"Using popular font Proxima Nova could get you sued","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Don’t you dare forget Papyrus! [VIDEO] But, the reality is 99.9999% of websites can use one of the free Google fonts. The other .0001% could too, but they want to show that they are not like all the other websites."},{"id":37170070,"author":"Terr_","month":"2023-08","story":"Going back to the old (pre-X) Twitter iOS app","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It has recontextualised every other redesign I can remember disdaining in my life. But does it go so far that it even excuses Papyrus? :P [VIDEO]"},{"id":36897904,"author":"esafak","month":"2023-07","story":"KernType – A Letter Spacing Game","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Papyrus! As it happens I have a friend who worked on the Avatar poster and he loves this SNL sketch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35926554,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-05","story":"Intel One Mono Typeface","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"He just highlighted Avatar, he clicked the drop down menu, and then he randomly selected Papyrus, like a thoughtless child just wandering by a garden yanking leaves along the way.\" [1] As iconic a moment as the creation of the first sans serif font [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] \"The earliest printing typefaces which omitted serifs were not intended to render contemporary texts, but to represent inscriptions in Ancient Greek and Etruscan. Thus, Thomas Dempster's De Etruria regali libri VII (1723), used special types intended for the representation of Etruscan epigraphy, and in c. 1745, the Caslon foundry made Etruscan types for pamphlets written by Etruscan scholar John Swinton.\" [LINK] ."},{"id":35050487,"author":"thegabriele","month":"2023-03","story":"Word-as-Image for Semantic Typography","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Avatar (billions in box office) and papyrus come to mind (classic: [VIDEO] ). Sometimes the triteness it's just...ok?"},{"id":28858433,"author":"45ure","month":"2021-10","story":"Typography of 2001: A Space Odyssey (2014)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It does seem to stir up some strong emotions. Years after Avatar's release, there's one thing Steven (Ryan Gosling) just can't get over. [VIDEO]"}]},"lugeruSbnAE":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a joke or a demonstration of Alexa waking up on cue.","m":[{"id":40839268,"author":"shagie","month":"2024-06","story":"Ask HN: How does Alexa avoid interrupting itself when saying its own name?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"If you watch you will get Alexa waking up.","text":"If you watch [VIDEO] you will get Alexa waking up. Meanwhile [LINK] and [LINK] and don't. Play them on a TV or tiny computer speaker. You can hear 3 kHz quite well. [LINK] And we can hear up to 20 kHz when young (that's 3 octaves higher than 3 kHz). [LINK] And while this is audio people talking about sound... [LINK] > 3k is commonly the most sensitive frequency for human ears - we use it for testing wow and flutter on tape machines; you can hear pitch variation there easiest. 3k is a ballpark, but a good one - I think it works anywhere in that region 2-4k, but usually 3k is THE ONE. > When I have a harsh fuzz guitar or trashy cymbal, I often try to just tuck down the 3k region, and suddenly it no longer covers everything. It still sounds thick and harsh, but nothing kills your ears. > ... > Cutting at 3k can help hide out of tune instruments and bad pitch on vocals as well. --- Late edit. Extracting the audio of the \"Alexa looses her voice\" and sending it into [LINK] - watch at the range around 5.1 kHz (about the center of the 3 kHz to 6 kHz octave). It starts out with: [LINK] That spike is the actress saying \"Alexa\" and you can see the unnatural gap in that range. Also, there is sound in that mp3 all the way out to 14 kHz."},{"id":35283655,"author":"eric-hu","month":"2023-03","story":"Cheating Is All You Need","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Kind of prescient.","text":"The South Park: Post Covid special had Amazon Alexa androids who were girlfriends to some characters. The boyfriend had to walk on eggshells with some topics or Alexa would go into a screaming fit. Kind of prescient. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29667280,"author":"type0","month":"2021-12","story":"Alexa is nagging you more because Amazon knows you don’t care about new features","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Will Alexa evolve into something crazy like what was depicted in South Park Post COVID ? I think it will. [VIDEO]"}]},"tq_KOmXyVDo":{"wy":"Cited in AI-art and video-stylization threads as an example of a manual, artist-driven alternative to AI tools, and as a demo of EbSynth.","q":{"x":"His method is the literal opposite of using an AI tool.","i":34377428,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46014103,"author":"wonger_","month":"2025-11","story":"Ask HN: How to generate flipbook-style animations from video?","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of EbSynth video-to-animation","quote":"","text":"Looks like rotoscoping: [LINK] . The closest you would get is maybe an edge detection filter. Or AI rotoscoping: [LINK] . Or maybe EbSynth, as demoed by this guy: [VIDEO] But it never looks as nice as the hand-drawn way."},{"id":34377428,"author":"Cipater","month":"2023-01","story":"ChatGPT Can't Kill Anything Worth Preserving","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the painstaking manual frame-by-frame method","quote":"His method is the literal opposite of using an AI tool.","text":">There's a popular youtuber named Joel Haver who films videos, then uses an AI tool to convert them into animations, so they can be magically put into a space or fantasy setting. Hang on, AI tool? Joel Haver converts every frame manually. His method is the literal opposite of using an AI tool. It's incredibly painstaking. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32966498,"author":"Hamcha","month":"2022-09","story":"VToonify: Controllable High-Resolution Portrait Video Style Transfer","stance":"reference","why":"Shows EbSynth giving Joel Haver a trademark style","quote":"","text":"As someone who is in touch with a lot of people acting as VTubers in order to keep privacy while being able to express themselves, I'm very happy to see this! EbSynth gave Joel Haver a trademark style[1], and I feel this might be the next step. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32171653,"author":"keawade","month":"2022-07","story":"MegaPortraits: One-Shot Megapixel Neural Head Avatars","stance":"reference","why":"Example of AI-assisted rotoscoping","quote":"","text":"We've got AI assisted rotoscoping already and while it looks a bit janky at times its still a whole lot faster than doing it all by hand. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31364231,"author":"Asooka","month":"2022-05","story":"We asked 100 humans to draw the DALL·E prompts","stance":"reference","why":"Joel Haver explains his rotoscoping animation process","quote":"He explains his process here","text":"A good example are Joel Haver's videos on youtube. He explains his process here: [VIDEO] . He produces animations by rotoscoping live footage, with only a few keyframes being done by hand and the rest by AI, using something similar to style transfer."}]},"u1Ds9CeG-VY":{"wy":"Used as a joke or shorthand for ridiculous movie hacking and unrealistic depictions of software work.","m":[{"id":21773178,"author":"MichaelApproved","month":"2019-12","story":"Music for Programming (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is a reference to the ridiculous scene from the 2001 movie Swordfish","text":"This is a reference to the ridiculous scene from the 2001 movie Swordfish where Hugh Jackman is writing a program to hack into a bank network. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47065781,"author":"whynotmaybe","month":"2026-02","story":"Tesla Sales Down 55% UK, 58% Spain, 59% Germany, 81% Netherlands, 93% Norway","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just like watching the movie Hackers and hearing how he hacked the gibson or the 7 screens setup in swordfish : [VIDEO]"},{"id":32866493,"author":"acidburnNSA","month":"2022-09","story":"The movie Hackers was released 27 years ago","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That \"building the worm\" scene has got to be my favorite depiction of software development of all time.","text":"That \"building the worm\" scene has got to be my favorite depiction of software development of all time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23358548,"author":"vijucat","month":"2020-05","story":"Career Choice Tip: Cybercrime Is Mostly Boring","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"as a counterexample of what hacking does NOT look like","text":"This article missed a great opportunity to post that hacking scene from Swordfish as a counterexample of what hacking does NOT look like: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22744373,"author":"foxfired","month":"2020-04","story":"Vim rendered on a cube for no reason","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Turns out he was just assembling the crypto algorithm using vim.","text":"And here I was making fun of the Swordfish hack scene [1]. Turns out he was just assembling the crypto algorithm using vim. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]}});
